tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Mordor Economy"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a manually-cleaned YouTube transcript (MJSullivan's own scrape and cleanup — no official downloadable transcript exists for this episode, matching the same real gap flagged for RR-13 and Frankly_47) plus official show notes. Real, direct origin explanation for one of this graph's own four core economic ScenarioFacets: Nate lays out, in his own words, the biophysical logic behind a 'Mordor economy' — a path where continued economic growth requires directing an ever-larger share of energy and GDP toward procuring energy itself (rising from a real historical low of ~5% in 1999, per Carey King's own research, toward 15% or more) and toward remediating the ecological damage from burning it, leaving less and less surplus for everything else society values. Grounds this in the maximum power principle and Kleiber's Law (organisms' metabolic energy use scaling to the 3/4 power of mass, a scaling relationship Nate argues also holds for national economies), and in Tim Garrett's own real framing of civilization as an energy-dissipating structure. Frames Mordor and the Great Simplification as the two real default trajectories, with the explicit stated goal of the whole show being to change the initial conditions that determine which one a society actually lands in."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/23-mordor-economy> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_209_BalazsMatics, tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_55_7KeyInterventionsForTheFuture ;
    dct:source <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/s/F23-The-Mordor-Economy-Graphs-for-Show-Notes.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens by explicitly naming its own two prior real airings of this idea — the 2021 Earth Day talk, and 'last week' on Art Berman's own podcast — before walking through the logic in full for the first time on TGS itself. The real analytical core is a direct biophysical rebuttal of a 'we'll just get more efficient' techno-optimist response: Nate cites a real, specific figure (33% more efficient in energy-to-GDP terms since 1995, alongside 50% more total energy used in that same period) as live evidence that efficiency gains have driven higher consumption, not lower, a real-world instance of Jevons Paradox applied at civilizational scale. The Bank of Japan owning over 50% of its own government's bonds is used as a single, concrete, real data point standing in for the much larger abstract claim that debt issuance lets economies keep growing on paper even as real net energy declines — Nate is candid about revising his own prior thinking here, directly naming a 'logical mistake I made a decade or so ago' in assuming GDP would track net energy decline directly, corrected once he accounted for debt's own real capacity to paper over that gap. Closes by explicitly naming the Great Simplification as the real alternative to a Mordor economy, framing this whole show's actual purpose as changing the 'initial conditions' that determine which path a society ends up on, rather than treating either as fixed or inevitable."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "CORRECTED 2026-08-30: an earlier draft of this stub claimed the episode title's use of 'Mordor' was 'structurally unrelated' to this graph's own ScenarioFacet.Mordor, calling it 'a coincidental echo... not a citation relationship' — this was wrong, based only on a title-level guess before real content was available. The real transcript and show notes confirm this episode is the direct, original explanation of the Mordor economy concept the ScenarioFacet is itself named after — dcterms:references added on ScenarioFacet.Mordor accordingly; see that individual's own scopeNote. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tim Garrett (real energy/thermodynamics researcher, directly cited, not yet a Persona in this graph); the 2021 Earth Day talk (real, distinct prior airing of this same material, external to the numbered TGS/Frankly/RR series so out of this class's scope); episode #37 (Martin Scheringer, PFAS pollution) — already a real, deferred citation elsewhere in this graph (RR-15's own scopeNote), independently reconfirmed here."@en ;
    .


