tgs:Monologue.Frankly_25_LossAversion a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Loss Aversion"@en ;
    skos:definition "After a snowshoe with the dogs, Nate on loss aversion, the cognitive bias by which losses weigh about twice as heavily as equivalent gains (tgs:Concept.LossAversion). He met it in his twenties managing money for billionaires at Salomon Brothers and Lehman Brothers: an 80-year-old holding $850 million in Treasuries furious over one delayed $50,000 coupon; a man livid when an $80 million windfall shrank to $68 million. It has an evolutionary logic -- extra food mattered little, lost food could mean death -- and he expects it to be one of the main biases of the coming 'financial biophysical rubber band snapping moment,' when musical-chair claims meet what reality can service. The story of the Great Simplification itself threatens identities built on progress; social media turns identity into a supernormal stimulus defended like calories; and loss aversion keeps cultures choosing fixes such as the Green New Deal that ask us to give up nothing. Counters: measure self-worth by natural, social, built and human capital rather than net worth, and widen loss aversion to the deep-time loss of species and ecosystems."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-03-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-03-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/25-loss-aversion> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cKqu3gH1Mz4> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.LossAversion, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital, tgs:Concept.WideBoundary ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "SETTING: back from a snowshoe with the dogs; a more involved Frankly on probability, certainty and identity is promised for next week. WALL STREET STORIES: in his mid-twenties Nate managed money and traded for billionaires at Salomon Brothers and Lehman Brothers. An 80-year-old owner of New York apartment buildings, holding $850 million in US Treasuries and waiting each day for coupon payments, became 'ballistic' when a CUSIP problem delayed one $50,000 interest payment. Another client whose company stock, worth about $2 a share, drew a $24 buyout -- an $80 million windfall -- was 'mad, livid, sad' when due diligence cut the price 15 percent, to $68 million. THE BIAS (tgs:Concept.LossAversion): years later he learned its name; from $10,000, gaining $1,000 and then losing $1,000 leaves you where you started, but research in humans and other species finds the loss feels about twice as strong. The evolutionary reason: with enough food to survive, a little extra made no difference, but a loss could mean death. WHAT IS COMING: he expects loss aversion to be one of the main biases of 'this financial biophysical rubber band snapping moment' in the coming decade, when our 'musical chair claims of digits and electrons and paper' (tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment) exceed what our biophysical situation can service and people lose what they think they own and their picture of the future; the 1929 crash, with people jumping from windows after losing 80 percent in a day, shows how immediate loss destroys perspective. IDENTITY: the Great Simplification story itself -- debt papering over claims, living with 30 percent less (tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification) -- threatens identities built on progress and on being richer in the future, so the idea of the end of growth triggers loss aversion before the physical reality does. On social media people attach identity to tribe and ideology, and contrary evidence registers as loss; identity there is a supernormal stimulus (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli; tgs:Work.RealityBlind) that feels like losing calories, status and reputation, which is why online discourse is so vitriolic, and why AI is a risk: we will not know what to believe and will feel our identities slipping. CULTURAL STORIES: fixes such as the Green New Deal are popular because we will not give up anything we are used to, limiting us to responses that cannot physically work; 'PV and wind turbines and other things can power a decent civilization, just not this one.' RESPONSES: do we want to be the things we own? Some of the happiest people he has met, in Ecuador and Zambia, have few possessions but a smile; shifting self-worth from material net worth to natural, social, built and human capital may be a great adaptive strategy, 'not to mention being true' (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital). A wider-boundary lens (tgs:Concept.WideBoundary): deep-time loss aversion -- our species has owned a world and is losing it, blinded to slow-motion loss by stimulating illusions of immediate gain; we feel aversion only to losing what we know we have, so perhaps we should reset it toward 'those things that are priceless in a lonely universe.' Loss aversion to the end of growth is not always the right response: 'ask any cancer survivor.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-19 from MJSullivan's transcript and the episode page's show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub; the replay ID is from the episode page, and no transcript PDF is published. The loss-aversion and supernormal-stimuli links in the show notes are pages of Reality Blind, so tgs:Work.RealityBlind is referenced. Not built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment and no bridge proposed. Spoken figures recorded but not checked: the client's $850 million and $50,000 coupon; the $80 and $68 million windfall (a 15 percent cut of $80 million is $68 million); losses felt 'twice as strong.' The transcript reads 'The Great implification,' kept as a typo for Great Simplification. The next week's promised Frankly on probability, identity and certainty is not identified here. Candidate concept named but NOT minted: deep-time loss aversion (folded into tgs:Concept.LossAversion)."@en ;
    .

