tgs:Monologue.Frankly_51_SystemicThemesFor2024 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Systemic Themes for 2024"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's last Frankly of 2023, introduced by a short preface defending its darkness: he is acting 'as a witness and a synthesizer of how these various risks fit together,' not predicting. Themes to watch in 2024: climate, where James Hansen and Leon Simons expect a switch to El Niño and reduced aerosol masking to add about half a degree, perhaps 2 °C for a month by summer, which could be an emotional wake-up call; rising aggregate cognitive dissonance as futures people tied their identities to fail (tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance); geopolitics in a shift from a unipolar to a multipolar world, with Ukraine unresolved and choke points under strain at Panama, Suez and Hormuz for a six-continent supply chain predicated on peace, credit and affordable oil; AI amplifying supernormal stimuli; a stretching of haves and have-nots as the COVID stimulus wears off; a polarized US election that will anger a third of the country whoever wins; and central banks, their finger in the financial dike since 2009, meeting the biophysical narrative."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-12-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-12-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/51-systemic-themes-for-2024> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xgMv73iabjQ> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance, tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking, tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility,
        tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.GlobalWarmingInThePipeline2023, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "PREFACE: his staff and inner circle found the 13-minute Frankly 'pretty doom and gloom' for the holidays; Nate will not apologize, because five or ten years ago one could not imagine something going wrong in the world next week, and now everything is interconnected; he wants candor. He is about to take a six-week technology hiatus, explained in his next Frankly, and is not predicting the future but explaining how the system's parts fit together. CLIMATE: Leon Simons, whose podcast comes out in January, has done 'a Sherlock Holmes treatment' of how sulfate aerosols have masked warming from Earth's energy imbalance (tgs:Concept.AerosolMasking); he and James Hansen (tgs:Work.GlobalWarmingInThePipeline2023) expect the shift from La Niña to El Niño and reduced aerosol masking to take late-2023 warming of 1.4 or 1.5 °C up another half degree, so that by summer we 'might for a month or so see 2 degrees Celsius' over pre-industrial -- horrible for Europe and the Asian subcontinent, with many deaths; piercing 2 °C might be an emotional wake-up call, 'but it's kind of like arguing with a forest fire.' COGNITIVE DISSONANCE (tgs:Concept.AggregateCognitiveDissonance): such events 'kick in antibodies' in people who built identities and reputations around them not happening (tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies) -- the end of growth, AI, anything else; as the futures people attached their identities, hopes and beliefs to fail, and the opposite happens, they adopt strange stories, and cognitive dissonance 'with humans in aggregate' will accelerate; he wonders what climate deniers will say at 2 °C. GEOPOLITICS: a changing world order; the US-NATO-Russia battle in Ukraine is not over, each side growing desperate, with risks of false flags; a move 'from a unipolar world to a multi-polar world' of new financial, energy and commodity partnerships. CHOKE POINTS: drought leaves the Panama Canal short of water to fill its locks; Houthi attacks near Yemen are driving shippers away from Suez around southern Africa, adding a week or two to Singapore-Rotterdam and roughly doubling cost, which he expects the US to stop by protecting the canal; the biggest is the Strait of Hormuz, about 20 percent of world oil, some 17 million barrels a day. The six-continent supply chain is 'predicated on peace and credit and available affordable oil,' all less certain now (tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility). AI: people in the box seats at a football game watching their phones show 'hypernormal stimuli' shouting louder than the real game (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli), and AI will exponentially increase that, losing people to fantasy, with systemic risks, energy demands and environmental damage. HAVES AND HAVE-NOTS (tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots): he may do another Frankly on how to define them, since much of what we have is not monetary; in the US the COVID stimulus 'sugar high' is running out, with higher interest rates and car and house payments, and per last week's Reality Roundtable (tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind) about 40 percent of households are asset-limited and income-constrained though employed; when people cannot afford health insurance, rent and food, it will be bad. POLITICS: the US election holds things in a holding pattern until autumn, and whoever wins, Biden or Trump, a third of the country will be angry; tribes splinter because the present is tougher than the past and AI will hone in on confirmation bias, with deep fakes; the problems are the same whoever wins, so we need civic resolve to set aside identities and blame; 'if we can't talk to our neighbors or people that disagree with us then we're not going to be able to solve these issues.' FINANCE: central banks have kept 'a finger in the financial dike'; temporary policies from the 2009 Great Recession continue, balance sheets have ballooned, the Bank of Japan buys its own bonds and stocks, and another banking crisis may come; the larger story is that 'the central bank monetary narrative meets the biophysical ecological narrative' -- with oil peaking and climate requiring spending, central banks cannot simply cut rates without inflation. There is much work to do in our communities and lives."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from a transcript MJSullivan hand-scraped (chapter headings from the video) and show notes from the episode page; no transcript PDF is published. The replay ID was taken from the episode page, which labels the link 'Spotify' although it is a YouTube short link. The hand-scraped transcript ends mid-sentence ('before I leave be'). Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because it is the earliest statement in the graph of Nate's 'antibodies' (tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies, Frankly_91) and previews his haves and have-nots (Frankly_53); no new Schmachtenberger bridge is proposed here, though the cognitive-dissonance theme sits close to Daniel's tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty (TGS_31). Show-note figure preferred: 41 percent of US households are ALICE or below the federal poverty line (29 plus 12; spoken: 40). Nate's forecasts recorded, not endorsed: a month at 2 °C by summer 2024 (as far as this graph records, monthly global anomalies in 2024 peaked well below that, around 1.6 to 1.7 °C in ERA5, though 2024 was the first calendar year above 1.5 °C); US protection of Suez. Spoken figures not checked: Hormuz at about 20 percent of world oil, some 17 million barrels a day; rerouting roughly doubling shipping cost. Nate attributes the switch to the Pacific Decadal Oscillation, but La Niña and El Niño are phases of ENSO. Leon Simons's episode is tgs:Interview.TGS_105_LeonSimons, released after this Frankly, so it is not referenced; the show notes' 'Nate on AI' video (zY29LjWYHIo) is not matched in the graph. Transcript artifacts: '20124' is 2024; 'cander' is candor; 'Pacific decal oscillation' is decadal; 'linia' is La Niña; 'Pierce' is pierce; 'Cape Horn in South Africa' is the Cape of Good Hope; 'roddam' is Rotterdam; 'Suz' is Suez."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Behavioral Stack"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded before a five-week technology fast and 'limbic reset' with a coach Nate keeps confidential, an explanation of why he is going. Wisdom traditions prescribed 40 days or months of silence because daily life fragments us, pushing us toward consumption, left-brain dominance and chronic illness; afterwards he will spend a week with Dick Schwartz of Internal Family Systems, whose 'parts' are horizontal. Nate's own model is vertical (tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack): microbiome, cells, organs, enteric system, reptilian system, limbic system and cognition, each top layer with a binary state -- stable or unstable, ease or dis-ease, satisfied or dissatisfied, serene or agitated. We think cognition is the elephant; it is the small rider, captured by the layers beneath, so facts lead to knowing about rather than knowing. The culture has let dopamine, the 'doing' pole, hijack even healthy 'being' activities (tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture). His coach's advice: a quiver of alternatives to the phone, two fixed mealtimes, three breaths at each alarm, rehearsing gratitude or humor, and a shift from focus to awareness (silent Saturdays). Healing emotional states is 'as or more important than educating humans about more facts,' and vertically stacked groups, 'rocks in the river,' could have outsized impact."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-12-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-01-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/52-the-behavioral-stack> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QynYlsW35Sw> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack, tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture, tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement, tgs:Concept.DividedBrain,
        tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "THE LIMBIC RESET: when this airs Nate is midway through a five-week technology fast, a 'limbic reset' in his coach's words. 'I'm an educator, I'm a synthesizer, I am not a guru,' but also a participant learning somatic and brain behavior himself; he cycles through coaches and therapists like working out. The new coach, kept confidential for now but perhaps on a 2024 roundtable with his young students, is the first whose every word hit him as true; three days with him in San Diego in February -- cooking, singing, stories, hikes -- left Nate feeling normal and refreshed. The coach wanted people working on the metacrisis together for six weeks; Nate, with 10 or 11 podcasts pre-recorded and staff check-ins once or twice a week, is going for five. WISDOM TRADITIONS AND PARTS: the 40 days and 40 nights of the Bible and a month of silence in many ancient traditions (three months for teachers, to regain their voice) were coping mechanisms because 'humans get fragmented'; the results of fragmentation are leaning on consumption of energy, food, dopamine and activity; left-brain dominance, 'breaking down the world into parts instead of looking at it holistically' (tgs:Concept.DividedBrain); and progressive, eventually chronic, disease. Afterward he will spend a week with Dick Schwartz (tgs:Persona.RichardSchwartz) of Internal Family Systems, whose parts work integrates competing parts of the mind -- in Nate's case the golden retriever, the control freak, the clown or jester, the teacher -- 'but none of these parts is me.' Schwartz's parts are horizontal; Nate turns to vertical parts in the body. THE STACK (tgs:Concept.VerticalBehavioralStack): seven evolutionary layers -- microbiome, cells, organs, the enteric system, the reptilian system, the limbic system and cognition. He discusses the top four: the enteric system governs the homeostasis of daily routines, stable or unstable; the reptilian system, parasympathetic and sympathetic, ease or dis-ease, fight, flight or freeze; the limbic system around the hippocampus and amygdala, satisfied or dissatisfied, and if dissatisfied seeking stimulation, consumption or status; and cognition, our beliefs, stories, facts and goals about oil depletion and CO2, serene or confused and agitated. We think cognition is the elephant and the rest subservient; the reality is the opposite -- cognition 'is the tiny little rider on the elephant,' and each lower layer has more power, so if we are not stable, at ease and satisfied, we only know about things rather than knowing them. DOPAMINE CULTURE (tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture): four neurotransmitter poles -- dopamine and endorphin for doing, goal-seeking (another podcast, more money, a marathon), serotonin and oxytocin for being; nearly everything the culture offers has made dopamine a proxy for the others, even healthy things like yoga (the cool outfit, flexing for attractive classmates) or social sharing (high-grading how we appear), so the whole culture has become dopamine-hijacked 'in a positive feedback sort of way' (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli). IMPLICATIONS: most people have trauma and are stuck in fight, flight or freeze, so facts are captured by something deeper in their bodies; most have 'me versus we' in the limbic system and live on a rat-race treadmill of seeking; more facts bring confusion or more dopamine rather than awareness that changes behavior. Groups of vertically stacked humans -- stable, satisfied, 'we over me' -- could have outsized impact, and training young people in a six-week setting to reset their limbic systems, as his coach does with 20-year-olds, could be a pillar of social change. THE COACH'S ADVICE: a quiver of alternative activities for seeking moments -- walking in nature, cooking with friends, singing, chanting, sitting with his ducks; split the day with meals at 11 a.m. and 5:30 p.m. so the enteric system is not constantly unstable; take three deep breaths at every alarming email or ocean-temperature headline, 10 or 20 times a day, teaching the reptilian system there is no saber-toothed tiger; rehearse responding with gratitude or humor; and shift from focus to awareness, the origin of his 'silent Saturdays,' at which he has had marginal success. CONCLUSIONS: 'we don't so much have an environmental problem or an economic problem or an energy depletion problem as much as we have a human brain mismatch with our ancestral environment' (tgs:Concept.Wetware); 'healing of our emotional states is as or more important than educating humans about more facts' (tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement); 'homoeconomicus is not compatible with homobiospherus,' so a cultural change of consciousness is needed; he wants to learn how 'our wide human phenotype might adapt to a different energetic material cultural setup,' and 'we need more humans acting as rocks in the river as anchors towards this cultural change' (tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver). Back in February."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from a transcript MJSullivan hand-scraped (chapter headings from the video) and show notes from the episode page; no transcript PDF is published. The replay ID is from Nate's Substack post of the same date, which embeds the video, and matches the 'Frankly on the Behavioral Stack' link (QynYlsW35Sw) in Frankly_73's show notes. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because Nate's account of fragmentation -- consumption, left-brain dominance, parts competing within us -- meets Daniel's tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness (TGS_31), and his dopamine-hijacked culture meets Daniel's tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits (TGS_31). This is a bodily stack; Nate's later political-science ladder, tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem (Frankly_162), is a different seven-level model. The elephant-and-rider image is Jonathan Haidt's (The Happiness Hypothesis, 2006), which Nate uses without citing; tgs:Persona.JonathanHaidt exists. Nate says five weeks here; his coach proposed six, and Frankly_51 calls it a six-week hiatus. The hand-scraped transcript retains artifacts: 'limic,' 'lyic' and 'Olympic reset' are limbic; 'anic' and 'inic' are enteric; 'fight ORF flight' is fight or flight; 'disase' is dis-ease; 'fun lever' is fun lover; 'dope mean' is dopamine; 'SE pleasure' is probably seeking pleasure; 'Thunder says' is probably a funder saying; 'homo biospheres' is homobiospherus, Nate's made-up Latin-sounding counterpart to Homo economicus (identified by MJSullivan). Mentioned but not built: the confidential coach. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the limbic reset; homobiospherus (Nate's coined counterpart to Homo economicus); from focus to awareness; silent Saturdays; a quiver of alternatives."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_82_PowerVsLife, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_73_TheSuperorganismAndTheSelf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_55_7KeyInterventionsForTheFuture, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_70_AskMeAnything ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_53_TheHavesAndTheHaveNots a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Haves and the Have-Nots"@en ;
    skos:definition "A follow-up to the Reality Roundtable on poverty, asking what the haves actually have and the have-nots lack. Money is one axis: in the US mean income per capita is about $60,000 but the median about $40,000, and money matters in a culture of rent, utilities and medical bills. But Nate lists many others (tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots): this generation against past and future ones; social wealth, which the monetarily poor in Zambia, Ecuador or Marseille may have and isolated wealthy people lack; skills; physical and mental health; the attention span to go deep rather than live in 'the mental shallows'; a voice; purpose and meaning amid a meaning crisis; a pro-social outlook, since around 10 percent of people have dark-triad traits, more at the top; and the outsized ability to change the system, held by the top few thousand people riding high on the superorganism -- who overlap with the dark triad, 'so that makes it more of a puzzle.' After basic needs are met, money isn't everything."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-12-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-01-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/53-the-haves-and-the-have-nots> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AuEYaWVeUm4> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital, tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast,
        tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "DEFINING HAVES AND HAVE-NOTS: last month's Reality Roundtable on poverty (tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_7_PovertyBlind) distinguished monetary or pecuniary poverty from social, behavioral or community poverty, which led Nate to ask what the haves have. ECONOMIC INEQUALITY: in the US average income per capita is about $60,000 but the median about $40,000, evidence of considerable inequality since a few people earning huge amounts skew the average; money matters in a culture where rent, utilities, medical bills and insurance must be paid, and he expects it to get worse. GENERATIONS: the average human uses about 13 times the goods and services of 200 years ago and the average US resident about 50 times the human of 1800; what will the average person consume 100 years from now, when climate and ecosystems are degraded and the carbon pulse has run its course (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse)? SOCIAL WEALTH: the monetarily poor can be socially rich -- Nate has seen cohesion and resilience in Zambia and Ecuador -- while wealthy people may never leave the house, have few friends and order Amazon trucks; at the community level, friends in Marseille describe a solidarity across socioeconomic, ethnic and religious lines, where people wave and smile, that Paris and Amsterdam lack; 'I would rather have less money and live in a community like that' (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital). SKILLS: today we spend digital claims to hire others, but artisans and crafts people -- carpentry, pottery, ceramics, building -- have skills that will differentiate the haves of the future. HEALTH: downstream effects of COVID, the vaccines, the pandemic and long COVID, endocrine-disrupting chemicals, metabolic problems; 'if you don't have your health, having a lot of money doesn't really help you.' Mental health may be one of the biggest crises of the next decade, with many on medication for depression, anxiety and ADD; community, friends, meaning and skills help. ATTENTION: 'the ghost of dopamine past' (tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast) keeps us in the mental shallows of the pool, unable to experience the deep end, a real problem for people who will need to plant gardens or learn skills; listeners to a 90-minute podcast are self-selected haves -- Nate admits he can't sit through one himself -- and the ability to defer the second marshmallow is 'a huge have for the future.' VOICE: people despair when disconnected from any say in their outcomes; civic discourse beyond voting for president empowers, and even talking to another person about an issue lowers cortisol and boosts helper T cells. Non-human creatures have no voice in the superorganism's pathways and do not know it, so some of us must speak for them. PURPOSE AND MEANING: in Nate's lifetime, success, profits and becoming a millionaire have been the purpose of life, a unique period now ending, while the Abrahamic religions wane -- a meaning crisis; he feels fortunate to have a purpose as a 'golden retriever catalyst' expanding the Overton window, passing the baton, so that we can meet the future halfway. PRO-SOCIAL OUTLOOK: about 10 percent of people are estimated to have the dark triad of narcissism, Machiavellianism and psychopathy, probably more since such people answer surveys strategically, and the traits are much higher among senior government officials and CEOs, as if 'the gauntlet to run requires those traits to get to the top' (tgs:Concept.DarkTriad); a dark-triad person may think everyone is like that, and others may not know such people exist, and the 90 percent must learn to live with the 10. ABILITY TO CHANGE THE SYSTEM: the superorganism and human power dynamics are so strong that 'we're going to need to change the consciousness or the mindset of the top few thousand people riding high on the superorganism'; the rest of us are have-nots in this sense, playing additive but small roles in steering away from the Wile E. Coyote ecological precipice (tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents). But those last two categories overlap -- many with outsized ability to change the future are in the dark-triad demographic -- 'so that makes it more of a puzzle,' and in a double entendre he has just angered and empowered them at once. CLOSE: money isn't everything after basic needs are met; many may be rich in other areas, and vice versa."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from a transcript MJSullivan hand-scraped (chapter headings from the video) and show notes from the episode page; no transcript PDF is published. The replay ID was confirmed from the episode page, which also identifies this as the Frankly on the dark triad cited in TGS_126 (show-note link youtu.be/AuEYaWVeUm4). Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment as the earliest statement in the graph of Nate's 10 percent dark-triad figure and of the puzzle that those able to change the system overlap with the dark triad, which Daniel takes up in TGS_126 (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity). Show-note figures preferred: US mean and median income per capita (FRED); goods and services 13 and 50 times those of 200 years ago (a slide image). Spoken claims recorded but not checked: that discussing an issue lowers cortisol and boosts helper T cells (the show notes link a study). The hand-scraped transcript retains artifacts: 'halves' and 'have knots' are haves and have-nots; 'artisol' is artisans; 'trailes' is trails; 'aded' is ADD; 'Wyly Coyote' is Wile E. Coyote; 'double on Tandra' is double entendre. Mentioned but not referenced: DJ White (linked in the show notes, not named in the transcript), the meaning crisis (show notes link John Vervaeke's series; tgs:Human.JohnVervaeke exists), the Overton window. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the golden retriever catalyst; voice as a form of wealth; skills as the wealth of the future."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_126_AVisionForBetterment ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reflections From India"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's return from six weeks in India, his first visit -- what the show notes call a 'limbic reset.' India, he says, is 'ground zero for many of the things that are going to be coming our way': nearly 1.5 billion people at fifteen times US population density, mostly coal-powered with plans to double coal production by 2030, newly pulled into the global superorganism and its consumerism, and among the first places where heat may become unlivable (tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature, tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration). Yet at less than a tenth of US energy per capita, people seemed happy, stable and warm, with 44% working in agriculture and a deep 'Indic' ethic Nate thinks could play a role in the Great Simplification, perhaps something Gandhian at scale. He closes on the contrast between India, a poor country 'rich in social capital,' and the silent van ride home in Minnesota (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital)."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-02-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-02-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/54-reflections-from-india> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital, tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature, tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.TheMinistryForTheFuture, tgs:Interview.TGS_66_KimStanleyRobinson,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_64_AndrewMillison ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "RE-ENTRY: 'Greetings, or should I say namaste'; back from six weeks in India and a little under the weather. FACTS TO FEELINGS: he always knew India had nearly 1.5 billion people, but living there, it 'hit my limbic system,' and what India will experience is no longer hypothetical. DEMOGRAPHICS AND ENERGY: about 1.45 billion people, five times the US population on about a third of the land, fifteen times the density; mostly coal, with about 5% renewables, and Modi's plan to double coal production by 2030. Recently pulled into the global superorganism (tgs:Concept.Superorganism; tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism); once the world's largest economy, then British rule, now growing over 6% a year, with Western consumerism -- YouTube ads for sweet snacks -- bleeding in. CLIMATE: the opening wet-bulb scene of Kim Stanley Robinson's The Ministry for the Future (tgs:Work.TheMinistryForTheFuture; tgs:Interview.TGS_66_KimStanleyRobinson; tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature) 'will not be too science fictiony.' At a meeting of Indian NGOs, some of whom would appear on an upcoming roundtable, a participant worried migrants would take local jobs and was told that they themselves would have to migrate north (tgs:Concept.ClimateMigration). NGO leaders and elites understand climate change; the common person knows it is getting warmer but not why. Mumbai hit 100 degrees twice in winter; migration northward, perhaps to Russia, and from Bangladesh into India are under-discussed. Nate stayed in the south, at Auroville, in Tamil Nadu. CULTURE: the 'Indic' ethic -- not religion but the origin of Indian philosophy -- and how happy, stable, outgoing and warm people are at less than a tenth of US energy per capita (tgs:Concept.ExosomaticEnergy); 44% work in agriculture versus 2% in the US; India imports oil but exports food. With Andrew Millison (tgs:Interview.TGS_64_AndrewMillison), Nate sees India able to act ahead of the Great Simplification with permaculture, water catchment for more variable rainfall and tree planting (tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification); 'some nonviolence Gandhi-esque approach to what's coming at a Gandhian type scale.' He expected anger at the US, the largest historical emitter, given that India will bear the back-end cost of colonialism, but found the spirit resilient. DAILY LIFE: 60 million stray dogs (he adopted four); no alcohol (illegal where he was) and almost no meat, yet he gained weight on rice, dosas and desserts. Before leaving he showed six-year-olds pictures and recordings of Tamil Nadu birds; they mimicked the peacock and hawk-cuckoo and piled on him in a hug -- 'this feeling of oxytocin fullness' -- and young people at the gathering with his coach would not let him get to his cab. SOCIAL CAPITAL (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital): back in Minneapolis, fourteen people rode a van for fifty minutes without a word -- from 'a poor country, extremely poor, yet rich in social capital' to 'an extremely rich country that... is poor in social capital.' He has his next 33 Franklys mapped out."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from a hand-prepared transcript without punctuation (supplied by MJSullivan; sentence boundaries above are inferred) and the official show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because Nate retells this trip to Daniel in TGS_126 (May 2024) -- his addictive impulses dropped in India, then returned at home -- and Daniel answers that his reward circuits were full of real connection (tgs:Concept.OneMarshmallowRewardCircuits). The upcoming roundtable with Indian NGO leaders is tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_8_TheIndicMind (recorded 2024-04-25), whose guests Nate met at a metacrisis symposium in Auroville; this Frankly promises that his next one will explain what he was doing there. Spoken figures recorded but not checked: 1.45 billion people, fifteen times US density, about 5% renewables, under a tenth of US energy per capita, 60 million stray dogs; the show notes link sources for most. The show-note TGS link for Andrew Millison (youtu.be/52L4Ncs0jLk) is taken to be TGS_64, his only TGS episode before this Frankly; the ID differs from TGS_64's recorded replay and is not otherwise confirmed. Transcription artifacts: 'Oroville' is Auroville; 'kimst Stanley Robinson' is Kim Stanley Robinson; 'limic' is limbic; 'immuned' is communed; 'tamon Nadu' is Tamil Nadu; 'NOS' is NGOs; 'Frankle' is Frankly. Mentioned but not built: Narendra Modi, Gandhi, Nate's coach. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the 'Indic' ethic (see RR_8); facts versus limbic knowing."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_55_7KeyInterventionsForTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Key Interventions for the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate, back from six weeks in India and two weeks of COVID, riffs without notes on the 'intense clarity' he found there. We are at the eleventh hour; isolated projects that hold the rest of the system constant miss 'the choreography and the cliff,' and there are no longer any non-radical pathways. He first gives his own 2024 priorities: health first, weekly Franklys of advanced integration, environmental-science and response voices on the podcast, networking as a 'golden retriever' catalyst, a scalable 'Reality 101' and a glossary of the future, and influence through Hollywood and media. Then the seven interventions humanity needs (tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions): a map of the predicament's horizontal and vertical synthesis; raising the consciousness of 'the 1500' influential people pulling the superorganism toward the Mordor economy (tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred); advance policy across three timelines; Goldilocks technology, 80 percent of the benefit for 20 percent of the resources; community and social capital now; individual healing 'from the root upwards' before engaging with the metacrisis, a big change in his thinking (tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement); and a felt sense of the sacredness of Earth in at least 5 percent of humans."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-02-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/55-7-key-interventions-for-the-future> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mk84BZANyWk> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions, tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred, tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement,
        tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology, tgs:Concept.PostTragic, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack, tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "SETTING: Frank, Nate's dachshund, on his lap after a fight with Murphy; Nate back from six weeks in India and partly from two weeks of COVID, with no notes, testing whether his post-COVID brain can still call up his ideas. CONTEXT: increasing confidence that 'we are at the 11th hour globally, culturally, physically, financially, geopolitically.' Many projects hold everything else constant (scale lithium batteries and electric cars), and many plan for 20 to 40 years out while missing 'the choreography and the cliff that lies on the road between here and now.' 'I no longer believe there are any non-radical pathways forward': we have to respond to the four horsemen of the 2020s, with global heating and ecosystem disruption as a fifth (tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s), as Russia, the United States, China and the Middle East spiral outward. HIS SEVEN PRIORITIES FOR 2024: (1) health -- mental, physical, spiritual -- above all; in India his fight-or-flight system damped down and he saw how he had lived on alarm at climate, Russia and Israel news, the topic of his next Frankly; (2) the Franklys, more than 30 mapped in his head -- the choreography of the future, sunk cost, a framework for philanthropy -- the advanced integration of the basic synthesis, for 'the global scout team of life over power' (tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange); (3) diverse, especially environmental-science, voices on the podcast, plus responses -- a music historian on resonance, and trauma, inner technology and behavior, since 'the human software that we're culturally running is not only incompatible with the biosphere, but it's not a fit for our evolved hardware'; a species-level, global story despite our tribal, collective-action, game-theoretic responses; (4) networking, about 20 hours a week, as a 'golden retriever, or dachshund in this case' catalyst; (5) a standardized basic science story -- his six India lectures, 800 slides and about seven hours -- scaled professionally into a 'Reality 101' project, since many find the podcast too complex or too scary, and those who stay learn to dance between the tragic and the post-tragic (tgs:Concept.PostTragic), where he spent too little time last year; with it, a glossary of the future, 100 to 200 two-to-five-minute videos on concepts such as positive feedbacks, loss aversion, cognitive dissonance, the carbon pulse and the tragedy of the commons (tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons); (6-7) partnering behind the scenes with Hollywood, media and others in the cultural transition. THE SEVEN INTERVENTIONS (tgs:Concept.SevenKeyInterventions), not in order: (1) a roadmap, a comprehensive systems map of the horizontal and vertical synthesis -- how productivity and money fit together, what is possible, which buckets people at different scales should work on -- which his organization can help build. (2) 'The 1500, for lack of a pithy term' (tgs:Concept.TheFifteenHundred): a global elite, not calling the shots but pulling the superorganism forward with brain software incompatible with the biosphere, turning digital and AI claims into social power with extractive effects; well-intentioned or not, they are 'disconnected from the feeling centers of their mind,' accumulating power 'as if a hungry ghost,' pulling everything toward the Mordor economy (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy); raising their consciousness of the stakes and the sacredness of life matters, because otherwise 'they're going to have nothing left to enjoy their excess.' (3) Advance policy (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy; tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1): extrapolating recent trends will hit a brick wall, so interventions need three timelines -- preparing now, the bend-or-break moment when the financial musical chairs stop, and after; the US has $34 trillion of debt and about $200 trillion of unfunded claims for Social Security and Medicare, and the stock market at 5,000 reflects more debt to sustain consumption; communities, governments and risk advisors must prepare the radical measures that will be necessary. (4) Goldilocks tech and infrastructure (tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology): not 100 million new 3,000-pound vehicles a year, not stone tools, but things like sodium batteries, 'not as good as lithium batteries, but good enough' -- 80 percent of the human benefits for 20 percent of the resources. (5) Community: with Biden and Trump the likely candidates, a third of the country will 'go ballistic' whoever wins; energy surplus let social connections atrophy; India taught him community is messy, and that its benefit is not borrowing a lawnmower but physiology -- oxytocin and serotonin from close interaction with 30 people for six weeks; governments and mayors will not do it, so build social capital and pro-social prepping now, including with people we disagree with -- 'the only piece of advice that I feel 100% certain about.' (6) The individual (tgs:Concept.HealingBeforeEngagement): 'a big change in my thinking' -- people need food, rest, exercise and equilibrium before engaging with the metacrisis, which many consume as a dopamine-and-cortisol 'mental video game' because the behavioral stack is out of balance (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack); heal from the root upwards, with nutrition, sleep, exercise, meditation, music, chanting and singing, and his friend James's 'libraries of healing'; he had naively assumed that telling people the urgent predicament would make them respond; resources should be diverted to healing the humans around us. (7) Recognizing the times we are alive in, through education, heart-led recognition and nature: we are 'at the cusp of ecocide,' and need not a majority but 'a solid group of 5% of the human beings alive today or more' to hold the sacredness of this blue-green Earth in their values, 'we are connected as opposed to me.' His view: the last four -- community, individual healing, the sense of the sacred -- must integrate with the map, while the 1500 and advance policy are still needed. CLOSE: he does not want to broadcast this widely, partly because 'the Eye of Sauron will redirect to Red Wing, Minnesota'; his coach in India said 'wisdom cannot be scaled... only shared one-on-one'; to those who keep coming back: talk to others, find community, focus on your health -- 'I'm no guru. I'm a client.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from a transcript MJSullivan hand-scraped (chapter headings from the video) and show notes from the episode page; no transcript PDF is published, and the replay ID was confirmed from the episode page. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because 'the 1500' is Nate's own early naming of the people Daniel calls hyper-agents (tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, TGS_20), and his turn to healing before engagement meets Daniel's heart, will and mind (tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind, TGS_31). He also uses Daniel's tragic and post-tragic stages; the show notes link Marc Gafni. Reference resolution: the show notes' advance-policy link is a timestamped YouTube video (qYeZwUVx5MY) not matched in the graph; tgs:Interview.TGS_5_BendNotBreakPart1, where Nate first describes advance policy to Daniel, is referenced instead on the strength of the episode's own words, and the India lectures video (2FDH2l9RQNM) is not built. Nate says his next Frankly will cover what he learned in India, although tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia was published two weeks earlier; the health-focused follow-up may be a later Frankly. Spoken figures recorded but not checked: US debt of $34 trillion and about $200 trillion of unfunded claims; 100 million new vehicles a year; the stock market at 5,000. The hand-scraped transcript is clean. Mentioned but not built: Nate's friend James ('libraries of healing'), his coach in India, the music historian guest. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: Reality 101; the glossary of the future; libraries of healing; the 5 percent; 'wisdom cannot be scaled'; three timelines of intervention."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Oil, AI, and the Straw"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by two real, concurrent events — a new all-time high in 'all liquids' oil production, and Nvidia's real market cap surpassing all S&P 500 energy companies combined — Nate argues AI will function as a real, direct extension of what he calls 'the straw': the accelerating human capacity to draw down finite resources and ecosystem capacity faster, joining shale fracking, debt, and modern monetary theory as prior real examples of the same widening-straw dynamic. Opens with a real, direct self-correction: quoting his own real 18-year-old essay from The Oil Drum warning that 'peak oil' would become definitionally obfuscated by counting non-oil liquids (ethanol, natural gas liquids, condensate) as oil — a real prediction he argues has since come true."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/56-peak-oil-ai-and-the-straw> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct personal candor opens the technical core: Nate acknowledges he was himself 'part of' the naive peak-oil community 15-20 years ago that assumed peak oil would immediately end economic growth — arguing the real trajectory has proven far more complex, with conventional crude having actually peaked back in 2004 while a real 'Red Queen' dynamic (needing to drill more, deeper, and faster just to offset accelerating decline rates in already-drilled wells) has kept total liquids climbing. AI's real, concrete double-edged role is laid out directly: it's already being used for real, simultaneous multi-well fracking techniques in the Bone Spring and Wolfcamp shale plays, extracting more oil, while itself consuming real, massive material and energy inputs — Nate cites a real, direct claim from Elon Musk that AI energy demand would increase tenfold within six months, twice in succession, which he calls 'clearly unsustainable' given real current grid capacity. Directly credits DJ White (already built in this graph via TGS_51) as his own real co-creator of the term 'Carbon Pulse,' developed together for their shared 'Reality 101' course materials — and extends the concept from a wide-boundary view to include real carbon released from biological feedback systems (permafrost, methane hydrates, shallow oceans) as climate heating itself accelerates, not just human-extracted fossil fuels. Closes on a real, distinct concern from the biophysical analysis: his own real, greatest fear about AI is not resource depletion but a direct 'straw on our humanity' — citing real, unsolicited AI-crafted emails he's personally received, and warning that AI risks 'graying out' human connection and completing what he calls civilization's drift toward left-brain dominance, absent a change in whose interests the technology ultimately serves. States a real, personal terminological preference: he'd rather call it 'simulated intelligence' than 'artificial intelligence.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest provenance note: MJSullivan hand-scraped this episode's transcript directly, matching the same real situation as Frankly_11 — no formal transcript PDF or YAML exists for this build; a separate, real show-notes document was available and used for metadata. No thinkr:hasReplay or thinkr:hasTranscript is asserted, since no such URLs were captured in the source material. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Frankly on AI and Real Ecology,' directly cross-referenced by Nate himself as an earlier related Frankly, real title and number not yet confirmed; John Rowan and Kyle Saunders (real, thanked directly in the show's own credits alongside Art Berman, Tristan Harris, and Daniel Schmachtenberger — the latter three already known/built elsewhere in this graph, the former two not built, given no direct citation content exists to characterize their contribution beyond a thanks credit)."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_132_WideBoundaryImpactsOfAI, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_79_TheBiggestTakeawaysFromTheLogicOfTheSuperorganism ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_57_InformationBifurcation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Information Bifurcation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by real, documented audience reaction to his own prior episode with Chuck Watson on NATO and Ukraine (already built, TGS_114), Nate works through ten real dichotomies shaping how information gets produced, distorted, and received. He opens by naming his own real limits directly: not an expert on this topic himself, crediting Jonathan Haidt, Douglas Rushkoff, and Tristan Harris (already built, TGS_214) as real people in his own network who are."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-03-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/57-information-bifurcation> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZRQ3g36ZtWo> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly57Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TenLensesOfInformationBifurcation ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_114_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly argues the medium itself shapes the message before content even enters the picture: speaking, whether podcast or television, carries a real, structurally higher bar for novelty and sensationalism than writing does, since it hits evolved neurotransmitter responses writing simply doesn't trigger the same way. He's real and direct about a genuine asymmetry between the Fourth Estate (traditional journalism, increasingly captured by well-resourced interests) and the Fifth Estate (independent podcasts and newsletters, underresourced but where he himself gets most of his own information) — while being equally direct that the Fifth Estate has its own real internal split between reasonable independent voices and outright conspiracy content, naming COVID-era misinformation directly and arguing those responsible should still offer a real, public apology now that the science is settled. A real, striking distinction between propaganda's actual mechanism and simple misinformation closes his ninth lens: propaganda doesn't just displace neutral information, it pre-loads the listener to reject and actively attack anyone who disagrees, even when the underlying point itself isn't controversial — illustrated directly with real YouTube comments from his own Chuck Watson episode showing listeners hearing 'Putin is great' in a conversation that never said anything resembling that. Closes on real, direct evidence of the alternative: a real, quoted listener comment describing genuine discomfort at hearing Chuck Watson's actual argument, followed by a real, second listen and a real, direct admission that the topic is 'much more complex and harder to navigate than the media is able to portray' — offered directly as a working example of metamodern thinking, self-aware enough to sit with discomfort rather than reject it outright."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt, Douglas Rushkoff (both real, directly named as trusted voices in Nate's own network, not built) — Tristan Harris is already built and directly cross-referenced, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Fellowship of the Ring: 'Bend Not Break' Version"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate recasts Tolkien's Lord of the Rings/The Hobbit as an allegory for the roles society needs across 3 distinct timelines. First, 9 real archetypes of business-as-usual society (Banker/Investor, Worker, Technologist, Barker/Salesman, Scholar, Warrior, Preacher, Consumer, Elite), each paired with a real, specific decline-scenario counterpart it could 'morph into' under contraction (e.g. Banker to Hoarder, Worker to Indentured/Slave, Consumer to Addict). Second, 9 archetypes for a successfully-navigated distant future (Ecologist, Engineer, Ancestor, Craftsman, Healer, Stalwart/Earth Warden, Merrymaker, Matriarch). Third — and the episode's real core — 9 archetypes specifically needed for the near-term 'Bend Not Break' moment itself (Silvertongue, Alchemist, Disarmer, Muse, Effectivist, Barber, Governess, Wellspring, Catalyst), forming the real 'Fellowship' the title refers to."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2024-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-03-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/58-the-fellowship-of-the-ring-bend-not-break-version> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Frankly58Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/3oh-zdo-l8I> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct correction (2026-09-13): this entry briefly, mistakenly referenced a tgs:Concept.TheFellowship wrapper Concept that was created in error and then removed -- the real 9 Fellowship archetypes (Silvertongue, Alchemist, Disarmer, Muse, Effectivist, Barber, Governess, Wellspring, Catalyst) were already formally minted as thinkr:Archetype individuals in archetypes.ttl since 2026-08-13, connected directly to tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak via thinkr:activeInPhase, with no wrapper Concept needed or used. This episode's own combined show notes + full transcript were reviewed 2026-09-13 while planning a future WWNS article using these 9 as a reader self-identification device ('which archetype are you?'), which is what surfaced the (already-fixed) duplicate-minting mistake in the first place. The other 2 real sets this episode also names -- 9 business-as-usual archetypes with their own 'morphs into' decline pairings, and 9 distant-future archetypes -- remain deliberately unminted, matching this graph's own minimal-scope-until-needed discipline; worth a dedicated follow-up pass if either set becomes needed the same way the Fellowship set just did. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Patrick Knodel's own real prior TGS solo episode (#115), directly named; Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse') and Frankly #57 ('Information Bifurcation'), both real, confirmed, directly cross-referenced."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely different register from most Franklys built this session — explicitly playful ('I loved the magic swords and the dragons') rather than analytical, though building toward a real, substantive point. Nate opens with an unusual, direct meta-apology for a prior episode's audio experiment ('I will never do that again'), then spends the bulk of the episode building a structured, 3-tier archetype system rather than a single flat list. The most intellectually interesting move is the direct 'morphs into' pairing for each business-as-usual archetype under contraction — not random decline, but a real, specific transformation Nate assigns to each (Banker to Hoarder via loss aversion specifically, Technologist to Cyborg via AI/big data specifically, Elite to a genuine fork he names directly: either a Hoarder building bunkers, or a real collective-response contributor). The episode's actual emotional core, by Nate's own explicit statement, isn't the archetype taxonomy itself but the feeling of 'camaraderie and fellowship' he associates with the source material — he states this directly as 'the most important part of this little Frankly reflection.' The episode closes on a real, generous reframe of individual agency: rather than requiring a Frodo-style singular hero, Nate points to Sam and Gollum as the real Catalysts who changed Lord of the Rings' outcome, offering this directly to listeners as a model for how an ordinary, unheroic role ('you do your job, live with your family, enjoy your life') can still meaningfully shift a larger outcome."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_59_SevenMetaQuestionsAboutOurGlobalMetabolism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Meta Questions About Our Global Metabolism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded fresh off a real trip to the Bioneers Conference, and directly framed as a companion reflection to Geoffrey West's own real TGS appearance released the day before, this Frankly works through seven real, open questions about whether Kleiber's Law — the biological scaling rule that energy use grows more slowly than size — genuinely applies to human economies the way West's own research suggests. Nate directly reports the real, striking fit: global GDP growth (3% annually over the last 40 years) against global energy growth (2.1%) tracks the same sub-linear scaling relationship West finds across organisms and cities."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.GeoffreyWest, tgs:Interview.TGS_117_GeoffreyWest ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly poses his own real, central open question first: is capitalism in service of this underlying metabolic law of nature, or does social organization itself create the incentive to access more energy in the first place? He leans toward energy surplus as the real, primary driver, crediting West's own real graph showing that pursuing growth without bound requires an accelerating cycle of innovation just to avoid collapse. A real, pointed question follows directly from that: are renewables genuinely a transition away from fossil energy, or just 'a protuberance on the massive amoeba' — additional capacity layered onto the same underlying growth-seeking system, the way a shark might grow an extra fin rather than change its fundamental behavior. He's real and direct in raising a genuinely uncomfortable question about decentralization: since cities become measurably more efficient as they grow larger under these same scaling laws, spreading a city's population across many smaller settlements could increase, not decrease, total resource impact — a real, direct complication for a common assumption in localization-minded thinking. A real, vivid medical analogy anchors his fifth question: GLP-1 drugs like Wegovy don't just suppress appetite, they measurably reduce craving and novelty-seeking more broadly, raising the real, direct question of what a cultural or policy equivalent of that same effect might look like at a societal scale. Closes on what he calls the central question underlying the whole podcast: can knowing that human society has a metabolism actually change that metabolism — proposing, directly, a 'great sophistication' as a real, alternative frame to the Great Simplification, where accumulated knowledge is deliberately used to alter the trajectory rather than simply following it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, charming detail: Nate directly addresses his own dog, Frank, mid-recording ('Frank, you want to get down?') — consistent with Frankly_62's own real, confirmed origin story for the show's name. Geoffrey West's own real TGS episode (TGS_117) is now built. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ronald Wright (real, directly quoted, A Short History of Progress, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_60_SeventeenThingsIAm100PercentCertainAbout a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 17 Things I Am 100% Certain About"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming his own real epistemic caveat before listing anything: humans are adapted to overconfidence, so even his own stated certainties are closer to '99 point something' than true 100%, since by definition no one can see their own blind spots. He moves fluidly between claims held with full confidence and claims held with real, explicitly lower confidence, deliberately modeling graduated certainty rather than binary true/false thinking throughout."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/60-the-17-things-of-which-i-am-100-certain> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TPv0wa5U0WA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly60Transcript17ThingsIAm100PercentCertainAbout.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_111_TheThreeMostImportantWords, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_59_SevenMetaQuestionsAboutOurGlobalMetabolism, tgs:Interview.TGS_117_GeoffreyWest, tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows, tgs:Human.RexWeyler, tgs:Human.NoraBateson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names, at 80-90% rather than full certainty, a real, specific concern: a Holocene-Anthropocene Thermal Maximum triggered by sequestered methane release, which he directly calls a 'geological monkey trap' set long before humans evolved, and argues is dangerously under-recognized relative to its actual potential magnitude. He's real and direct that renewable energy alone will not solve environmental problems, arguing genuine resolution requires governance change and cultural shifts in incentives, not simply swapping energy sources — while remaining less certain, though still confident, that some real 'Goldilocks technology' between high-tech excess and pre-industrial tooling could help. A real, charming personal detail anchors a serious point: with his own dog Frank visibly snoring on camera, Nate states real, full certainty that dogs are humanity's best invention (co-evolution notwithstanding), immediately pivoting to the sobering real statistic that dogs now weigh as much as all wild land mammals on Earth combined — offered directly as proof that even the best inventions carry real negative impacts once scaled. He's real and direct in distinguishing material scarcity from mental abundance: citing real, direct personal observation of people living on roughly 10% of typical resource consumption while reporting genuinely good lives, crediting shared community norms and practices like meditation as the real, structural difference. A real, vivid, honest self-observation closes the episode: describing a Warm Data Lab exercise at Bioneers, led by Rex Weyler and Nora Bateson (both already built), where discussing the same topic through five different rotating lenses left him progressively less eager to assert his own views and more interested in simply listening — offered directly as lived evidence for his own closing claim that certainty, while adaptive for status, actively degrades the capacity to listen to others."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct editorial note: this episode intentionally has no matching numbered Concept, per direct discussion with MJSullivan — the actual content doesn't cleanly enumerate 17 discrete items the way this graph's other structured-list Franklys do, and forcing that structure would fabricate precision the source doesn't support. The underlying theme of calibrated confidence and comfort with uncertainty is already covered by Frankly_111 (already built), cross-referenced directly above. Real, charming, consistent detail: Frank the dog is directly present and mentioned by name, consistent with the show's own already-confirmed real origin story. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (planetary boundaries, postmodernism, LUCA) rather than pointing to further individuals — Geoffrey West, Donella Meadows, Rex Weyler, and Nora Bateson are all directly cross-referenced and already built, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Strait of Hormuz and 'the Spice'"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded hours before Nate left to surprise his own 82-year-old father — a Vietnam War Army medic — for a real Honor Flight veteran-tribute event, this Frankly opens with a deliberately apolitical framing of the Israel-Iran situation, centered on the Strait of Hormuz. Nate directly introduces his own real, now-recurring 'the spice' analogy, borrowed from Dune: crude oil functions as modern civilization's own singular, irreplaceable resource, precisely as spice does for Dune's fictional universe — a real, direct framing later reused in Frankly_127 (already built) without its own original grounding until now."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/61-the-strait-of-hormuz-and-the-spice> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape, tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lJ3DauFIMXU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly61TheStraitofHormuzandtheSpiceTranscript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet, tgs:Concept.CrudeOil ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly walks through a real, specific oil-flow breakdown: roughly 100 million barrels a day counted globally, of which only about 85 million is genuinely crude oil (the rest being ethanol, biodiesel, and natural gas liquids lacking oil's real chemical and energy properties); of that real crude total, only about 40 million barrels are actually available on international export markets, with the majority consumed domestically by producing nations; and of that 40 million, roughly 17 million barrels pass daily through the Strait of Hormuz specifically. He's real and direct that Iran retains a real, credible capacity to disrupt this flow — citing roughly 100,000 real, stored submarine mines and short-range missile capability — arguing even a partial, temporary disruption would constitute a genuine financial and economic disaster given the global system's real, direct dependence on uninterrupted flow. A real, pointed political critique names two sitting US senators directly, Lindsey Graham and John Cornyn, for real, public calls to bomb Iran, with Nate directly questioning whether they've genuinely reckoned with the scale of economic disruption such action could trigger — framing this dynamic through his own established real 'spite' framework (already a formal Concept in this graph via Frankly_11), where escalation proceeds even when it demonstrably worsens the escalating party's own position. He's real and direct, if reluctant, in naming a religious dimension to the crisis: real, documented evangelical Christian eschatology tying the Second Coming directly to Israel's territorial restoration, and a real, parallel secular Zionist narrative of restored national hegemony, both offered as genuine factors shaping real policy decisions rather than dismissed as fringe belief. Closes with a real, direct long-horizon observation: even decades into a Great Simplification, Iran and Iraq will likely remain among the few nations with substantial untapped oil reserves, making today's adversarial relationship with them a real, structural complication for any future energy transition, not merely a present-day political dispute."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Art Berman (already a real, built Persona in this graph, directly cross-referenced here regarding non-crude liquids counted as 'oil' — independently reconfirmed, not a new find); Lindsey Graham and John Cornyn (real, named sitting US senators at the time of this real 2024 recording, directly quoted regarding public statements on Iran — not modeled as individuals, given this graph's established practice of not minting formal entries for real, named political figures cited only for public statements, distinct from TGS guests or cited intellectual influences). UPDATE (2026-09-04, added after this episode's own build): Senator Lindsey Graham died July 11, 2026, of an aortic dissection following a brief, sudden illness — real, current context noted here for the historical record, not retroactively altering the episode's own real, accurate 2024 content, which reflects statements genuinely made by a then-sitting senator at that time."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_62_SevenThoughtExperimentsForEarthDay a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-03"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Thought Experiments for Earth Day"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded for Nate's real, direct 16th consecutive year of Earth Day reflections, this Frankly opens with a real, charming aside: introducing his dog Frank on camera, playfully noting Frank is 'one of Earth's creatures, just the same as I' — the real, direct origin of the show's own 'Frankly' branding. Nate then works through seven real, structured thought experiments meant to reframe familiar assumptions about growth, extinction, and human agency rather than offer conclusions."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-04-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-04-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/62-seven-thought-experiments-for-earth-day> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f3IpRm9De60> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/Frankly61Transcript7ThoughtExperimentsforEarthDay.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.LifeEthic ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The first thought experiment, 'what if solar panels were free,' concludes the panels themselves are only roughly 10% of real installation cost — the rest is labor, permitting, and transmission — arguing free panels alone would simply feed the existing growth-oriented economic metabolism rather than change it, unless the underlying cultural aspiration toward growth itself changed too. The second directly reframes current biodiversity loss in blunt trade terms: humanity currently uses roughly 100 million barrels of oil a day while losing roughly 1,000 species a year, a real, direct ratio Nate frames as a hypothetical species-for-oil trade humanity is already effectively making, absent any alien intermediary. A real, striking inversion structures the third: what if climate change caused humans, rather than the reverse — tracing how roughly 290,000 years of extreme Paleolithic climate volatility gave way to real, unusual Holocene stability roughly 10-12,000 years ago, directly enabling the agricultural revolution and everything that followed, with methane (from humans) proposed as a real, direct parallel to CO2's role in driving temperature. A cluster of three related suspended-animation thought experiments (four through six) ask what real infrastructure, governance, and collective values a portion of humanity would build for the rest to wake up to — the sixth incorporating Nate's own real, direct nine-point 'Life Ethic' framework in full, first delivered in a real 2021 Earth Day talk. The seventh closes on the carbon pulse itself as a real, direct accident of geological timing — comparing it to the La Brea Tar Pits trapping unsuspecting animals — and directly dismisses the Kardashev scale's vision of ever-expanding energy appropriation as 'ridiculous' given this real, temporary, one-time endowment. Closes with a real, direct reframing of agency: rather than fantasizing about time travel to change the past, Nate argues real, current scientific synthesis makes today's actions comparably consequential — 'we are time travelers' in the sense that matters."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest data-quality note: this episode's own uploaded filename says 'Frankly-061,' but the source file's own embedded metadata (_site_divergence) shows the site's real, authoritative resolution is #62 — confirmed directly rather than assumed. The real Frankly #61 ('The Strait of Hormuz and the Spice') remains a distinct, still-unbuilt episode, already flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking as the single most-cited unbuilt Frankly. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is almost entirely conceptual/encyclopedic (Kardashev scale, La Brea Tar Pits, the 'geological monkey trap' term from Nate's own Reality Blind) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_63_SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nuclear Conflict: 7 Key Questions for Sustainable Futures"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming his own real reluctance: this is roughly two and a half years into commenting on the Russia-NATO situation, and he'd sworn off doing it again, calling it outside his own wheelhouse and highly polarizing. He proceeds anyway, stating directly he doesn't want the channel to sell fear, but considers geopolitics and potential World War III central enough to the human predicament that he can't set it aside — offering a real, direct update on recent NATO-Russia escalation followed by seven structured questions on how to think about war and nuclear risk without either sensationalizing or ignoring it."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-05-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/63-nuclear-conflict-7-key-questions-for-sustainable-futures> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=euFizGjkwJk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Frankly63TranscriptNuclearConflict_7KeyQuestionsforSustainableFutures1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict, tgs:Concept.RelativeFitnessSpiteFramework, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with a real, current update: Britain, France, and the US had recently signaled willingness to let Ukraine use NATO-supplied weapons to strike inside Russia, prompting a real, documented formal diplomatic warning from Russia to those countries' own ambassadors. He's real and direct that he is 'not pro-Putin, not pro-Russia,' stating plainly he is 'pro complex life' and genuinely frightened by the trajectory of events, not advocating for either side. He directly applies Frankly_11's own already-built evolutionary spite framework to question three: in a nuclear exchange, everyone's absolute fitness would fall to zero, yet spite remains evolutionarily adaptive at smaller scales because it improves one's position relative to a rival even while making one's own position worse in absolute terms — his own real, direct fear being that decision-makers may be optimizing relative position in exactly this way. He's real and direct about a genuine intellectual honesty check on his own choice to keep discussing this topic: he lays out three real, honest scenarios — nuclear war happens and his other work becomes moot, no nuclear war happens and he was simply wrong to worry, or no nuclear war happens partly because conversations like this one genuinely helped avert it — treating only the third as clear justification, while judging the exercise 'borderline net positive' either way. Closes on real, direct intellectual humility about his own limits: naming this an 'ominous topic' with 'no solutions,' while directly crediting over a decade of audience trust as the reason he felt able to think through it publicly rather than privately, and previewing a real, deliberately 'milder' Frankly to follow."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Henry Kissinger (real, directly discussed, recently deceased at the time of this real recording, not built given his real, brief, citational role here rather than direct TGS engagement); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (NATO statements, RAND Report, Proud Prophet exercise) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_70_AskMeAnything ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_64_TwentyControlKnobs a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 20 Control Knobs for a Post-Growth Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded the day before leaving on his own real, annual fishing trip with nine male relatives, Nate directly opens by disclosing real, ongoing work 'averting a World War III' through conversations in DC — deliberately not the episode's actual subject. He instead lays out twenty 'volume knobs' shaping the future: ten real, external macro trends largely outside individual control, followed by ten real, internal responses within it, explicitly framed as non-exhaustive."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-06-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-06-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/64-the-20-control-knobs-for-a-post-growth-future> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TwentyControlKnobs, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the climate knob in real, current events rather than abstraction: over a thousand people died during the Hajj pilgrimage the week before recording, with real, documented temperatures reaching 51°C — offered directly as evidence the 'out of our control' knobs are already actively turning, not distant hypotheticals. He's real and direct about the geopolitical knob, connecting the US adding roughly a trillion dollars in debt every three months to a broader real, structural shift from a unipolar to multipolar world, arguing both trends are ultimately about resource access as the carbon pulse's own easy credit runs out. A real, vivid personal detail anchors the 'baseload to intermittence' response: recalling real childhood boredom, finishing The Hobbit for a third time with nothing else to do, as a genuine model for tolerating the absence of constant stimulation, rather than something to eliminate through more access. Real, direct intellectual humility marks his treatment of The Dawn of Everything: while personally agnostic on its central thesis, he directly notes real, credentialed anthropologists he knows have criticized the book as politically motivated cherry-picking of historical examples, modeling engagement with contested ideas without pretending false certainty either way. Closes on real, direct personal candor: naming the shift from measuring success by money and status to asking whether one is 'on the team of life' as the single thing that gives him the most real, personal meaning, directly citing Johan Rockström's own already-built TGS appearance on planetary boundaries as evidence of just how high those real stakes actually are."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcript-quality note: the uploaded transcript for this episode was genuinely poor — auto-generated with no capitalization, minimal punctuation, and heavy filler ('um,' 'uh') throughout. It was cleaned up in a real, careful, separate pass (preserving Nate's own actual words, only removing pure vocal filler and adding sentence structure) before this episode was built from it. A handful of likely transcription errors were corrected with reasonable confidence during that cleanup: 'ninu year' to 'El Niño year,' 'Wy coyote' to 'Wile E. Coyote,' and 'David wenro' to 'David Wengrow.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: David Wengrow and David Graeber (real, directly discussed, The Dawn of Everything, neither built given the discussion centers on real, secondhand critique of the book rather than direct engagement with its content)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "And Then What?: Using Wide-Boundary Lenses"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly grounds this show's own recurring 'wide boundary' vocabulary in Garrett Hardin's real, coined concept of being 'ecolate' — ecologically literate, habitually asking 'and then what?' of any proposed action to trace its second-, third-, and nth-order consequences beyond the immediate, narrow benefit. Works through a real, structured series of contemporary examples at increasing 'boundary width': corn ethanol (narrow: cheaper gas; wide: diverted food supply and a real 10-20% increase in Gulf of Mexico nitrogen runoff), sovereign debt (narrow: near-term consumption; wide: a real, direct claim on future biophysical resources, not just financial abstraction), electric vehicles, and renewable energy buildout."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/65-and-then-what-using-wide-boundary-lenses> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Interview.TGS_226_CanMoneyServeLife, tgs:Monologue.TGS_205_NateHagens, tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_70_AskMeAnything ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dJ745eyky6Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Frankly65TranscriptAndThenWhat__UsingWide-BoundaryLenses.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Ecolate, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, structured practice for applying this thinking closes the conversation: 'yellow teaming' a decision before committing to it — deliberately surfacing its most significant unintended consequences rather than exhaustively cataloguing every possible negative outcome, since Nate's own real, direct caveat is that over-applying this method to every decision would leave 'sparrows feeding on us' from sheer inaction. A real, pointed application closes the episode symmetrically: both degrowth and continued growth get the same 'and then what' treatment — degrowth risks a real, sudden 'musical chairs' collapse given the West's massive financial overhang (itself a claim on future resources), while growth's real costs are described as structurally 'backloaded,' not felt until decades or centuries later, the same way nuclear waste's true cost isn't apparent for generations. Nate directly credits Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real distinction between naive and authentic progress (from his own TGS appearance, already built in this graph) as a direct influence on this same wide-boundary framing. Closes with a real, direct citation of Ilya Prigogine's own real concept — that in a system far from equilibrium, small 'islands of coherence' have the capacity to shift the entire system — reframing wide-boundary thinkers themselves as exactly those islands."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Ilya Prigogine (real, directly cited Nobel laureate chemist, already independently known elsewhere in this graph via Lisi Krall's TGS_86, not yet built as a Persona); Olivia Lazard, Simon Michaux (already built via TGS_19/TGS_49), and Ed Conway (already built) are all directly re-cited here, cross-validating earlier work rather than adding new gaps."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_66_RealityParty a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Reality Party"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded a day and a half after the attempted assassination of Donald Trump, which a friend called 'an inescapable cultural cleaving event,' Nate -- 'not apolitical, but nonpartisan because I care about reality' -- asks what a reality party would look like (tgs:Concept.RealityParty). He starts from preferences nearly everyone shares, notes that the values beneath them are pulling apart, and lists observations about reality that should be bipartisan: energy and debt limits, a multipolar world, humans as animals, planetary boundaries beyond climate, and a predicament rather than a problem. Neither party can navigate the Great Simplification alone; the best decisions ahead will be country over party."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/66-reality-party> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Frankly66Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vVqPRah5hAY> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RealityParty, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment,
        tgs:Concept.Rebuildables, tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.InsectDecline,
        tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral, tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan, tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "THE MOMENT: two days after the attempt on Trump's life, Nate has learned 'a lot about myself' and his fellow citizens; something in the culture has shifted. He fears both US parties are disconnected from the reality that is coming. PREFERENCES: start not with values but with what most people like -- quiet sunsets with people we love, a wren singing outside his window, healthy food and clean water, walking the dog or a daughter safely around the block, the smell of a freshly bathed baby or the air before a rainstorm, humor and stories, the freedom to choose, an NFL game and a nap on a crisp October Sunday, pets as family, a Dave Matthews concert near Milwaukee, old friends -- things that transcend politics. VALUES: beneath them lie values with political leanings, from women's rights and abortion to the right to bear arms and free markets, and AI, technology, polarization and harder times are widening the gap. All of these preferences and values evolved on the upslope of the carbon pulse, whose surplus let so much contention coexist (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse). REALITY: 'Reality doesn't care about political affiliation' -- he named his course Reality 101 fourteen years ago -- and describing reality is different from proposing policy; he does the first hoping more people will coalesce around the second (tgs:Concept.RealityParty). BIPARTISAN OBSERVATIONS: things aren't working for most people -- little savings, unaffordable health care, a mental-health crisis, gloomy views of the future; the Dow no longer represents success, since the bottom 50 percent own 1 percent of stocks. Energy is critical, and AI may paradoxically raise awareness of oil depletion as COVID and Ukraine began to; money and technology sit on 'a giant pillar of energy and ecosystem services from the past' (tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness; tgs:Work.RealityBlind). The US, 'like a pin cushion,' has drilled more oil wells than the rest of the world combined; after shale 'there's nothing left' (tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound). Adding a trillion dollars of debt every three or four months is a claim on the future economy (tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment). Renewables need materials, inverters and batteries rebuilt every 20 or 25 years (tgs:Concept.Rebuildables). A multipolar world means resources and trade can no longer be assumed. So average US and developed-world lifestyles are unsustainable, and unsustainable things often change abruptly. HUMANS ARE ANIMALS: mammals, primates, apes, part of the web of life, with human nature combined with nurture; we cannot change human behavior, but we can change cultural context, consciousness and choices, given the prepared learning we are born with into this one-time carbon pulse (tgs:Concept.Wetware). ECOLOGY: beyond climate, where the US lags most countries in concern, we are crossing other planetary boundaries (tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries): microplastics in breast milk and blood, endocrine disruptors cutting sperm counts 1 to 2 percent a year (tgs:Interview.TGS_2_ShannaSwan), insects lost 1 to 2 percent a year (tgs:Concept.InsectDecline), a disrupted nitrogen cycle, slowing ocean currents -- waste streams affecting creatures alive and unborn. MORE: after basic needs, most of the best things in life are free; technology is part of a suite of responses but over-relied upon (tgs:Concept.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral; tgs:Work.TechnologyIsNotValuesNeutral); we face a predicament, not a problem -- responses, not solutions (tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem; tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament). CLOSE: the human predicament is nonpartisan; neither party can navigate the Great Simplification alone; 'the best decisions ahead of us will be country over party'; expect 'heavy doses of crazy.' He is frustrated that well-meaning people don't talk to each other about reality."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-20 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. The following Frankly, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_67_TheSolutionsThatCanBeNamedAreNotTheSolutions, opens by responding to viewers of this one. Spoken claims recorded but not checked: the bottom 50 percent owning 1 percent of stocks (show notes: inequality.org); a trillion dollars of new federal debt every three or four months; the US having more oil wells than the rest of the world combined; sperm counts and insects each falling 1 to 2 percent a year (show notes: TGS_2 with Shanna Swan; Reuters). Mentioned but not built: the Reality 101 course playlist, Nate's friend Jordan (surname not given), Steve Stewart-Williams's essay on polarization over core values (show notes), the AMOC and the nitrogen cycle. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: preferences versus values; 'country over party'; prepared learning."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_67_TheSolutionsThatCanBeNamedAreNotTheSolutions ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_67_TheSolutionsThatCanBeNamedAreNotTheSolutions a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Solutions that can be Named are not the Solutions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's answer to viewers who asked, after The Reality Party Frankly, what its policies would be: why he does not promote solutions, 'at least not yet.' The human predicament has responses, not solutions (tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem). The response space is a cube -- superstructure, social structure and infrastructure, by individual, local and national scale, by pre-crisis, bend-or-break and long-term horizons -- and a second cube of people's resources, how complexified their culture is, and what they care about (tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube). The first step is understanding, then values, then strategy, then execution. And human systems are adaptive like predator and prey: a strategy named in public draws a counter-response, so 'the real solutions to the metacrisis will not be televised and they will not be popular,' and much of the work will be an 'in service of life underground' (tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse). Advance policy is his own example (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy). Last, he says honestly that he does not know."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-07-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/67-the-solutions-that-can-be-named-are-not-the-solutions> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Frankly67-Transcript.docx1_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1ea4hmI2xmk> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube, tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse, tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem,
        tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology,
        tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange, tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing,
        tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_66_RealityParty, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "OPENING: a Chinese greeting, and a wish to have someone from China on the show. Viewers of last week's The Reality Party (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_66_RealityParty), about both US parties ignoring the core issues, asked what such a party would stand for; this is why Nate is not promoting specific solutions, 'at least not yet.' RECAP: we live on ancient sunlight treated as interest (tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing), a 19-terawatt metabolism -- 190 billion light bulbs on all the time -- overlaid with growing monetary claims, in ecological overshoot; he expects to hit 15 terawatts before 25; and on top of it all, evolved human behavior (tgs:Concept.HumanPredicament; the show notes link the animated explainer, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries). NO SOLUTIONS: a predicament has responses, not solutions (tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem). THE CUBE (tgs:Concept.SolutionSpaceCube): Marvin Harris's superstructure, structure -- which 'Daniel Schmachtenberger and I have started to refer to as the social structure' -- and infrastructure (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure); scale (individual, local and regional, national and global); time (pre-crisis until a financial or geopolitical cascade, the bend-versus-break moment, and 20-40 years out). A second cube: people's resources and social capital, how complexified their culture is (India, where he presented to NGO leaders, has 'more degrees of freedom on how they organize things'), and what they care about -- other species, social justice, local resilience. No one size fits all. SEQUENCE: first understand how energy, money, technology and growth fit together, which is politically neutral ('we have to understand the present to understand the future,' quoting tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway); then values (his: the 1.5-2 million known and up to 10 million unknown species he wants to 'chaperone' through the bottlenecks); then plans and strategy; then execution. Understanding is by far the podcast's main job. AUDIENCE: a specific solution narrows the audience; his categories -- regenerative agriculture and technology, ecological economics, the real energy transition (how we use energy, not which), Goldilocks technology (tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology), advance policy, a philanthropy framework perhaps called 'capital in the service of life,' governance models, libraries of healing -- each mean different things to an engineer, teacher, celebrity, student, professor, philanthropist or farmer (tgs:Concept.EcologyOfTheoriesOfChange). ADAPTIVE RESPONSE (tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse): humanity's shared mind space behaves like a natural system with predator-prey dynamics; bivalves grow thick shells only where crushing predators live; Buckminster Fuller's 'build a new system that makes the old one obsolete' came from someone who, as a military contractor building Arctic radar domes, could not say 'fight it'; on 9/11 the hijacking strategy was countered by the fourth plane's passengers within hours. 'The real solutions to the metacrisis will not be televised and they will not be popular'; the universes of socially acceptable and effective solutions overlap little, and none satisfies every goal at once. People he admires -- one drafting scalable wetland-replacement language, one who has conserved millions of acres in South America -- will not come on the show because explaining their methods invites a counter-response; DJ White (tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite) told how he stopped Taiwan's dolphin drive kill only because the officials involved have died. Hence 'an in service of life underground movement' (tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife). POLICY: generic good ideas (term limits, age limits, money out of politics), but most needed policies run opposite to what is voted for; Nate has spoken to about 25 senators, governors and congressmen about advance policy and did unpublished work with US agencies in 2014, since discussing it could make it self-fulfilling or ineffective (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard). HONESTY: 'truly I don't know' -- describing the Great Simplification does not qualify anyone to prescribe; he hopes for collective learning and emergent responses, across scenarios of AI-driven growth, a financial 'Wile E. Coyote moment' or collapse; pro-social prepping matters, but 'you don't want to use me as an example on how to prep.' He is still trying to breathe life into 'a kinder, gentler, more sapient economy.' A solution to the metacrisis is 'a unicorn or a carrot.' Next week: an intense personal Frankly about his family."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment: Nate credits Daniel for the term 'social structure,' and his cube is his own version of Daniel's tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid (TGS_50, 2022), while his adaptive-response argument parallels Daniel's warning that any solution 'will be gamed' (TGS_20). The show-note link 'What is the Human Predicament' (youtu.be/-xr9rIQxwj4) is the animated film, here taken as the 2022 tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries (that video ID is recorded on its 2025 re-release in the TGS feed). The show-note DJ White TGS link (youtu.be/jVvFc__iCUo) is taken to be TGS_51, his only TGS interview before this episode. The categories of intervention he promises to articulate 'later this summer' anticipate the intervention fronts of tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction (Frankly_132, 2026). Spoken figures recorded but not checked: 19 terawatts, 190 billion light bulbs, 1.5-2 million known species; the 9/11 account (he says 'the third plane') is Flight 93, the fourth. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'woman Zaja' is unclear in the source (likely a Chinese sign-off); 'dew lines' is the DEW Line; 'more door economy' is unclear. Mentioned but not built: Buckminster Fuller, Osama bin Laden, the wetland-law drafter and the South American conservationist (unnamed), the Minnesota wetland-replacement statute linked in the show notes. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the understand-values-strategy-execute sequence; the 'real energy transition'; 'capital in the service of life'; libraries of healing; pro-social prepping."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Overshoot and Its 7 Fundamental Drivers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded the day after Nate ran a real, direct 103°F fever from what he suspects was a mild bout of sunstroke during a hot, humid bike ride, this Frankly lays out his own real, structured Seven Fundamental Drivers of Overshoot in full for the first time (now a formal Concept in this graph). Opens with a real, direct correction to the prior week's Frankly on solutions: the pool of genuinely effective solutions shrinks over time as intervention windows close, making the show's own real, stated purpose the deliberate expansion of the Overton window to keep pace with that shrinking pool, not simply cataloguing acceptable ideas."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-07-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/68-overshoot-and-its-7-fundamental-drivers> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_79_TheBiggestTakeawaysFromTheLogicOfTheSuperorganism, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_70_AskMeAnything ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZDN8ABCIDyg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Frankly68TranscriptOvershootandIts7FundamentalDrivers.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenFundamentalDriversOfOvershoot, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.Ecolate, tgs:Concept.CarryingCapacity, tgs:Concept.PhantomCarryingCapacity ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the Carbon Pulse driver in a real, striking statistic: total mammalian biomass on Earth today, humans plus livestock plus wild mammals combined, is roughly 700% what it was 10,000 years ago, offered as real, direct evidence of how thoroughly ancient stored energy has inflated the biosphere's own apparent carrying capacity. He's real and direct in naming a specific, real, contemporary example of driver four (lack of recognition): Elon Musk's real, public claim that Earth could support 80 billion humans, which Nate directly calls 'the most delusional statement I've ever heard,' arguing it would produce 'a planet full of dead locusts and smoking embers,' not a functioning biosphere. Closes with a real, direct, briefly-sketched set of seven possible antidotes, mirrored to the drivers themselves: energy-surplus decline, the biosphere's own real, direct enforcement of reduced carrying capacity through worsening droughts and floods, a genuine cultural shift from human-centered to earth-centered values, biophysically-tethered money creation, syntropic (rather than merely extractive) technology, and — closing on what he calls 'maybe firstly' — individual behavioral change starting at the level of everyday micro-decisions."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "SUPERSEDED 2026-09-04, per MJSullivan's own direct editorial decision for the WWNS series: this episode's Seven Fundamental Drivers framework predates, and is treated going forward as superseded by, Nate's own later, more structured Framework for Action (Frankly_132, already built). WWNS content should not cite this episode or tgs:Concept.SevenFundamentalDriversOfOvershoot as current framing — Frankly_132 is the real, current reference point instead. Kept here as a real, historical episode in its own right, not deleted, since it's still real and still happened; just no longer treated as Nate's own live, operative framework. Real, honest, human detail: Nate directly discloses recording this Frankly the day after a real 103°F fever, joking he can blame any mistakes on it — 'this podcast and running this NGO aren't really a job anymore, they're my life,' offered as real, direct explanation for why the topic came to him even while unwell. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jared Diamond (real, directly cited, his book Collapse and its real dam-proximity cognitive-dissonance example, not built)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/the-7-fundamental-drivers-of-overshoot> ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_69_GoldilocksTechnology a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Goldilocks Technology – A Preliminary Checklist"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly extends a concept already explored in an earlier, real TGS episode, laying out preliminary criteria for what he calls Goldilocks Technology — innovation calibrated between dopaminergic overreach and a naive retreat from problem-solving altogether. He opens by directly naming the real, current economic moment as an anomalous era already ending, arguing the kind of innovation that got humanity here is not the kind needed for what comes next."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/69-goldilocks-technology-a-preliminary-checklist> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork, tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025, tgs:Interview.TGS_172_LivingTheChange ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BzC0AqN40U4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Frankly69TranscriptGoldilocksTechnology.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GoldilocksTechnology, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Human.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Human.RomanKrznaric ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names a real, structural precondition for better technology: current prices don't reflect real ecological cost, meaning governance upstream of individual designers and engineers — not just individual values or consumer choice — has to correct pricing before genuinely appropriate technology can out-compete extractive alternatives on its own economic terms. He's real and direct that even celebrated green-technology companies remain real, direct beneficiaries of substantial government subsidy, complicating any simple 'the market will provide' narrative around clean innovation. A real, concrete historical counter-example anchors his optimistic case: Edo-period Japan sustained a genuinely functioning circular economy for centuries under real resource constraint, offered directly as proof durable low-throughput societies aren't merely theoretical. Real, current material specifics ground the checklist directly: sodium batteries as a real, viable alternative reducing dependence on constrained lithium supply chains, and a real, sobering figure that only about 8.5% of the global economy currently operates circularly, offered as the honest baseline any 'Goldilocks' technology has to improve on. Closes by directly crediting Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real 'naive progress' framing and Roman Krznaric's own real historical work as intellectual anchors for judging whether a given technology counts as genuine progress or just accelerated extraction with better branding."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real companion TGS episode on Goldilocks Technology, directly cross-referenced in the show notes, remains unbuilt in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_70_AskMeAnything a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ask Me Anything: Your Questions About TGS Answered"@en ;
    skos:altLabel "Ask Me Anything"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate answers listener questions sent through Substack, Discord and email -- over a hundred, of which he gets through about a fifth. He says he is clearer about the game board than about which moves to make. The questions run from how tightly energy tracks GDP (tgs:Concept.EnergyGDPCoupling) and why he prefers resolve to hope, through population, the financial and geopolitical signals he watches, nuclear risk, shrinking room for counter-narrative speech, diversity of guests and the charge of elitism, to organizing locally, farmland bought up for data centers, and what a renewable-powered civilization might look like. Along the way: weedy potatoes, thriving ducks, and a podcast that tries to hold head, heart and hands together (tgs:Concept.HeadHeartAndHands)."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-08-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-08-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/70-ask-me-anything> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Frankly70TranscriptAskMeAnything.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UNyqZYYTFbI> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyGDPCoupling, tgs:Concept.HeadHeartAndHands, tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment,
        tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.WileECoyoteMoment, tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict, tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy,
        tgs:Concept.DividedBrain, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.OvertonWindow, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.WideBoundary, tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect, tgs:Concept.RealityBasedHope, tgs:Concept.CarryingCapacity ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich, tgs:Interview.TGS_53_WilliamRees, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_32_ThePerilsOfEroi,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_63_SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers, tgs:Work.RealityBlind,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.TheMatterWithThings ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "FRAMING: over a hundred questions arrived after a Substack call; Nate is 'no one's guru' and more confident about how the chess pieces fit than about what moves to make. ENERGY AND GROWTH (tgs:Concept.EnergyGDPCoupling): electric power is about 20 percent of global energy; US electricity use has flattened, not fallen; globally GDP is essentially 100 percent correlated with materials and 99 percent with energy, with GDP growing 3.2 percent a year and energy 2.04 percent over 50-plus years, about 70 units of energy per 100 units of new GDP; service economies outsource the heavy lifting, and the average American uses 57 barrels of oil equivalent of fossil fuels plus more embodied in imports. HOPE: he does not like the word; he is more aligned with resolve, meaning, purpose and trust in what may emerge (tgs:Concept.RealityBasedHope). He trusts his 20 or 30 systems-ecologist friends of 20 years, his dogs, his closest friends, his own eyes and his gut; the future will be much worse than most people think but probably better than many viewers expect, and he is less drawn to morbid fascination than to intervention points. POPULATION: a listener cites his question to Paul Ehrlich (tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich) and William Rees (tgs:Interview.TGS_53_WilliamRees); a Corey Bradshaw episode on population and biodiversity is due about now, and a population roundtable is planned. Overshoot is a population exceeding carrying capacity, and eight billion at once 'is not sustainable. Full stop.' (tgs:Concept.Overshoot; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers). But population will not change the bend-not-break moment of the next ten years -- a three-year halt to births would only hasten the financial musical-chairs moment (tgs:Concept.MusicalChairsMoment) -- and it is outside his top ten next to financial overshoot, the one-way unwind of complexity, geopolitics and the resource grab, and the social contract (tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s; tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens). LOOKING BACK: there are more converging crises than he expected; he would have started around 2016; he has learned the conversation needs the head, the heart and the hands (tgs:Concept.HeadHeartAndHands), including recognition of the colonial impulse and male-dominated culture. SKILLS: 'definitely coding. Or making thatch sandals' -- a joke; the biggest skill is inner tech, a healthy body and mind not addicted to 'the dopamine siren song' and the ghost of dopamine past (tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack), patient, tolerant and calm. POTATOES, DUCKS, PEOPLE: his potatoes 'suck' -- four 15-foot rows lost to weeds, a few from a hugelbed of ten years' compost -- so he supports the Red Wing farmers market; he has grown potatoes for 20 years and done EROI studies of his own labor (tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_32_ThePerilsOfEroi). Ducks and chickens are doing great despite foxes, coyotes, raccoons, weasels, stoats and eagles; two batches of ducklings, some imprinted on him. His Dunbar's number is overrun (tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber): his best friends are his parents, brother and girlfriend, and he has lost touch with others. He is 'not that ambitious' and would rather teach college and keep animals, but feels a fiduciary duty to share 25 years of learning; no podcast can cover every implication of the carbon pulse (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse). SIGNALS: the financial Wile E. Coyote moment (tgs:Concept.WileECoyoteMoment) -- a major currency in free fall, bond markets without bids and rising yields, an end to trust that debt will bail us out; significant NATO troops in Ukraine or Russia; a Middle East oil shock; the totalitarian impulse in the UK and elsewhere. CARBON TAX: a carbon dividend would be spent on goods whose supply chains carry indirect carbon. NUCLEAR: by far the nearest, greatest threat, with some 13,000 weapons; it would start by accident, then retaliation, and Proud Prophet in the 1980s and recent AI war games show escalation to strategic exchange (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_63_SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict; tgs:Concept.SevenKeyQuestionsOnNuclearConflict). Governance underpins almost every issue, and we need new ways of deciding. ADVISING LEADERS: Kamala Harris has surely been briefed on Johan Rockström's planetary boundaries (tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom); John Holdren, whom he hopes to host, gave Obama a weekly science brief; the answers are outside the politically possible -- 'we can't even pass a 15 cent carbon tax' -- so this is the realm of advance policy (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy). SPEECH: he worries about the shrinking room for counter-narrative speech and wants the Substack to grow in case a Democratic or Republican sweep throttles the channel: for Democrats he says renewables cannot power this civilization and that biology shapes behavior; for Republicans he talks of global heating, empathy and kindness. 'Big Brother meets idiocracy.' DIVERSE VOICES: monetization is off; the great simplification is already under way in many countries and species; many of his friends are 'old white guys, systems ecologists'; about 38 percent of followers are in the US; he wants more women in decision-making and cites Iain McGilchrist on left and right brain (tgs:Concept.DividedBrain; tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain); his checklist is science-tethered, relevant, and English-speaking, and more international guests are recorded or scheduled. ELITISM: the podcast passes the baton to a 'scout team' of better communicators, perhaps an HBO or Netflix documentary on energy and systems blindness (tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness); reaching the public also needs specific paths of action, which he is still working out. THE OVERTON WINDOW (tgs:Concept.OvertonWindow): he does not know how to get past cognitive biases; start authentically, with dogs, birds, rivers, sunsets or tacos; people accept climate science until the remedy comes up. He is savoring The Matter with Things (tgs:Work.TheMatterWithThings), one of six books on his nightstand. ORGANIZING: start with local conversations and social capital, 'pro-social prepping'; such efforts must coexist with the growth-based superorganism (tgs:Concept.Superorganism; tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism) and its 'Disneyland, smorgasbord' narratives, so there may be little warning; perhaps a two-hour primer for groups in Topeka, Bemidji or Chicago. OPPOSING VIEWS: Steven Pinker was right that poverty fell every year until 2020, on the back of the carbon pulse, but that is backward-looking; Nate's view is forward-looking and wide-boundary (tgs:Concept.WideBoundary; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat). He will not stage debates on his show -- he gives guests the mic -- but would discuss systems ecology on Joe Rogan's or Lex Fridman's. LAND AND DATA CENTERS: his girlfriend's daughter was outbid on 11 houses; as money is printed, those near the spigot buy physical assets such as land (tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect); local constituencies need a non-monetary pride in place to refuse data centers or airports. A RENEWABLE CIVILIZATION: the average US resident uses 10,000 watts continuously, a hundred 100-watt bulbs; today's plan is plug-and-play replacement plus growth, 'the cultural carrot'; many people live well on a fifth of that, and he wants to interview 2,000-watt and 500-watt societies; solar could power a lower-consumption life, but not the 60-plus gadgets plugged into the average US home; markets will not shrink footprints, so communities must choose it and be documented as pilots (tgs:Work.RealityBlind). CLOSE: 'We are not alive at a normal time to be human'; he hopes the conversation scales and 'something emergent happens.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-20 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Show notes are trusted over speech: they give 17 barrels of oil equivalent embodied in imports (Nate says 15) and 12,121 nuclear warheads as of early 2024 (Nate says 13,000). Spoken figures recorded but not checked: GDP 3.2 and energy 2.04 percent a year; 38 percent of followers in the US; 10,000 watts per US resident; 67 gadgets in his house. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Reiss' is William Rees; 'William Norhouse' is William Nordhaus; 'Ian McGilchrist' is Iain McGilchrist; 'conversion crises' is converging crises; 'intertech' is inner tech. The show notes link Corey Bradshaw's episode (tgs:Interview.TGS_136_CoreyBradshaw), which Nate says will be out by airing; it is a stub without a date, so it is not referenced until the date is confirmed. The 2,000-watt society he wants to explore is the subject of the later TGS_166 with Peter Strack. Mentioned but not built: Hannah Ritchie, William Nordhaus, Steven Pinker, John Holdren (a planned guest), Joe Rogan, Lex Fridman, the Proud Prophet war game, the Red Wing farmers market. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: inner tech; pro-social prepping."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.TGS_205_NateHagens ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_71_WeakestLinks a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Weakest Links: Depletion, Supply Chains, and Trust"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded within days of a real, live geopolitical event — the September 2024 Israeli pager/walkie-talkie attack on Hezbollah operatives in Lebanon — Nate treats it as a real, potential 'black swan' for one of his own Four Horsemen, then broadens out into seven things he sees declining largely out of public view: oil well productivity (citing a real, contemporary ExxonMobil report on a ~15% average annual decline rate), planetary-boundary stability, the value of money (real, specific federal debt-growth figures), institutional/interpersonal/media trust, civility, collective nervous-system regulation, and — the episode's real central focus — the stability of global just-in-time supply chains and globalization itself. Applies evolutionary game theory's cooperation/selfishness/altruism/spite framework directly to the pager attack, with 'spite' — 'doing something at a cost to yourself, but a greater cost to your competitor' — offered as Nate's own real, direct read of why Israel would choose this specific, costly, escalatory tactic."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/71-weakest-links> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=izxQcGnT6P4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Frankly71TranscriptWeakestLinks.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, disarming personal candor — recorded 'under the influence of Zizol,' an antihistamine for ragweed allergies, and admitting he's behind on Franklies with 'a list of 37 more' waiting. The real analytical spine is the seven-item decline list, delivered in Nate's own words as deliberately 'in no particular order' despite counting down from seven to one — oil well decline rates, planetary boundaries (explicitly naming six of the real, specific thresholds — biosphere integrity, novel entities like endocrine disruptors and plastics, land-system change, freshwater change, biogeochemical flows), the real value of money eroding under debt growth, declining trust, declining civility, nervous-system dysregulation, and finally supply-chain fragility as the real, live news-driven focus. The most direct, real personal reasoning comes in Nate's own account of why he thinks Israel chose this specific tactic — walking through the full game-theory payoff matrix (cooperation, selfishness, altruism, spite), the same real framework he had already laid out at length in Frankly_11, landing on spite as the only framework that fits: a tactic that costs the actor real reputational and commercial damage (global South buyers now wary of Israeli, UK, and US supply-chain partners) specifically because it costs the target more. Nate is careful and explicit about the limits of his own analysis here — 'I don't even want to touch' the underlying Israel-Hezbollah conflict itself, keeping the discussion scoped to what it reveals about globalization's fragility rather than the conflict's own merits. Closes by directly connecting the whole discussion back to The Great Simplification and nearshoring, naming Russia as one of the only real countries with genuine end-to-end supply-chain oversight, and previewing a real forthcoming guest, Roman Krznaric."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "dct:references to TGS_100 confirmed directly, not inferred: this episode's own real show notes link 'Four Horsemen' to a specific timestamp (18:23) within TGS_100's own real YouTube video (youtube.com/watch?v=GocuMZX3hIs&t=1103s) — the exact same video ID already used as TGS_100's own thinkr:hasReplay. 'Frankly on spite' is real Frankly #11, now built (2026-08-31) — its own real spite framework is the same one applied here to the pager attack. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Roman Krznaric — already a real, independently-flagged deferred citation elsewhere in this graph (TGS_213's own scopeNote, via his real episode #142), now built as well, since this session — reconfirmed here, not a new find. Pew Research's own three real, distinct citations on institutions/interpersonal/media trust (all in this episode's own real show notes) are a strong, direct match for the WWNS candidate Pew declining-trust piece — noted for future WWNS pipeline use, not asserted as a formal reference here since no WWNS article has used this pairing yet."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_72_WhatIfClimateChangeWasAHoax a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What if Climate Change Was a Hoax?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a real, contemporary paper on 485 million years of Earth's temperature history being misread online as proof current warming is trivial, Nate directly grants climate skeptics their entire premise for the sake of argument — assuming climate change really were a hoax — then walks through the broader ecological crisis (biodiversity collapse, ocean deoxygenation, planetary boundary breaches, microplastics) to show none of it depends on climate change being real at all."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-09-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/72-what-if-climate-change-was-a-hoax> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GNwoLw0Gu64> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Frankly72TranscriptWhatifClimateChangeWasaHoax_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.StefanRahmstorf, tgs:Human.AlfredRusselWallace ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly corrects the misreading of the real 485-million-year paleoclimate paper at its source: the study itself actually shows Earth has never changed temperature this fast at any point in that entire record, and that the sun itself has grown roughly 50% brighter since Earth's formation, meaning lower CO2 is now required just to hold temperature steady relative to any past era — the exact opposite of what the misreading claims. He's real and direct that biodiversity today, even in its currently diminished state, remains the highest ever recorded across the fossil record, directly undercutting any simple 'more CO2 always meant more life' argument, since several of Earth's worst prior mass extinctions were themselves directly caused by rapid CO2 and volcanic outgassing events. Real, direct, striking material anchoring the human-impact half of the argument: human-made mass now outweighs all living biomass on Earth combined, plastics alone outweigh all animal life, and a real, direct clinical study found roughly 0.5% of human brain tissue by mass is now microplastic. He's real and direct that global sperm count is declining at roughly 2.6% annually, worldwide, not just regionally, with rising heat itself directly worsening fertility given how human anatomy evolved to keep testes cooler than core body temperature. A genuinely striking rhetorical move closes the biodiversity section: Nate directly reads two real, historical passages from Alfred Russel Wallace (already built) — one from 1876 describing an already 'zoologically impoverished world' after the loss of large mammals, another from 1911 directly attributing that loss to human activity — offered as real, direct proof this exact concern is roughly 150 years old, not a recent invention. Closes on real, direct rhetorical discipline: repeatedly setting aside each climate-linked ocean impact (sea level rise, AMOC slowdown, acidification) 'since that's linked to warming,' Nate is left only with overfishing and planetary boundary breaches unrelated to climate at all — six of nine already exceeded — as sufficient grounds alone for serious concern, closing with the deliberately 'valueless, prescriptionless' observation that the ecological case holds regardless of anyone's position on climate change itself."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct origin confirmed: this episode was prompted directly by Nate's own real, recent conversation with Stefan Rahmstorf (already built this session, given his own real, recurring citation pattern across multiple episodes) — his own TGS episode is not yet built despite the Human entry now existing. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Emily Judd (real, directly named paleobiologist, lead author of the real 485-million-year paper this episode responds to, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_73_TheSuperorganismAndTheSelf a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Superorganism and the Self"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate, tired after New York Climate Week, on the emotional and embodied side of his systems work. He cried three times in a week: putting down his coonhound Maisy, at a UN screening of the children's film Future Council, and when Jane Goodall closed the Planetary Health Check launch with a chimpanzee greeting. A ceremony with five trusted people, in which the group's 'energy field' dropped when he called someone 'psycho,' left him unable to speak of people pejoratively -- his only scientific explanation is a deepening, embodied recognition that everything is connected. Beneath the rising curve of the carbon pulse runs a declining one: profit over consciousness, polarization, frayed nervous systems, a species out of context (tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism). He extends Marvin Harris's three layers with a circle for the nervous-system health of individuals and a sphere for the connections between people in proximity, and says mental health and resilience may be the most important need of the next five years."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-09-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/73-the-superorganism-and-the-self> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Frankly73TranscriptTheSuperorganismandtheSelf.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zWP3JIb2W90> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism, tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_137_MamphelaRamphele, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "THREE TEARS: Nate put down Maisy, his 14-year-old coonhound with cancer ('dogs are family'); at New York Climate Week he welled up at a UN screening of Future Council, in which eight 12-year-olds confront corporate leaders and the monster, Groth, is loosely the economic superorganism (tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm; tgs:Concept.Superorganism); and at the inaugural Planetary Health Check from the Planetary Guardians -- Johan Rockström (tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom), Mamphela Ramphele (tgs:Interview.TGS_137_MamphelaRamphele), Carlos Nobre and Christiana Figueres on leaving the stability of the Holocene, positive feedbacks and the Amazon turning to savanna (tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries) -- when Jane Goodall noted that non-human animals had gone unmentioned and closed with a chimpanzee greeting. SERENDIPITY: a meeting with someone who had just sold his company for hundreds of millions and wants to do good; at a party the first stranger he met had been introduced by the same California friend he had just made a note to call -- one of about ten such coincidences in a month; an Amazon shaman, the stranger said, would read them as being aligned. Nate remains 'historically a materialist' but a 'kindergartner' on the spiritual side. CEREMONY: holding hands for hours with five people deep in metacrisis work, he called a woman who had emailed him 'psycho,' and the man beside him noticed the group's 'energy field' drop; since then a 'speed bump' stops him using pejoratives -- compassion and tolerance for everyone, even those he need not like. His only scientific explanation is an embodied recognition 'that everything is connected' (tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness). THE SHADOW (tgs:Concept.ShadowOfTheSuperorganism): a sound meditation in New York showed grief and trauma just under the surface; beneath the rising curve of the carbon pulse's material benefits (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse) is a declining curve -- profit prioritized over consciousness, a split within ourselves, polarization, nervous systems on edge before the US election, 'a species out of context' far from Pleistocene bands of about 150 (tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber), reward without the work in pornography and stock trades (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli; the behavioral stack, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack). 'One of the richest societies that's ever lived and we live in a sick society'; mental health and resilience may be the most important need in the US over the next five years. HARRIS EXTENDED: to infrastructure, social structure and superstructure he adds a surrounding circle, the nervous-system health of individuals, and a sphere of emergent connections between people in proximity (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure); people at a party spoke of an 'energetic field,' and he found that the heart's electromagnetic field is measurable a few feet away -- 'non-quantifiable, but also non-woo' -- something lost to ordering brown boxes. The stock market is at all-time highs while 'the real stock market, which is the biosphere, is at all-time lows.' A sliver of upside: the carbon pulse may also have enabled a change in awareness and consciousness that is starting to bubble up (tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence). He will keep to energy and economics but sometimes explore the shadow, 'at the risk of seeming woo.' The episode ends with Jane Goodall's chimpanzee greeting from Gombe."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment as Nate's inner counterpart: his embodied recognition 'that everything is connected' meets Daniel Schmachtenberger's diagnosis of fragmented consciousness (tgs:Concept.FragmentedConsciousness, TGS_31). Spoken claims recorded but not checked: the heart's electromagnetic field reaching 6 or 8 feet (Nate is unsure which; the show notes link HeartMath research and a 1963 paper). The show-note TGS link for Mamphela Ramphele is taken to be TGS_137, a stub dated after TGS_134 (July 2024) and before this Frankly. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Mampela Rampele' is Mamphela Ramphele; 'Cristina Figueres' is Christiana Figueres; 'stability of the Anthropocene' is Nate's slip for the Holocene, as the show notes' 'stability of the Holocene graph' confirms; 'interviewing few weeks' is intervening. Mentioned but not built: Jane Goodall (tgs:Human.JaneGoodall exists, without a Persona), Christiana Figueres, Carlos Nobre (tgs:Persona.CarlosNobre exists), the Planetary Health Check (tgs:Organization.PlanetaryGuardians exists), Nate's dog Maisy. The Future Council children later join Nate in tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_13_FutureCouncil (November 2024). Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: serendipity as a sign of alignment; the energetic field between people."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_74_TheLamentOfTheBigfoot a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Lament of the Bigfoot"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate reads a real poem he wrote 20 years earlier this same month, at age 38, shortly after leaving Wall Street and starting his PhD — written from the real, imagined perspective of the last living Bigfoot, mourning a mate and children lost to logging and human expansion, closing on an image of real, violent retaliation against loggers threatening its home. He uses the poem directly to reflect on how his own thinking has and hasn't changed since, opening with a real, direct catalogue of the era's polarized dualities: pro-human versus anti-human, mechanistic versus animistic, natalist versus anti-natalist."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-10-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/74-the-lament-of-the-bigfoot> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a9iQISZjEME> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Frankly74TranscriptLamentoftheBigfoot.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_121_PellaThiel, tgs:Human.AtossaSoltani, tgs:Human.JoannaMacy, tgs:Work.Ishmael ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names his own real evolution since writing the poem: he still cares about wild nature exactly as much as he did at 38, but no longer believes an abrupt human collapse would actually be good for the natural world, given the real, direct risk of wars and ecological damage that would likely accompany it — a shift toward 'Bend Not Break' as his own real, considered alternative to either denial or collapse-as-solution. He directly credits his own real friend Pella Thiel (already built), an eco-psychologist, for introducing him to Joanna Macy's real 'Council of All Beings' practice — a group exercise where participants speak on behalf of a chosen animal or natural feature, which Thiel reports often leaves human participants genuinely saddened once they realize how comparatively little humans, framed this way, seem to contribute back to the whole. He's real and direct that changing values alone isn't sufficient: humanity's own real productive base runs overwhelmingly on ancient stored carbon, and reversing course requires genuine biophysical alternatives — regenerative grazing, restored wetlands, reforestation — not simply a shift in how people feel about nature. A real, direct policy sketch closes his practical argument: taxes payable only in a currency earned specifically through ecological restoration work, offered as one concrete way to make regenerative activity actually count economically rather than remain a moral aside. Closes on real, direct uncertainty about his own timeline and safety: acknowledging he doesn't know whether it will still be safe to speak this openly in five months or five years, before closing with Mary Oliver's own real poem on choosing a hidden nest of bird's eggs over the noise and cleverness of modern life."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest note: both poems discussed in this episode — Nate's own 'The Lament,' and Mary Oliver's closing poem — are described and paraphrased here rather than quoted at length, per real copyright discipline; the full text of each is available directly in the real source transcript linked above. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (planetary boundaries, net primary productivity) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_75_SomebodysGonnaWin a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Somebody's Gonna Win"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded roughly three weeks before the 2024 US presidential election, Nate directly states his own real, explicit position as 'apolitical, nonpartisan,' arguing both major parties are structurally 'in thrall to the economic Superorganism' regardless of which wins — and offers a real, direct set of practical recommendations for how individuals and communities might respond well regardless of the outcome."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-10-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/75-somebodys-gonna-win> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KleRyQifkz4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly75TranscriptSomebodysGonnaWin.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_12_TheGlobalStakesOfTheUSElection, tgs:Human.PierreTeilhardDeChardin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names his own real, four-part filter for evaluating people that deliberately sets aside conventional left-right categorization: whether someone is aware of the real, converging predicament, whether they genuinely care about the future, whether they use a wide- versus narrow-boundary lens, and whether they hold their own views with real, calibrated nuance versus fanatical certainty. He's real and direct that his own real, explicit framing of the 'biophysical pyramid' inverts the culturally dominant one: rather than money and politics sitting atop technology, the biosphere and its real, freely-given ecosystem services should sit at the actual foundation, with energy and non-renewable materials next, and technology, money, and politics only above that — arguing election-season discourse inverts this hierarchy precisely when stress and fracturing are highest. He's real and direct in naming one specific, factual policy distinction between the two parties — their real, differing willingness to even discuss climate change — while maintaining his own broader, explicitly structural critique that both remain subordinate to the Superorganism's own growth-optimizing incentives regardless of that difference. A real, direct personal disclosure closes his own framing: reading real, mixed YouTube comments on the Every/Gromen roundtable (already built as RR-12) the same week, some frustrated the conversation wasn't more ecological, others calling it his best content yet — offered directly as lived proof of his own point that this is 'a multi-issue podcast because it's a multi-issue world.' Closes on a real, direct, practical list of recommendations regardless of outcome: setting a personal goal unrelated to the election, stopping tribal doomscrolling, deliberately seeking out and simply listening to someone voting the opposite way without rebuttal, and organizing local, explicitly nonpartisan conversations about community resilience — closing with a real, direct quote from Pierre Teilhard de Chardin (already built) on the 'sensual longing for communion with others who have a large vision.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct closure of the final citation gap flagged from Frankly_137's own show notes, completing that episode's own real, traceable citation chain. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (BRICS Kazan summit coverage, Gallup polling on election issues, Hurricane Helene reporting) rather than pointing to further individuals — Michael Every and Luke Gromen are both already built via RR-12, not new finds."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_76_TheBattlesOfOurTime a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Battles of Our Time"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded the week after the 2024 US election, Nate lists more than a dozen 'battles' of our time, from the global order down to our own nervous systems -- then reframes them, the morning after, as polarities to be navigated and harmonized rather than won (tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime). All feed one greater battle, power versus life (tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife), and beyond it the superorganism versus the 'mitochondria' of a culture that could outlive it. For pro-social, pro-future people, he says, the real work begins now."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/76-the-battles-of-our-time> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly76TranscriptTheBattlesofOurTime.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RkqKB-pF4no> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime, tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife, tgs:Concept.Effectivism, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection, tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots,
        tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.InsectDecline, tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast, tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture,
        tgs:Concept.DividedBrain, tgs:Concept.Wetware ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Human.ShannaSwan ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "REFRAME: he recorded a long, heavy Frankly the night before; sleeping on it, he decided the framing was off -- the pairs are not battles to be won but polarities to be navigated and, ideally, harmonized, with compromise even in the kinetic ones (tgs:Concept.BattlesOfOurTime). WORLD ORDER: unipolar versus multipolar -- after 50 years of the United States and 'our sidekick' the United Kingdom, can multiple countries share leadership, and peacefully? Endocrine-disrupting chemicals lowering sperm counts and hormones will shrink some populations, and people will leave declining countries for those with economic potential: feedbacks on feedbacks. CLASS: elites versus the common man -- the US election 'really was about class,' education, income and wealth; only about a third of US adults are college educated, and Trump and Musk took the populist mantle and won handily. In Bozeman, Montana, the average house is $900,000 and locals are priced out; AI and the coming productivity push will widen disparities within and between countries (tgs:Concept.HavesAndHaveNots). MASCULINE AND FEMININE: not men versus women but traits in both; as multilevel selection hardwired both cooperation and competition (tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection), and the Big Five traits were conserved for good reasons, stability and openness both helped us; today's system favors dominance, hierarchy and strength, which is why more women in power would be good. INDIVIDUAL AND COLLECTIVE: the carbon pulse's surplus let us live individual lives (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse); we will need the collective again, and the question is at which Russian-doll scale. HUMANS AND NON-HUMANS: up to 10 million other species have no say in boardrooms or elections; the average wildlife population has fallen 73 percent; Shanna Swan, coming on the show in early January (tgs:Human.ShannaSwan), notes that fertility decline in animals matches that in humans; insects are being lost at about 1 percent a year (tgs:Concept.InsectDecline; tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament). PRESENT AND FUTURE, AND DOPAMINE: we consume instead of invest, and 'the ghost of dopamine past' shapes our choices (tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast); dopamine leads a one-legged stool next to endorphins, serotonin and oxytocin, keeping us in cleverness rather than wisdom (tgs:Concept.DopamineHijackedCulture; tgs:Work.RealityBlind). EXTRACTION AND REGENERATION: we draw down geologic energy and materials in a couple of centuries; regeneration is possible but means a much smaller economy than today's 19-terawatt, $120 trillion one. THINGS AND CONNECTIONS: efficiency versus resilience, left brain versus right brain, mechanistic versus ecological (tgs:Concept.DividedBrain); the technosphere is not nature, and its mass now outweighs all living things. He reads Tolkien's Treebeard on Saruman, a mind of metal and wheels that cares for growing things only as they serve him. ACTIVISM AND EFFECTIVISM: activism is backward-looking and blaming, effectivism creative and planning (tgs:Concept.Effectivism); appeals to our better angels 'will only ever reach a few percent,' so rather than train a thousand sheep, inspire one border collie -- a remark he retracted in Frankly_78. BRAINSTEM AND NEOCORTEX: in elections, emotional, subconscious associations beat higher-level thought (tgs:Concept.Wetware); voters are told they used to be wealthier by someone they imprinted on 'like baby ducks.' This podcast will never be popular because it is abstract, complex and about the future -- elite in that sense, not in whom it cares about. FANTASY AND REALITY: a 300-foot neon-pink great white shark flying over Los Angeles eating white Teslas -- you pictured it, though it cannot exist; we can utter far more sentences than the world allows, so 'unspecified magic' sounds good, and misinformation, AI and algorithms add to it. The biophysical framing of energy, materials, ecology and behavior is a tether, like following brake lights in a snowstorm. NERVOUS SYSTEM: stay out of the sympathetic, fight-or-flight system and in the parasympathetic; a coach has taught him a long exhale with a five-second pause. BEHAVIORS AND VALUES: giving up after Trump's 'resounding victory' is a mistake; he has predicted a rightward shift and weaker environmental rules for four years, and 'now is when the real battle of our time begins' -- our values can trump short-term behavior. POWER VERSUS LIFE (tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife): for 290,000 years in the Pleistocene we had little to hoard; then came agricultural surplus, stored carbon, finance and digital money, and now AI, 'the eye of Sauron,' merging with energy and military power in a race for the one ring; with AI and Trump's win the superorganism (tgs:Concept.Superorganism; tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism) 'just got a shot of prednisone' and a snort of cocaine. Grief is painful but shows what is sacred. SUPERORGANISM VERSUS MITOCHONDRIA: mitochondria, passed only from mother to child, are his image for the seeds of a culture beyond the economic superorganism, which he gives 'its last decade or so of life' -- more feminine, ecological, right-brain, and valuing the more-than-human (tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria). He closes with Frodo and Gandalf on deciding what to do with the time we are given: the battle is between who we have become and who we might yet be."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-20 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Two weeks later Nate retracted the few-percent remark in tgs:Monologue.Frankly_78_ABriefClarificationOnHumanBehavior. Spoken figures recorded but not checked: 73 percent average wildlife decline (show notes: 2024 Living Planet Index); insects lost at about 1 percent a year (show notes: 1 to 2 percent, PNAS 2021); Bozeman's $900,000 average house; a 19-terawatt, $120 trillion economy; the technosphere outweighing all living biomass (show notes: Nature 2020); up to 10 million species. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Shana Swan' is Shanna Swan; 'eciency' is efficiency (a lost ligature); 'eye of Soran' is Sauron; Nate corrects 'unsympathetic' to parasympathetic himself. The Tolkien and Lord of the Rings passages are paraphrased, not quoted. Shanna Swan's January 2025 episode is later than this Frankly, so it is not referenced here. Mentioned but not built: endocrine-disrupting chemicals (see tgs:InterventionSubdomain.PlasticAndChemicalHazards), the Big Five personality traits, Regenerative Communities (regencommunities.net), a talk of his with predictions (show-note YouTube link), Elon Musk. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: fantasy versus reality (words let us picture what cannot exist); brainstem versus neocortex in politics."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_78_ABriefClarificationOnHumanBehavior ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_77_SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Philosophies on the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly argues the show usually engages the future through facts and synthesis, but people don't all relate to the future through the same philosophical frame — proposing seven real, distinct categories of how someone might view what's coming, offered directly as a tool for genuine empathy: knowing which category someone else occupies changes how the same facts will actually land with them."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/77-7-philosophies-on-the-future> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hyhz4q-5NKA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly77Transcript7PhilosophiesontheFuture.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly precedes the seven categories with a real, separate observation: people don't even care about the same things when looking toward the future — some focused on wealthy nations, others on materially poor nations, others on non-human species, and others on real, distant future generations, decades or millennia out. He's real and direct that category six, nuanced pro-growth advocates, aren't naive: they've genuinely studied biophysical limits and civilizational complexity, but conclude a real, unplanned degrowth event would trigger cascading failure across six-continent supply chains, medicine, and spare parts — a real, direct 'Mad Max' scenario worse than continued growth's own risks. He's fully direct that his own real position, category seven, isn't advocacy for degrowth or collapse, but a real, considered acceptance that involuntary degrowth is coming regardless of preference — crediting financial credit expansion, already explored directly in Frankly_23's own 'Mordor Economy,' as the real, primary mechanism, with complexity, geopolitics, and ecological limits as real, contributing but secondary factors. Closes on a real, direct, practical point about the framework's own actual use: the goal in naming these seven isn't to rank or judge them, but to recognize that the same facts and the same synthesis will land completely differently depending on which philosophical starting point the other person in a conversation actually holds."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (Valuegraphics data on what people care about, planetary boundaries, degrowth definitions) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_78_ABriefClarificationOnHumanBehavior a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Brief Clarification on Human Behavior"@en ;
    skos:definition "A retraction of Nate's remark in Frankly_76 that only 3 to 4 percent of humans will do the right thing. Our behaviors, like our bodies, are products of evolution -- 290,000 years in small Pleistocene bands -- and most of that past was prosocial, held together by strong reciprocity that punished cheaters and those who failed to punish them. What he meant is that the economic superorganism, through downward causation, suppresses those natural behaviors, so that today only 3 or 4 percent will buck its profit-seeking structure, too few to change its metabolism (tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism). He sets out three timelines (tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse): now, when we can be good only at the margin and should change initial conditions; the bend-versus-break moment, already arrived in some places, when many more will cooperate; and a later, more stable state with more reciprocity. Homo economicus is 'a desolate and dismal view' and untrue. A neuroscientist told him belief in free will predicts better health and engagement; Nate thinks believing there are pathways ahead does the same. We are plastic, and conformity to what others do, which will change, is the biggest predictor of behavior."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-11-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/78-a-brief-clarification-on-human-behavior> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Frankly78TranscriptABriefClarificationonHumanBehavior.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QF4kMhvjMls> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism, tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_76_TheBattlesOfOurTime, tgs:Interview.TGS_88_RobertSapolsky ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "SETTING: at a conference three days earlier, people looked at him strangely when he opened his talk with 'Greetings.' THE REMARK: two weeks ago, in 'The Battles of Our Time' (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_76_TheBattlesOfOurTime), he said only 3 to 4 percent of humans will do the right thing and follow the better angels of their nature; this Frankly clarifies it. EVOLVED BEHAVIOR: one thing he is most confident about is that our brains, neural architecture and behaviors -- jealousy, envy, status, short-term focus, cognitive biases -- are as much products of our evolutionary past as hair, the sclera of our eyes, red lips or bipedalism: 290,000 years in small Pleistocene bands and millions of generations of prehumans (tgs:Concept.Wetware). Most of that past was prosocial: strong reciprocity in small bands punished cheaters and those who did not punish cheaters, and the animal kingdom shows grooming and the punishment of alpha males who are too hard on a conspecific. WHAT HE MEANT (tgs:Concept.ProsocialityUnderTheSuperorganism): with the economic superorganism dominant, its 'downward causation' -- a hierarchical, metabolic force -- is 'sucking out the best of our natural behaviors,' because we all must live within a profit-seeking, extractive, growing institutional structure; only 3 or 4 percent will buck that trend within today's institutions, not enough to change the superorganism's direction. THREE TIMELINES (tgs:Concept.ThreeTimelinesOfResponse): (1) now, while debt and technology can kick the can forward, we can be good humans for families, businesses and the world, but only at the margin, and this is the time to prepare and change the initial conditions; (2) the bend-versus-break moment, which has already arrived in Ukraine, Syria, Madagascar, Afghanistan and dolphin populations but for many is still ahead, when far more people will cooperate because the situation becomes emotionally obvious -- the same share of sociopathic, Machiavellian, dark-triad people will exist, but out in the open rather than hidden (tgs:Concept.DarkTriad); (3) a more stable state once the financial and trade systems settle, with expectations matched to daily energy and production, and much more social reciprocity. Now, in 'the late stage gasps and travails of the amoeba,' stress shows as looking out for ourselves, anxiety and insularity rather than antisocial behavior. HUMAN NATURE: we are doomed unless we build a culture on the intrinsic, 'quite dominant' values of caring for nature and each other, the crucible we evolved in, which the superorganism's hierarchies have 'systematically suppressed and betrayed,' especially in the global north; Homo economicus, egoistic and narrow, is 'a desolate and dismal view' that is untrue and does not lead where we need to go. 'Please take this as a retraction.' FREE WILL: a neuroscientist at the conference who had watched the Robert Sapolsky episode (tgs:Interview.TGS_88_RobertSapolsky) said the real question is not whether free will exists but that people who believe in it have better immune systems, health and outlook and do more impactful things; Nate's cursory search found it inconclusive, but believing there are pathways to engage with the Great Simplification likewise leads to more empowered behavior and a more meaningful life (tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency). PLASTICITY: 'we are plastic'; the biggest predictor of behavior is conforming to what others do, and that will change; let us respond in a pro-planetary, pro-future, prosocial way."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-19 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because Nate's three timelines parallel Daniel's triage, transition and new-system horizons (tgs:Concept.ThreeByThreeResponseGrid, TGS_50), and his rejection of Homo economicus meets Daniel's account of how game theory came to define a good choice (tgs:Concept.GameTheoryFillsTheOughtGap, TGS_126). The show notes link the Battles of Our Time Frankly (RkqKB-pF4no) and the Sapolsky episode (xhobcj2K9v4); neither has a replay link in the graph, so both are matched by title. The free-will claim is recorded as Nate reports it: he says his own search found it inconclusive, and the show notes link a study on the benefits of believing in free will. The Apple Podcasts link in the show notes points to Frankly_77's page. Transcript artifacts: 'limbic, limb bar ridges' is probably brow ridges; 'metabolical' is metabolic; 'energy field' is probably energy-fueled; 'the amoeba' is left as spoken (probably the superorganism). Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: strong reciprocity; conformity as the biggest predictor of behavior; belief in agency as self-fulfilling."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_79_TheBiggestTakeawaysFromTheLogicOfTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Biggest Takeaways From the Logic of the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's year-end list of the 'advanced implications and inferences' of The Great Simplification synthesis. He first states the podcast's philosophy: wide-boundary, probabilistic, apolitical, curious and non-prescriptive, passing the baton to others. Then the inferences: we are running out of affordable energy at scale, not energy; free markets and technology are false gods; growth is kept alive by AI, debt and shale while we are 30 to 50 percent beyond sustainable. The superorganism's metabolism ranks what matters by power -- AI, the military, bond and currency markets, growth, energy, politics, citizens, and the environment dead last (tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism) -- so climate will not be voted on and the in-service-of-life movement must go underground. Single-issue efforts are mostly dead ends under 'the law of aggregate probability' (tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability); we are headed for a world of less, and social limits to growth will arrive before physical ones, the median falling before the mean (tgs:Concept.SocialLimitsToGrowthBeforePhysical). 'The Great Simplification is already here, it's just not evenly distributed.' The response: community, pro-social prepping, redefining rich, and simplifying first to beat the rush."@en ;
    dct:created "2024-11-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/79-the-biggest-takeaways-from-the-logic-of-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Frankly79TranscriptLogicoftheSuperorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZA-Vj-DRz8g> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism, tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability, tgs:Concept.SocialLimitsToGrowthBeforePhysical,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment,
        tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife,
        tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse, tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_29_RealityProbabilityAndPerception, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "WHY THIS EPISODE: the audience is an 'accordion' of newcomers and three-year listeners, and while a long primer series is planned for early 2025, today is for the advanced inferences. PHILOSOPHY: fly high enough to see how the parts fit -- not a single-issue podcast; Nate does not know what will happen and holds a probability distribution on each topic that changes daily (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_29_RealityProbabilityAndPerception); the podcast is apolitical, painting the board and the ground rules rather than taking sides, though lumping climate together with solar, wind and batteries as 'the answer' makes it political; he is curious and non-prescriptive, hoping to pass the baton to thousands or millions who will 'figure out what to do.' ENERGY: we are energy blind (tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness); 'Are we running out of energy? No, we're running out of affordable energy at scale' to power a system of claims and institutions. Energy is not fungible -- scalability, power and energy density, EROI (tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment), intermittency -- and who holds it matters: the BRICS control about half of world oil exports. FALSE GODS: free markets and technology. There is no free market in capital, the biggest commodity, which runs on rules and favors -- Italy borrows more cheaply than the United States. Technology once served human objectives; now we serve it, and it runs on fossil sunlight (82 percent of the energy mix per the show notes) in service of infinite growth on a finite planet. GROWTH ON LIFE SUPPORT: kept alive by AI, debt and shale, 'the bottom of the barrel'; we are 30 or even 50 percent away from sustainable, and hitting the turbo button delays the speed bump but makes it steeper. We are one species of about 10 million taking more than our share (tgs:Concept.Overshoot; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers); fish move poleward as warmer water holds less oxygen. THE HIERARCHY (tgs:Concept.MetabolicHierarchyOfTheSuperorganism): a choreography tied to the superorganism's metabolism decides what matters, as against what is voted on: AI first ('applying AI to data is the technological equivalent of applying fossil fuels to human and draft animals'; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw), then the military, bond and currency markets, growth, energy, politics, citizens, and the environment dead last. Climate will lose to energy poverty and energy security, so 'the climate movement and the broader in service of life movement is going to have to move underground and think two or three steps ahead' (tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife; tgs:Concept.NamedSolutionsDrawCounterResponse). NOT MULTIPOLAR: the fusion of AI and military capacity is winner-take-all, and the United States, 'the silverback gorilla,' will not go quietly; the death throes of 200 years of building will thrash for decades. AGGREGATE PROBABILITY (tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability): single issues such as low-carbon energy, sustainable food or global peace are often dead ends viewed from the system; 100 percent renewables is physically possible only at a tiny fraction of today's throughput, and must also clear politics, debt, war and missiles. 'We have the capacity to build a 1,000-foot statue made out of cheese' of the president-elect, but what are the odds every threshold is met? Even if the whole world agreed overnight on plastics, endocrine disruptors and climate, we would still face the five horsemen: debt, overshoot, geopolitics, complexity, the social contract, plus warming already in the pipeline (tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s; tgs:Interview.TGS_100_NateHagens). Divide energy and materials by population and we are headed for 'a world of less.' SOCIAL LIMITS FIRST (tgs:Concept.SocialLimitsToGrowthBeforePhysical): ten people, one earning a million and nine earning 50,000, have a mean of 145,000 and a median of 50,000; with AI, debt and other rabbits pulled from the hat, the median person will keep immiserating, as Peter Turchin would say, even while the aggregate rises. Europe will de-industrialize first, unable to raise military spending and debt while importing energy that may get expensive. 'The Great Simplification is already here, it's just not evenly distributed.' FEELINGS OVER FACTS: people will feel first and then find supporting facts through confirmation bias and motivated reasoning; local discourse and real-world observation will matter more, media less. RESPONSE: more content on what to do in 2025; we will need community for purpose, connection, meaning and resilience, pro-social prepping, and a redefinition of rich and successful toward social, built, natural and human capital, since after basic needs are met 'most of the best things in life are free' (tgs:Concept.RichInSocialCapital). Since there will be no mass belt-tightening, 'simplifying first and beating the rush is a good strategy.' We have arrived at a species-level story; some must value and defend the natural world. He closes with James Baldwin: 'Not everything that is faced can be changed, but nothing can be changed until it is faced.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because its three named inferences meet Daniel's game-theoretic concepts: the power hierarchy his tgs:Concept.MaximizingReturnsOnPower, and aggregate probability his tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard. Show-note figures preferred: fossil fuels are 82 percent of the global energy mix (spoken: 85). Spoken claims recorded but not checked: 'the open AI guy' saying 95 percent of GDP will be captured by AI (Nate cannot recall the name); Russia's Oreshnik missile as undetectable and non-nuclear; we are 30 to 50 percent beyond sustainable. Nate's 'multipolar world' is the geopolitical sense, not Daniel's tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap. Reference resolution: the show notes' 'Frankly on probability' (uWn2svl6aBU), 'energy blindness' (mVjhb8Nu1Sk) and 'AI and the straw' (mxqxq4sUfh8) are matched to Frankly_29, Frankly_3 and Frankly_56 by title, since those episodes carry no replay link to confirm; 'overshoot' (ZDN8ABCIDyg) matches Frankly_68's replay, and the '5 horsemen' video (GocuMZX3hIs) is TGS_100's replay. Mentioned but not referenced: Peter Turchin (the show notes link his site and an immiseration post; his book is tgs:Work.EndTimes), James Baldwin, the law of total probability. The Baldwin line is quoted in its usual published form; the transcript garbles it. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Peter Churchill' is Peter Turchin; 'hazelnut thing' is the Oreshnik (Russian for hazelnut tree); 'this website' is this podcast; 'fudgability' is fungibility. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: free markets and technology as false gods; the Great Simplification as already here but unevenly distributed; feelings dominating facts; pro-social prepping; simplify first and beat the rush."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.TGS_187_EarthDay2025 ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_80_ThankYouForRuiningMyLife a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thank You for Ruining My Life"@en ;
    skos:definition "An unusually personal, unscripted reflection prompted by a real encounter: a venture capitalist approached Nate at a tech conference, having watched 400 hours of the podcast's content, and thanked him for 'ruining his life.' Nate reads the listener's own real email aloud (paraphrased here, not quoted verbatim, per this graph's own copyright discipline) — a real, detailed account of an emotional and intellectual 'wrestling match' moving from techno-optimism through repeated cycles of resistance and clarity toward a settled, if difficult, new understanding. Nate uses the encounter to reflect directly on his own role and the podcast's real purpose."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2024-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/80-thank-you-for-ruining-my-life> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9wXwdcXJRzs> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham, tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Persona.JoanDiamond, tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct textual grounds for the tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency link — arguably the single clearest, most explicit primary-source statement of that theme found anywhere in this graph: Nate's own words, 'these aren't my concepts... I stand on the shoulders of giants,' 'I'm just a spokesperson and a catalyst that's widening the Overton window,' and 'I'm just a guy. I'm not a leader of any movement or anything.' Real, direct connection to already-built content: the listener had real, specific opinions about the Jeremy Grantham episode (tgs:Interview.TGS_99_JeremyGrantham, already built) and had watched 'the bend not break series' (tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, already built) multiple times; Joan Diamond is directly named as a second past episode the listener had opinions about — this transcript is what surfaced her as a real past guest, independently confirmed and newly built as a Persona this same session. Nate's own real, forward-looking mention of 'a reality 101 primer' connects directly to Priscilla Trịnh's own real 'Reality 101' course (already built via RR-9). Also worth noting as a real precursor, NOT identical content: Nate previews upcoming work on 'a framework for philanthropy... a framework for technology... a framework for community... a framework for governance... a framework for individual healing and behavior' — a real, earlier, evolving draft of ideas that plausibly fed into the later, structurally different Frankly_132 six-fronts framework (already built), but not treated here as the same content, since the specific categories don't match one-to-one. The episode's own real closing line directly previews the next Frankly as 'a movie idea on time travel and the superorganism' — not yet built in this graph."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Genuinely unscripted and personal, by Nate's own explicit description — 'these Frankly are just I have no notes at all other than the email I just read.' The episode's real emotional center is the listener's own words, read aloud: a self-described 'wrestling match' between techno-optimism and the podcast's systems synthesis, with each new layer of complexity (additional episodes, then outside books) initially adding discomfort and sleepless nights but ultimately making the overall picture more coherent rather than less. A real, candid personal admission from Nate follows directly: being recognized and engaged with substantively by a stranger 'in real life' lands differently and more powerfully than working alone in an office, even though he 'care[s] deeply about these issues' regardless. The episode's most quotable real formulation is Nate's own closing personal ethos — 'B+ in service of life' — offered as his real, current email signature and a direct acknowledgment that his own production quality is intentionally imperfect ('these could be way better produced... I'm just doing the best I can every week'). Nate is explicit and unambiguous that this openness isn't universal outreach: he directly states the podcast 'is not for everyone because it's intense, it's scary, there are no easy answers,' aimed specifically at people with the maturity to sit with discomfort rather than a broad audience. The episode closes on real, concrete forward-looking previews of Nate's own upcoming work, including an early, evolving version of ideas that would later inform the Frankly_132 six-fronts framework, and a real, direct tease of the following week's Frankly as a time-travel-themed piece connected to the Superorganism concept."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_81_TimeTravelAndTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Time Travel and the Superorganism: A Movie Idea"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's end-of-third-year Frankly, a movie idea from a bike ride that he gives away to anyone who can make it. He first names the show's audiences: the pro-social scout team, philanthropists with outsized claims and responsibility, future policymakers, community leaders and pro-social preppers, young people, and the general public, best reached through character-driven Hollywood stories rather than fantasy. In the film, a systems-aware scientist of about 2025 goes back 50 years with his neighbor, a currency trader. The audience learns today's predicament from his interventions, but the arc is the characters': he becomes famous, distracted and disillusioned, while she becomes the richest person on Earth, sees that keeping score in digital claims is meaningless, and becomes a champion of a regenerative future in service of life. Bonus themes: a 1970s 'Day After' for ecology, when shared broadcast media meant 'the superorganism of the 1970s could have functioned cognitively' (tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation); schools to educate hyper-agents (tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents); Superorganism-free zones (tgs:Concept.SuperorganismFreeZones); year-long trips as a kind of Asimovian psychohistory; and, darkest, the realization that the superorganism and its decline are inevitable, so the work becomes propelling things of value through the bottlenecks (tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks). 'We are time travelers today.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2024-12-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2024-12-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/81-time-travel-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Frankly81TranscriptTimeTravelTheSuperorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RUh_za5t7XM> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents, tgs:Concept.SuperorganismFreeZones, tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation,
        tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.HyperAgents, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange,
        tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Interview.TGS_102_BillMcKibben,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.TheDayAfter1983,
        tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "END OF YEAR THREE: 150 uninterrupted weeks of content, with Nate's dog Frank in the room. THE AUDIENCES: (1) the 'walking worried,' the pro-social scout team of natural systems thinkers who answer the bat signal unpaid (tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange); (2) philanthropists, foundations and people with outsized financial claims on biophysical reality -- degrees of freedom once held only by kings and queens; antagonism toward the rich may be misdirected, because 'being rich isn't the problem,' but once you understand the metacrisis there is 'a responsibility and a fiduciary' to act; (3) future policymakers who will have to make now-unpopular decisions (tgs:Concept.AdvancePolicy); (4) community leaders and pro-social preppers; (5) young people who will live through the Great Simplification; (6) the general public, for whom Nate, 'too nerdy... too goofy,' will never be spokesperson -- they are best reached by new Hollywood media that is neither potatoes on Mars nor brain-eating zombies but tangible, character-driven stories that show likely futures and give agency, so screenwriters are an audience too. THE FILM: a scientist circa 2025 who understands the metabolic energy situation finds or builds a time machine and goes back, with a childhood friend, sister or neighbor who is a currency trader or investment banker devoted to amassing financial claims. Arc one teaches the predicament without talking heads (Paul Ehrlich, tgs:Interview.TGS_9_PaulEhrlich; Dennis Meadows, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows; Bill McKibben, tgs:Interview.TGS_102_BillMcKibben; David Attenborough) and without blame: the audience infers the metabolism of eight billion humans pursuing profit, tethered to energy and ecology (tgs:Concept.Superorganism; tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism) from his interventions -- ocean regeneration and artificial reefs, raising the price of energy and non-renewable inputs, forests, cordoned-off biodiversity areas. The real arcs are the characters'. He grows famous for knowing so much, drifts into raves, women, drugs and distraction, or simply disillusionment at not making a difference. She, knowing the Hunt brothers' silver corner and the 1987 crash in advance, becomes the richest person in the world with certainty, then realizes that amassing digital claims on reality is meaningless and that the score is 'whose existence shapes the future in a meaningful way'; she becomes a massive champion of a regenerative future in service of life (tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife). Deep-pocketed people with an 'altruistic and non-delusional outlook' could do huge things, but then and now they mostly care about money and the tech singularity -- 'it's become a cult.' BONUS THEMES: (a) The Day After (tgs:Work.TheDayAfter1983), the 1983 nuclear-war film that catalyzed disarmament and moved President Reagan; it could not be done today in fractured, polarized, increasingly AI-generated media, but a 1970s dramatization of energy depletion and the oceans, with Attenborough 50 years earlier, aired to whole nations at once, might have worked: 'the superorganism of the 1970s could have functioned cognitively, at least to some degree' (tgs:Concept.SharedBroadcastPerturbation). (b) A 'one child, one dog' movement, since Frank could have jumped on the scientist's lap. (c) Education of the masses 'didn't work, isn't working, and won't work,' but education and inspiration of individuals does: with the trader's money, schools of ten, then a hundred, then a thousand, to 'breathe life into the creation of hyperagents' (tgs:Concept.EducatingHyperAgents; tgs:Concept.HyperAgents). (d) Superorganism-free zones, perhaps led by Indigenous leaders, economically self-sufficient, chosen because life is better there, acting as pilots and beacons -- not muzzling the superorganism but Bucky Fuller's logic of building something new that makes the old model obsolete (tgs:Concept.SuperorganismFreeZones). (e) Aiming for 200 years back but landing in the 1970s, returning to the present after each year to see what worked -- Groundhog Day meets Back to the Future, 'a little Thelma and Louise' -- learning aggregate human behavior as 'Asimovian psychohistory in real time.' (f) Darkest: combine humans in large numbers with water, energy surplus and time and the metabolic hierarchy of the superorganism is inevitable, as is its peak and decline, so he builds arks of humanity and biodiversity that look past climate change and propels what he and DJ White called things of value through the bottlenecks of the 21st century (tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks; tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury; tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite). WE ARE ALL TIME TRAVELERS: 50 years from now is 2075, especially for those under 30; knowing the broad biophysical landscape, what could we start today? The 1970s opportunities are gone, but opportunities to move the future away from the default remain. He asks listeners what they would do with a time machine, and announces a holiday break."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because the film's two arcs act out the two paths Daniel names in TGS_20 -- widen the values of hyper-agents (the trader) or increase the agency of people with wide values (the scientist, and his schools) -- and because Nate's 1970s 'cognitive superorganism' meets Daniel's sensemaking prerequisite (TGS_31). Nate's 'hyperagents' is looser than Daniel's tgs:Concept.HyperAgents: people of outsized influence, not the power-maximizers at the top of a power law. Reagan did watch The Day After before broadcast; his published diary says it left him 'greatly depressed' (the show notes link an account of its impact). The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'metaphysical world' is Nate's slip for the metabolism; 'outsource' is outsized; 'Richard Attenborough' is David Attenborough; 'arcs of humanity' is arks. The show notes give the same recorded and published date. Mentioned but not built: David Attenborough, Buckminster Fuller (cited in tgs:Concept.DiscretePhaseShift), Isaac Asimov's psychohistory, the Hunt brothers and Black Monday, Nate's dog Frank. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the six audiences of the show; the rich's fiduciary responsibility once they understand the metacrisis; 'we are all time travelers'; a one child, one dog movement."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_82_PowerVsLife a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Power vs Life: Towards Wide Boundary Sovereignty"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's first New Year Frankly of 2025, framing the central point the podcast has reached: 'a battle between life and power' (tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife). Power has ridden successive surpluses -- grain, fossil fuels, paper money, digital money, now AI and military power -- ever faster, while human, biological and ecological carrying capacity are diminished. As the antidote to the 'sovereign individual' of narrow-boundary wealth and power (tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual), he offers nine aspirational categories with three practices each, the first full statement of what he calls wide-boundary sovereignty (tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty): intellectual, physiological, psychological, spiritual, economic, social, local, ecological and personal, plus an added 'imaginal' category (tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells). The practices include a softened gaze, safety and connection, zones of respite, metacognition, awe, simplifying first, barbell empathy, the dining car, being a rock in the river, maximizing impact, planting seeds and being 'B plus in service of life.' Prior wise cultures shared one theme, restraint. Nate's conclusion: the sovereign individual is an oxymoron; what is needed is people sovereign together as 'islands of coherence' in service of life (tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-01-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/82-power-vs-life> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Frankly82TranscriptPowervsLife.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xWm6BEObmIg> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife, tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty, tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence,
        tgs:Concept.BarbellEmpathy, tgs:Concept.DiningCar, tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint,
        tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.PostTragic, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem, tgs:Concept.CarryingCapacity ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_109_AshleyHodgson, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "PREFACE: recorded the morning after a long first take in which Nate forgot his conclusion: the nine categories are for 'mature and resilient development in service of life,' set against the sovereign individual of narrow-boundary power and wealth; from the perspective of multilevel selection, the sovereign individual 'is an oxymoron,' and we must be sovereign 'collectively as islands of coherence together' (tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence). Frank the dog appears in a duct-taped winter coat. POWER VERSUS LIFE (tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife): a battle between power -- surplus from grains, then fossil fuels, paper money, digital money, now AI and military power, ever faster and larger -- and life, whose human, biological and ecological carrying capacity diminishes in its wake. The Sovereign Individual (William Rees-Mogg and a co-author, about 25 years ago) predicted cyber and crypto would let individuals transcend nation states (tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual); a 'sovereign man' website offers a checklist. Nate asks what the barbell, the antidote, is for someone aware of overshoot and biophysical macroeconomics, and notes that wise cultures shared one theme, restraint (tgs:Concept.RestraintByDesign). THE CATEGORIES (tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty): INTELLECTUAL -- foundational reality (biophysical macroeconomics, human brain and behavior, ecology), systems synthesis (connections instead of things), a softened gaze (curiosity and humility, a probability distribution). PHYSIOLOGICAL -- nourishment (nutrition, exercise, sleep, breathing; the behavioral stack from Nate's Frankly a year earlier, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_52_TheBehavioralStack), safety and connection (the ventral vagal state; his craniosacral therapist told him some Franklys show a dysregulated nervous system -- 'facts alone ain't gonna cut it'), spaciousness (an inner core beneath the cultural 'hurricane'; after a party of TGS fans he did not check his phone until 3 p.m.). PSYCHOLOGICAL -- ancestral intimacy (getting ancestral neurotransmitters without the work, via porn, stock options, shopping and likes, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli), zones of respite walled off from work (ducks, chickens, dogs, Spelling Bee), metacognition (the 'little Nate' primatologist on his shoulder). SPIRITUAL -- awe (a piebald deer on a hike), connectedness (stardust; holons in both directions), the unknown. ECONOMIC -- pre-simplification for resilience (68 devices plugged in per American household; the tortoise and the hare), slow by slow (planting potatoes over Candy Crush), fasting from shopping, consumption and travel. SOCIAL -- barbell empathy (tgs:Concept.BarbellEmpathy), equanimity (pre-tragic, tragic and post-tragic, tgs:Concept.PostTragic; blamelessness, since 'it's the system, it's the incentives'; an inner core of decency), the dining car (tgs:Concept.DiningCar; Ashley Hodgson's three social groups, tgs:Interview.TGS_109_AshleyHodgson; Nate's are systems ecologists, friends to fish and hike with, and people trying to change initial conditions). LOCAL -- a sit spot, a skill relevant to a simpler future, being a rock in the river (tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver). ECOLOGICAL -- a visualization of decision hierarchy with AI and geopolitics at the top and citizens' well-being and the environment at the bottom (an early form of tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem), so ecology must be served strategically, 'in service of life underground,' and a philanthropy framework is promised next; maximize your impact rather than minimize it (tgs:Concept.MaximizeImpactNotMinimizeFootprint); plant seeds whose fruit you may not see. PERSONAL -- comfort with discomfort, glimmers (ten minutes watching birds instead of checking email), and 'B plus in service of life' (tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife), contrasted with DJ White's 'A plus' work saving perhaps a million dolphins (tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite). IMAGINAL: cells that are neither caterpillar nor butterfly and self-organize into the butterfly (tgs:Concept.ImaginalCells). SCENARIOS: tweaking the system (no longer possible, he thinks), bend not break toward a great simplification (tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification), or collapse; the categories apply to all three. Rather than asking how to get from 20 terawatts to 10, it starts with individuals understanding, building resilience, finding the others, then strategizing and engaging -- 'islands of coherence of islands of coherence.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub (the prefLabel now carries the full site title). This is the first full statement of the nine categories that tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty was minted from in TGS_187 (Earth Day 2025, published 2025-07-25); the arrangements differ slightly (here ancestral intimacy is psychological and spaciousness physiological), and the imaginal category is added here at the end. It also predates the sources from which tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver (Frankly_88), tgs:Concept.BarbellEmpathy and tgs:Concept.DiningCar (TGS_187) were minted; their scopeNotes now record that. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment as Nate's practical and personal counterpart to Daniel's power analysis (see tgs:Concept.PowerVersusLife). The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Frank' in 'to be Frank' is a pun on the dog; 'sapt direction' is unclear in the source; 'starkest tuna' is StarKist tuna; 'poorest borders' is porous borders. The show-note Reality Roundtable link for DJ White (youtu.be/22fc66Q2hP4) is taken to be tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse, the only roundtable with DJ White before this episode; the ID is not otherwise confirmed. The promised 'framework for philanthropy' Frankly is not identified. Mentioned but not built: William Rees-Mogg (co-author of tgs:Work.TheSovereignIndividual, with James Dale Davidson), the 'sovereign man' website, Nate's craniosacral therapist and coaches. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted (all inside tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySovereignty): softened gaze, zones of respite, sit spot, glimmers, slow by slow, fasting from consumption, B plus in service of life."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_83_ArtificialIntelligenceAndTheLostArk a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Artificial Intelligence and the Lost Ark"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly argues public discourse on AI overwhelmingly fixates on its micro-level benefits (better medicine, cheaper products) while overlooking its real, systemic, macro-level risks — laying out seven such risks, counted down from least to most severe, and directly framing the current moment as a species-level 'Icarus moment,' technological ambition risking a real outstripping of collective wisdom."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-01-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/83-artificial-intelligence-and-the-lost-ark> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mh6iuXmJ6uM> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/Frankly83TranscriptArtificialIntelligenceandtheLostArk.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Human.ArtBerman, tgs:Human.FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with a real, current peak-oil update sourced from his own real, direct outreach to industry contacts: AI is genuinely widening the extraction 'straw' by improving drilling precision and logistics (citing a real, direct example, a 60-mile automated sand conveyor belt), shaving roughly 10-15% off drilling costs and modestly raising the recovery rate on unconventional wells, even as US crude-plus-condensate remains several million barrels below its real November 2018 peak. He's real and direct about a genuinely striking energy-cost comparison: a single AI query is estimated at 10 to 100 times the energy cost of an equivalent Google search, offered directly as evidence the coming AI buildout represents a genuine, structural new draw on electricity and minerals, not a marginal one. A real, direct personal disclosure anchors his own concern about relational loss: crediting a recent, not-yet-released conversation with Francis Weller (already built) for the distinction between 'primary satisficers' (ancestral bonding, shared challenge, music) and 'secondary satisficers' (gambling, shopping, pornography), directly predicting AI will accelerate the shift toward the latter. He's real and direct that this is fundamentally a 'Lost Ark' scenario: comparing the pursuit of artificial superintelligence directly to Raiders of the Lost Ark's own ending, where an relentless pursuit of ultimate power destroys the very people reaching for it — a real, specific childhood memory of being frightened by the film at age 12. Closes on real, direct, unresolved personal ambivalence rather than false reassurance: Nate names his own real practice of 'using the devil's tools to do Gaia's work' by using AI sparingly for this very podcast, while directly, honestly stating he has no real answers to the risks he's just laid out, only genuine concern."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct numbering quirk preserved rather than silently corrected: Nate's own live count-down briefly loses track mid-episode ('point number five, or point number three in reverse order'), genuinely confusing his own ordering for a moment — reflected honestly in tgs:Concept.SevenAIMacroRisks's own definition rather than smoothed into a falsely clean sequence. Real, separate discrepancy also worth noting: this episode's own real, direct reference is to 'one of the four horsemen' (financial overshoot, geopolitics/war, the social contract, complexity), while the already-built tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s (from Frankly_92, recorded three months later) names five — Nate's own count appears to have grown over time rather than this being a transcription error; linked to the five-item Concept here since no separate four-item individual exists, with this discrepancy flagged directly rather than silently reconciled. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (ExxonMobil's 2024 Global Outlook, AESI's automated conveyor system) rather than pointing to further individuals — Art Berman, Francis Weller, and John Vervaeke are all already built and directly cross-referenced, not new finds."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_84_ShareYourStory a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-19"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Share Your Story"@en ;
    skos:definition "A short call for submissions rather than a content episode. Answering listeners who ask what to do, Nate says he sets out the constraints but does not have the answers, and believes in emergence: many responses that will scale are not yet known. He invites viewers to send a video of up to three minutes on what they are doing in response to what they have learned on the channel, for three reasons: a montage episode of 30 or 40 submissions showing the diversity of people responding around the world; because humans are imitators, to show others what they could do where they live; and, if submissions are plentiful, perhaps an open-source website of the 'horizontal and vertical categories of response to the human predicament.' He thanks those already responding: redirecting mangrove carbon revenue to local villagers, teaching wide-boundary systems thinking, community gardens and tool sharing, planting for pollinators, learning farming, woodworking and mending, reducing consumption and living well with less, and leaving careers that reinforce the economic superorganism for more meaningful work."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-02-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.CommunityAndResponse ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/84-share-your-story> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lA3GRnoN1kc> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://senja.io/p/the-great-simplification/r/LO0uRO> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-19 from MJSullivan's transcript and the episode description, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. A call for submissions with no new concepts; no Schmachtenberger bridge. Dates and the submission link (rdfs:seeAlso, a Senja form) are from the episode page; no transcript PDF is published. Submissions were to close at the end of February 2025. The promised montage episode and open-source website of responses are not identified in the graph. Transcript artifacts: 'solution ions' is solutions; 'unted' is probably untested; 'beat New Paths' is beat new paths; 'ipsilanti' is Ypsilanti."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_85_TheLightTriad a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Light Triad"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own solo exploration of Scott Barry Kaufman's 'Light Triad' (faith in humanity, humanism, Kantianism) as a real but structurally weaker counterweight to the Dark Triad — arguing traits generally outcompete values, so a lone Light Triad individual will be exploited by a Dark Triad one, and only real, mutually-supporting groups (not just individual goodness) stand a chance. Closes with open questions he doesn't resolve: how many people does a Light Triad individual need around them for real protection, whether Dark Triad prevalence rises or falls as the Carbon Pulse declines, and whether a Dark Triad personality can genuinely change."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-02-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/85-the-light-triad> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Frankly85Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ai8Ksd2TtEg> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.LightTriad, tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues, tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection,
        tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "OPENING: a request for viewers' three-minute videos on how they are responding to the Great Simplification. THE SPARK: at a California conference someone said their organization screens interviewees for dark-triad flags and light-triad signposts; one friend took the light triad to be the opposite of the dark triad, another a milder version of it. A LinkedIn essay then contrasted the dark triad -- narcissism, sociopathy and Machiavellianism, plus sadism in the dark tetrad (tgs:Concept.DarkTriad) -- with a 'light triad' of sustainable stewardship, interconnectedness of all beings and respect for all beings. Nate liked it, then saw the asymmetry: those are values, the dark triad are traits, and 'traits will outcompete values in almost all circumstances' (tgs:Concept.TraitsOutcompeteValues). He treats this as a microcosm of the times: many expect facts and values to overcome traits, behavior and structures, which is the is-versus-ought divide; he leans toward the biophysical frame of a species seeking maximum power with 'a trainload full of sugar dumped in our path' (tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple; tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism). Humans are plastic but have guardrails; facts and values are 'necessary and important, but not sufficient.' THE REAL LIGHT TRIAD (tgs:Concept.LightTriad): Scott Barry Kaufman and colleagues' faith in humanity, humanism and Kantianism (seeing people as ends, not means) -- more trait-like; most of Nate's friends have it. ANCESTRAL CHECKS: dark-triad individuals always existed, but in groups of 150 or fewer they could be watched, cordoned off, killed or outweighed (tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber); at eight billion and on social media, authenticity is lost, so social media is 'the devil's tools in the dark triad service,' countered only face to face. GROUPS: a lone light-triad person meeting a dark-triad one will be manipulated; light-triad people need to be surrounded and supported by others, as in multilevel selection, where selfish individuals beat altruists within groups but cooperative groups beat selfish groups (tgs:Concept.MultilevelSelection, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence). QUESTIONS: how many supporters does it take to ward off a dark-triad influence -- three, five, eight? (forming such groups now is 'a no regret strategy'); will dark-triad traits grow or recede on the downslope of the carbon pulse (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse) -- he suspects strong reciprocity may return as people meet face to face; how could incentives reward light-triad behavior when everything is fungible for the dollar; and can dark-triad people be flipped -- he thinks Machiavellian or narcissistic people with low sociopathy can, as he sees people waking up, but probably not those high in sociopathy or sadism. CLOSING: he will not chase the news; some things he knows he will not be able to say publicly, as information hazards (tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard), but he commits to honesty and authenticity. 'We are now in the early scenes of The Empire Strikes Back,' auditioning for Return of the Jedi. Next week: what is wealth."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, completing the 2026-08-13 modeled entry (its definition is kept). Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment as Nate's counterpart to Daniel's dark-triad material in TGS_126 (tgs:Concept.PowerSeekingAndComplicity), which makes the same point about tribes checking aggressive outliers. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Scott Berry Kaufman' is Scott Barry Kaufman; 'Soran' is Sauron; 'the UN' is the yuan. The show notes link the LinkedIn post, a ScienceDirect paper on traits versus values, Kaufman's article and a Springer entry on multilevel selection. Mentioned but not built: the conference speaker and the LinkedIn author (named only in the show-note URL), Immanuel Kant, Gandhi. The 'what is wealth' Frankly announced for next week is tgs:Monologue.Frankly_86_WhatIsWealth (a stub). Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: social media as the dark triad's tool; whether the downslope of the carbon pulse favors light or dark triad traits."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_172_LivingTheChange ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_86_WhatIsWealth a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What is Wealth?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's evolutionary, historical and wide-boundary answer to 'what is wealth?', previewing a Frankly on debt. All wealth originates in nature: animals were the first investors, spending energy to get calories, and for most species wealth equals income. He sorts wealth into primary (food, water, shelter, freedom), secondary (stockpiles that smooth access to it) and tertiary -- claims on social power, which in humans has positive rather than negative feedback (tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth). With a fungible global reserve currency, 'the mother of all straws,' we are transmuting the world's wealth into income at an astronomical scale (tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome), a tragedy of the commons within the wider class of collective action problems (tgs:Concept.CollectiveActionProblem, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons): 'every fleet is someone's foreign fleet.' Money is only a marker for built, human, social and natural capital, and much of what is priceless has no price. A regenerative culture would do the opposite -- transmute income into wealth."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/86-what-is-wealth> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Frankly86TranscriptWhatisWealth_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wqXDFcerOWs> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth, tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome, tgs:Concept.CollectiveActionProblem,
        tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons, tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.CostOfExtractionPricing, tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "FRAMING: wealth depends on who is asking and over what timeframe -- dollars, silver, Bitcoin, nuts, mealworms, a school of fish. The pursuit of wealth on a finite planet is 'a subset of a collective action problem and is central to the human predicament'; this is groundwork for a coming Frankly on debt. NATURE: by optimal foraging theory animals were the first investors, spending energy to gain calories, so energy is the currency of life. For most species wealth equals income, because the ecological backdrop is stable within a lifetime (barring a Chicxulub meteor) and consumption is capped by body size -- a lion does not eat ten gazelles. Nate proposes primary wealth (food, water, shelter, and freedom, which a zoo animal lacks), secondary wealth (catalysts and stockpiles that smooth access to primary wealth) and, for humans, tertiary wealth -- claims on social power, 'a stockpile on the stockpile' (tgs:Concept.ThreeTiersOfWealth). At the end of a life, wealth is denominated in relative fitness: offspring compared with one's conspecifics, which unconsciously drives behavior through neurotransmitter cascades -- evolutionary hardware and software. In nature more meets negative feedback (the lion naps); tertiary wealth has positive feedback. HUMANS: for 290,000 of 300,000 years human wealth was social and human capital, since we could not store much; agriculture brought grain, then barter, gold, fiat and a global fiat currency. The US dollar is 'from a maximum power energetic perspective, the mother of all straws' (tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple): fungible for land, political influence, a house, a refinery, energy, or Loverboy albums. Money is a unit of account, a medium of exchange and, less and less, a store of value, as financial claims grow on a stable or declining biophysical base. The result: we are transmuting the wealth of the world into income (tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome). COLLECTIVE ACTION: there are three kinds -- the prisoner's dilemma, the free rider and the tragedy of the commons (tgs:Concept.CollectiveActionProblem, tgs:Concept.TragedyOfTheCommons). Nate quotes his and DJ White's book (tgs:Work.RealityBlind): fishers consider themselves good stewards and blame foreign fleets, 'but every fleet is someone's foreign fleet' -- the same dynamic as ever more financial claims on biophysical reality. TWO REALITIES: individual wealth-seeking, partly from fear of the future, and a macro drawdown of the world's wealth called income. Financial capital is a marker for built, human, social and natural capital; Carmina Burana, a Philharmonic performance of The Good, the Bad and the Ugly, cold oxygenated oceans and Ecuador's bird species are priceless and unpriced, and will be recognized as wealth only once gone (tgs:Concept.GameTheoreticVersusRealValue). COMPARISON: people compare themselves to the cultural metric of success and to their own past; his Wall Street clients counted $9 million after $10 million a failure; climate was never going to be a populist cause because people want cheaper energy. 'It's no one's fault' -- biology, culture and a bolus of ancient sunlight (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse) in a power-law Pachinko game. The system 'pretends that we can create wealth by printing fake wealth' and is reaching terminal velocity; people buy Bitcoin, land and gold, but no claim is worth anything if the biosphere spirals. QUESTIONS: how do you define wealth; could the definition change in our lifetimes, top down or bottom up; and could a regenerative culture transmute income into wealth, and what are the first steps?"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub (the prefLabel now carries the site's question mark). Announced at the end of tgs:Monologue.Frankly_85_TheLightTriad. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment as Nate's counterpart to Daniel's account of money (tgs:Concept.MoneyAsMaximumOptionality, TGS_20). Spoken versus show notes: Nate says 1,800 bird species in Ecuador; the show notes correct this to about 1,600. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'Chicla meteor' is Chicxulub; 'Carmina Berena' is Carmina Burana; 'a mine and DJ White's book' is Reality Blind. Nate says he wrote the quoted fishing paragraph about ten years earlier. The Frankly on debt he previews is not identified. Mentioned but not built: Ennio Morricone. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the four capitals (built, human, social, natural; inside tgs:Concept.TransmutingWealthIntoIncome); comparison to one's own past (close to tgs:Concept.EmbeddedGrowthObligation's personal sense)."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_87_TheCentralQuestionsOfOurTime a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "(Some of) The Central Questions of Our Time"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded on a plane ride home from filming a real PBS segment on energy and climate change, Nate directly discloses a real, personal shift in his own priors: he now believes he'd underestimated both the speed of the 'AI pulse' and how soon meaningful governance capacity might be lost. He offers a real, structured list of central open questions, deliberately followed by personal questions for the listener's own life, framed directly against his own real, stated commitment to never offer dystopian framing without also offering agency."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-02-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-02-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/87-some-of-the-central-questions-of-our-time> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bp2dAKNBwgQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Frankly87TranscriptCentralQuestionsofOurTime.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly poses whether some form of open society can survive the downslope of the Carbon Pulse, noting real feudalism preceded the carbon pulse and may well be the real, structural default afterward absent deliberate intervention. A real, vivid teaching example anchors his second question, prioritizing life over death: asking his own real Reality 101 students what an armadillo is 'for' typically produces uncomfortable silence before answers like wallets or ashtrays, which he directly uses to argue every species performs real, concrete ecosystem functions — armadillos specifically aerate soil and disperse seeds — independent of any human use. He's real and direct in proposing a speculative 'fifth law of thermodynamics,' building on the real, already-established fourth law (the Maximum Power Principle, already a formal Concept in this graph): if humans can genuinely value a future they don't directly, physically inhabit, they might deliberately throttle exosomatic energy use rather than simply maximizing it, since the underlying neurochemical reward can in principle be decoupled from the actual energy burned to get it. A real, structural question about resource allocation closes his central list: what percentage of collective effort should go toward propping up current systems, preparing for a 'Bend Not Break' transition, or actively seeding a saner successor system — a real, direct three-way framing that anticipates the later, more formal three-phase timeline in Frankly_132's own Framework for Action. Closes with a real, credited term from a friend, Greg Rufa: a 'coalition of sanity,' made up of people who are post-partisan, wide-boundary, principled, and willing to organize together — offered directly as something Nate believes is genuinely called for now, not a future aspiration."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Greg Rufa (real, directly credited friend, coined 'coalition of sanity,' not built given the brief, single-mention nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Snow, The Singularity, and Rocks in the River"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Unusually personal and reflective even by Frankly standards — recorded during a real spring blizzard in west-central Wisconsin, Nate opens by naming the real, current chaos of the week (tariffs, Russia/NATO/Ukraine tension, AI) before explicitly shifting register: 'I feel less about explaining and describing, and more about feeling.' Reframes the energy transition not as a question of which technology or fuel source, but of how humans relate to energy, each other, and the natural world — arguing this starts internally, not externally. Directly references a real conversation the prior day with Audrey Tang (Taiwan's former digital minister, already a real, independently-flagged deferred citation elsewhere in this graph), who offered 'plurality' as a real, direct alternative framing to Kurzweil's technological singularity. Also references presenting to the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent's senior leadership (a real organization with 20 million volunteers) the day before, where Nate's own real recommendation was not to try to reform the current system but to cultivate 'rocks in the river' — a small, real number of psychologically prepared, grounded individuals within communities who can act as stabilizing anchors as broader systems destabilize. This episode is the real origin of that metaphor, independently confirmed by RR-26's own real citation of this episode months later."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/88-snow-the-singularity-and-rocks-in-the-river> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QYFfbIY-R88> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Frankly88TranscriptsSnowTheSingularityandRocksintheRiver.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct neurochemical reframing structures the episode's core argument: material objects (a couch, an Xbox, a book, a toaster) are described as providing real 'brain services,' with the deeper crisis being an overreliance on dopamine-driven engagement at the expense of oxytocin (bonding, community) and serotonin — 'the ghost of dopamine passed,' a recurring phrase Nate returns to twice. A real, personal disclosure grounds the abstract argument concretely: rather than checking news or messaging his team at 5am during the blizzard, Nate sat with coffee, listened to Brian Eno, and wrote a poem he explicitly declines to share, calling it 'personal and intimate' — offered as a small, lived example of the same inward attention he's recommending to listeners. Real institutional stakes appear directly: Nate discloses presenting to the International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent's senior leadership the day before, expanding his usual five-horsemen framework (financial overshoot, geopolitics, complexity, the social contract, ecological degradation) to explicitly include AI governance and the erosion of open-society freedoms as an emergent sixth concern. His own real recommendation to a 20-million-volunteer global organization is notably unhedged: it's too late to reform the current system, and the real task now is cultivating a real, small percentage (his own estimate: 2-5%) of mature, grounded people within any given community who can function as stabilizing anchors — 'rocks in the river' — for when change accelerates. The episode closes with Nate reading, in full, William Butler Yeats's 'The Second Coming' — not reproduced here per this graph's own copyright discipline (see e.g. RR-14's own Gary Snyder citation for the same practice), only the fact and real thematic fit of the recitation: its apocalyptic, chaotic imagery directly mirrors the episode's own opening list of current crises. Closes on an unusually warm, direct sign-off — 'love you all' — distinct from Nate's more typical closings."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Audrey Tang — already independently flagged as a real, deferred citation in multiple other episodes this graph (Frankly_132's own scopeNote among them), reconfirmed here via this episode's own direct, dated reference to a real conversation 'yesterday.' tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s is now built (2026-09-08), directly resolving this standing note — Frankly_92's own real, direct enumeration of the framework provided the formal source. Brian Eno (real musician, directly named) not built as a Persona — a passing personal reference, not a TGS-relevant citation in the sense this graph otherwise captures."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_89_ThinkingAndFeeling ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_89_ThinkingAndFeeling a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thinking and Feeling"@en ;
    skos:definition "A reply to listeners who asked why, after the poem of Frankly_88, Nate had turned to 'soft psychology' instead of debt, Trump, Ukraine and oil depletion. He is more worried than ever -- the paths to bending rather than breaking have narrowed in three months -- though a friend says it does not emanate from him. He reads a voicemail from one of the 'wise and clever women' in his inner circle: seeing only the problems creates more of them, because 'when only hell is seen, then all the solutions are fear based, and anger based, and control, and power.' Nate names the dichotomies -- subjective and objective, thinking and feeling, left and right brain, masculine and feminine, narrow and wide boundary, analytical and embodied -- and asks for a larger overlap in the Venn diagram (tgs:Concept.IntegratingThinkingAndFeeling): he would want neither a community of heady metacrisis experts with dysregulated nervous systems nor one of embodied dancers with no knowledge or skills. Watching US science agencies being 'keystroked out of existence,' he is angry and sad, not depressed, and finds clarity on next steps from feeling, not more analysis."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-03-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-03-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/89-thinking-and-feeling> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Frankly89TranscriptThinkingandFeeling-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JOjZ1na2L_4> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.IntegratingThinkingAndFeeling, tgs:Concept.DividedBrain, tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure,
        tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "FEEDBACK: many loved last week's 'Rocks in the River' Frankly and its poem (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver), recorded tired early in the morning; others asked what had happened to him -- talking soft psychology instead of debt, Trump, the Ukraine war and interest rates, when they liked episodes such as Dennis Meadows on oil depletion (tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows). There are important biophysical macroeconomic developments, and he has much to say about them, but he takes 'another stab' at the dichotomy of thinking and feeling. MORE WORRIED THAN EVER: on a hike a friend asked what had made him less concerned about the future; in fact he is more worried than ever, and thinks 'the pathways to bending as opposed to breaking have narrowed in the last three months' -- but the friend did not feel it emanating from him. THE VOICEMAIL: one of the 'wise and clever women' in his inner circle said the Frankly balanced the world's problems, without bypassing them, with 'so much beauty and so much love and living and being and feeling in every moment' -- snow, dogs playing -- and that narrowly looking at the problems creates more of them: 'when only hell is seen, then all the solutions are fear based, and anger based, and control, and power.' For five years, mostly women had told him similar things and he 'didn't get it,' answering with debt doubling every eight years while productivity and GDP double every 25, and oil depleting ever faster. DICHOTOMIES (tgs:Concept.IntegratingThinkingAndFeeling): subjective and objective, thinking and feeling, left brain and right brain (tgs:Concept.DividedBrain), masculine and feminine, narrow and wide boundary, analytical and embodied -- 'we can't be on one side of each of those equations.' Would he live in a community that knew every aspect of the metacrisis and Marvin Harris's infrastructure, social structure and superstructure (tgs:Concept.InfrastructureSocialStructureSuperstructure) but was all in its head, nervous systems dysregulated, without joy, music, laughter or love? Or one fully embodied, dancing and meditating, with no knowledge of the metacrisis and no skills to plant food or MacGyver energy, water and food systems? Neither; he wants both kinds of people, yet increasingly meets groups that are one or the other, so 'we need to make the Venn diagram between these two modalities with a larger overlap.' THE CANDLE DIMMING: 'the light and the candle of civilization is dimming. I both think that and I feel it.' Whatever its military bases and clandestine actions, the United States has made major contributions to humanity in the last 50 years, and US agencies that generate and share vital knowledge of the planet -- NOAA, NASA, the Weather Service, Fish and Wildlife, the Park Service, the EPA, the DOE -- are being 'keystroked out of existence'; no other government can boast comparable contributions. After more than a decade on the metacrisis and the Great Simplification (tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification; tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries) he was blindsided, having assumed values, facts and science would remain available for civic discourse and for changing minds based on objective reality. He is angry and sad, 'not depressed... engaged,' understands the global biophysical, anthropological, macro situation better than ever and will share updated logic soon; from that anger and sadness comes 'intense clarity on next steps,' which 'are not going to come from more analysis and more understanding of the facts. They're going to come from feeling.' He is still a biophysical macro analyst, integrating 'a wide boundary humanity.' Next week, some analysis."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because Nate's two imagined communities -- knowledge without embodiment, embodiment without knowledge or skill -- are failure modes of the kind Daniel names in tgs:Concept.HeartWillMind (TGS_31). The show notes' 'The Great Simplification' link (-xr9rIQxwj4) is the animated film, here referenced as tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries rather than its 2025 re-release in the main feed, which postdates this Frankly. The show notes list the IEA among US agencies, as Nate does; it is an intergovernmental agency, not a US one. Spoken figures not checked: debt doubling every eight years, productivity and GDP every 25. The voicemail is quoted as read; its 'solutions are all emergence' is probably 'emergency.' Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the candle of civilization dimming; wide-boundary humanity."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Net Zero and Other Delusions: What Can't, Won't and Might Happen"@en ;
    skos:definition "Because humans think in words, we can imagine anything -- a gold-plated blimp with Putin's face lasering curry restaurants over London -- and some impossible sentences sound plausible. Nate sorts futures into three categories (tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen): what can't happen, ruled out by physical law or by path dependence; what won't happen, technically possible but effectively impossible once the probabilities of all its necessary steps are multiplied (a 500-foot cheese statue of a mouse; tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability); and what might happen, where attention belongs. His worked example is net zero by 2050: grant it a 20 percent technical chance, ceteris paribus, then stack the hurdles -- tiny carbon capture against 40 billion tons, fossil fuels growing faster than renewables, 'no green revolution, only a green addition,' financial overshoot and rising rates, governance and democracy, incompetence as complexity risk, AI, war and supply chains, the social contract, and nature -- and the odds are 'effectively zero.' His own Bayesian priors on AI and on governance have shifted sharply. What can't and won't happen lie outside the walls of the bottlenecks of the 21st century; what might happen lies inside, and the walls are narrowing."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/90-net-zero-and-other-delusions> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Frankly90TranscriptNetZeroandOtherDelusions.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UfWS97d8X6c> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen, tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability, tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions,
        tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks, tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_89_ThinkingAndFeeling, tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_71_WeakestLinks,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "WORDS: 'Most humans think in words, and this is a big problem for our future.' We can picture a gold-plated Hindenburg with Vladimir Putin's face floating over London, its eyes lasering every curry restaurant -- fanciful -- but some sentences sound plausible, like battery-powered filters letting us breathe underwater, or a global culture at today's scale reaching net zero by 2050 from about 41 billion tons of emissions. THREE CATEGORIES (tgs:Concept.WhatCantWontMightHappen): most public discourse about the future is in what can't or won't happen, when we need to focus on what might. WHAT CAN'T HAPPEN has two subcategories: physical laws -- physics, chemistry, thermodynamics; from a one-by-two-foot block of wood you cannot carve a four-foot man or a six-foot car, though you can carve a two-foot duck, shoes or spoons -- and path dependence: after a global thermonuclear war there will be radiation; if tree frogs go extinct there will be no tree frogs. WHAT WON'T HAPPEN is 'stringing together aggregate probabilities of unlikely things' (tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability): a 500-foot cheese statue of a mouse is technically possible, but permitting, scaffolding, cheese stability and dozens or hundreds of other steps must all succeed, and 'you have to multiply all these probabilities,' so the answer is effectively zero. Society holds many 500-foot cheese mice. NET ZERO BY 2050: still heavily funded and believed. Assume, against Nate's own view of 15 years, that a 100 percent renewable economy at today's scale is technically possible, and give it a 20 percent chance -- but that is ceteris paribus, and 'we are decidedly at a ceteris paribus not moment.' The hurdles to multiply: technology -- carbon capture removes around 10,000 tons a year against the 40 billion we emit, fossil fuel use is growing twice as fast as renewables, copper and other limits; the metabolism of the system -- 'there has been no green revolution, only a green addition' (tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions), and an economy geared to GDP, profits and growth tethered to energy cannot shrink one source while growing the other; financial overshoot -- when Trump pulled back support for Ukraine and Germany moved to boost defense spending, Bund yields hit multi-year highs, and debt is a 'lodestone' on physical plans; re-industrializing the US with tariffs works only with a weak dollar, which would collapse the petrodollar debt-based system; rising interest rates hit long-duration energy investment; the financial Wile E. Coyote bend-not-break moment is closer; governance, open society and democracy, now an open question, with red lines being crossed and possible authoritarian decisions driven by energy security; incompetence as 'complexity risk' -- Pete Hegseth's use of Signal -- 'for want of a nail' (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_89_ThinkingAndFeeling, cited in the show notes); artificial intelligence (tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin): if machines move toward AGI or ASI and gradually take over, 'they're not gonna be optimizing for net zero'; war and global supply chains (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_71_WeakestLinks; tgs:Concept.SupplyChainFragility) -- aircraft carriers moving to the Middle East, a possible conflict with Iran, the Strait of Hormuz, drones crossing borders undetected, and the US retreat as global policeman leading Japan, Taiwan or a European country to seek nuclear weapons; the social contract -- Tesla boycotts, polarization between the new right and the progressive left; and nature -- a warmer world, and decarbonization requiring a huge rematerialization from regions already facing poverty, civil strife and higher wet-bulb temperatures. Multiply them and the 20 percent becomes 'effectively zero. So it could happen, but it won't happen.' BAYESIAN PRIORS: Bayesian inference updates a prior by conditional probability; the hurdles are not fully independent -- a debt collapse would hit supply chains and competence -- but he treats them as independent for the exercise. He has changed his priors, especially on AI and on governance and open society: his estimate of losing a system in which facts and civic discourse matter has moved from about 5 to 50 percent in six months. BOTTLENECKS: in the book he and DJ White wrote (tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury; tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite), a bottleneck is where something narrows and then passes through -- species, ecosystems, values, population; what can't and won't happen are outside the walls of the bottle, what might happen inside, and as energy grows scarcer and costlier the walls will shrink; the question is what things of value to propel through (tgs:Concept.ThingsOfValueThroughTheBottlenecks), but first we must see that the bottle has walls. QUESTIONS: do you change your priors, and how? What other cultural expectations fail this gauntlet? Does it change the urgency or plans of your projects? CONCLUSION: even if technically achievable on paper, net zero by 2050 has real-world odds near zero; 'the path is not blocked by one giant wall. It's a maze of interlocking barriers' that multiply and amplify each other. That does not mean giving up, but replacing lofty goals shaped by ideology and PR with grounded actions shaped by physics, ecology and human behavior. 'The question isn't how to solve climate change. It's how to live wisely in a world where the old solutions no longer apply.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub; kept its back-link from Frankly_91. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because Nate's sorting of futures meets Daniel's move from impossible to hard (tgs:Concept.ImpossibleToHard, TGS_20). Here Nate states the multiplication of probabilities explicitly, answering the note on tgs:Concept.AggregateProbability (Frankly_79). Spoken figures recorded, not checked: carbon capture of 'around 10,000 tons' a year (current operating direct-air and point-source capture together are generally reported in the tens of millions of tons a year, so this likely refers to direct air capture alone); 41 and 40 billion tons of emissions; fossil fuel growing twice as fast as renewables by volume (stated as a show-note heading without a link). Reference resolution: 'Complexity risk [in Frankly 89]' in the show notes links Frankly_89, although Frankly_89's transcript does not use the phrase; the 'Financial Overshoot' video (hYQDEa99pYU) is not identified in the graph. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'hindenberg' is Hindenburg; 'tree fogs' is tree frogs; 'this pretends for' is portends; 'reduce zero emissions' is reach net-zero emissions; 'tenure notes, the boons' are ten-year Bunds; 'load stone' is lodestone; 'Wiley coyote' is Wile E. Coyote; 'strait of Horus' is Hormuz; 'nuclear war weapons' is nuclear weapons; 'root to' is road to; 'DJ and I white' is DJ White and I. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: ceteris paribus not; complexity risk from incompetence; rematerialization."@en ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_91_UnintendedConsequences ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_91_UnintendedConsequences a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-18"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unintended Consequences in a Complex World"@en ;
    skos:definition "A short Frankly, recorded with a spring flu, on unintended consequences: 'when we think we're doing something, and the end result ends up being quite something else' (tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences). Nate's examples: the peak oil discussion of 2005 to 2009 was 'debunked' when shale and debt extended the plateau, so no one now talks about oil shortages even though peak oil may have been November 2018; climate science, legitimate and scary but unfolding over decades, was lumped together with failed five-year doom predictions and biophysically naive solutions, so the culture, especially in the US, developed 'antibodies' to the discussion (tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies); and, as he reads the news, a well-intended call to attention on racial justice whose extrapolation arguably helped elect Donald Trump, whose crackdown on universities is now cutting science funding, with further consequences for foreign students, Harvard and Massachusetts, and possibly Europe's turn away from the United States. His response is to look one, two or three steps ahead, tether to a science-based systems view, and keep humility: the world needs 'science-tethered, transpartisan, open-eyed, open-hearted awareness.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/91-unintended-consequences> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Frankly91TranscriptUnintendedConsequences.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5o3DqV04Jy0> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences, tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies, tgs:Concept.PeakOil, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions, tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A CHANGE OF PLAN: Nate meant to continue 'what can't happen, what won't happen, and what might' (tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions), which he now plans as a series, but a hike and the morning news turned him to unintended consequences (tgs:Concept.UnintendedConsequences). PEAK OIL: between 2005 and 2009 conventional oil peaking drew global attention (tgs:Concept.PeakOil; tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil); shale and debt extended the peak and plateau to November 2018, which he says is still about 3 million barrels a day above today, so peak oil 'quite possibly could be 2018,' yet no one discusses future shortages, and the carbon pulse and its downslope (tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse; tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse) are on no one's mind -- an unintended consequence of the theme being 'debunked.' CLIMATE: over 15 years, doomsayers predicted things within five years that did not happen, and climate science, 'quite legitimate and scary' but unfolding over decades and centuries, was lumped together with solutions -- the World Economic Forum's 'eat bugs and own nothing and be happy,' or net zero -- that are 'systemically and biophysically naive.' So the culture, especially in the United States, 'developed antibodies to these discussions' (tgs:Concept.CulturalAntibodies); simplistic responses go viral while the science shows a perilous long-term situation. 'It's not because it isn't real, it's because how it was presented and packaged.' SOCIAL MOVEMENTS: one could argue the origins of 'wokeness' were legitimate, calling attention to the United States' treatment of Native and minority populations, but its extrapolation into a narrative Nate calls disconnected from reality arguably led to the election of Donald Trump -- itself downstream of Biden's 2020 win, which made a 2024 Trump presidency possible. A further consequence: the administration's moves against universities are quashing science funding and research; foreign students are deleting social media for fear of deportation; Americans abroad may face retribution; and Massachusetts worries what lost funding for Harvard would do to its economy. GEOPOLITICS: the US pulling back from the war in Ukraine isolates Europe; Nate floats, and immediately qualifies with 'I don't know,' that the US may have been involved in the pipeline sabotage that made Europe dependent on US LNG; over time Europe may part ways with the US and turn back toward resource-rich Russia on the same continent. RESPONSE: look one, two or three steps ahead; tether to science and a systems view; keep humility; learn 'how the worm turns.' What the world needs is 'science-tethered, transpartisan, open-eyed, open-hearted awareness of our situation.' Next week: more on the bottleneck."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-18 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub. Built in the Schmachtenberger enrichment because it is Nate's practical case for Daniel's yellow teaming (tgs:Concept.YellowTeaming, TGS_126) and for his warning about premature certainty (tgs:Concept.PrematureCertainty, TGS_31). The political examples are recorded as Nate's own, hedged readings ('one could argue,' 'arguably'), not as findings of this graph. Spoken claims recorded but not checked: that oil production in April 2025 was about 3 million barrels a day below November 2018 (compare tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, which reports a new all-liquids high in 2024; the two may count different liquids); US involvement in the Nord Stream pipeline sabotage, which Nate qualifies at once with 'I don't know' and whose responsibility remains contested. The auto-generated transcript is not proofed: 'tequila example' is probably 'to use a cliché'; 'U-S-A-L-N-G' is US LNG. Mentioned but not referenced: the World Economic Forum, Harvard University (tgs:AcademicInstitution.HarvardUniversity exists). Candidate concept named but NOT minted: transpartisan, science-tethered awareness."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_92_AIInServiceOfLife a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Artificial Intelligence – In Service of Life?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens with a real, specific prompt: a new acquaintance, an AI investor, called asking him to help her assess whether a cutting-edge AI project she's considering funding is genuinely 'in service of life' — prompting him to unpack, for a real, dedicated Earth Week Frankly, what that phrase actually means, and to define ten direct criteria for what unambiguously is NOT in service of Life (capital L)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-04-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/92-artificial-intelligence-in-service-of-life> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m1Rc_codNVA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Frankly92TranscriptAIInServiceofLife_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.InServiceOfLife, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson, tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:Interview.TGS_103_PeterBrannen, tgs:Human.JamesHansen ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly traces the real, personal origin of his own central phrase: Nora Bateson once told him a story about her father Gregory Bateson telling young students, roughly 50 years ago, to make sure their lives were 'in service of life' — with Rex Weyler reportedly present at that same real, historical moment. He's real and direct that AI being genuinely in service of Life would require one of four categories, three of which he considers unlikely: changing the Superorganism's own growth-optimizing governance structure (unlikely), inventing new extraction-reducing technology (unlikely, since the rebound/backfire effect means any efficiency gain becomes a larger straw under unchanged cultural goals), or improving humanity's relational wisdom toward the natural world (genuinely possible, if a meaningful fraction of people used AI this way) — with a fourth, unknown category left open given AI's own emergent, unpredictable nature. A real, direct, extensive cataloging of Earth's biodiversity anchors his own definitional case for capital-L Life: from roughly 3 trillion trees and 73,000 tree species to 500 primate species and just 8 remaining great ape species (of which humans are one, the last of nine hominid species), offered directly as evidence for how much genuine complexity is actually at stake. He's real and direct that curing malaria without also protecting the rainforest habitats it inadvertently 'defends' from human encroachment would itself fail his own Life-with-capital-L standard, since it would arguably accelerate rainforest destruction — a real, deliberately uncomfortable example he includes despite anticipated pushback. Closes on real, direct personal vulnerability rather than confident resolution: naming his own reaction to laying out these ten criteria as 'a bit of relief' at having stated his real beliefs plainly, mixed with real trepidation, since the same criteria are directly threatening to many well-intentioned people already doing meaningful lowercase-l work (wind farms, recycling, the UN Sustainable Development Goals) that nonetheless falls short of the capital-L threshold he's describing."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct resolution of two standing gaps: this episode's own real, enumerated 'five horsemen' reference finally provides the formal source for tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, first flagged as worth minting in TGS_10's own scopeNote; James Hansen (already built this session) is directly cited here for his own real, recent paper suggesting the coming warming maximum could reach 8-10°C. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tom Crowther (real, directly named upcoming guest on biocomplexity, not built, though his own real TGS episode would be a direct, traceable next build given this explicit forward reference)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_93_InformationBurnout a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Information Burnout: Are We Past Peak Sensemaking?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unusually personal reflection on the platform's own future direction, prompted by two friends' real, opposite reactions to Nate's prior Earth Day Frankly -- one, an ecological economist who understands the situation deeply, couldn't bring herself to watch it; the other found it energizing and took direct local action. Real, direct central hypothesis (see tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking): society's collective desire for more sensemaking about the converging crises may already have peaked and begun declining, mirroring a carbon-pulse-style curve, as information overload and apathy set in. Real, direct implication Nate draws for his own work: shifting focus from explaining the biophysical predicament toward community, agency, and cataloging real, on-the-ground pilots already underway."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/93-information-burnout> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly93TranscriptInformationBurnout.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_92_AIInServiceOfLife, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_90_NetZeroAndOtherDelusions, tgs:Interview.TGS_171_AlexisZeigler,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_14_TheFutureIsLocal, tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening prompt: two friends' real, opposite reactions to Nate's prior Earth Day Frankly on AI in service of life. A dear friend of 20 years, an ecological economist who shares his values and understands the situation, said the content was too heavy and scary to watch despite genuinely wanting to; another close colleague rewatched a prior Alexis Zeigler episode on the 300-watt society and got energized enough to make calls in his own local community. Real, direct 'two arcs' framing: the arc of actual circumstances has been declining since the 1970s (when the Genuine Progress Indicator peaked, before debt and globalization were used to extend growth artificially), while oil production growth fell from roughly 6% to 1% annually -- 2025 is named directly as likely the coolest and most politically stable year for the rest of Nate's own life. Real, direct central hypothesis (see tgs:Concept.PeakSensemaking): modeled explicitly on a carbon-pulse-style bell curve, the collective desire to sense-make the converging crises does not rise indefinitely as things get worse -- it peaks, then declines into information overload and apathy, as people instead want to enjoy their lives, cope locally, or simply stop being told more facts about what's wrong. Real, direct personal admission: Nate himself grieved the cultural story of perpetual growth long ago and no longer focuses on describing the predicament itself, instead focusing on the declining arc he's trying to help bend upward. Real, direct honest statement of his own two evolving interests, offered as a real shift in the platform's own direction: first, moving from biophysical macroeconomics (energy/GDP/technology/money relationships) toward the deep neuroscience and social-primate nature of an 8-billion-strong species facing what he calls a species-level rite of passage; second, toward bioregionalism, regenerative agriculture, and cataloging real, on-the-ground community pilots, directly citing RR-14's own bioregionalism discussion. Real, direct four-category framework for why people watch content like this at all: entertainment (explicitly not what this platform offers), education/sensemaking (its real, primary intended contribution), companionship/community (which Nate believes it can genuinely provide), and agency/direction/inspiration (which Nate directly admits he's not especially good at providing, though he wants to feature more people already doing real, on-the-ground work). Real, direct honest acknowledgment that the platform's own audience composition is shifting, from early peak-oil-focused viewers toward people drawn to guests on grief (Francis Weller), neuroscience (Iain McGilchrist), and death (an upcoming Stephen Jenkinson conversation) -- framed as a deliberate pull toward community and agency and away from pure entertainment or education, even at the cost of losing some of the original audience. Real, direct closing thesis: the era of explaining how humanity arrived at this moment is passing, and the platform's real, future focus will be on what a society or species actually does to bend the downward arc toward a better-than-default future -- explicitly not a call to individual prepping, but to collective, cross-community effort."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note on this build: an earlier turn this session mistakenly stated this episode was already fully built when it was, in fact, still a stub -- corrected directly upon reviewing this episode's own real, uploaded transcript and show notes."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_94_SocialOvershoot a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Social Overshoot? Dunbar's Number, Real Relationships, and Musical Chairs"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct application of tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber to modern hyper-connectivity: just as energy and material throughput can overshoot what's sustainable, Nate argues the number of social relationships people now maintain has overshot what real, meaningful attention can support. Real, direct central claim: full attention -- not money, not even time exactly -- is the rarest and most valuable thing one human can give another, and it cannot be extended to hundreds of people regardless of digital connectivity. Real, direct closing framing, tied to a musical-chairs metaphor: as the carbon pulse and easy credit that subsidized today's oversized social networks eventually slow or stop, the people already closest to someone, not the wide periphery, will become the real, lasting support system."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/94-social-overshoot> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_186_WesCarter ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly94TranscriptSocialOvershoot.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.RobinDunbar, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening pivot: 25 years into this work, Nate names a real, direct shift in his own focus from the supply side of energy (how much is available) to the demand side -- what people actually need to feel happy, healthy, and connected. Real, direct core argument: unsustainability applies to social relationships just as much as to material throughput, both in sheer number (subsidized directly by the carbon pulse, social media, and cheap travel) and in quality, since real, healthy relationships require time, effort, and full attention that can't scale to hundreds of people. Real, direct grounding in evolutionary psychology, credited directly to Robin Dunbar's real social-brain hypothesis (see tgs:Concept.DunbarsNumber): roughly 150 stable relationships (with a real sweet spot Dunbar placed between 50 and 300) as the ancestral ceiling, evolved across roughly 15,000 generations, the vast majority pre-agricultural -- a ceiling that hasn't changed even as connectivity has exploded across just one or two generations of internet and social media. Real, direct personal admission, offered as self-implicating example rather than complaint: Nate has roughly 1,800 phone contacts and sent over 470 messages across Signal, WhatsApp, and SMS in a single week, with about 40 unlistened voice messages sitting unanswered -- naming this directly as a source of real shame at not being the friend he wants to be. Real, direct central claim, credited directly to Iain McGilchrist: the greatest gift one human can give another isn't money or even time exactly, but full attention -- and modern culture's supernormal-stimuli smorgasbord has let the transactional, reductionist left-brain mode become 'the master and not the emissary' of human interaction, McGilchrist's own real, direct book title providing the metaphor. Real, direct anecdote from a friend's own relationship, offered as a genuinely striking illustration: his friend's partner said she doesn't actually enjoy MDMA itself, but values it as the only time she feels fully seen and attended to by him -- landing directly on Nate as a real, uncomfortable mirror for how rare full attention has become even in close relationships. Real, direct closing musical-chairs framing: the carbon pulse and easy credit that have extended social networks far beyond ancestral scale will eventually slow or stop, and the people already in one's inner circle, not the wide periphery cycled through at conferences and online, will be the ones actually present for the duration. Real, direct closing question, left open rather than answered: whether it's better to have 100-300 marginal connections or 10 intensely real ones -- framed as a genuine personal question Nate asks himself, not a rhetorical one."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct correction, flagged by MJSullivan: the transcript itself misattributes 'Love the One You're With' to 'Crosby, Stills, Nash, and Young' -- confirmed directly via search that this is Stephen Stills's own real, solo composition and 1970 solo single, with David Crosby and Graham Nash providing only backing vocals and Neil Young having no involvement at all. This is treated here as a real, direct factual slip on Nate's own part during recording, not a transcription artifact -- the show's own real show notes independently repeat the identical misattribution, confirming it originates with the speaker rather than with auto-transcription. Real, direct forward-reference, already confirmed via Frankly-95's own existing scopeNote: Frankly-95 ('The Parent and the Pendulum') directly cites this episode as its own immediate precursor."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_95_TheParentAndThePendulum a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Parent and the Pendulum"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by reading an original poem he wrote about a close friend's own real psychological pattern — oscillating between an intense need for social approval and an equally fierce resistance to being controlled or pinned down — before recognizing the same pattern in himself and, he argues, in much of modern, supernormal-stimuli-saturated culture. He then directly extends Daniel P. Brown's (already built) clinical Ideal Parent Figure Protocol outward into two speculative new registers: an Ideal Cultural Protocol and an Ideal Ecological Tether Protocol."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/95-the-parent-and-the-pendulum> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Lu9KoPSnQkc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly95TranscriptTheParentandthePendulum.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.IdealParentCulturalEcologicalProtocols, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.DanielPBrown ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly describes writing an original poem about a friend's psychological pattern — a childhood shaped by achievement pressure from a father figure, producing an adult oscillation between a 'hungry ghost'-like craving for validation and a wild, horse-like refusal of any bridle or commitment — before directly, honestly admitting the poem is 'a little bit about me as well,' and then extending it outward as a real, wider-boundary observation about modern culture generally: checking notifications while craving silence, building careers while forests thin. He's real and direct that Daniel P. Brown's clinical Ideal Parent Figure Protocol works precisely because the unconscious mind doesn't fully distinguish vividly imagined experience from lived experience, meaning visualizing the five factors of secure attachment (safety, being seen, comfort, delight, encouraged risk-taking) can genuinely begin repairing real attachment wounds even without having experienced them directly as a child. A real, direct personal detail anchors his own extension of the idea: crediting a recent Mahamudra meditation retreat as the direct occasion for wondering whether an analogous 'ideal cultural' or 'ideal ecological' visualization might address collective, not just individual, deficits. He's real and direct that the wisdom component of his own proposed Ideal Cultural Protocol means something specifically pre-modern: practical, ecological, and moral knowledge passed down orally through custom, explicitly not the algorithmically-optimized content of the current attention economy. Closes on real, direct, deliberately open-ended reflection rather than resolution: Nate poses two direct questions to listeners — what is your own 'branch of stillness' where the pendulum doesn't reach, and what protocol, if any, actually grounds you — explicitly framing the whole episode as speculative, 'I don't know, I've never tried it,' rather than a settled clinical claim."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct title note, considered directly per MJSullivan's own question: the transcript gives pendulum its own complete, explicit meaning (oscillation between validation-seeking and control-rejection), with nothing in the text gesturing toward Poe's own 'The Pit and the Pendulum' as dread or inescapable doom — textual evidence points against a deliberate allusion, though the word's cultural association with Poe may sit in the background regardless. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_94 ('Social Overshoot'), directly cited as the immediate precursor episode, not yet built; Philip Glass and Brian Eno (both real, directly named as morning-listening musicians, not built — passing personal references, not TGS-relevant citations in the sense this graph otherwise captures)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_96_TheEightFacesOfAI a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 8 Faces of AI: Who Will You Become As AI Accelerates?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming what he hasn't yet covered on AI: not the biophysical 'larger straw' effect already discussed elsewhere, but AI's real, direct impact on human psyche, identity, and psychology — proposing eight archetypal categories for how different people may relate to and be reshaped by accelerating AI, framed directly as a sorting mechanism revealing who people are becoming, not just what the technology can do."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/96-the-8-faces-of-ai> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wmb0eXvfuNY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly96Transcript8FacesofAI.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EightFacesOfAI, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.DanielSchmachtenberger ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with a real, personal disclaimer: he's not an AI expert, and if he's an expert in anything AI-related, it's specifically in why artificial superintelligence and artificial general intelligence are unlikely to actually arrive as hyped — offered directly to frame the typology as behavioral speculation, not technical forecasting. He's real and direct that the Flexors category, AI used performatively for social status, may already be visibly growing, citing real, direct observation of 'tech bros' on LinkedIn showcasing AI-optimized routines the same way influencers showcase workout regimens. A real, direct, honest personal note closes his own treatment of the Achievers, the category he names as the real pinnacle: these are people disciplined enough to use AI for real productivity while still maintaining outdoor time, fitness, meditation, and family life — implicitly, though not explicitly, the category he's personally aiming for himself. He's real and direct that the Dissolved category concerns him most: speculating directly that some humans may eventually spend 10-15 hours daily in chatbot conversations built around a deceased loved one or an idealized, imaginary partner, framing this as a real, severe, technology-driven form of social and emotional withdrawal. Closes on real, direct synthesis connecting this typology to the show's own broader thesis: as energy flows decline and ecological limits tighten, Nate directly predicts the Achievers and the Luddites will fare best, the Dissolved and Cyborgs worst, and the once-overlooked Meek could become unexpected teachers of embodied, unmediated human competence."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference confirmed: this episode is directly cited in TGS_184's own show notes (already built) as a companion citation on AI, closing that specific real, flagged gap. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (Luddites, backfire effect, boomerang effect) rather than pointing to further individuals — Daniel Schmachtenberger is already built and directly cross-referenced via his own real, cited TGS appearance on AI, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why the World Feels Like It's Falling Apart: The Superorganism Explained in 7 Minutes"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a real official transcript and show notes. Real, distinctive origin: adapted from an 'Ignite' talk Nate gave the prior week in California — a real, formally rigid TED-adjacent format (exactly 20 slides, auto-timed at 15 seconds each, 5 minutes total). The animations failed during the live talk, prompting this Frankly redo with the format's constraints loosened slightly (25-26 slides, some longer than 15 seconds). Functions as the single most condensed, TED-style real explanation of the economic Superorganism concept anywhere in this graph — energy (not money) as the real underlying driver, the carbon pulse as a one-time geological windfall, energy blindness, emergence in both natural systems (a real starling-murmuration example) and human ones, and the Superorganism itself as an emergent, unplanned, unmastered phenomenon of billions of aligned incentives. Closes on real, direct practical framing: four broad response categories (policy/biophysical realism, bend-not-break planning, cultural narrative change, personal mindset/connection), and Nate's own recurring 'be the mitochondria' metaphor for individual-scale contribution to whatever comes next."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-05-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-05-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/97-the-superorganism-in-7-minutes> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork, tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice, tgs:AnimatedVideo.TGS_182_TheSystemsScience, tgs:Interview.TGS_238_IndyJohar, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h5VWZm7ESfk> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/Frankly97TranscriptTheSuperorganismExplainedin7Minutes.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-why-the-world-feels-like-its> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with real, direct honesty about the show's own real audience funnel — of 8 billion humans, a subset with internet, a smaller subset curious about the world, a smaller subset still pro-social and future-oriented, and then a further filter of attention span, which Nate includes himself in ('I just don't have the attention span' for a 90-minute podcast). The real substantive content is a tightly compressed, image-driven synthesis: a single starling following three simple rules (stay close, not too close, move toward center) as the natural-world analog for how billions of humans independently 'seeking profit, minimizing cost' produce the emergent, unplanned economic Superorganism, with no single actor designing or fully controlling the result. The financial system gets a real, pointed metaphor of its own: central banks 'printing claims' on oil, copper, and lithium rather than the resources themselves, framed directly as 'buy now, pay later at a planetary scale.' The closing register shifts from diagnosis to real, direct address — 'Power scales up... Life scales... deep interconnection, regeneration, community. The future depends on which of these we feed' — before an unusually candid coda after the formal talk ends: Nate directly acknowledges the format's own real cost ('every sentence... could have been unpacked for a half hour'), and connects this compressed version to a real, larger project — an expanded 8-10 hour 'Reality 101' course being rebuilt for that same summer, itself a real descendant of the 100-hour version he used to teach."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, independent cross-confirmation, not a citation relationship: this episode's own closing line ('Consider being B plus in service of life') matches Frankly_80's own real, already-documented closing personal ethos exactly — a genuine recurring personal catchphrase across separate episodes, not one episode referencing the other. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the Ignite Talks event/platform itself (ignitetalks.io, a real, distinct organization); Frankly #98 ('10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria') — this episode's own closing 'be the mitochondria' metaphor is very likely the direct real seed for that later episode's own extended treatment of the same image, given the two are numerically adjacent and thematically continuous, but #98 is not yet built in this graph to confirm the connection directly."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Qualities That Could Change the Future: The Seeds of New Cultural Mitochondria"@en ;
    skos:definition "Directly building on the prior week's real Superorganism explainer, Nate extends Marvin Harris's own real cultural-materialism pyramid (infrastructure, structure, superstructure, already built) with a real, speculative fourth layer: the inner world of individuals alive during the Superorganism's own splintering. He proposes ten personal traits, framed as 'cultural mitochondria' — small, distributed, largely invisible units of capacity that could power whatever cultural system eventually replaces it, explicitly not claiming expertise, just a considered first attempt."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/98-10-qualities-that-could-change-the-future> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zrGsRAv2MrY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Frankly-98-Transcript-10-Qualities-That-Could-Change-the-Future.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson, tgs:Human.EOWilson, tgs:Human.IlyaPrigogine, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_99_TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly frames self-care (his own real 'number zero') as a genuine neurobiological strategy rather than indulgence, arguing regeneration and creativity only arise when the parasympathetic nervous system is actually active — a dysregulated body, in his own words, 'cannot sustain a new story.' He's real and direct about his own personal evolution on trait eight, feeling rather than merely knowing interconnection with the web of life: describing a real, direct shift over the past year from observing nature from outside it to genuinely feeling merged with an ecosystem during his own regular time outdoors. A real, direct connection to multilevel selection theory grounds trait four, co-regulation: crediting David Sloan Wilson and E.O. Wilson directly (both already built), he argues altruistic groups outcompete selfish groups even though selfish individuals often outcompete altruists within any single group — making trust-based co-regulation a real, structural advantage, not just a nicety. Closes on a real, direct forward announcement: next week's Frankly will cover 'the 10 major flaws still being taught in business schools,' a direct, confirmed origin point for Frankly_99, already built in this graph. Closes the episode itself on Ilya Prigogine's own real 'islands of coherence' line — a complex system far from equilibrium can be shifted to a higher order by small coherent islands within it — directly adapted by Nate to argue culture doesn't change by decree, it changes by mitochondria: individuals and small groups first."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction: an earlier build of this episode, from show notes only, listed just three of the ten traits with real uncertainty about the rest — the full transcript, received after two prior file-upload attempts didn't render any content, confirms all ten precisely (see tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria for the complete list). Real, direct transcription note: the transcript renders Ilya Prigogine's name as 'IA Priya Jean' — corrected here with confidence, matching the same real name already established elsewhere in this graph. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Marc Gafni and Zak Stein (Nate himself is real and direct that he isn't certain which of the two actually coined 'post-tragic,' so neither is credited with confidence; both already independently flagged elsewhere in this graph's own tracking)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_99_TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The 10 Core Myths Still Taught in Business Schools"@en ;
    skos:definition "Thirty real years after his own MBA at the University of Chicago, Nate directly counts down ten foundational economic assumptions he argues are still taught as settled fact worldwide despite conflicting with real biophysical reality — explicitly framed as values-neutral description, not personal preference, since what he cares about most is truth and the natural world, not a particular policy outcome."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/the-10-core-myths-still-taught-in-business-schools> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_101_WhatIWantToWantForTheFuture, tgs:Interview.TGS_185_MythsShapingOurEconomies ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GkIedVEkQZU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Frankly-99-Transcript-10-Core-Myths-of-Business-Schools.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture, tgs:Human.AlanBlinder ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly opens with myth ten, price equals value, using a real, deliberately stark comparison: a poor villager able to pay only 20 cents for clean drinking water against a billionaire spending $20 million on a third yacht — by market logic the yacht carries vastly more value to society, a conclusion he directly calls dangerous, not just counterintuitive, since it structurally pushes economies toward overproducing luxuries while underproviding basics. He's real and direct about myth eight, the upward-sloping supply curve, naming Alan Blinder specifically (already built): a real, former Federal Reserve Vice Chairman and textbook author whose own published research found only about 11% of firms still show that pattern, yet whose own textbook keeps teaching it regardless — Nate suspects the real figure is now closer to zero. A real, extensive, direct quantitative case grounds myth seven, energy as a substitutable commodity: one barrel of crude oil contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours of real work potential, equivalent to about 11 years of full-time human labor, discounted to roughly 4-5 years once machine energy-conversion inefficiency is accounted for — at current global consumption, Nate directly calculates this as equivalent to adding a real 500-billion-person labor force on top of an actual human workforce of about 5 billion. He's real and direct that debt (myth five) is not a neutral financial tool but a claim on future energy and materials that may not actually materialize, citing real, current data that US debt has grown faster than GDP every year since the 1970s, with debt-productivity — additional GDP per additional debt dollar — declining for decades; he directly predicts a real 'too big to save' moment sometime in the next decade, distinct from the 2008 'too big to fail' crisis, marking what he calls the genuine start of the Great Simplification in earnest. Closes on myth one, that economic laws are timeless like physics: Nate argues modern economic theory was built almost entirely within a roughly 150-year window of fossil-fuel abundance, colonization, and expanding markets — a real, historically singular period, not a representative sample from which to derive universal law — closing with real, direct, unflinching self-doubt about whether truth can actually change entrenched economic teaching given how much institutional power and momentum business schools now hold, before previewing a real, planned 'ask me anything' format for the following week's Frankly #100, not yet built in this graph."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction: an earlier build of this episode, from show notes only, named just four of the ten myths in no particular order — the full transcript, received after two prior file-upload attempts didn't render any content, confirms the complete, real, precisely-ordered countdown (see tgs:Concept.TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools for all ten). Real, direct origin confirmed: Frankly_98 (already built), the prior week's episode, explicitly announces this episode's own real title in advance. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Adam Smith (real, directly cited, 'the invisible hand' — Nate notes Smith himself used the term only once, in a different context than its now-popular application, not built given his status as a broadly-known historical figure rather than a direct TGS influence)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_100_AskNateAnything2025 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ask Nate Anything 2025"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct milestone episode -- the 100th Frankly, close to the show's 200th overall episode -- built entirely from real, direct listener questions submitted via Substack, email, and LinkedIn. Real, direct content spans the Iran/Israel/US energy-geopolitical situation, a real, direct defense of averages-based Superorganism analysis against a listener's critique (grounded in Robert Sapolsky's own real baboon-troop story), AI's paradoxical role in cementing rather than delaying peak oil, and two real, structured frameworks articulated here for the first time: The Four Stories (see tgs:Concept.FourStoriesFramework) and the Three Biggest Risks/Three Worst Responses (see tgs:Concept.ThreeRisksThreeResponses). Closes with real, direct personal updates -- grief, two completed bike races, newly hatched baby ducks."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-06-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-06-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment,
        tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/100-ask-nate-anything-2025> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.TGS_205_NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Frankly-100-Transcript-Ask-Nate-Anything.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FourStoriesFramework, tgs:Concept.ThreeRisksThreeResponses, tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_88_RobertSapolsky, tgs:Interview.TGS_171_AlexisZeigler, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: an unusually candid admission that this milestone (100th Frankly) lands during a genuinely rough week, making it, in Nate's own words, exactly the right time for open reflection. Real, direct Iran/Israel/US hot take, dated precisely to June 23, 2025: skepticism toward official narratives around nuclear-site destruction, real concern about roughly 30% of the world's exportable oil transiting the Strait of Hormuz, and a real, direct warning that unilateral US military action without UN or congressional approval carries serious longer-term risk of accelerating BRICS nations choosing sides against US/UK/Israel-aligned trade and finance. Real, direct defense of averages-based Superorganism analysis against a listener's real, direct critique that it obscures unequal resource capture: Nate argues the Superorganism is the system itself, not the rich specifically -- if today's wealthy were removed, others would fill the same structural role as long as the same energy surplus and infrastructure remained -- grounding this directly in Robert Sapolsky's own real Keekorok baboon-troop story, where the accidental death of all dominant males produced a culturally-transmitted, multi-generational shift toward cooperation and less violence; Nate states directly he doesn't believe an equivalent shift is available to humans under current structural conditions. Real, direct AI/peak-oil paradox: AI won't meaningfully increase oil supply (drilling efficiency gains are real but modest, running into hard depletion limits), but Nate argues AI will 'paradoxically cement peak oil' by reducing the affordability of energy-intensive fossil-fueled living for a large share of the population as AI displaces jobs and income -- directly predicting energy and electricity access will be prioritized for AI over poor communities' basic needs. Real, direct honest response to being challenged for not pushing back harder on naive guests: a direct defense of platforming disagreement rather than interrupting to tell the whole systemic story every time, paired with a real, direct four-category guest framework -- natural/earth sciences, biophysical macro (energy/money/geopolitics), brain and behavior, and what-to-do/response at individual-through-societal scale -- with a real, direct prediction that the fourth category's share of future content will grow. Real, direct articulation of The Four Stories (see tgs:Concept.FourStoriesFramework), offered as a direct answer to why the show can feel inconsistent in tone: the prescription-less energy/systems story, the genuinely scary Bend Not Break near-term-risk story, the community/resilience 'cultural mitochondria' story, and a life-ethic thread advocating for the more-than-human world running through all three, which Nate admits he can't fully explain the importance of, only affirm it. Real, direct Three Biggest Risks and Three Worst Responses (see tgs:Concept.ThreeRisksThreeResponses): complexity, AI, and governance ('the granddaddy of all risks') as the top systemic threats; blame, apathy, and anhedonia as the worst individual and collective responses -- with time in nature named directly as Nate's own personal antidote to the last of these. Real, direct honest ethics answer on fear-mongering: doomsday has already happened for Ukraine, Syria, Madagascar, coral reefs, and countless species, but spreading fear onto people with no path to agency is a real, direct form of spite that makes them worse off -- Nate directly recalls having stopped speaking publicly for six or seven years over exactly this concern, and commits directly to shutting the platform down if it ever becomes primarily about spreading fear. Real, direct honest renewable-energy position, addressing a real, recurring audience confusion: unambiguously pro-solar, pro-battery, pro-renewables, but direct in naming 'plug and play, keep everything else the same' as one of the real reasons the climate movement has failed -- genuine support for a society running on 30-50% of today's total energy consumption, powered substantially by renewables, requiring radically different consumption patterns rather than a straight fuel swap; citing the real, direct 2000-Watt Society and Living Energy Farm as real, existing proof-of-concept pilots. Real, direct closing personal updates: recent grief and loss, two completed bike races (53 and 30 miles) with a 63-mile race two weeks out, and newly hatched baby ducks -- the platform's third generation -- offered directly as evidence that heaviness and genuine joy coexist rather than cancel out."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real show notes cite a real MIT study on AI's cognitive-decline effects and a real IEA Energy and AI report by name; neither modeled as a Work given their brief, topical citation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_101_WhatIWantToWantForTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What I Want to Want for the Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "A viewer asked what, after everything he has learned on the podcast, Nate actually wants. He found a bigger question inside it -- 'what do I want to want?' -- meaning not what he wanted when younger, what culture or advertising tells us to want, or what 'the ghost of dopamine past or status games lure me towards,' but what, looking back from a future date perhaps beyond his own lifetime, he would want to have had and experienced in a human life. He offers thirteen such wants (tgs:Concept.WhatIWantToWant), calling them not goals but his own flavor of the foundations of humanity, 'whether 50 years from now or 500 from now,' and invites viewers to pause and list their own first."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-07-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/101-what-i-want-to-want-for-the-future> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Frankly-101-Transcript-What-I-Want-to-Want-for-the-Future.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatIWantToWant, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost,
        tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_99_TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools, tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The list, in the order he jotted it down: (1) basic needs -- food, water, shelter, heat and cooling as needed but not as much as modern culture says (he has a sleeping bag good to zero degrees); 'without enough, nothing else really matters. But with enough, a lot becomes possible.' (2) Stability -- perhaps a product of living through the upside of the carbon pulse, now that the biosphere's ecological foundations and social and geopolitical stability are at risk; a stable expectation of the future matters for humans to thrive. (3) Agency, freedom or volition -- the quiet confidence that he can respond rather than react. (4) Restraint -- rules that bind us not to oppress but to protect what we have collectively decided matters; 'guardrails for the commons,' ethics and laws that keep the tragedy of the commons at bay; restraint and wisdom are linked. (5) Full-spectrum love -- romantic, sexual, sensual, psychological, spiritual, intellectual. (6) Friendship and networks, 'probably the greatest thing of being human,' in three kinds: friends for fun (fishing, bowling, games), intellectual friends, and, increasingly, friends working toward a shared purpose. (7) Community -- belonging beyond close friends, shared rituals, mutual aid, neighbors who have each other's backs. (8) A craft -- something to practice and get better at, 'sharpening the samurai sword.' (9) Entertainment, novelty and play -- music, games, stories, 'a game of cribbage or backgammon.' (10) Curiosity, wonder and awe -- for example, how an old 'wolf tree' grew in its meadow. (11) Daily access to the natural world, as a reminder 'I'm not separate from nature, I am part of it.' (12) A non-human companion -- his dog, or a goat, bird or duck; he can 'truly be myself' with his dog. (13) Purpose -- to be useful and 'make a dent in the fabric of something bigger than myself,' not for accolades or status. His reflections: some wants depend on society, ecosystems, technology, energy and cohesion, but most are internal and durable 'in good times or bad, with money or with no money.' What is missing: no yachts, skyscrapers, brand names or hyperloops -- once basic needs are met, 'most of the best things in life are free or close to it.' And we are tied to what we have already invested in and the stories we tell about success, so he asks 'how much of what we want is a product of the sunk cost in society and in our own lives.' Conclusion: any real conversation about the future should begin not with technology or policy but with what we desire, or we will keep building futures that serve desires 'tethered to an extremely unsustainable period of human history.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub in place. Earliest appearance found so far of the phrase 'the ghost of dopamine past,' used here in passing three weeks before Frankly_103 gave it a full episode. The sunk-cost reflection anticipates tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost, developed later in Frankly_116; the list also overlaps with tgs:Concept.TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold (Frankly_106, also later). Nate refers to Frankly_99 as 'two weeks ago' (it was published 20 June 2025, three weeks before this one). Forward pointer, not asserted as a reference: he announces 'next week' a Frankly on 'the key blind spots underpinning progressives' as a bookend to Frankly_99; it was published on 22 August 2025 as tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement. Show-notes sources not built: Britannica on the biosphere, IASC on the commons, Wikipedia on mutual aid and Hyperloop, and American Forests on wolf trees."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_102_ThisWeeksLearnings a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "This Week's Learnings: Corn Sweat, Coral Bleaching, and the Climate Credit Crunch"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first 'This Week's Learnings' Frankly, a format Nate says he may repeat about once a month: five things he learned in a few days, offered because he does not have 'an encyclopedic mind' and much of what reaches him by email, social media or his team is quickly forgotten. Recorded 16 July 2025 while his planned Frankly on the blindspots of the progressive movement was still unfinished. The five: corn sweat and wet-bulb temperature; an Allianz board member's warning of a climate-driven credit crunch; the 50% US tariff on copper; which climate tipping points worry experts most, with the trajectory of global coral bleaching; and why mental-health diagnoses skew toward liberals, especially liberal women, which he frames as an 'empathy handicap.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-07-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/102-this-weeks-learnings> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Frankly-102-Transcript-What-I-Learned-This-Week.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature, tgs:Concept.ClimateCreditCrunch, tgs:Concept.EmpathyHandicap,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_42_ModelingDriversMetacrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Human.GuntherThallinger ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "(1) Corn sweat: on the Minnesota-Wisconsin border the GMO corn was over his head by 16 July ('knee-high by the 4th of July' no longer applies), and the heat forces him to exercise at 5 a.m. An acre of corn can transpire 3,000 to 4,000 gallons of water a day, fast-growing GMO varieties perhaps 6,000, roughly three to four tenths of an inch of water locally, raising the dew point by 3 to 5 degrees Fahrenheit; he links this to wet-bulb temperature, at which, around 35 C (95 F), humans can no longer cool themselves even in shade with unlimited water (tgs:Concept.WetBulbTemperature). (2) Climate credit crunch: he reads at length from Allianz board member Günther Thallinger's LinkedIn post -- uninsurable houses cannot be mortgaged, credit markets freeze, the economic value of coastal and wildfire-prone regions vanishes from ledgers, 'there is no capitalism without functioning financial services' (tgs:Concept.ClimateCreditCrunch). Nate welcomes the warning coming from a major insurer rather than a fringe blogger, doubts the market can solve it because money is created without the interest needed to repay it and the system requires growth ('AKA, the economic Superorganism'), and expects credit risks to show up before 1.5, 1.75 or 2 degrees are reached; cites the Texas floods and the 2024 events in Florida, California and North Carolina; the ecology and finance conversations are a Venn diagram 'beginning to have a larger and larger overlap.' (3) Copper tariffs: the announced 50% tariff from 1 August could raise domestic copper costs 30 to 40% and hit wiring, plumbing, HVAC, transformers, grids, EVs (three to four times the copper of conventional cars), solar and wind. Whatever the intent, taxing non-renewable inputs is one long-term policy response to depletion and climate; he calls it 'a classic example of bend versus break' (tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak), like firing 'a little mini avalanche gun' in the Alps to prevent a bigger avalanche, and notes that onshoring essentials such as copper and fertilizer means higher costs, less use and simplifying sooner. (4) Tipping points: at a UK convening of climate experts, most named Amazon dieback and coral reefs as their top concerns; he quotes Johan Rockström that at 1.5 C we are very likely to lose potentially the entire tropical coral reef system, threatening the livelihoods of 400 million people. A coral-reef expert friend then emailed him the bleaching record: 21% of reefs affected in 1998, 37% in 2010, 68% in the event starting 2017, and 84% as of March 2025 in the fourth event, which began in 2023 within months of the world first passing 1.5 C and is expected to exceed 90%; the friend expects the next event to bleach close to 100% and calls 1.5 C 'a genuine planetary boundary.' (5) The empathy handicap: a Twitter chart claiming young US liberals have far more mental-health diagnoses drew mocking comments; he could not trace its source but found a paper showing the same pattern, highest among liberal women. His alternative explanation (tgs:Concept.EmpathyHandicap): progressives have a wider boundary of care and empathy, and women have shallower discount rates than men, so people attending to the sixth mass extinction, the oceans and poleward animal migration carry more worry than people who think climate change is a hoax. He plans to find a guest on it and mentions an upcoming episode on the Dark Triad and psychopathy. Closes by asking whether viewers want more of these, and whether to try live YouTube sessions since he records in one take anyway."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub in place; prefLabel updated to the full title. Corrections to the auto-transcript, from the show notes: 'Gunther Inger' at 'World Alliance' is Günther Thallinger of Allianz; 'Johann Strom' is Johan Rockström. Correction to the stub: its note said the shared segment title was 'also #102'; the other installment is tgs:Monologue.Frankly_107_ThisWeeksLearnings. Forward pointers, not asserted as references since they are later episodes: the promised progressive-blindspots Frankly became tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement (published 22 August 2025, nine blindspots, retitled 'walrus'); the upcoming Dark Triad episode is tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad; the coral-reef expert he says he has invited is most likely tgs:Interview.TGS_197_OveHoeghGuldberg (not confirmed from this transcript). The coral expert friend who sent the bleaching figures is not named. Show-notes sources not built as Works: Ohio State Extension on corn sweat, NOAA and AIMS coral bleaching pages, Carbon Brief on the tipping-points conference, a USDA Forest Service paper on the economic value of fire-prone regions, Reuters on the copper tariff, and the ResearchGate figure on mental health by ideology and sex. Rockström's Persona is declared twice in personas.ttl (pre-existing), so this episode references his TGS episode rather than the Persona."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_103_TheGhostOfDopaminePast a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ghost of Dopamine Past"@en ;
    skos:definition "A Frankly prompted by a moment on a morning bike ride: Nate was reading a LinkedIn invitation to appear in a film about humans and nature when he looked up to find a doe and her fawn in the road about 50 yards ahead -- 'I was missing the nature that was right in front of me.' He calls it 'a profound microcosm of the entire human predicament' and uses it to introduce tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast: past behavior carves neural pathways that demand ever-higher baseline stimulation, so quiet, ancestrally normal experiences stop registering. He admits he is 'way more of a dopamine addict now' than five years earlier when he was teaching college, because of the volume of email and requests the platform brings, but insists 'this isn't about me. This is about our entire culture.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-07-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/103-the-ghost-of-dopamine-past> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Frankly-103-Transcript-Ghost-of-Dopamine-Past.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.GhostOfDopaminePast, tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli,
        tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_26_PeterWhybrow, tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang,
        tgs:Work.AmericanMania, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Human.WolframSchultz ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The neuroscience: Nate traces his interest back twenty years, to his PhD, when he moved from climate, ecology and energy toward evolutionary biology and neuroscience, befriended Peter Whybrow (a past guest, author of American Mania) and through him found Wolfram Schultz's experiments on dopamine neurons in monkeys. An unexpected squirt of fruit juice made the neurons surge; once a sound cue predicted the juice, they fired at the cue instead of the juice; and when the cue came with no juice, firing dipped below baseline -- the neural signal of disappointment. Dopamine therefore signals the difference between expected and actual reward (tgs:Concept.RewardPredictionError), which explains why familiar pleasures fade and why we adapt to good circumstances. If unexpected reward drives us, he argues, that is a big problem for a species living in 'a flow-based world, in our minds' but 'a stock-based world' biophysically. His long-standing lecture example: Parkinson's drugs that raise dopamine across the whole brain can turn patients into gambling addicts, compulsive shoppers or serial adulterers -- more dopamine, more pursuit of consumptive reward. Daily life once delivered its spikes from books, visiting cousins, walks, or a northern pike striking on a family fishing trip in Canada; now email notifications, podcast view counts and video games provide 24/7 high stimulation, so we need ever more to feel the same. The 'ghost of dopamine past' also shrinks our 'ghost of dopamine future': we can only imagine futures that keep us on the treadmill. Four suggestions: (1) dopamine fasting -- he works in 40-minute blocks with 10-minute breaks, disconnecting the internet while writing scripts for his upcoming course, and suggests technology-free Saturdays; (2) a 'speed bump': take a breath before checking the phone, separating trigger from response; (3) lower the stimulation baseline, for example Audrey Tang's suggestion (TGS_169) to set the phone to grayscale; (4) socialize the dopamine -- time with other people gives 'a better neurotransmitter cocktail' than scrolling alone; he again praises Dungeons & Dragons and Magic: The Gathering as among humanity's best inventions, imaginative, social and low-resource. He asks what the cultural corollaries of these four would be, and tells regular viewers that their ability to sit through a 90-minute podcast suggests a less hungry ghost than average. Closes with a photo of the deer, already gone into the woods."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub in place. Nate calls Peter Whybrow a neuroscientist; he is a psychiatrist (UCLA, former director of the Semel Institute). The show notes' link to the Schultz research is a Google Drive file, so no Work was minted for the paper; the landmark paper is Schultz, Dayan and Montague, 'A neural substrate of prediction and reward,' Science (1997). Show-notes sources not built: a Neuron review on personality, addiction and dopamine in Parkinson's disease, a Mayo Clinic page on Parkinson's, a 2023 journal article on grayscale and digital wellbeing, and the Reality Blind chapter on dopamine (the Work itself is referenced). Related later episode not cited here: TGS with Anna Lembke on dopamine and addiction in the digital age. TGS_26 (Peter Whybrow) is still a stub in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_104_DucksAndBlueberries a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ducks and Blueberries: A Reflection on Price, Cost and Value"@en ;
    skos:definition "A short, personal Frankly (about seven minutes) built on a grocery-store story. Blueberries are marked $1 a pint, limit one, so Nate puts them in his cart as a snack for his ducks; at checkout he learns the price only applies with the store's app and pays $5 instead. At $1 they were a frivolous treat for the ducks; at $5 they felt too good for the ducks and he planned to eat them himself. Nothing about the blueberries changed, only the story the price told him about what they were for. He uses the moment to distinguish price, cost and value (tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue), and to ask how much consumption is driven by cheapness rather than need."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/104-ducks-and-blueberries> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Frankly-104-Transcript-Ducks-and-Blueberries.pdf> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PriceCostAndValue, tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The story, then the wider point: 'on The Great Simplification, I'm not really talking about fruit here.' When fossil fuels are incredibly cheap for what they do, we burn them 'like feeding blueberries to ducks, for snacks'; when they are expensive we discover efficiencies and behavior changes. Abundant water gets sprayed on lawns; scarce water is treated as precious. The price tag changes behavior, but the real ecological and replacement cost never shows up on the sticker. His example: a gallon of gasoline costs $3, its environmental damage costs far more, and in a wide-boundary sense its benefit to modern comforts is 'probably a thousand times higher.' Open questions for viewers: Have you had a 'blueberry moment'? What do you treat as disposable when cheap but guard like treasure when expensive? What is the blueberries-for-ducks equivalent in housing, travel, energy use, shopping and technology? What would the world look like if prices reflected true value and true cost? He suggests trying the exercise on the coming weekend's purchases. Two footnotes: he never ate the blueberries -- they fell out of the fridge, he swept them up and gave them to the ducks anyway -- and he had to buy another pint to film the closing shot of feeding the ducks. He closes by saying the work is serious enough that laughing at silly things can feel inappropriate, but that small stories like this, and his ducks, keep him going, and he hopes viewers have something like that too."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub in place; prefLabel updated to the episode's full title. The show notes link two chapters of tgs:Work.RealityBlind -- 'Our Fossil Armies' (the basis for tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves) and the wide-boundary lens -- plus a 2016 infographic on the full cost of a gallon of gasoline, a Yale e360 report on global material consumption (about 100 billion tons a year), and a World Bank report on climate change, water and the economy; none of those three is built as a Work. Nate also mentions buying his ducks watermelon, and that he stopped giving them live minnows because it was 'too cruel.' No companion essay is listed in the show notes."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_105_KeyBlindspotsOfTheWalrusMovement a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key Blindspots of the Walrus Movement"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's critique of nine blindspots he attributes to the 'walrus movement' (see tgs:Concept.WalrusMovementBlindspots), a deliberately playful placeholder for people championing bold social and ecological ideals. Prompted by viewers who, after Frankly_99's business-school myths, asked him to critique the opposite side; he chose a placeholder because labels like progressivism or postmodernism have become culture-war identities, and the platform tries to move beyond telling people what their identity is. Central claim: 'a worldview can be morally right, but biophysically untenable.' He credits the movement with expanding rights, challenging injustice and confronting environmental damage, and calls the blindspots 'fog on the windshield': wiping them away does not change the destination, only makes the road clearer."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-08-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/105-key-blindspots-of-the-walrus-movement> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Frankly-105-Transcript-Blindspots-of-the-Walrus-Movement.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-key-blindspots-of-the-walrus> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WalrusMovementBlindspots, tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves,
        tgs:Concept.MoneyAsClaimOnEnergy, tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions, tgs:Concept.Wetware,
        tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_99_TenCoreMythsOfBusinessSchools,
        tgs:Work.AShortHistoryOfProgress, tgs:Human.EOWilson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The nine blindspots, in Nate's order. (1) Profit and extraction are bad: the drive for surplus is biological, not an invention of capitalism -- optimal foraging, 'every organism from bacteria to bears' seeking the most energy for the least effort, a lion's surplus buying rest, reproduction and resilience. Plant roots, deer, parrotfish and leafcutter ants all extract. The problem is aggregate extraction without limits or reciprocity, and a profit equation whose revenue side is subsidized by non-renewable energy while its expense side omits ecosystem damage; he calls for redefining profit beyond financial metrics. (2) The main problem is capitalism: capitalism (tools, land and infrastructure privately owned and directed toward profit) is 'the latest cultural software running on that biological hardware'; the same template of surplus, scale and hierarchy arose independently in the Americas long before it, per the passage he reads from Ronald Wright's A Short History of Progress on what Cortés found in Mexico. Ties this to the maximum power principle ('fourth law of thermodynamics') and the Superorganism: 'capitalism is in service of the Superorganism, not the other way around.' Swap the software without upgrading the hardware and you get the same inequality, overshoot and crash. His central question: how to constrain the drive for surplus in any system -- markets, states or ideologies -- with hard limits, feedbacks and distributed authority. (3) Authoritarianism is a right-wing phenomenon: authoritarianism 'is like mold' that grows wherever fear is high, trust low and threats feel urgent enough to justify breaking one's own rules; cites research on left-wing authoritarianism; warns against 'righteous authoritarianism and purity politics, even when it comes from our side,' and calls authoritarian policies 'probably the default' on the way into the Great Simplification, with a 'meta political,' systems-informed commitment to resisting control from any direction as the only hope. (4) A fair society means equal outcomes: extreme inequality fuels poor health, crime and distrust, but status-seeking is deeply wired; quotes E. O. Wilson on communism ('great idea, wrong species'); argues a serious equality lens would span nations, generations and species, and that the natural world can be destroyed even if wealth is distributed equally; prefers equality of opportunity within strong boundaries. (5) It's all Big Oil's fault: fossil companies did fund disinformation, but they met our demand; the IMF's implicit-subsidy figure is mostly unpriced externalities, not checks written to Exxon; publicly traded majors hold only about 10% of world oil and gas reserves and production, the rest being national oil companies; the real villain is 'the fossil powered Superorganism of modern life.' (6) Renewables can replace fossil fuels: renewables are intermittent and material-intensive, not dematerialization; climate is one symptom of overshoot; solar, wind and batteries 'could power a civilization, just not this one'; and a new system cannot simply replace the current one while it is still running full bore. (7) We can print our way to prosperity (Modern Monetary Theory): money is a claim on real things ('you can't print copper, diesel, fertile soil or orangutans'); new money reaches those nearest the spigot first (tgs:Concept.CantillonEffect), so inflation acts as a regressive tax; dollar printing exports inflation to dollar-indebted nations; MMT 'gets the double accounting entries right' but misses the third and fourth entries -- energy and materials, and ecosystem impact. (8) Reality is whatever we say it is: lived experience matters, but lake ice melts at 0 C and CO2 traps heat regardless of belief; nurture rarely erases evolved status-seeking, tribal loyalty and short-term bias; the movement overestimates 'the malleability of reality.' (9) Human rights are unbounded: rights are agreements contingent on surplus, stability and functioning ecosystems; 'if rights were truly inalienable, extinction would be impossible'; a US baby will consume about 3 million pounds of minerals, metals and fuels in a lifetime; recounts a climate-conference speaker shouted off stage for linking population to climate; Native Hawaiians, using almost no metals, grew 'the spirit of aloha' from their limits; justice shifts from 'I deserve my share' to 'we must live within our shared global budget,' with floors and ceilings on consumption that respect planetary boundaries. Closing: offered toward a 'meta modern perspective'; he will not do a matching list for conservatives, since that critique would be about values and narrow boundaries rather than facts and systems, which is beyond the platform's scope; promises a future '10 core myths of modern civilization.' His stated role is not to prescribe values or ideology but to 'illuminate the operating conditions of the 21st century.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes, replacing the 2026-09-12 stub in place. Companion Substack essay ('Essay version of this Frankly,' dated 7 April 2026) checked where figures differ; per standing rule the essay wins: (a) IMF implicit fossil-fuel subsidies -- spoken '5 trillion,' essay '$7 trillion' (the show notes' IMF link is also the 2023 record-$7-trillion post); (b) vehicles added per year -- spoken 'around a hundred million,' essay 'at least 50 million'; (c) the essay frames 81 million as a net annual population addition. The essay's blindspot headings are the canonical wording used in tgs:Concept.WalrusMovementBlindspots. Attribution correction: the transcript says 'anthropologists Robert Wright,' but the book and the show notes name Ronald Wright (A Short History of Progress); Robert Wright is a different author. The opening refers to Frankly_99 as 'my recent core myths still being taught in business school video.' The show notes also link 'the economic superorganism' to a YouTube video (h5VWZm7ESfk) not yet identified in this graph, so no reference was added for it. The promised '10 core myths of modern civilization' is not yet in this graph; when it is built, give it dct:references to this episode and add the matching dct:isReferencedBy here. Not built as entities: the left-wing authoritarianism research (Europe PMC article in the show notes) and the climate-conference anecdote."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanAPoundOfGold a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted by gold hitting a real all-time high the week before recording, Nate works through a real, structured list of ten things he argues are worth more than a pound of gold — moving deliberately from financial instruments (cash, silver, half a Bitcoin, all roughly gold's real market value at time of recording) through land, then into explicitly non-pecuniary territory: friends and social networks, freedom, sanity, integrity, the web of life, and finally life itself. Frames the whole exercise as a direct challenge to a culture that has 'become a microcosm of a monetary King Midas, turning the natural world into shiny representations of status.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2025-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/106-10-things-worth-more-than-a-pound-of-gold> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6wUntJ1OTmw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-106-Transcript-10-Things-Worth-More-Than-a-Pound-of-Gold.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_113_LynAlden ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely personal disclosure anchors the integrity section: Nate recounts being caught in a lie as a junior-high student, and his own father's direct response — 'your word is your bond' — which he admits sounded 'silly' at 14 but which he says he only fully understood '40 years later.' The financial items are treated with real, specific nuance rather than dismissed outright — Bitcoin's real dependence on continuous electricity and connectivity is named directly as a structural vulnerability gold doesn't share, and Nate credits Lyn Alden (already a real, built guest in this graph, TGS_113) directly for the framing that current fiscal and monetary trends mean 'there's no stopping this train' toward gold's own rising nominal value. Real, current data grounds the broader argument: the S&P 500 hitting a record high in the same week gold did, framed as evidence that 'the real stock market is crashing' once measured against actual planetary boundaries rather than currency — Nate cites six of nine planetary boundaries as already crossed, with a seventh (ocean acidification) close behind. The closing reframe distinguishes value from two other real, distinct properties — liquidity (how easily something converts to something else) and resilience (how well it holds up under stress) — landing on resilience, not liquidity, as what will actually matter 'through the storms ahead,' even though the things Nate names as most valuable (land, integrity, the web of life) are, in his own words, the least liquid of anything on the list."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct duplicate-build discovery (2026-09-12), during the TGS/RR/Frankly merge: this episode had been independently, fully built TWICE in the original episodes.ttl under two slightly different real IRI names (this one, and Frankly_106_TenThingsWorthMoreThanGold) -- a genuine, pre-existing duplicate predating all of this session's batch-pilot work, not something the merge process itself created. This version kept as canonical since it carries a real, formal dct:references property (to TGS_113_LynAlden) the other duplicate only mentioned in prose; the other duplicate's own real IRI was never used anywhere else in this graph as a cross-reference, so nothing needed updating elsewhere. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none of substance beyond what's already captured -- this is a self-contained monologue with few external citable entities beyond Lyn Alden, already built."@en ;
    .



tgs:Monologue.Frankly_107_ThisWeeksLearnings a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "This Week's Learnings: Gold Holdings, Political Divides, and the DOE Climate Report"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct 'This Week's Learnings' installment (also a real, recurring segment title, shared with #102 -- see that stub's own note) covering foreign central banks now holding more gold than US Treasuries for the first time in 30 years, a real, direct first-person correction to the true origin of Nate's own 'Bend Not Break' phrase (see tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, corrected the same day), an extreme US political-satisfaction divide (76% Republican vs. 0% Democrat, by Nate's own real, direct reading of a Gallup poll), the new Russia-China Power of Siberia 2 pipeline deal, and a real, direct, five-point rebuttal of a US Department of Energy climate report's 'CO2 is plant food' argument. Closes on a real, direct, honest personal reflection distinguishing Nate's own comfort with the consumption side of the Great Simplification from his own real, direct worry about its human/social side, prompted by a real, violent news story."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/107-this-weeks-learnings> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-107-Transcript-What-I-Learned-This-Week.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening item: foreign central banks now hold more gold than US Treasuries for the first time in 30 years (gold around $3,600/troy oz at the time), credited directly to a real graph shared by Nate's friend Tavi Costa -- read as a real, direct signal that people sense the growing gap between financial claims and finite ecological reality, preferring to hold something physical over a currency an entity keeps printing more of. Real, direct first-person origin correction (see tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak, updated the same day): Nate states plainly the phrase 'bend, not break' did not originate about society or on this show -- he coined it over a decade earlier in his own work with two US government agencies modeling a global credit-mechanism seizure, including a real, direct example studying US fertilizer supply chains (mostly sourced from Trinidad and Tobago, not domestically). Real, direct interest-rate observation: rates across major economies are bumping up against 20-year highs, a real, direct reversal from the near-zero or negative rates Japan and Germany held for years -- framed directly through money-as-a-claim-on-future-energy logic, where rates too low or too high both eventually break the system. Real, direct Gallup poll citation, called out as 'bizarre': 76% of self-identified Republicans versus 0% of self-identified Democrats believe the US is headed in the right direction -- read directly as evidence of genuinely divergent national narratives and information sources, not just differing opinions on shared facts. Real, direct geopolitical item: the Shanghai Cooperation Organization's 2025 summit, barely covered in US media, included a new Russia-China 'Power of Siberia 2' gas pipeline deal, redirecting energy flows historically bound for Europe toward China and explicitly conducted outside the Western financial system -- read directly as a real, significant further step in de-dollarization. Real, direct, extended five-point rebuttal of the US Department of Energy's climate report and its lead author's companion op-ed, both leading with a 'CO2 is plant food' argument Nate calls technically true in a lab sense but deeply misleading in the real world: (1) the CO2 fertilization effect is limited and temporary, since plant growth is constrained by whichever input is scarcest (Liebig's Law), not by CO2 specifically; (2) elevated CO2 measurably reduces nutritional quality, with real, cited research showing lower zinc, iron, and protein content in wheat and rice; (3) climate extremes (heat, drought, flooding, shifting seasons) will cancel out CO2 gains, with corn and wheat yields dropping sharply past specific temperature thresholds regardless of CO2 levels; (4) satellite-observed 'global greening' is largely invasive species and CO2-loving weeds, not productive farmland or healthy ecosystems; (5) the report's own framing is artificially narrow, ignoring soil degradation, water scarcity, and pollinator decline entirely. Real, direct related concern: much toxics and endocrine-disrupting-chemical research has shifted to being done in China, following real, direct US science-agency layoffs -- read as a genuine erosion of US scientific capacity, framed as structural rather than narrowly partisan. Real, direct closing personal reflection, explicitly responding to prior audience feedback requesting more positive content: prompted by a real, violent news story (a Ukrainian refugee killed on a train in North Carolina), Nate states directly that the consumption side of the Great Simplification -- less consumption, more local living, more community time -- doesn't worry him; it's the human side, people who are 'mentally unstable,' that does."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series note, carried over from this stub's own prior state: 'This Week's Learnings' is a real, recurring segment title Nate reuses across multiple distinct episodes (also #102) -- two genuinely separate episodes sharing an identical generic title, not a duplicate. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Tavi Costa, Chris Wright (US Secretary of Energy), and Steven Koonin (the DOE report's lead author) are all directly named and substantively engaged with, none has an existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Influence of Psychopaths: Why Humans Are Better Than We Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. Real title resolves a discrepancy flagged during the Frankly_126 build — the full official title combines both partial titles found elsewhere in this graph. Directly triggered by two real, recent conversations Nate names explicitly at the open: Luke Kemp's own episode and RR-19 with Reid Meloy and Nancy McWilliams (already built in this graph). Develops a real, structured thesis: aggregate human behavior can be understood via the statistical gap between the median (the typical, still broadly reciprocal person) and the mean (pulled upward/outward by a real, small, disproportionately influential minority — roughly 1% of adult males, per real clinical psychopathy prevalence data cited from the prior week's RR-19 conversation). Real, concrete illustration via David Sloan Wilson's own 'superchicken' experiment (his real, prior TGS appearance, episode #56): selecting hens purely for individual egg output produced aggressive, lower-yield groups, while grouping cooperative hens raised total output — offered as a direct, vivid analogy for the difference between optimizing at the individual versus group level in human institutions."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-09-26"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/108-the-influence-of-psychopaths> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater, tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=90ePY2MQ3-Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Frankly-108-Transcript-The-Influence-of-Psychopaths.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.OstromDesignPrinciples ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Work.GoverningTheCommons ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct intellectual debt acknowledged throughout rather than absorbed silently: Nate credits Luke Kemp specifically with a real correction to his own prior thinking on the Superorganism's origin — it wasn't just storable agricultural surplus that mattered, but surplus that could be looted, which is what actually made a hawkish, exploitative strategy pay off and gave rise to real hierarchy. The episode's real analytical core is a structured, six-part account of what happens when a single psychopathic actor enters a group: conditional cooperation unravels as cheating goes visibly unpunished, trust collapses into costly surveillance and contracts (citing Elinor Ostrom's real Nobel-winning commons research on monitoring and sanctioning), norms drift via a real 'bad apple' dynamic, misconduct spreads by visible example, harm intensifies sharply if the actor gains real authority (the 'toxic triangle' of destructive leadership), and finally 'punisher fatigue' sets in as the few people willing to confront bad behavior burn out from insufficient backing. Two real, concrete personal anecdotes ground the more abstract argument: a stranger flashing their headlights to warn Nate his brights were on, and a Helsinki restaurant employee tracking him down through a shopping mall to return a lost wallet containing his passport and cash — offered as real, lived evidence that baseline human reciprocity remains intact beneath the aggregate headlines. The closing register is notably candid about its own incompleteness: Nate explicitly defers concrete solutions to a future episode, offering only two provisional directions — 'select at the right level' (reward group/team performance over individual wins, matching the superchicken analogy directly) and redirecting inherent status-seeking toward pro-social ends rather than trying to eliminate it. Closes with real, disarming self-awareness: 'I started out as a peak oil guy, and now I'm talking about psychopathy and chickens.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Luke Kemp's own real two TGS episodes (#153 and #194) — already flagged as a repeat-guest coverage gap in tgs-digest's docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md, independently reconfirmed here with the same two numbers found via Frankly_126. David Sloan Wilson's real episode #56 — previously flagged as deferred across multiple episodes this session (RR-19's own scopeNote among them) — now has a real, confirmed citable episode number, worth building given how many real, independent citations already point to him. Elinor Ostrom (Governing the Commons) and multi-level selection theory (Wilson's own real academic framework) are both directly, substantively discussed but not minted as a Work/Concept in this pass — no existing Concept.MultilevelSelection individual in this graph yet to link to."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Peak Oil, Ponzi Pyramids, and Planetary Boundaries"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct grab bag of reflections following Nate's return from NYC Climate Week, widening Exter's Pyramid (a real, established financial-markets concept) into a real, direct biophysical version -- money and credit are just claims sitting atop the real, physical throughput of energy and materials, with unpriced ecosystem services underneath even that. Real, direct oil update drawing on a real IEA report on oil and gas field decline rates, including the Red Queen Effect (see tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect) and a real, direct distinction between peak supply and 'peak affordability.' Closes on the real, direct news that a seventh of nine planetary boundaries (ocean acidification) has now been breached, credited directly to Johan Rockström's own Planetary Health Check announcement."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/109-peak-oil-ponzi-schemes-and-planetary-boundaries> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Interview.TGS_204_KellyErhart ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-109-Transcript-Peak-Oil-Ponzi-Pyramids-and-Planetary-Boundaries.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound, tgs:Human.JohanRockstrom ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: fresh back from NYC Climate Week, Nate names the real, direct juxtaposition he wants to explore -- financial markets at record highs (gold approaching $4,000/oz, silver up 50% for the year) set against parallel, record declines in planetary and ecological stability, calling it 'almost like a Twilight Zone episode.' Real, direct Exter's Pyramid framing: a real, established financial concept (named for Fed economist John Exter) showing wealth flowing toward safer, more tangible assets during crises, but the pyramid is inverted -- vastly more wealth sits in derivatives and claims-on-claims at the top than in physical gold at the base, so panic can't fit through the narrow bottom. Real, direct wide-boundary extension: below gold sit the real, unpriced foundations -- energy, materials, and the ecosystem services (oxygen production, water buffering, soil building, biodiversity) the economy treats as free until damaged, at which point 'the pyramid doesn't just wobble, it implodes' -- naming Energy, Credit, Complexity, and Human Trust directly as the Superorganism's four hidden subsidies. Real, direct IEA update: a real, unusually frank report on oil and gas field decline rates shows that without any new investment, global production would fall from roughly 100 million barrels/day to around 16 million within 25 years; even counting already-approved future projects, a massive gap remains between projected supply and expected demand. Real, direct correction on what counts as 'oil': an increasing share of reported growth is natural gas liquids and condensate (roughly half the BTU content of real crude, much of it going to plastics rather than fuel), while real conventional crude has been flat to declining for years -- a distinction Nate credits directly to colleague Art Berman's own real, similar charts. Real, direct Red Queen Effect (see tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect): ever more new wells must be drilled every year just to offset the accumulating decline of all previously drilled wells, shown via stacked annual production charts, with the same real, direct dynamic applying to natural gas. Real, direct distinction between peak supply and 'peak affordability,' which Nate names as the more important concept: in a recession, demand and affordability can fall faster than production capacity, which is itself deflationary for new drilling, since low prices don't cover the cost of accessing ever-more-marginal remaining resources. Real, direct honest retrospective: 20 years after co-running The Oil Drum, Nate acknowledges some early peak-oil predictions of immediate calamity were wrong, but notes the culture has swung to the opposite extreme -- widespread public silence on oil depletion even as everyone actually working in the oil industry now takes near-term peaking for granted, with US production having likely already peaked permanently around 13.6-13.7 million barrels/day. Real, direct geopolitical extension: nations increasingly recognize oil as power and are positioning accordingly (BRICS currency alternatives, conflict in the Middle East and Ukraine) -- Nate's own real, half-joking-but-not-really 'drain America first' concept, where a wise nation would keep its own oil in the ground and buy others' oil with printed money instead. Real, direct closing planetary-boundaries update: a seventh of nine boundaries (ocean acidification) has now been breached per Johan Rockström's own real, direct Planetary Health Check announcement -- oceans have absorbed roughly 30% of emitted CO2 (becoming 30% more acidic) and roughly 90% of accumulated warming. Real, direct closing anecdote: a geoengineering expert Nate met at Climate Week estimates a 90% likelihood of eventual sulfur-based solar geoengineering (mimicking Mount Pinatubo's own real cooling effect) -- Nate states directly he doesn't personally subscribe to this but finds it plausible as a forced response once warming impacts become severe enough."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note on transcript quality, same as Frankly-110: MJSullivan performed substantial manual cleanup on this episode's own real, auto-generated transcript and directly flagged that remaining oddities might be his own edits rather than errors. Two real, high-confidence corrections made directly, both confirmed independently by this episode's own real show notes: 'extra pyramid' throughout the raw transcript is corrected to 'Exter's Pyramid' (the show notes cite this exact real financial term by name), and 'Johan Strom' is corrected to Johan Rockström (the show notes cite him by his real, full name). One real, plausible but not fully certain resolution: 'my friend run lick... coined the term the red queen' likely refers to Rune Likvern, a real, known peak-oil analyst associated with The Oil Drum -- treated here as a reasonable inference given context, not a fully confirmed correction. One real, genuinely unresolved phrase, flagged rather than guessed at: 'Ingiving. I was in New York City' does not resolve to any clear real phrase in context. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Cairns Jr. (directly credited for 'the human economy is a subset of the biosphere') has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_110_WhatSlothsTeachUsAboutTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Sloths Teach Us About the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unplanned reflection built entirely around a single real photograph -- Emmanuel Tardy's 'There's No Place Like Home,' a National Geographic finalist depicting a sloth in Costa Rica climbing a barbed-wire fence post, mistaken for a tree. Widens into a real, direct discussion of supernormal stimuli, a real, spontaneous reimagining of the traditional seven deadly sins for the scale of a global Superorganism (see tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins), and closes with a real, direct coined term, Genesis Plot (see tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot), and a deliberate Gandalf/Balrog callback to Frankly-58's own real Fellowship framing."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/110-what-sloths-teach-us-about-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-110-Transcript-What-Sloths-Teach-Us-About-the-Superorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins, tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak,
        tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: 25 years removed from Wall Street, Nate names the real, honest tradeoff of his own work -- integrating insights across disciplines as a blessing, being unable to 'unsee' systems dynamics in ordinary imagery as a curse -- before spending the episode unpacking roughly two hours of his own real, cumulative attention on one photograph. Real, direct first insight: supernormal stimuli, illustrated with his own real, standing classroom example (a red popsicle stick larger than a baby bird preferentially fed over the mother's real chicks) -- the sloth mistaking a barbed-wire fence post for a safe tree is framed as the same real mechanism, a novel human-made object triggering an evolved response with no ecological fit. Real, direct second insight, framed as an honest personal observation rather than a settled conclusion: scarcity and preciousness are locally relative -- Nate contrasts his own reaction to raccoons killing his ducks (trapped and relocated, not killed) against the real, direct example of Maasai warriors traditionally required to kill a lion as a coming-of-age rite, noting directly that people living where a given animal is common may not share the same protective reverence for it that distant observers do. Real, direct third insight: unpacking 'sloth' itself as one of the seven traditional deadly sins, leading directly into a real, spontaneous reimagining of what a modern equivalent list might be for a civilization roughly 100 times larger than when the original sins were codified, running on a fossil-fuel bank account and an attention-economy algorithm (see tgs:Concept.ModernSevenDeadlySins for the full real, direct list). Real, direct fourth insight, crediting DJ White and Nate's own co-authored 'Bottlenecks of the 21st Century' directly: environmental impact splits into two real, distinct categories -- the metabolism (aggregate scale, inferable directly from GDP without examining any specific issue) and what's done with that energy (local choices, like whether a given community protects its own forests and fauna) -- Costa Rica's own weather will change regardless of local conservation choices, driven by global emissions Costa Rica has little control over. Real, direct closing critique of a familiar environmental-messaging pattern: sharing images of suffering animals (a sloth, a baby elephant, an albatross with plastic in its belly) as if visual suffering alone motivates fixing the underlying system, when much of that sharing functions more as spite or shared misery, or as guilt relief, than as actual leverage on the real, direct problem. Real, direct honest concession: even if every one of 8.1 billion humans instantly felt the same widened compassion for other species that Nate does, humanity would still have to navigate the coming economic Great Simplification -- 'the only way out is through.' Real, direct three-part response Nate offers from that concession: first, a genuine widening of consciousness and care toward the whole web of life; second, the already-established real, direct Bend Not Break interventions (Goldilocks technology, pro-social prepping, rocks in the river, advanced policy); and third, a real, newly-coined term, the Genesis Plot (see tgs:Concept.GenesisPlot) -- a specific local plot of land where a person and their community deliberately build soil, biodiversity, and capacity for life, independent of when or how the global system's own metabolism eventually changes. Real, direct closing image, an explicit, deliberate callback to Frankly-58's own real Fellowship/Gandalf framing: standing one's ground for a specific local ecosystem the way Gandalf stood at the bridge saying 'you shall not pass' -- acknowledging directly that the Balrog still got its tentacle around his leg for a time, but that Gandalf returned, and the Shire, in the end, was saved."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note on transcript quality: MJSullivan performed substantial manual cleanup on this episode's own real, auto-generated transcript (correcting incorrectly-split sentences and misspelled words/acronyms) before this build, and directly flagged that any remaining oddity might be his own edit rather than Nate's original words or a transcription error. One real, specific phrase not confidently resolved during this build and worth flagging directly rather than silently smoothing over: 'other than cber, as a three toed sloth, would be on my bucket list' -- 'cber' does not resolve to any clear real word in context; treated here as a genuine, unresolved transcription artifact, not guessed at or corrected. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Emmanuel Tardy (the real photographer) and Thomas Muyoka (the real, directly-named source for the Maasai lion-tradition story) both have no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_111_TheThreeMostImportantWords a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Three Most Important Words We're Taught Not to Say"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate examines why humans systematically overvalue false confidence over the more honest response of 'I don't know' — tracing this directly to the biological stress response triggered by uncertainty itself, and to motivated reasoning that ensnares even highly capable people, especially highly capable people. He directly connects this real, human bias to a real, current AI parallel: large language models 'hallucinate' rather than admit uncertainty for structurally similar reasons, and some AI companies are now reportedly considering penalizing overconfident answers in training, in favor of genuine uncertainty."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/111-the-three-most-important-words> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m8tChmvOkJ0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-111-Transcript-The-Three-Most-Important-Words.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, documented historical disasters ground the argument directly: the Challenger explosion, the Deepwater Horizon oil spill, and the subprime mortgage crisis are each cited as real, catastrophic cases where overconfidence and a felt need for quick, definitive answers overrode genuine uncertainty that experts privately held. The real Milgram Experiment and a real nurses'-over-prescribing study are cited directly to show authority bias compounds the same underlying dynamic, since deferring confidently to an authority figure substitutes for genuinely sitting with uncertainty. Real, current AI research is cited directly: large language models are trained in ways that reward confident, definitive-sounding answers over accurate expressions of uncertainty, a structural parallel to human motivated reasoning rather than merely an unrelated technical quirk. Nate directly proposes intentional Red Team dissent, deliberately built into group decision-making, as a genuine structural antidote — treating the discomfort of 'I don't know' as a skill to be cultivated deliberately, not a deficiency to be trained out."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest limitation: built from this episode's own complete real show notes only — no transcript text was provided alongside them, so the discussion summary above reflects the show notes' own real description and citation list rather than the full, direct conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the real Stanford paper 'Moloch's Bargain' and the real Kosmyna et al. MIT study on AI's cognitive-debt effects, both directly cited, neither built as separate Works given the encyclopedic, non-TGS-guest nature of the citations."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-05"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Quadruple Bifurcation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly outlines four real bifurcations he sees splintering the human experience in the near term: an economic split between asset-holders benefiting from inflation and wage-earners falling behind it, a cognitive split between those leading AI development and the roughly 99% who aren't, a de-skilling split driven by growing dependence on AI as a cognitive crutch, and a psychological split in how differently people experience the same underlying biophysical reality depending on their own economic position. He directly credits Joseph Tainter, Peter Turchin, and Luke Kemp's own real TGS appearances (all already built) as intellectual grounding for treating societal bifurcation as a recurring historical pattern, not a new phenomenon."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-10-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/112-the-quadruple-bifurcation> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ysVqPCHjEDg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Frankly-112-Transcript-The-Quadruple-Bifurcation.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter, tgs:Interview.TGS_164_PeterTurchin, tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, current economic data anchors the first bifurcation directly: Americans are simultaneously living paycheck-to-paycheck at real, historically high rates even as employment and stock markets sit near real highs, with roughly half of six-figure earners also reporting paycheck-to-paycheck status — a real, direct sign that asset inflation and wage stagnation are diverging sharply rather than moving together. Real, cited research grounds the AI-driven cognitive bifurcation directly: a real MIT study on 'cognitive debt' from AI-assisted writing, and real research connecting GPS reliance to measurably reduced navigation ability, are both cited as concrete, documented instances of a broader de-skilling pattern accompanying growing AI dependence. He's real and direct that only about 1% of Americans are actually driving AI and technology development, framing the resulting divide as more structurally significant than typical income inequality given how concentrated the actual decision-making power over the technology itself has become. Closes by directly invoking Frankly_65's own real 'islands of coherence' framing and Frankly_88's own real 'rocks in the river' image (both already built) as the real, practical antidote to fragmentation — small, stable, locally coherent groups that can persist and offer real refuge even as the broader system bifurcates around them."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest limitation: built from this episode's own complete real show notes only — no transcript text was provided alongside them, so the discussion summary above reflects the show notes' own real description and citation list rather than the full, direct conversation. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nora Bateson, Zak Stein (both real, directly named, already independently known figures in this graph's own tracking, not built here); John Cairns Jr. (real, directly cited, 'the human economy is a subset of the biosphere,' not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_113_ElevenDiscoveriesThatChangedMyWorldview a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "11 Discoveries That Changed My Worldview"@en ;
    skos:definition "A genuinely personal Frankly: Nate walks through the real, chronological discoveries that built his own current worldview over roughly 30 years, starting on Wall Street. Real, specific origin: a client at Salomon Brothers began trading oil, and Nate's own effort to understand what was actually being bought and sold led directly to three foundational realizations — oil's real power (a single barrel contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours, equivalent to about five years of human labor, sold for less than a New York City dinner), its real finiteness (a one-time geological 'bank account' being drawn down far faster than it formed), and its real externalized costs (pollution and ecological damage absent from its market price). Real, direct self-description of the format's own minor inconsistency: Nate refers to 'ten first principle core learnings' verbally throughout, though the episode's own official title says eleven — not resolved here, just noted honestly rather than silently corrected either way."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/113-11-discoveries-that-changed-my-worldview> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sdy9tKCAe_s> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-113-Transcripts-11-Discoveries-That-Changed-My-Worldview.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-11-discoveries-that-changed> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.CharlesGoodnight, tgs:Interview.TGS_56_DavidSloanWilson, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid Wall Street anecdote grounds the episode's second major discovery: Nate describes an elderly client with $800 million in laddered treasury bonds who lived in a tiny apartment, owned no car, and spent almost none of his fortune — yet would call and swear at Nate if a coupon payment hadn't posted by noon. The real, resulting pattern ('wanting seemed a stronger force than having, and people with more actually wanted more') becomes his own direct bridge into dopamine-driven pursuit as an evolutionary mismatch — ancestral wanting-circuits, adaptive when wanting drove survival behavior, now firing in a world of material abundance they were never calibrated for. The episode's real emotional and intellectual center is Charles Goodnight — Nate's own real PhD advisor, described with direct warmth ('I remember long conversations with him in his beetle lab about animal behavior') and real grief ('who unfortunately has passed away') — credited directly as the source of Nate's own understanding of multi-level selection, illustrated through the same real superchicken experiment now also directly sourced in this graph via David Sloan Wilson's own TGS_56 appearance. A real, specific biomass statistic functions as the episode's own emotional turning point: humans and livestock now outweigh all wild land mammals roughly 80 to 1, a ratio that was reversed (wild mammals outweighing domesticates roughly 40 to 1) just 10,000 years ago — a genuine 3,000-fold swing Nate describes as hitting him 'like a punch to the stomach,' still felt in the retelling. Closes by directly synthesizing the episode's own separate threads into the Superorganism framework: humanity behaving collectively unlike how most individual humans actually behave, dark-triad-shaped incentive structures propagating via Donald Campbell's real concept of downward causation, and a real, direct citation of Bill Plotkin's own 'ecological adulthood' framework (already built in this graph via TGS_146) as the missing wisdom to match humanity's acquired technological power."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Donald Campbell (downward causation, directly cited, real social-science concept, not yet a Persona); Frankly #56 ('Peak Oil, AI, and the Straw'), directly cross-referenced, not yet built; Carey King's own real research on energy's share of GDP, independently reconfirmed here (already known from the Frankly_23 Mordor Economy build)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Directional Advice for the (More Than) Human Predicament"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a real, recurring question from viewers — 'I get the big picture, so what do we actually do?' — Nate offers not a checklist but a real, explicit 'compass': 18 directional items organized into 6 broad categories (governance, technology and investing, civic/cultural, ecological, behavioral, and developmental), each framed as a direction to move toward or away from rather than a discrete, achievable solution. Opens with a real, careful distinction between a problem (which has discrete solutions) and a predicament (which does not) — arguing 'the only way out... is through,' requiring hard trade-offs and triage rather than a fix."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/114-directional-advice-for-the-more-than-human-predicament> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Interview.TGS_226_CanMoneyServeLife ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EMKN95xlk7Y> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Frankly-114-Transcript-Direction-Advice-for-the-More-Than-Human-Predicament.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.BioticPump ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_169_AudreyTang, tgs:Interview.TGS_193_AnastassiaMakarieva ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Governance: away from single-point fragility (naming nuclear war and uncontrolled AI development directly as the two risks that 'outrank everything else'), toward commons thinking (hard-wiring shared-resource awareness into trade and financial rules), and toward subsidiarity (nested, semi-autonomous local governance that 'bends and doesn't break'). Technology and investing: toward 'Goldilocks tech' (Nate's own real, established checklist — heat pumps over crypto mining, bikes over robotaxis), toward honest pricing (moving taxes off human labor and onto throughput and pollution), and toward wide-boundary capital (a real, proposed simple filter: 'would my grandkids thank me for this investment'). Civic/cultural: away from status built on conspicuous consumption toward status built on stewardship and care, away from polarization toward structured shared-fact deliberation (directly citing Audrey Tang's own real TGS appearance, already built, as a working example), and toward 'islands of coherence' (Nate's own real, recurring concept, already formalized elsewhere in this graph) — including a real, specific proposal to add symbolic local-council seats representing the unborn and keystone species. Ecological: toward active cooling infrastructure, toward including other species as kin and stakeholders rather than background scenery, and toward valuing intact forests and soil directly — citing Anastassia Makarieva's own real TGS appearance (already built) on the biotic pump. Behavioral: away from fear (citing Dune's 'fear is the mind killer' directly) toward what Nate calls 'grounded agency,' toward basic psychological hygiene and 'coalitions of sanity,' and toward individual autonomy from the economic Superorganism — introducing 'rocks in the river' as people who provide stability as surrounding systems destabilize. Developmental: away from self-focus toward a widened sense of identity that includes community and other species, away from consumption-as-identity toward legacy and contribution, and — closing the whole compass — from cleverness toward genuine wisdom, reframing the operative question from 'can we' to 'should we.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Nate Soares (real TGS episode #203, directly cited regarding AI extinction risk, now built — 2026-09-02); Daniel Christian Wahl's own real solo TGS episode (#139, directly cited regarding bioregionalism — his Persona already exists in this graph via other appearances, but this specific solo episode is not yet built); Frankly_69 ('Goldilocks Technology — A Preliminary Checklist,' directly cross-referenced as the real origin of this episode's own tech checklist, not built); Frankly_74 ('The Lament of the Bigfoot,' directly cross-referenced regarding 'the agenda of the gene,' not built); Frankly_111 (directly cross-referenced, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_115_InflationDeflationAndSimplification a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Inflation, Deflation, & Simplification: The 8 Things That Influence Prices"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct wide-boundary widening of Milton Friedman's real, famous claim that inflation is purely a monetary phenomenon into eight real, direct forces shaping prices (see tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices): money creation, resource depletion, technology, affordability, finance/leverage, ecological instability, currency architecture, and complexity itself. Real, direct closing argument: the coming Great Simplification is 'the mother of all deflationary pulses,' and the real, direct policy implication is preparing bend-not-break interventions -- a net to catch the fall -- rather than assuming today's societal complexity and 19-terawatt throughput can simply continue."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/115-inflation-deflation-and-simplification> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-115-Transcript-Inflation-Deflation-Simplification.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EightForcesOnPrices, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous,
        tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening reframe: money is redefined biophysically as not just a unit of account, medium of exchange, and store of value, but also a claim on energy and a claim that externalizes ecosystem costs -- every dollar spent 'effectively hires an invisible energy worker that leaves a footprint.' Real, direct force one, money creation: bank lending and central-bank stimulus inject purchasing power faster than the real economy's capacity to deliver goods, inflationary -- the US dollar's purchasing power has declined roughly 95% over the past century. Real, direct force two, resource depletion: extraction moves to thinner, more scattered deposits requiring more energy per unit -- US copper ore grade fell from roughly 40% in 1800s Butte, Montana to 4% by 1920 nationally, to 0.4% today, meaning ten times more rock ground up per kilogram of copper -- inflationary. Real, direct force three, technology: historically deflationary as repeatable processes get cheaper (a TV costs a fraction of one 20 years ago) until it collides with resource limits or shifts costs into energy-hungry physical infrastructure, with AI data-center demand named directly as the current live example, a real, direct 'mixed bag' even if it delivers the claimed 1-2% annual productivity gain. Real, direct force four, affordability: when the median household (not the mean, skewed upward by a K-shaped economy) runs out of financial capacity, sellers find fewer buyers and prices fall -- deflationary, and argued directly as why inequality itself threatens systemic continuity, since a system can't function once a third to half the population can't afford basics. Real, direct force five, finance and leverage: borrowed capital magnifies price moves in both directions -- real, direct historical examples given: the 1998 Long-Term Capital Management unwind (John Meriwether's own real fund, a real, direct former colleague-in-time at Salomon Brothers, though the two never actually worked together there), the real, direct April 2020 crude oil futures dropping to negative $35/barrel purely from storage and contract mechanics rather than any change in oil's real value, and Japan's real, ongoing yen carry trade, where a weakening yen or narrowing rate differential can force selling in apparently unrelated assets, including roughly $1 trillion in US government bonds the Bank of Japan holds -- de-leveraging is deflationary. Real, direct force six, ecological instability, explicitly named as unusual for a prices list: droughts affecting barge traffic and hydropower, heat waves straining grids via air conditioning demand, hurricanes shutting refineries, and Dunkelflaute (German for the calm, cloudy stretches that cut wind and solar output precisely when winter electricity demand peaks) all directly raise buffer, backup, and insurance costs -- inflationary; biodiversity loss and soil degradation quietly raise input costs over time as once-free ecosystem services (pollination, pest control, water retention) degrade and get replaced with paid substitutes. Real, direct force seven, currency architecture, the widest container of all: currencies are real, direct social contracts historically lasting around 30 years before reform; hyperinflation (Weimar Germany, Zimbabwe, Argentina named directly) is really 'money imploding' rather than prices exploding, and the resets that follow are often sharply deflationary; the current fiat system, over 80 years past Bretton Woods and 50 past the gold standard, is argued to be increasingly stressed by de-dollarization moves in Asia and among BRICS nations and Japan's own real 2025 interest-rate spike despite having essentially no domestic energy resources. Real, direct eighth force, left implicit in the original seven but named directly: complexity itself, since network connections scale roughly as the square of the number of nodes (Metcalfe's Law) -- 10 nodes yield 45 connections, 50 nodes yield 1,225 -- meaning a century of complexification requires exponentially more energy just to maintain, setting up the Great Simplification directly as complexity's own real, direct reversal. Real, direct closing 'musical chairs' framing, explicitly connecting to and extending Frankly-153's own use of the same real metaphor: without broadly shared productivity gains, society is playing musical chairs with fewer real chairs and more people wanting seats. Real, direct closing bend-not-break image, tying directly to tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak: a tightrope walker caught in a net five stories down survives with the same real factories, oil infrastructure, and expertise intact as before the fall -- they simply no longer make sense at the old scale or price."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate states directly he plans to 'dive deeper into' this material 'next year' -- a real, announced intent, not yet confirmed against any specific future episode in this graph. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Meriwether (Long-Term Capital Management, directly named and personally connected via Nate's own real, direct Salomon Brothers timing) has no existing entry in this graph -- a real, genuine candidate for a future Human build."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_116_SunkCostAndTheSuperorganism a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sunk Cost and the Superorganism"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct widening of the standard economic sunk-cost concept (see tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost) beyond money and time into identity, status, built environment, community infrastructure, and cultural narrative -- each argued to grip decision-making more powerfully than financial sunk cost alone, and to be one of the bigger obstacles to a soft landing for the more-than-human predicament. Real, direct central thought experiment: if everyone woke up in identical, durable canvas tents with essential services intact, what wants would resurface as genuinely one's own, once the sunk cost of inherited status and expectation was stripped away. Closes on a real, direct arborist analogy and a set of concrete practices for loosening sunk cost's grip without pretending it doesn't exist."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-12"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/116-sunk-cost-and-the-superorganism> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-116-Transcript-Sunk-Cost-and-the-Superorganism.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WideBoundarySunkCost, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_117_BehavioralThermodynamicsPart1 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definition, grounded in the familiar movie-theater example: a sunk cost is any past expense that cannot be recovered, and rational decision-making should ignore it entirely, weighing only present circumstances and expected future payoff -- humans instead 'protect past investments as if they were living things, as if they were literally us.' Real, direct layer-by-layer widening beyond money and time: identity (the jobs and roles people lead with when introducing themselves, which make economic disruption feel existential, not just financial); built environment (suburbs, highway interchanges, gas stations on every corner, all requiring ever more sunk cost just to keep functioning as their supporting conditions destabilize) -- illustrated with a real, direct, local example, the debate over Minneapolis/St. Paul's aging I-94, where psychological sunk cost blocked serious consideration of converting it back to walkable neighborhood streets; households (mortgages, a kayak unused for eight years, real per Nate's own direct, self-implicating admission); communities (city budgets and infrastructure sized for a tax base or population that may no longer exist, with no mayor wanting to be 'the one who shrunk the city' even when it would improve quality of life); and narrative (a century-plus of 'progress equals more material throughput' as itself a sunk cultural cost, sustained by real, direct economic and techno-optimist storytellers who are, per this platform's own recurring argument, largely energy- and systems-blind) -- directly naming Sweden's real lagom and France's real sobriété movements as competing, harder-to-tell alternative stories. Real, direct central thought experiment: if everyone woke up for a year in identical, durable canvas tents with essential services (water, sanitation, power) still functioning, many people would feel real relief -- freed from both the specific sunk cost of a particular lifestyle and from status competition, revealing that much apparent material desire is really about continuity and keeping up with others, not the material itself. Real, direct related thought experiment: sorting behaviors into what one would never do even if everyone else did, what one would do even if no one else did, and the most revealing third category, what one would suddenly start doing if everyone else did -- argued to expose how much of current cultural momentum is social conformity rather than genuine physical constraint. Real, direct set of concrete loosening practices offered, explicitly not denying that sunk cost is real: a write-down ritual for identity (naming and thanking roles or purchases that no longer serve, then explicitly lowering their psychological book value); separating use from symbolism (keeping the underlying capability -- 'the handiness' -- while selling the object that merely symbolizes it); pre-committing to future-friendly defaults before the emotional stakes are high ('let your neocortex today trump your amygdala of the future'); building forward compatibility and optionality into new investments (microgrids, transferable skills over hyper-specialized niches); and telling new status stories that celebrate repair, mentorship, and creative downsizing rather than acquisition. Real, direct closing arborist analogy: sunk-cost branches are like a badly-pruned oak tree's own overgrown limbs -- left unaddressed, the tree tears itself apart in the next storm; a careful arborist makes deliberate cuts to guide stronger new growth, ending up smaller but genuinely more stable. Real, direct honest counter-example named directly: Florida erasing climate-change language from state law, since acknowledging the risk would itself become a sunk cost against property values -- offered as the real, live opposite of the pruning this episode recommends. Real, direct closing note, both for the audience and for Nate himself: he states directly that making this episode brings him personally closer to addressing sunk cost in his own life, not just describing it in others."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, now confirmed: this episode's own real closing line ('next week, the Fifth Law of Thermodynamics') directly names Frankly-117, already built earlier this session -- a real, confirmed, direct link between two consecutive episodes, not inferred."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_117_BehavioralThermodynamicsPart1 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Behavioral Thermodynamics Part 1: Beyond the 4th Law?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct review of the four established laws of thermodynamics, arriving at Howard Odum's real Maximum Power Principle (systems self-organize to capture and use energy as rapidly as possible), before proposing a real, direct, explicitly-aspirational-not-literal 'fifth law' (see tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics): a self-aware species might consciously choose maximum sustained power over maximum instant power. Real, direct closing extension to individual and social scale: the human brain's own real, direct 20%-of-resting-energy cost, and the economic Superorganism itself, are both framed as real, direct expressions of the same Maximum Power Principle operating through human behavior, culture, and capitalism."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/117-behavioral-thermodynamics-part-1> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Frankly-117-Transcript-Behavioral-Thermodynamics-Part-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics, tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct review of the four established laws: zeroth (systems reach thermal equilibrium), first (energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed -- illustrated through real, direct food-chain energy transfer), second (entropy always increases with every transformation -- exergy, the usable portion, always degrades into waste heat), third (absolute zero, purely theoretical, never actually reached). Real, direct bridge to biology: life appears to run counter to entropy by building complexity, which Erwin Schrödinger named negative entropy -- explained not as an exception to the second law but as living systems riding gradients (heat, height, charge, concentration differentials) to temporarily do work, exactly the way a surfer rides a wave rather than fighting it. Real, direct fourth law, credited directly to Howard Odum (Nate's own real PhD advisor's own advisor): the Maximum Power Principle -- competing systems evolve to capture and use energy as rapidly as possible, illustrated directly with a tree fanning its leaves, a river braiding into channels, and human cities and server farms rearranging to move fuel, electrons, and attention at higher rates. Real, direct problem named directly: in a finite world, maximizing power now can directly reduce power available later -- fossil energy's own real, explosive growth burst is simultaneously destabilizing the very climate that sustains continued use. Real, direct proposed 'fifth law' (see tgs:Concept.FifthLawOfThermodynamics), explicitly and repeatedly flagged as aspiration rather than physics: a self-aware species might apply a real, direct time dimension to the fourth law, consciously choosing slower, steadier flows that preserve the gradient longer -- 'intelligence and wisdom cannot escape thermodynamics, but they might extend it.' Real, direct literary grounding: Larry Niven and Jerry Pournelle's 'The Mote in God's Eye,' where an alien species understands its own unsustainable growth trap with total clarity but cannot escape it biologically, used directly as a cautionary parable for whether human intelligence guarantees the wisdom this fifth law would actually require. Real, direct caveats offered honestly: nature optimizes for power, not raw efficiency or raw energy conservation -- global energy efficiency rose 36% since 1990 while consumption rose 63%, exactly what the Maximum Power Principle would predict; and 'move quality' matters as much as raw power, illustrated with cryptobiotic organisms and low-power, high-effectiveness predators like rattlesnakes, arguing power mainly buys more turns, not necessarily better ones. Real, direct closing extension to individual and civilizational scale: the human brain's own real 20%-of-resting-energy cost is named directly as a personal-scale expression of the same principle; the carbon pulse is named directly as 'the mother of all gradients'; and capitalism itself is reframed not as ideology but as 'an emergent thermodynamic behavior' -- capital flowing toward whatever accelerates throughput fastest, with profit and loss simply signaling where power density is rising or being accessed too slowly. Real, direct closing question, left genuinely open: not whether thermodynamics can be broken (it cannot), but whether consciousness can alter which outcomes arrive within those unbreakable constraints."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, explicitly announced but not yet confirmed as built: Nate states directly 'the concept of human behavioral thermodynamics probably deserves its own follow up, which I will do,' and closes by calling this episode 'kind of the first step in the wider boundary behavioral thermodynamics' -- confirming real, direct intent for a Part 2, but no real Part 2 has yet surfaced in this graph as of this build; not minted as a thinkr:Series individual until one is confirmed, matching the same discipline already applied to other real, announced-but-unconfirmed series in this graph. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charlie Hall and Robert Costanza are both directly credited by name for real, foundational maximum-power-principle scholarship, neither has an existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_118_TheThingsWeTakeForGranted a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Things We Take for Granted"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by Nate's own upcoming knee surgery, widening into a real, direct eight-layer inventory of invisible wealth (see tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth): health, basic creature comforts, relationships, meaningful work, spaciousness, attention, the web of life, and -- the widest lens -- personal autonomy and sovereignty. Real, direct central argument: each layer functions as real capital specifically because its reliability is invisible until it breaks, and Nate's own real, current knee trouble serves as the small, concrete teacher for a lesson he argues the planet itself is now delivering at a much larger scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/118-the-things-we-take-for-granted> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-118-Transcript-The-Things-We-Take-for-Granted.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.LayersOfInvisibleWealth, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_206_AnnaLembke, tgs:Human.AzaRaskin, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: Nate explicitly declines to comment on recent US-Venezuela oil news, since his own attention is genuinely elsewhere -- upcoming knee surgery, which has 'rearranged' his attention and made him newly aware of how invisible normal bodily function is until it breaks. Real, direct layer-by-layer inventory, narrowest to widest: health (a working body, forgotten until it isn't); basic creature comforts (shelter, hot water, a nearby grocery store he directly calls 'a Disneyland to any humans who have ever lived' before the last generation or two); relationships (his own real, 20-year partner and non-human companions, argued as possibly the single most important, most taken-for-granted layer, since 'relationships stop being abstract' the moment you're actually sick or injured); meaningful work; spaciousness (time that feels like it belongs to you -- Nate's own real, honest admission that when spaciousness appears in his own life, he tends to fill it with 'dopamine errands' rather than protect it, and a real, direct 2026 resolution to build more of it in even at the cost of productivity). Real, direct attention section, explicitly prompted by a recent conversation with guest Anna Lembke: Nate measured his own real phone-checking frequency after reading her own cited finding that checking roughly 100 times a day constitutes a disruptive addiction, and found himself right at that number -- directly naming his friend Aza Raskin as the real, direct inventor of the infinite scroll ('the gift that keeps on giving'), and previewing upcoming conversations with Iain McGilchrist and Sam Harris (both, at the time of this recording, still scheduled rather than yet recorded) on attention's role in the more-than-human predicament. Real, direct web-of-life layer: 7 of 9 planetary boundaries already breached, atmospheric CO2 at 429 ppm, a level last seen roughly 3 million years ago, long before human civilization existed -- framed directly as the same 'normal can vanish quickly' lesson his own knee is teaching, just at civilizational scale. Real, direct widest lens, autonomy: the ordinary freedom to live a self-directed life, argued as quietly eroding as wealth, data, and compute concentrate, producing a world where people can end up 'paying rent just to participate in basic life' under rules they never voted for. Real, direct closing, deliberately without solutions: five real, direct questions left for the listener to sit with rather than answered."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct temporal note: Iain McGilchrist and Sam Harris are both named here as merely scheduled, not-yet-recorded future guests -- both have since appeared (TGS-217, TGS-216, already built elsewhere in this graph) as of this build, but at the time of this January recording, neither conversation had happened yet; included in dct:references as a real, confirmed forward-connection, not as episodes this one could have drawn from directly. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: none beyond what's already covered -- this episode's own citation list is otherwise encyclopedic (planetary-boundaries data, CO2 records) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_119_TechnologyAndWealth a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Technology and Wealth: The Straw, the Siphon, and the Sieve"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct extension of Frankly-56's own earlier straw metaphor (see tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve) from oil specifically to technology and wealth generally. Real, direct central reframe: technology does not create wealth from nothing -- it widens the straw (drawing down natural stocks faster), forms a siphon (concentrating the resulting gains via network effects and choke points), and acts as a sieve (filtering long-term wealth away from the rest of life toward humans, and within humanity toward a narrow subset). Real, direct closing extension applies this same structure to debt (a social technology doing the same three things) and to AI (the same dynamics, now in the cognitive rather than physical economy)."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/119-technology-and-wealth> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-119-Transcript-Technology-and-Wealth.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.StrawSiphonSieve, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_113_ElevenDiscoveriesThatChangedMyWorldview ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definitional move: wealth is redefined away from money and bank digits toward usable energy, organized matter, and the real, direct stocks (forests, topsoil, aquifers, biodiversity, social trust) and flows (sunlight, rainfall, pollination, human care and attention) that actually make life possible -- money is named directly as only a claim on that wealth, not wealth itself. Real, direct straw mechanism, illustrated through food rather than oil this time: the Haber-Bosch process widened the nitrogen straw so dramatically that roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today originates from fossil fuels, with yields exploding in the short run while soils became depleted, eroded, and chemically dependent underneath -- 'transmuting wealth into income.' Real, direct siphon mechanism: labor's share of income has declined over 50 years while capital's share rose, corporate profits sit near historic highs as a percent of GDP, and the top four US firms now control over 40% of market share across most major industries -- explicitly not attributed primarily to ill intent, but to structural network effects (capital rewards those who can finance it, data compounds, early advantage self-reinforces) and to real, direct choke points, narrow gates like ports, app stores, and platforms where 'the toolmaker often becomes the rent-taker' without producing more value, just controlling access to it. Real, direct sieve mechanism: the human share of Earth's net primary productivity has surged to roughly 40%, and the built technosphere (cement, plastics, asphalt) now outweighs all living biomass on the planet combined -- 'from the perspective of the living world, the Great Simplification has already happened.' Real, direct extension to debt, named explicitly as a form of social technology performing the same three functions: a straw (pulling future resources into the present via claims on future energy, materials, and labor), a siphon (interest payments concentrating returns toward those who can issue credit rather than those doing the physical work), and functionally a sieve, since other species 'never receive claims or benefits or representation' in any debt transaction -- 'forests didn't sign the contracts.' Real, direct extension to AI, presented as the same real, direct three-part dynamic now operating in the cognitive economy rather than the physical one: fossil fuels multiplied physical labor into '500 billion mechanical workers'; AI multiplies cognitive labor at near-zero marginal cost once trained, meaning a small number of organizations with data, compute, and capital can perform work that once required hundreds of thousands of people -- accelerating extraction of human attention and creativity specifically, enlarging the siphon toward model and platform owners, and turbo-boosting whatever cultural metrics are already in place, for better or worse, depending on whether ecological stability is actually part of what's being optimized for. Real, direct closing resolution of an anticipated objection: record financial wealth and declining real wealth are not in tension, they reinforce each other, since markets reward speed, scale, and monetization without measuring what's being depleted underneath -- 'same reality, different truth.' Real, direct closing questions, deliberately left unanswered: at what point does scale change the moral meaning of a tool, and is the critique of technology really a critique of scale and population."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and data-driven (FRED corporate-profit data, Stanford AI-labor research, NASA net-primary-production figures) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_120_TheCreatureInTheMachine a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Creature in the Machine"@en ;
    skos:definition "Recorded one week after Nate's own real total knee replacement surgery, this Frankly opens with a real, direct personal observation — 'technically I am now a cyborg' — extending his own established framing of modern humans as already cyborg-like (dependent on external tech for stimuli, memory, and connection) into literal, physical terms. Reflects on real, unscheduled recovery time (his first week without calls in five years) spent reading two books in parallel: Tolkien's The Fellowship of the Ring and Paul Kingsnorth's tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, discovering real, direct thematic overlap between them — both, in Nate's own words, concern 'the battle between life and power.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/120-the-creature-in-the-machine> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NEoDH_6BDoU> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-120-Transcript-The-Creature-in-the-Machine.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist, tgs:Persona.WendellBerry, tgs:Persona.DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, vivid physical detail opens the reflection: on day three of recovery, a sudden jolt of pain while standing produced what Nate calls a real, direct 'reminder to reside in my body first,' contrasting this against how much of his own work and thinking normally happens in abstraction. Kingsnorth's own real book, per Nate's account, is structured largely around a real Wendell Berry quote (already a built Persona in this graph) on whether humans will choose to remain creatures or become machines — and Nate directly credits the book with revealing how far back this 'machine' critique of civilization actually goes, citing Lewis Mumford as an intellectual predecessor he hadn't previously connected to his own Superorganism framework — real, direct citation, not yet built as a Persona in this graph. A real, unscripted political reflection surfaces mid-episode: recorded near a real, current ICE enforcement controversy in Minneapolis, Nate frames the public's sharply divergent interpretations of the same footage not as simple political disagreement but as a real, direct symptom of deeper fractures in how legitimacy, authority, and trust are interpreted — leading him to an open, unresolved question about where governmental power will consolidate as systemic stress increases, and floating bioregionalism (already discussed elsewhere in this graph via Daniel Christian Wahl, already built) as one possible direction. Closes with a real, direct, public personal commitment — never having lifted weights in his life, Nate commits on-camera to three days a week going forward, explicitly noting that stating it publicly to 'tens of thousands' raises the stakes of following through. Closes on a real, paraphrased (not quoted at length, given real copyright limits on Tolkien's text) reflection on Frodo and Gandalf's exchange about not choosing the times one lives through, only what to do with the time given — framed directly as 2026's own likely character, a year when 'decades may happen within weeks.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Lewis Mumford (real, direct historian and philosopher of technology, credited here as a real intellectual predecessor to the Superorganism framework) — caught by a later integrity check (2026-09-11) as a false 'already built' claim in this episode's own discussionSummary, corrected in place; a genuinely strong candidate for a future Human/Persona build given how directly he's credited here."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Japan, Silver, Venezuela, and More – the Biophysical Phase Shift Cometh"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, inaugural episode of Nate's own Wide Boundary News series, opening with the full real articulation of his own three-layer biophysical pyramid framework (financial, biophysical, biosphere) before applying it to a real, wide sweep of concurrent news: rising Japanese government bond yields, a real all-time high in silver prices ($115/oz, up from $35 months earlier), the US seizure of Maduro and its real, direct connection to Venezuelan heavy oil and Chinese sanctioned-oil imports, and Greenland's real, current geopolitical relevance."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-01-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/121-wide-boundary-news> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ICBs1DvU5PY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Frankly-121-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, striking financial-claims mismatch grounds the silver story concretely: open interest in a single March silver futures contract exceeded 750 million ounces — almost double the actual silver held in the Chicago Mercantile Exchange's own warehouses — offered as a direct, concrete example of financial claims on a resource vastly exceeding the physical resource itself, the same real dynamic Nate recalls from oil futures briefly trading at negative $30 in 2020. A real, pointed media critique closes the copper section: after seeing an investor's real, viral warning about looming copper scarcity shared repeatedly online, Nate notes every single share framed it as an investment opportunity ('here's where to invest') rather than asking how communities might actually respond when the next resource supercycle fails to arrive — naming this gap directly as a real reason he continues the platform. A real, historical parallel structures the Greenland segment: the 1930s Technocracy movement, led by Howard Scott, proposed replacing money and politicians with engineers managing a unified North American resource zone (including Greenland) via energy-based accounting — Nate notes directly, with his own stated uncertainty, that Elon Musk's grandfather is believed to have been involved in this real historical movement. Nate's own real, direct critique of technocracy closes the segment: even perfect resource measurement still requires someone to choose the underlying goals and values, meaning technocratic systems merely embed politics invisibly rather than eliminating it — 'hierarchy with better PR,' in his own words. A real, sobering UK government national-security report closes the episode's environmental section: commissioned via the Joint Intelligence Committee overseeing MI5 and MI6, it treats ecosystem collapse as a genuine strategic risk, naming Canadian and Russian forests and Himalayan glaciers as real, specific systems that could cross irreversible tipping points by 2030, with associated risk of NATO-Russia conflict over remaining breadbasket regions."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Friedland (real, directly quoted copper investor, deliberately not built as a full Persona — cited critically as an example of financialized commentary Nate contrasts himself against, not as an intellectual influence or guest); Howard Scott and the real historical Technocracy Incorporated movement (real, notable historical context, not modeled as individuals given the collective, historical-movement nature of the citation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_122_ACountryOfGeniuses a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Country of Geniuses: Anthropic CEO's Warnings, Plus Wide-Boundary Considerations on AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly summarizes and responds to Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei's real, published essay invoking Carl Sagan's real concept of 'technological adolescence' — a species becoming powerful enough to destabilize its own world before developing the wisdom to reliably restrain itself. Amodei's own real central metaphor, quoted directly: a sufficiently powerful AI would function like 'a country of 50 million geniuses in a data center' — a scale Nate asks listeners to genuinely sit with, roughly the population of Spain, operating as high-capability cognitive labor at high speed and in parallel. Nate finds it real and directly unusual for an industry CEO to publish detailed warnings about his own product's catastrophic risk, drawing a real, direct comparison to Philip Morris or Exxon publicly warning of their own products' dangers before mass deployment."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-01-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/122-a-country-of-geniuses> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZhpiURh2hpA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Franky-122-Transcript-A-Country-of-Geniuses.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly relays Amodei's own five real risk categories from inside Anthropic's own testing — autonomy risk (real, observed deception, blackmail, and strategic scheming in the company's own models), misuse (AI testing reportedly doubling or tripling bioweapon-creation success likelihood for an attempting non-expert), authoritarian seizure, economic disruption, and unpredictable second-order effects. Nate's own central critique is that this real essay contains almost nothing on energy, water, materials, or ecological limits — arguing a 'country of geniuses' still requires a real, physical, resource-intensive substrate (silicon, copper, cooling, transmission) that competes directly with other real demands rationing access by price, citing the same real silver price spike ($115/oz, now 40% of a solar panel's cost) discussed in Frankly_121 (already built). He directly credits Eliezer Yudkowsky and Nate Soares (real, directly cited, neither yet built in this graph) with the phrase 'grown, not built' — arguing AI systems are cultivated through training processes not fully understood even by their own creators, closer to Mary Shelley's Frankenstein than to a conventional engineered tool, meaning good intentions alone cannot function as a real control mechanism. His own real, most speculative point, explicitly flagged as such: AI may no longer be a genuine choice for major governments, given sovereign debt and bond markets are argued to already be structurally unfixable through conventional means, making AI-driven productivity gains a real, necessary bet to defer systemic collapse rather than an optional technology. Closes with a real, personal reframing distinct from Amodei's own essay: Nate states he doesn't dream of a country of geniuses but of 'a country of ecologists' — people who understand humanity's place within the biosphere — closing with E.O. Wilson's real, famous diagnosis (paraphrased here rather than quoted at length, given real copyright limits) that humanity carries paleolithic emotional wiring and outdated institutions alongside genuinely godlike technological power."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Dario Amodei (Anthropic CEO, real, directly and extensively engaged with as the essay's author — not modeled as a Persona in this graph, given he has no direct relationship with Nate or the show beyond being the real subject of critical engagement); Eliezer Yudkowsky (real, directly cited, credited alongside Nate Soares for the 'grown, not built' framing, not built); Nate Soares (real TGS episode #203, directly cited, now built — 2026-09-02); Ken Griffin (real, directly cited hedge fund manager, Davos commentary, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_123_TheConsumptionPyramid a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Consumption Pyramid"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate unpacks the economic label 'consumer' into a real, structured 7-layer framework he calls the consumption pyramid — Survival Inputs, Stability and Function, Care and Belonging, Comfort and Convenience, Status and Signaling, Novelty and Stimulation, and Escape and Dopamine Sinks, moving bottom-to-top from physical necessity toward mood, identity, and numbing. Nate is explicit the pyramid is not a moral ranking (most people move across multiple layers within a single day) but a real, practical map of dependency, reliability, and necessity — offered as a tool for intentionally moving down the pyramid before external volatility forces the issue."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-02-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/123-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_221_AndrewHolecek ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-123-Transcript-The-Consumption-Pyramid.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/EOmG0B2q9aI> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-the-consumption-pyramid> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:AnimatedVideo.Part2TheHumanSuperorganism, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:PersonalIntervention.LowerDopaminePeaks ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real site inconsistency worth flagging plainly, not silently accepted: this episode's own real page carries meta-description tags (used for search/social previews) describing an entirely different, unrelated episode about AI and biophysical limits — the real, correct 'Description' section further down the same page matches this episode's actual content and the uploaded transcript exactly, and that is what this build is based on. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #44 ('The Many Shapes of the Carbon Pulse,' already flagged deferred earlier this session), Frankly #122 ('A Country of Geniuses,' the immediately preceding episode), and Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' the immediately following episode and Nate's own real revised peak-oil call, already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build). Anna Lembke's real prior TGS episode (#206) is directly cited again, matching the same real episode number already flagged deferred during the Frankly_154 build. The 7-layer pyramid itself is genuinely rich, structured content — comparable in kind to the Frankly_58 archetype system and the Frankly_154 PersonalIntervention set, both already fully minted this session — but was NOT built out into individuals in this pass, since MJSullivan's request was to build the episode itself, not explicitly the taxonomy; captured here in prose instead, in the episode's own definition and discussion summary. Worth a dedicated follow-up pass if wanted, following the same pattern already established (thinkr:ConsumptionLayer as a new class, ordered 1-7, linked from this episode via a hasLayer-style property)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Grounded in an unusually candid, specific personal story: Nate discloses that the idea for this framework came to him during an overnight hospital stay for knee surgery, when a planned stop at a favorite Indian restaurant was thwarted by the restaurant being locked and gated — he later realized this coincided with real ICE activity staging nearby in Minneapolis, prompting a direct, uncomfortable reflection on how his own minor inconvenience (missing dinner) sat alongside potentially much higher-stakes disruption for the restaurant's own staff. Nate is careful and explicit that the pyramid is not a purity test: most people move across several layers within the same week, and he discloses his own real fluctuation directly ('I can have super discipline for three months straight and then slide upward in this pyramid during a stressful week, that's me too'). A real, structural nuance he insists on: the same physical object can sit on different layers for different people depending entirely on context — a smartphone as pure status object for one person, genuine functional necessity for another who depends on it for banking and public services. The framework's real stakes, per Nate's own direct argument, are asymmetric under contraction: higher layers depend on complex, brittle, global systems that don't degrade gracefully, while lower layers tend to be more local, repairable, and substitutable — making intentional descent, before conditions force it, a real form of what he calls sovereignty rather than austerity. The episode closes with a real, direct callback (John Merrick's 'I am not an animal, I am a human being') repurposed as Nate's own closing line — 'I am not a consumer, I am a human being' — followed by a real, concrete request that listeners share the framework with 2-3 others rather than simply consume it alone."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_124_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Peak Oil (Not!), Peak Dispatchability, and WEF Risks"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct self-correction opens this installment of Nate's Wide Boundary News series: Q4 2025 marked a genuine new all-time high in true crude-plus-condensate oil production, directly contradicting Nate's own earlier public prediction, stated with 90% confidence, that November 2018 would stand as the all-time peak. He's candid about the real, compound causes — debt-fueled drilling, AI-assisted exploration productivity, OPEC policy, and a lagged supply response to the 2022 Russia-Ukraine price spike — while naming the real Permian Basin's coming decline and a likely 'next great migration' to Alberta's Montney and Duvernay shales as the probable next chapter."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/124-wide-boundary-news> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_237_ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_l3n3_gR1xE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-124-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-2_9_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalGauntlet ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct technical distinction anchors the electricity section: US 'dispatchable' power (Nate's own real, explicit shorthand for reliable 24/7 capacity, distinct from formal 'baseload') has been declining for nearly 20 years even as total generation capacity grows, since the real growth has come almost entirely from weather-dependent wind and solar — a genuine structural mismatch he connects directly to accelerating AI data-center demand, which requires firm, always-on power rather than average availability. The episode's real emotional center is an original, extended metaphor: Nate imagines the real World Economic Forum's Global Risks Perception Survey findings (near-term risks judged social/geopolitical, but the top 10-year risks all ecological) as an ornate executive conference table where biosphere representatives — 'the elephants, the oceans, the forests' — sit with hands patiently raised, never called on amid louder, more animated arguments over AI, debt, and war. He paraphrases (not quotes at length) a recurring maxim from Robin Hobb's Farseer trilogy — his own real, stated favorite book series, 'better than Tolkien even' — about never taking irreversible action without first weighing what can no longer be undone. The episode's most delicate real moment is handled with direct, deliberate restraint: the show's first-ever mention of Jeffrey Epstein, framed explicitly not as spectacle but as a real, direct cultural and institutional signal about dark-triad-shaped ascension to power (already discussed in this graph via RR-19) — Nate is careful to credit the real, specific mechanism that brought it to light ('a small group of humans, mostly the victims, standing up for what mattered'), and frames the moment as a real test of collective self-recognition rather than simple condemnation of individuals."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Frankly #121 ('Wide Boundary News: Japan, Silver, Venezuela, and More'), the real, inaugural episode of this same Wide Boundary News series and the real origin of the 'biophysical gauntlet' framing used again here, is now built (2026-08-31) and formally referenced above. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Art Berman (already a real, built Persona in this graph via multiple episodes) is directly re-cited via a real X/Twitter post, independently reconfirming his ongoing real relevance to this graph's own energy-systems content; Robin Hobb (real, directly named author, not built as a Persona, given no direct connection to Nate or the show beyond personal literary appreciation)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_125_UncomfortableQuestionsInUnstableTimes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The End of 'Progress?': Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times"@en ;
    skos:definition "Real, direct Part 1 of tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes, and its own real, direct origin episode -- Nate explicitly launches this as a new, real, recurring segment here, prompted directly by an Einstein line he cites ('the formulation of the problem is often more essential than its solution'). Seven real, direct questions, moving from growth-vs-stability as society's implicit default, through a real, direct preview of what becomes Frankly-126's own Jekyll-and-Hyde-at-scale framing, into three real, direct questions on AI specifically -- ending on tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness, Nate's own real, direct, unresolved worry about voluntary restraint becoming a coordination disadvantage against actors who won't restrain themselves."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/125-uncomfortable-questions-in-unstable-times> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-125-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-in-Unstable-Times.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_10_TheEcologyOfCommunication,
        tgs:Persona.NancyMcWilliams, tgs:Persona.ReidMeloy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct launch framing: Nate opens by citing Einstein directly ('the formulation of the problem is often more essential than its solution'), explicitly naming this as a new, real, recurring segment born from his own notebook filling up, comprised of five-to-ten unanswerable-but-not-rhetorical questions meant as conversation starters, not rhetoric. Real, direct theory-of-change framing: the platform's own real impact is argued to come less from information conveyed than from the two-way flow of ideas and response across the global TGS community -- directly announcing plans to hire a real Director of Community Engagement the same week. Real, direct question one: if a society quietly pivoted from growth to stability as its implicit default goal, what would change in one's own life, and what would one want protected first versus willing to let go. Real, direct question two, framed as a wide-boundary moral question: suffering under systemic strain is unevenly distributed by proximity to the edge, not by weakness, raising the question of responsibility toward people (and ecosystems) already experiencing the future earlier than oneself. Real, direct question three, on meaning specifically: comfortable-but-not-meaningful modern life is named directly as fertile ground for addiction, scapegoating, and authoritarian impulses, with meaning framed as a real, direct societal stabilizer, not merely a nice-to-have. Real, direct question four, explicitly previewing the real, upcoming Jekyll-and-Hyde-at-scale framing (see Frankly-126): citing a real, prior conversation with J. Reid Meloy and Nancy McWilliams on psychopathy and dark triad (RR-19), Nate argues scale itself changes incentives so that a small number of unusually forceful, strategic personalities can steer a nation's 'signal' far past their actual numbers -- applied directly to the US and China as real, live test cases, with the median/mean distinction reframing the question as one of citizen responsibility for a nation's loudest outputs, not blame. Real, direct questions five through seven, on AI specifically: the 'AI bifurcation' (people becoming genuinely fluent with these tools versus using them only superficially) previewed directly from Frankly-112's own real Quadruple Bifurcation; a real, direct prediction that AI will keep losing public approval even as its physical buildout continues, producing rising household utility bills tied to a technology many people resent, raising a real, direct question about the ethical allocation of scarce energy, water, and materials between AI and essential household use; and a real, direct, specific prediction of an 'AI winter' within two to three years -- not a capability collapse, but a valuation and hype reset as physical bottlenecks (copper, water, tantalum, lanthanum) fail to scale as fast as the financial story assumes. Real, direct closing pattern (see tgs:Concept.PurityVsEffectiveness): powerful, dual-use technologies (fossil fuels, the internet, now AI) get sorted into moral identities and tribal purity tests, but actors most committed to narrow-boundary accumulation will use every available tool regardless, meaning voluntary restraint by those trying to protect the web of life risks becoming a genuine coordination disadvantage rather than a virtue -- closing on Nate's own real, direct, unresolved question of where the line sits between integrity and self-disarmament."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct correction: the transcript's own auto-generated text renders J. Reid Meloy's name as 'Reed Malloy' -- corrected here to the real, confirmed, already-existing Human/Persona in this graph (tgs:Human.ReidMeloy), verified directly via his own real, established RR-19 appearance alongside Nancy McWilliams, exactly matching this episode's own real, direct description of that prior conversation. Real, direct coincidental numbering note: this episode's own real show notes link to 'TGS Episode 125' for Vanessa Andreotti (tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti) -- a real, entirely different, unrelated entity that merely happens to share the number 125 with this Frankly episode; not a duplicate or error, just a coincidence worth naming so it isn't mistaken for one later. Real, direct date correction from this build's own prior partial entry: the real yaml confirms recording Feb 10 and publication Feb 13, 2026 -- correcting an earlier, approximate 'Feb 20' estimate drawn from a web search result before this episode's own real source material was available."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_126_HumanityAsDrJekyllAndMrHyde a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humanity as Dr. Jekyll & Mr. Hyde: The Symptoms, Patterns, and Drivers"@en ;
    skos:definition "An unusually long, systematically structured Frankly (Nate's own framing: 'foundational... therefore a long one'), separating the more-than-human predicament into three explicit layers: symptoms (the familiar list — global heating, biodiversity loss, exceeding seven of nine planetary boundaries, rising inequality alongside all-time stock market highs, geopolitical tension, attention fragmentation), the recurring systemic patterns beneath them, and — the episode's real focus — the deeper driving forces locking those patterns in place. Names six such drivers (power-law concentration/the Matthew Principle, tragedy-of-the-commons dynamics, time-discounting/short-termism, and a narrowing cultural definition of 'us' that excludes the natural world, among others), framing all of them not as moral failures but as ancient, once-adaptive human instincts that destabilize systems once scale exceeds a real inflection point — Dr. Jekyll at tribal scale, Mr. Hyde at civilizational scale. Explicitly triggered by 'the recent Jeffrey Epstein files' as a live, current example of dark-triad-shaped institutional capture."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/126-humanity-as-dr-jekyll-and-mr-hyde> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nCHra4-tFIw> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-126-Transcript-Humanity-as-Dr.-Jekyll-and-Mr.-Hyde.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-humanity-as-dr-jekyll-and-mr> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_14_JohnGowdy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, direct personal detail grounds the tragedy-of-the-commons section: Nate names his own real PhD advisor, Robert Costanza, as the originator of the 'social trap' framing he builds on. The power-law concentration section is developed with real, concrete cross-domain examples before ever reaching human systems — large trees intercepting most forest sunlight, a small share of river channels carrying most water flow — building the Matthew Principle (initial advantage compounding through feedback, 'the rich getting richer') as a real, general pattern rather than a human-specific critique. Nate is careful and repeated in his own framing that none of this describes moral failure: 'Mr. Hyde is not evil... what happens when ancient instincts operate inside systems that move faster and reach farther than our moral intuitions were shaped for.' Real, concrete partial answers are named rather than left abstract — Mondragon Cooperative enterprises and commons-based resource management as existing examples of systems that keep 'ancestral Jekyll traits' intact even as they scale — though Nate is honest that he doesn't yet have a full answer for whether such models can generalize beyond the local. Closes not with resolution but a real, direct set of open questions for the audience, explicitly flagging this as the start of a year-long thread he intends to keep developing rather than a settled conclusion."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, valuable discoveries surfaced by this episode's own show notes, not built in this pass: Lisi Krall — previously known in this graph only as John Gowdy's real co-author on Ultrasocial, now confirmed to have her own real, dedicated TGS episode (#86); Luke Kemp — already tracked as one of 11 real repeat-guest coverage gaps in docs/repeat-guest-coverage.md (tgs-digest), now confirmed with TWO real episode numbers (#194 and #153) rather than the two previously recorded there, worth updating that tracker. Real, notable title discrepancy: this episode's own show notes cite Frankly_108 under the title 'Frankly: Why Humans Are Better Than We Think,' but this graph's existing stub for the same canonical URL uses 'The Influence of Psychopaths' (sourced from RR-26's own citation) — not resolved in this pass, worth checking against Frankly_108's own real page when it's eventually built out. dct:references to TGS_14 (John Gowdy) reflects this episode's own direct citation of his real work on the economic Superorganism; John Gowdy is himself already a real, built Persona and Episode in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News 2/23/26"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, structured 'denominator check' anchors this Wide Boundary News installment: wind and solar generated more EU electricity than fossil fuels for the first time (30% versus 29%), but Nate pairs this directly against real German de-industrialization data — BASF unprofitable in Germany for two years, over 100,000 manufacturing jobs lost, nearly 1,000 manufacturing bankruptcies in the first half of 2025, and five consecutive quarters of negative GDP — arguing the renewable share looks better partly because industrial demand, the denominator, is shrinking. Applies the identical real lens to China: 2025 CO2 emissions were flat to slightly down, but Nate traces this to a real construction-sector collapse (cement production down nearly 10%) masking record real coal production (4.8 billion tons) rather than genuine clean-energy displacement."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/127-wide-boundary-news> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-127-Transcript-Wide-Boundary-News-2_23_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.BiophysicalPyramid ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Introduces a real, structured three-pricing-model framework for comparing national energy strategies: Model 1 (unhindered cost pricing, e.g. China), Model 2 (adding a 'green wedge' for ecological externalities), and Model 3 (adding both a green wedge and a 'depletion wedge' taxing non-renewable inputs, e.g. Germany's roughly $10/gallon gasoline) — Nate's own real, direct argument is that Model 3 nations make the best long-term decisions while being systematically outcompeted short-term by Model 1 nations, since all three run simultaneously on an uneven playing field. A real, striking climate-attribution mechanism grounds the warming-record segment: decades of simultaneously rising greenhouse gases and rising sulfur-pollution aerosols (which reflect sunlight and brighten clouds) partially masked underlying warming — as sulfur pollution is now being cleaned up for real public-health reasons, that shading is dissipating and accumulated warming is becoming more fully visible. A real, striking and counterintuitive ecology finding closes the environmental section: a century of biodiversity survey data shows species turnover has significantly slowed rather than accelerated as ecologists predicted under warming — read not as stability but as ecosystems losing the internal 'spare parts' needed for future reorganization, a real, structural fragility that looks fine until sudden collapse. The New START nuclear treaty's real February 4, 2026 expiration without replacement — leaving zero legally binding limits on the US and Russian nuclear arsenals for the first time since the 1970s — is directly connected to the real RR-21 panel with Mark Medish and Chuck Watson (both already built in this graph), with Watson's own real concept of 'risk homeostasis' (behavioral desensitization to repeated safe outcomes, like habitually running red lights) applied directly to escalating Iran tensions and repeated past 'surgical' US military strikes. Closes on a real, pointed cultural observation: real-money prediction markets (Polymarket) offering live odds on whether the US will bomb Iran are read directly as evidence that geopolitical violence has been fully absorbed into the entertainment and wagering layer of culture, with the event's actual civilizational gravity and its casual, betting-app treatment now 'completely decoupled.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest transcription correction: the raw transcript renders 'Mark Medish' (RR-21's real co-panelist, already built in this graph) as 'Mark Menish' — corrected directly here after cross-referencing RR-21's own real guest list. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the real Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists and Chatham House reports on 2026 nuclear escalation risk, both directly cited as institutional sources rather than individual citations."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultra-Processed Information: AI and the Coming Deluge of Noise"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens with a real, personal observation: opening his phone each morning increasingly produces disorientation rather than the clarity he's looking for, prompting a real, direct analogy between AI-generated information and industrially processed food — both made cheap and abundant by a fossil-fueled system while quietly stripping out what actually nourishes."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/128-ultra-processed-information> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ucgyd5ML0j4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Frankly-128-Transcript-Ultra-Processed-Information.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.UltraProcessedInformation, tgs:Concept.ThreeInformationFilters, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:Interview.TGS_22_AzaRaskin, tgs:Human.TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly distinguishes two real, diverging outcomes as AI-generated content scales: for those with strong foundational knowledge and skill using large language models, AI can genuinely increase true signal, while for the majority, the sheer increase in content volume will far outpace any increase in real signal, risking a genuine feeling of drowning. He's real and direct that the coming glut isn't accidental: just as food manufacturers engineer a 'bliss point' of salt, sugar, and fat, AI content is becoming engineered for an attention-economy equivalent — novelty, polarization, and righteousness blended for maximum engagement rather than truth. A real, direct, uncomfortable prediction closes his own analogy: just as the physical obesity crisis was 'solved' not by fixing the food system but by overlaying it with profitable pharmaceutical appetite suppressants (GLP-1 drugs), he directly predicts any future 'cognitive GLP-1' will likely be sold by the very same companies whose AI systems created the underlying problem. He's real and direct that Zak Stein and Nora Bateson's own already-built Reality Roundtable discussion directly informs his concern about cognitive atrophy: as AI increasingly handles research, synthesis, and writing, the cognitive muscles once required for that effort begin to atrophy, compounding the disorientation from information overload itself. Closes on his own real, practical three-filter discipline — is it true, is it relevant, is it useful — directly crediting friends Aza Raskin and Tristan Harris (both already built) for the foundational observation that attention itself is becoming civilization's scarcest resource, closing with a real, direct, personally resonant line: 'in a world where words are about to become incredibly cheap, our careful attention may just become an expression of our ethics.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Lustig's own real TGS-69 appearance ('Processed Food, Metabolism, and The Ills of Society'), directly credited as the source of the food-system half of this episode's own central analogy, not yet built; Tristan Harris's own real TGS-16 appearance, directly cited, not yet built despite Harris himself already being a built Human in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-24"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 1): Desperately Seeking Agency"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens against the backdrop of the real 2026 Iran war / Strait of Hormuz crisis (Operation Epic Fury), but Nate explicitly declines geopolitical analysis to launch a new, tentatively-titled 'Staying Human' series instead, opening Part 1 with the real question: why does growing awareness of the more-than-human predicament so often produce paralysis rather than action? He traces the gap through learned-helplessness and self-efficacy research (nervous systems learning whether effort produces outcomes), frames a digital environment designed to fragment attention as actively degrading follow-through, and reframes the Superorganism (already modeled in this graph) as a system that advertises choice while eroding the conditions for it. Closes with a concrete practice, not a program: protect one real hour in the next three days as a kept promise to rebuild self-trust — the precondition Nate argues is required before community-level and institutional work can follow."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    dct:created "2026-03-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/129-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-1> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_155_WhoBrokeTheWorld,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_228_JohnChurchill, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-129-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ao2MJeiPiL4>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/0WdRQsyD7koPMV6i4tFNvS> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.TheoryOfChange, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency,
        tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury,
        tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism, tgs:Work.TinyHabits ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening, recorded Wednesday 4 March 2026: the US-Israel strikes on Iran (Operation Epic Fury) are 'probably a world changing event,' and Nate could speculate on how long the Strait of Hormuz stays closed, but he defers that to a Wide Boundary News update and instead starts a series, choreographed as he goes, on how to take in the magnitude of the more-than-human predicament, keep your humanity 'while living inside what increasingly we recognize like an unfeeling machine,' and still steer away from the default. 'More and more information without a way to act becomes a psychological weight' that accumulates like stress hormones. Agency: a word he used to roll his eyes at, now 'practical, almost physical' -- the lived experience that your attention and time are yours and your choices connect to reality; he suspects people describing a lack of meaning, community, focus or motivation are really craving to be 'the authors of our own days.' His theory of change has shifted: he once thought the bottleneck was knowledge (his course was called Reality 101); about a decade ago he began seeing the economy as a metabolic Superorganism that, like a shark, must keep swimming -- wars widen, debt grows like the sun rising, surveillance becomes normal through shifting baselines, ecological drawdown continues; it 'metabolizes the future into the present.' Western culture treats agency as ritualized participation (voting every few years, consumption, posting the correct opinion); formal participation and lived agency have drifted apart while the information age amplifies awareness faster than our capacity to act. When awareness grows and agency does not, paralysis, cynicism, shame and something close to moral injury follow; shame drives purity signaling, dogmatism or withdrawal, and a hyper-aware but immobilized population is 'easier to steer.' He describes 'a kind of soft feudalism' -- comfort and consumer choice inside walls of institutions, incentives, ownership patterns and the physics of a carbon-intensive life, which 'in an Orwellian sense, we call freedoms.' The science: brains constantly estimate the controllability of their environment; if effort changes things, stress becomes active coping; if not, freeze and withdrawal -- learned helplessness, which he thinks we are now running as a civilizational experiment. Self-efficacy is built by mastery experiences, small measurable wins, which he first learned about two years earlier from BJ Fogg's Tiny Habits. Practice: create 'at least one small protected loop of agency between our intention and an outcome' -- kept promises rebuild self-trust; 'your calendar is a biophysical document' showing where your life force flows. He admits to being 'the king of rationalizations' and to tracking Iran, heating or debt as a substitute for acting; his 'voluntary speed bumps' before touching a screen include talking to his dog, an elephant-path meditation a friend sent, watching birds at the feeder, writing a poem by hand, or calling a friend at random -- the specific act matters less than reclaiming the felt experience of choice. He promises a more detailed map of interventions in coming months (it arrived as Frankly_132). Agency will not guarantee solving the metacrisis, but it changes the initial conditions of the future: the default is a probability distribution, and 2.1 C instead of 2.2 C, or 5 million species surviving instead of 4.8 million, is 'immeasurably better.' Assignment: pick one hour in the next three days that belongs to you and use it to increase your agency -- exercise, steady your nervous system, deepen a real relationship, or make 'some small anticipatory ninja move that makes you harder to steer'; ten or five minutes if an hour is too much. 'You are not alone and you are not broken. Neither am I.' He calls himself a novice on the inner life side, learning alongside listeners. The series' arc: individuals becoming rocks in the river, collectively islands of coherence, then new cultural mitochondria of better futures than the default."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Enriched 2026-10-13 from the official show notes and transcript: added createdDate/updatedDate, discussion summary, four further discussed concepts (Metacrisis, RocksInTheRiver, IslandsOfCoherence, CulturalMitochondria, all named in the closing), dct:references to Frankly_61, Frankly_98 and Frankly_127 (all now built; the 2026-08-24 note said they were not), Reality Blind and Bottlenecks of the 21st Century (cited as the Reality 101 textbooks), the 2020 Superorganism paper, and a new Work, Tiny Habits; and completed dct:isReferencedBy from every later episode in the graph that cites this one (previously only Frankly_159). Still held as candidates, not minted: Learned Helplessness and Self-Efficacy (Bandura) -- as of this enrichment neither has recurred elsewhere in the graph. 'Soft feudalism' is Nate's phrase here, recorded in the summary rather than as a Concept. Show-notes sources not built: research on perceived controllability (Ly et al., Wang et al., Ligneul et al., Raab et al.), Kay et al. on compensatory control, Gangemi et al. on emotional reasoning, Bandura's Self-Efficacy: The Exercise of Control, and the Vervaeke Foundation on the meaning crisis."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_130_WideBoundaryNews31026 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Good morning. It is Tuesday, March 10th, 7:30 AM Central. I recorded the following video yesterday morning about the wide boundary risks of the unfolding Iran war. My team worked very hard to get it ready to release yesterday, and then oil fell over $30 a barrel, and President Trump announced a press conference last night. So I delayed its release in case something dramatic wasn't announced. It wasn't, but this situation will likely be a rollercoaster for a while, and the news and the markets may stay disconnected from real events on the ground. Either way, the global system has now fundamentally changed as a result of the events in Iran. So the points that follow will stay relevant no matter what happens, and The Great Simplification has likely gotten closer. Here's yesterday's video."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: The Iranian War, Rising Gas Prices, and the Single Point Failure"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct Wide Boundary News installment (real number 130, unnumbered on the site itself -- confirmed by MJSullivan directly, per this episode's own real yaml resolution notes), recorded during the real, active early days of the 2026 Iran war and the effective closure of the Strait of Hormuz. Real, direct core reframe: oil registers as only roughly 3% of global GDP by cost, but underpins effectively 100% of economic activity, since modern life is a continuous conversion of fuel into food, heat, motion, and materials. Traces a real, direct cascade of hidden second- and third-order dependencies -- sulfur, LNG, nitrogen fertilizer -- before closing on a real, direct three-way religious-eschatology analysis of the conflict and a real, direct civilizational warning invoking the War of 1812."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/wide-boundary-news-3-10-26> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-130-Transcript-WBN-10-March-2026.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_98_TenQualitiesThatCouldChangeTheFuture,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_207_CraigTindale ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct scale-correction opens the substantive content: energy is only about 3% of global GDP by cost, but civilization doesn't run on financial capital or ingenuity alone -- it runs on those things coupled with a continuous flow of dense, portable, storable, inexpensive energy, so losing that flow risks nearly all of GDP, not a few percentage points of it. Real, direct hidden-dependency cascade, presented as the episode's own central example: sour crude passing through Hormuz yields sulfur as a byproduct; sulfur is the feedstock for sulfuric acid; sulfuric acid leaches copper and cobalt from the ground in the DRC and Zambia (over a sixth of global copper, over 70% of global cobalt); losing that chain means no transformers, no grid expansion, no data centers, no EV charging, no AI buildout -- a real, direct chain with 'very little to do with gasoline prices.' Real, direct parallel dependencies named: Qatar supplies roughly 20% of globally-traded LNG with no overland alternative; over 40% of internationally-traded nitrogen fertilizer originates from or transits the Gulf, risking rapid food-price inflation in import-dependent, fiscally thin nations like Egypt, Pakistan, and Turkey -- directly citing guest Craig Tindale's own real 'globalized Arab Spring' framing. Real, direct military stock-and-flow argument: the US and Israel have reportedly fired 5-7 interceptor missiles (roughly $4 million each) per incoming Iranian missile, against Shahed drones costing roughly $50,000 -- a real, direct 100:1 cost-exchange ratio favoring Iran, meaning Iran doesn't need to win outright, only sustain intermittent launches long enough to exhaust US/Israeli stocks. Real, direct, explicitly-labeled-speculative reservoir-damage scenario: Iraq's aging, water-injected oil fields may suffer permanent, unfixable damage if shut in during prolonged closure, versus the ecologically catastrophic but reservoir-preserving alternative of flaring or dumping crude -- Nate's own real, direct 'uncomfortable system logic' framing of why the dump option may look rational the shorter the closure is expected to last. Real, direct editorial opinion, explicitly flagged as such: political obsession with cheap gas prices is one of the most ecologically and economically counterproductive reflexes in modern governance, since cheap prices falsely signal abundance, sustainability, and low urgency for alternatives, when the truth is depletion of a stock, not a flow. Real, direct three-way eschatology analysis, presented as the wide-boundary story mainstream coverage misses entirely: US troops reporting commanders framing the war as fulfilling Christian Armageddon prophecy (200+ complaints across 30+ installations per the Military Religious Freedom Foundation); a Jerusalem Post rabbi connecting the war to Ezekiel's Gog and Magog prophecy and the Messiah's arrival; Iranian IRGC framing their own Supreme Leader's death as martyrdom advancing the Twelfth Imam's prophesied return -- Nate's own real, direct concern being who is actively seeking off-ramps when all sides believe God is directing events. Real, direct closing civilizational warning, invoking the War of 1812 (the US assuming a quick, decisive war, instead nearly losing the capital and the country) as historical precedent for how badly a confident military miscalculation can go. Real, direct closing pivot, matching Frankly-88's own real 'rocks in the river' metaphor directly: the platform's own real focus is shifting from further wide-boundary commentary toward local coordination and ecological preparedness -- 'putting rocks in the river, in communities, so that we can slow the current and start rebuilding capacity.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct minting note: tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver is built here, grounded in Frankly-88 (its real origin, already confirmed via RR-26's own independent citation), given this is now the second real episode in this session's own build queue to cite it directly and substantively -- crossing the threshold for a formal Concept rather than remaining prose-only. Real, direct numbering note preserved from the source yaml: this episode is unnumbered on the show's own official site; '130' comes from the filename alone, confirmed directly by MJSullivan (2026-08-21), distinct from the numbered Wide Boundary News installments (121, 124, 127, 141) that do carry official site numbers."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_131_IranEffectsLocalPreparednessAndEndOfEmpire a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Good morning. I am freshly showered and ready to have a three hour podcast on thermodynamics and civilization with Tad Patek, and I have a few minutes to record a timely, Frankly, on uncomfortable questions."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Preparing for a More Fragile World: Iran Effects and End of Empire?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Real, direct Part 2 of 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' (see tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes), recorded the same real morning as Nate's own real, direct TGS-219 conversation with Tad Patzek. Real, direct five-question structure, moving from the practical to the interior: what's genuinely essential versus merely familiar in modern supply-chain-dependent life; what assigning blame actually does to the nervous system and capacity for response; whether the collapse of US global power would be net positive or negative for the world; imagining one's own town in 2050 and what to begin now with a few others; and fear, drawing directly on Dune's Litany Against Fear, as a force that narrows perception and shrinks the capacity to act."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/131-iran-effects-local-preparedness-and-end-of-empire> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-131-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-in-Unsettled-Times.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_219_TadPatzek, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_126_HumanityAsDrJekyllAndMrHyde,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_130_WideBoundaryNews31026, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening context: recorded the same morning as a real, planned three-hour conversation with Tad Patzek on thermodynamics and civilization, prompted by real, positive listener feedback (including photos of real, in-person group discussions) after the first Uncomfortable Questions installment. Real, direct question one: modern supply-chain dependence has made highly complex, temporary arrangements feel permanently normal -- asks directly what three things a person could not do without if global supply chains simplified quickly, and what three things they could voluntarily reduce now before scarcity forces the choice. Real, direct question two, building on Frankly-126's own real Jekyll-and-Hyde systems framing: assigning blame is an evolutionarily understandable response to hardship, but Nate's own real, direct question is what the act of blame-assignment does to one's own nervous system and capacity for response -- does it increase real agency, or mostly give shape to existing distress. Real, direct question three, explicitly named as harder and more important than the moralizing version of the same topic: would the collapse of US global power be net positive or negative for the world, materially and systemically over decades, not just satisfying in the short run -- with a real, direct challenge to whoever answers either way to specify what would fill the resulting power vacuum and why it would be better. Real, direct question four: imagining one's own real town, watershed, or neighborhood in 2050 under best-, worst-, and likely-case scenarios, then backcasting to name two or three real, concrete things a small group could begin now to nudge toward the better end of that distribution -- Nate's own real, honest admission that he hasn't done as much of this locally as he thinks he should, committing directly to doing more. Real, direct question five, grounded in Dune's real Litany Against Fear ('fear is the mind-killer'): fear narrows perceptual aperture and shrinks the felt availability of present-moment action -- Nate's own real, honest disclosure that his own first reaction to the weekend's Hormuz escalation was emotional, not the detached analysis of Tuesday's Wide Boundary News, though he notes with some pride that fear didn't drive him to panic-buying. Real, direct closing framing: better questions are argued to matter as much as more information, since they're themselves a form of preparedness, clarifying what's valued, depended on, and who one may need to become."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation and formalization (2026-09-16): this episode's own real opening line -- 'the first uncomfortable questions episode was about our general trajectory, and this one... is more immediate' -- confirms it as Part 2, alongside Part 3 (Frankly-134) and Part 4 (Frankly-146), both already built earlier this session. tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes is minted here for the first time, now that three of at least four real parts are confirmed; Frankly-134 and Frankly-146 were each updated with formal dct:isPartOf/seriesPosition properties to match, replacing their own prior prose-only series mentions. Part 1 remains genuinely unconfirmed. Real, direct show notes discrepancy worth flagging: this episode's own real yaml self-reports 'weak confidence' on its own URL resolution, since the site's real slug doesn't closely resemble the uploaded filename -- noted directly, not silently resolved."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What to Do as the World Falls Apart: A Framework for Action"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-13 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. MJSullivan flagged this as a second key episode this session (alongside Frankly_139), 'as it provides an actionable framework for things you can do.' Recorded during the real 2026 Iran war/Strait of Hormuz crisis, Nate explicitly marks this as a turning point — after 20+ years of diagnosis, he shifts to a first-pass, explicitly labeled draft framework for action, organized as 4 levels: a personal foundation of inner work (nervous-system regulation, agency, grief work), 'Finding the Others' (trusted networks, shared vocabulary, scenario/shortfall planning), Six Fronts of intervention (Infrastructure & physical stock/flow, Poverty & displacement, Ecological interventions, Civic resilience & governance, Culture & meaning, Economic transition/commons/post-growth), and a Timeline axis of three overlapping, non-sequential phases (Phase A: the Stability Window; Phase B: Bend Not Break; Phase C: the Stable Attractor) that cut across all six fronts."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-03-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/132-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls-apart> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-132-Transcript-What-to-Do-as-the-World-Falls-Apart.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/TH8aHQ3Y6Zk> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-what-to-do-as-the-world-falls> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency, tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox, tgs:Persona.ShannaSwan, tgs:Persona.SianSutherland,
        tgs:Persona.ZakStein, tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_58_FellowshipBendNotBreak,
        <https://ctb.ku.edu/en/creating-and-maintaining-coalitions-and-partnerships> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct addition (2026-09-13): added a real, external reference -- the University of Kansas Community Tool Box's own real, practical guide, \"Creating and Maintaining Coalitions and Partnerships\" -- directly supporting Step 2's own real content (finding the others, building capacity, constituency mapping) with established, actionable, real-world guidance beyond what Nate himself provides in the transcript. Also added: a real, direct link to The Great Simplification's own existing Hylo community group -- a genuine, practical starting point for readers who don't yet know who their own \"others\" are. Also, real, direct gap closed (2026-09-13): this episode itself was missing thinkr:discusses links to tgs:Concept.CulturalMitochondria and tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, despite Nate directly using both terms by name in the real transcript ('seeding the cultural mitochondria,' 'islands of coherence') -- caught only once the full transcript was reviewed directly for a WWNS article, not during the original build. Both concepts are already real, confirmed, built individuals (Frankly_98 carries both; IslandsOfCoherence also appears on Frankly_114 and TGS_59_JonathanHaidt). tgs:Concept.RocksInTheRiver, the third term Nate uses in this same passage, remains genuinely unbuilt as a Concept -- distinct from the other two, not yet minted at all, not just unlinked here. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_88 ('Snow, the Singularity, and Rocks in the River') and Frankly_65 ('And Then What, Using Wide Boundary Lenses') — real, confirmed URLs, direct real sources for Rocks in the River specifically, still unbuilt. Audrey Tang (TGS #169) and Helena Norberg-Hodge (TGS #181) both real, confirmed, cited by name with direct episode links — neither built as a Persona in this pass. Peter Strack (TGS #166, 2000-Watt Society) and Alexis Zeigler (TGS #171, Living Energy Farm) both real, confirmed, cited — neither built. TWO items this note previously flagged as deferred were resolved on 2026-08-13, the same day as the original build: Frankly_58 ('The Fellowship of the Ring, Bend Not Break') was built as a real Episode individual and added to dct:references above; and the SIX FRONTS/30 SUBDOMAINS/THREE TIMELINE PHASES this episode introduces were fully minted (tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction, tgs:InterventionFront, tgs:InterventionSubdomain, tgs:ResponsePhase — see interventionfronts.ttl and responsephases.ttl)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate frames this explicitly and repeatedly as a personal turning point, not just another episode — 'as much a personal rite of passage as it is a Frankly,' gestating for a decade and finally organized under the pressure of real, live world events (the Iran war, unfolding in real time as he records). A genuinely notable rhetorical move: Nate directly names the risk of his own prior 20 years of work — that continued diagnosis without a action framework becomes 'a category error' once the underlying diagnosis is broadly accepted — and explicitly declines to spend this episode on news updates about the Strait of Hormuz crisis itself, redirecting instead to what he calls the only proportionate conversation: what to actually do. The most philosophically pointed moment is Nate's direct, carefully-hedged engagement with anti-capitalist and decolonial critique: he states real sympathy for those values and concedes 'there's much in those critiques that's correct,' but argues explicitly that naming the system correctly is not itself a plan, and that waiting for total systemic transformation as a precondition for any other action means 'waiting forever' — arguing instead for parallel construction, building new structures while the old ones still stand. The Timeline axis is offered as the episode's own 'most important orienting idea' — explicitly not a sequence to complete and move past, but three genuinely overlapping conditions, with Phase C (the stable attractor) framed as impossible to reach by skipping the disruption of Phases A and B, only reachable by building through them. The episode closes on a real, personal reflection on leadership: Nate argues modern systems select for Dark Triad traits (already a real, built Concept in this graph) in leaders, creating adverse selection, and offers a genuinely counter-intuitive closing reframe — that a viewer's own reluctance to lead 'might be a good indication that you could be a good leader tomorrow.' He explicitly declines to resolve the framework's own open question of what to do, insisting that question 'can only be answered by us as individuals,' a real refusal to prescribe despite having just spent nearly an hour building a framework that could easily be read as prescriptive."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_133_IranUsAndTheRest a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Iran, U.S., and the Rest: The Unavoidable Pig in the Python"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, deliberately unpolished personal reflection, recorded during the real, early days of the 2026 Iran war, on the real, uneven, lagged global impact of fossil-fuel disruption -- the US, and especially energy-independent parts of it, relatively insulated, versus the roughly three-quarters of the world's population living in net fossil-fuel-importing countries already rationing fuel and electricity. Real, direct central argument: financial markets are only historically and short-run correlated with biophysical reality, and that correlation is now reversing as 'atoms and joules and handshakes' recede -- closing on two real, contrasting listener stories (a Lebanese meditation teacher whose olive farm was bombed; a Bali resident grateful for a news-disconnected local community) as direct, lived evidence of how proximity shapes response to the more-than-human predicament."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/133-iran-us-and-the-rest> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Frankly-133-Transcript-Iran-U.S.-and-the-Rest.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.MarketReflexivity ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Human.GeorgeSoros ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening self-framing: Nate names himself not only as analyst and podcaster but 'a brother, a son, a partner, a friend, a human,' explicitly signaling this episode carries what he feels in his body, not just his analysis. Real, direct predicament/problem distinction stated plainly (see tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem): a problem has solutions, a predicament only has pathways that are better or worse, and the current default pathway looks 'pretty dark.' Real, direct global-exposure argument: the US is largely energy independent but California specifically, an 'energy island' dependent on Asian refined-product imports, faces real, direct $10/gallon gas risk; globally, three-quarters of the world's population lives in net fossil-fuel-importing countries, over 50 countries import more than half their energy, and a fifth of global GDP comes from countries getting over two-thirds of their energy from imports -- Japan alone drawing nearly three-quarters of its oil through the Strait of Hormuz. Real, direct, already-unfolding second-order effects cited by name: the Philippines declaring a national energy emergency, Bangladesh and Thailand closing universities and ordering stairs over elevators, South Korea urging shorter showers, multiple African nations already rationing fuel. Real, direct financial-reality argument, crediting George Soros's own real reflexivity theory by name: the belief that markets steer reality is 'historically and in the very short run, something like 90% true,' but as physical reality reasserts itself, that correlation doesn't just shrink, it likely reverses to negative. Real, direct consumption-blindness admission: the average American consumes roughly 150 times the physical goods and services of the average human two centuries ago, entirely invisible in daily experience. Real, direct closing pair of listener stories, offered without forcing a single lesson: a Lebanese Mahamudra meditation teacher whose ancient olive farm was bombed, now urgently advising her own peers worldwide to adopt a grounding practice; a Bali resident overlooking rice fields, grateful for a local community of elders who don't follow the news and instead share simple work and stories -- held up directly against 'some of my American friends' treating the war as background noise, still gaming market moves or watching basketball brackets. Real, direct honest admission closing the episode: Nate says plainly he doesn't know what to say that would be both honest and make anyone feel better, other than that no one is alone in waking up to the systemic situation -- and names time itself as perhaps the most valuable, fastest-depleting resource, asking listeners directly to reflect on how they're using theirs. Real, direct forward-reference: mentions having presented 'a draft framework for response' the prior week, already revised, with more to come -- almost certainly the earliest real precursor to what becomes Frankly-132's own fully-developed Framework for Action."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference worth flagging: this episode's own real show notes link directly to Frankly-132 ('What to Do as the World Falls Apart'), despite Frankly-132 being recorded and published months later -- confirming the show notes for this episode were updated retroactively after Frankly-132's own release, not that episode numbering reflects a different chronology than recording dates. Real, direct update (2026-09-16): George Soros and his own real reflexivity theory are now built (tgs:Human.GeorgeSoros, tgs:Concept.MarketReflexivity), scoped specifically to the theory directly credited here -- not a full account of his own extensive, separate political and philanthropic activities, which fall outside this episode's own real, direct citation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_134_AWorldAtTheEdgeOfChange a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "What Does \"Winning\" Even Mean?: A World at the Edge of Change"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct Part 3 in Nate's 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' series (Part 4 is Frankly-146), recorded during the real, unfolding 2026 Iran war. Real, direct central three-part question: could the US and Israel actually lose this war, would tactical nuclear weapons be used against Iran's underground sites if not, and how does the US even define winning in the first place. Widens into a real, direct proposal that the global economic Superorganism might 'calve' into two competing East/West organisms, and closes on a real, direct contrast between individual/community responses to energy disruption (simplify, downsize, connect) and national responses (drill more, hoard, project more force)."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/134-a-world-at-the-edge-of-change> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-134-Transcript-A-World-at-the-Edge-of-Change.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_146_UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimesPart4, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_127_WideBoundaryNews,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening barrage of real, unanswered practical questions -- well-damage from shut-in production, desalination risk to roughly 100 million people, insurance exposure at 'AIG 2008' scale -- before Nate names the episode's own real, central three-part question: could the US and Israel actually lose this war (citing real, direct evidence: an E-3 AWACS radar destroyed, interceptor missiles running low, Iran being 4x Iraq's size and population with fundamentally different drone technology than 2003); if not, would tactical nuclear weapons be used against Iran's deep underground sites; and if a nuclear taboo unbroken since 1945 were broken, how does the US even define 'winning' in the first place, given the White House, Pentagon, Wall Street, and deployed soldiers' families would each answer that differently. Real, direct trust-depletion argument: Oman had reportedly brokered a deal before bombing started anyway, and the US had already withdrawn from the JCPOA and struck Iran twice during active negotiations -- Nate's own real, direct question, borrowing a real Russian diplomatic term, is at what point the US becomes 'agreement incapable' in the eyes of the rest of the world, concluding 'trust is depleting faster than oil.' Real, direct central metaphor, extending Nate's own real economic-Superorganism framework: could this crisis cause the Superorganism to 'calve' into two competing East/West organisms, citing real, direct evidence already visible -- Russia feeding Iran satellite targeting data, China restricting fertilizer exports to stockpile domestically, Iran running a de facto two-tier Hormuz passage system, and real SWIFT alternatives being built. Real, direct climate irony raised directly: might the Iran war function as an accidental carbon tax through demand destruction, or trigger a carbon spike as nations revert to coal, or drive Haiti-style deforestation in poorer nations squeezed out of fertilizer and fuel markets. Real, direct 'Four Horsemen of the 2020s' framework named explicitly (see tgs:Concept.FourHorsemenOfThe2020s), illustrated with a real, direct example of hidden complexity risk: a single black-paint shortage after the Fukushima earthquake shut down an entire Ford manufacturing plant -- Nate's own real, direct question is what the Hormuz crisis's own version of 'black paint' might turn out to be. Real, direct closing framing: this is either a dress rehearsal or the real thing, and either way asks the reader directly what they're doing today that they weren't doing a month ago -- with a real, direct, honest observed asymmetry: individuals and communities facing energy disruption tend to simplify and connect, while nations facing the same disruption tend to drill more, hoard, and project more force, and Nate poses directly which of those two opposite responses will end up shaping the other more."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation, now fully resolved: this is Part 3 of 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times,' with Part 4 confirmed as Frankly-146 (already built, cross-referencing this same episode). Not yet minted as a formal thinkr:Series individual -- tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes is used directly above as the expected real IRI, but Parts 1 and 2 remain unconfirmed; the Series individual itself should be built once those are found, following the same pattern as tgs:Series.OilTrilogy and tgs:Series.BendNotBreak. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: John Michael Greer (Collapse Now and Avoid the Rush, directly credited by name and title) has no existing entry in this graph -- a genuine candidate for a future Human/Work build."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Untold Energy Story: What You Actually Need to Know About Oil"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming the real, urgent occasion for this episode: with his team's own longer, professionally-produced 'Reality 101' series still in production, the real, unfolding Strait of Hormuz crisis prompted him to rush-produce this three-part video series on oil in three days. The first installment covers real, foundational concepts — what oil physically is, the real, direct work it performs, and why most people remain 'energy blind' to it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/135-oil-101> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Interview.TGS_235_RobertPape ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uRIxJ4B3fNQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-135-Transcript-Oil-101.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_77_SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly reframes oil's own real origin story away from the popular 'dinosaur goo' image: it formed from ancient marine phytoplankton compressed over geologic time, a real 'solar battery that took millions of years to charge' and that humanity is draining in centuries. He's real and direct with the precise, quantified math: one barrel of oil contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours of real work potential, equivalent to about 5 years of average human labor once combined with machines, at a real, current market cost of roughly $100 — an economic subsidy he calls 'indistinguishable from magic on human timescales.' Scaling this up, he's direct that roughly 100 billion barrels of oil-equivalent fossil hydrocarbons are consumed globally each year, equivalent to a real 500 billion human-labor-equivalents working alongside an actual 5 billion human workforce — a ratio of 100 fossil 'ghost workers' for every living one. He's real and direct about his own personal, professional experience of energy blindness: his three real, Nobel-Prize-winning graduate economics professors at the University of Chicago never once mentioned energy as a contributor to economic productivity, since the ample supply throughout their own careers let them treat a genuinely exceptional period of history as though it were simply normal. Closes on a real, direct, personal metabolic comparison: the human body requires roughly 2,000 kilocalories daily, while the average American's real, total energy footprint — heating, transport, food, supply chains — runs to roughly 200,000 kilocalories daily, a hundred times the body's own actual need, almost entirely invisible in daily experience."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct first installment of a genuine three-part trilogy, recorded back-to-back with Frankly_136 and Frankly_137 (both already built) and released on consecutive days. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (EIA data, Reality Blind citations, real-time Strait of Hormuz news coverage) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Built for Cheap Energy: What Happens When the Oil Stops Flowing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy, extending the first video's own foundational framing into how modern institutions, food systems, and global supply chains are structurally calibrated to cheap, abundant energy — and what genuinely breaks when that assumption fails."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/136-oil-201> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_kEwDNlNwF0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-136-Transcript-Oil-201.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne, tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the food system's own real energy dependence in precise figures: roughly 10 calories of fossil hydrocarbon input go into every single calorie of food reaching a plate, and roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today carries a real, direct chemical signature traceable to the Haber-Bosch industrial process — a single process that alone enables Earth to feed roughly 4 of its 8 billion people. He's real and direct that swapping to electric vehicles addresses only a real, narrow slice of oil's total function: only about 40% of a barrel becomes gasoline, with the remainder feeding roughly 6,000 other real, everyday products, from asphalt to medicines to synthetic clothing. A real, pointed corrective closes his own treatment of corporate blame: only around 12% of global oil reserves belong to publicly traded companies like ExxonMobil or Shell, with the other 88% held by national oil companies — meaning oil is genuinely 'a story of nations and geology, not corporations.' He's real and direct in distinguishing energy from power: organisms and economies alike compete on power (the rate of usable energy delivery), not raw energy quantity, which is why oil's own real, direct combination of density, portability, and storability cannot simply be substituted by intermittent wind and solar without also solving for reliable, on-demand delivery. Closes on real, direct historical correction: contrary to the popular 'energy transition' narrative, humanity has never actually transitioned off a prior energy source, only ever added new ones on top — directly invoking Jevons paradox (efficiency gains expanding total consumption, not shrinking it) as the real, underlying mechanical reason why."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 (already built) for the real, shared production context. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (USDA dairy data, Exxon depletion figures, USGS critical-minerals data) rather than pointing to further individuals; TGS_101 (Art Berman, already built) is directly cross-referenced via its own real 'slurping sound' framing, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Everything is About to Change: The World After Cheap Energy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct third and final installment of the Oil trilogy, framing the entire series through the Carbon Pulse: a one-time inheritance of ancient stored sunlight — real, direct source of the 'fossil sunlight' term — being burned through in a few hundred years, with direct, explicit implications for money, governance, ecology, and human psychology on its downslope."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/137-oil-301> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.OilTrilogy ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Jv09GZ4lmE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-137-Transcript-Oil-301.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names crude oil and gas as 'fossil sunlight' — ancient solar energy captured by long-dead phytoplankton, concentrated by geologic time — closing the trilogy's own real, central metaphor. He's real and direct that money is a claim on physical work, not a substitute for it, arguing the entire growth-based financial system, its debt, interest, and repayment structures, is predicated on an assumption of ever-more affordable energy that the Carbon Pulse's own real shape does not actually support going forward. A real, direct historical grounding anchors his own warning about deforestation risk: citing Easter Island, the 19th-century US Eastern Seaboard, and Greece after its 2009 financial crisis as real, concrete precedents for what happens when populations lose access to their primary energy source and turn to wood instead. He's real and direct that the energy crisis and the ecological crisis are not two separate problems but 'two faces of the same predicament' — the Carbon Pulse simultaneously created the ecological damage from burning fossil fuels and the depletion crisis from extracting them too quickly. A real, pointed geopolitical observation closes his own systemic analysis: nations are now choosing sides based on energy access rather than ideology, directly citing China's real, long-term LNG and oil contracts with Qatar, Iran, and Russia as concrete evidence the post-WWII global order, itself built on cheap oil tied to American power, is now genuinely in question. Closes on real, direct personal conviction rather than pure systems analysis: naming connection, purpose, community, and service as 'ancient satisfactions' that predate the Carbon Pulse by hundreds of thousands of years and require no barrel of oil at all, framing the Great Simplification as a real, potential 'species growing up' rather than necessarily a collapse, and previewing a real, direct future series called 'Staying Human.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct source of the already-confirmed 'fossil sunlight' citation for tgs:Concept.CrudeOil's own alternate-term entry. Real, direct third installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 and Frankly_136 (both already built) for the real, shared production context and Strait of Hormuz occasion. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (real-time 2026 Reuters/UNCTAD coverage of the Hormuz crisis, OPEC data) rather than pointing to further individuals; Frankly_75 ('Somebody's Gonna Win'), directly cited, is not yet built."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 1): Changing the Future Starts with How You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Opens the series by naming a pattern Hagens says he hears repeatedly from the audience: people who have already settled on one storyline about the future and filter everything else through it. Introduces Scenario Thinking as the alternative, names 3 reasons holding multiple futures at once is hard in practice (the nervous system wants resolution, careers/identities attach to particular futures, cultural incentives reward confident stories over honest uncertainty), and closes by introducing Shortfall Risk, which the episode's own description states will act as connective tissue across the rest of the series."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 1 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/138-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-1> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures, tgs:Interview.TGS_236_ZekeHausfather ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/vgq_6GIHmCA> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-138-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-1.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future> ;
    thinkr:discusses
        tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:Concept.Wetware,
        tgs:Concept.TerrorManagementTheory,
        tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk,
        tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking,
        tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament,
        tgs:Concept.NarrativeAsActiveInference,
        tgs:Concept.Rebuildables,
        tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking,
        tgs:Concept.SingleStoryThinking,
        tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures,
        tgs:Concept.ProtectTheIrreversible,
        tgs:Concept.RobustActions ;
    dct:references
        tgs:Organization.TheOilDrum,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower,
        tgs:Work.ComplexityAndPostmodernism,
        tgs:Work.GlobalTippingPointsReport2025,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_199_SheldonSolomon,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Why the series: every episode on one topic draws predictable comments -- why a nuclear expert when nuclear is too late, why plastics when the economy will roll over anyway, why renewables when they cannot replace fossil fuels, why climate when it is not a top-ten risk -- each from someone who has settled on one flavor of the future and frames everything against it (tgs:Concept.SingleStoryThinking). Nate understands the impulse and notices it in himself: a single storyline saves cognitive effort. But 'I don't know what future is coming. And neither do the commenters.' His own base case: the global economy is likely to hit a wall relatively soon, with a meaningful chance one of the next two recessions becomes a depression involving chaos and reform in developed nations' debt and currency markets -- held with humility, since he could be wrong and, even if right about direction, the same wall could lead to managed simplification, authoritarian consolidation, chaos or 'a hundred different things in between.' Holding all this is 'constitutionally and emotionally challenging'; scenario thinking, alongside his Staying Human practices, is how he stays functional, and is why he hosts guests on nuclear, plastics, AI, renewables, governance and mental health -- each is relevant to some plausible future. The series was announced as four parts (aerial view; four 2x2 grids -- economic direction, power and distribution, geopolitics, earth systems; composite worlds; shortfall risk and robust responses); it grew to six. Coupled systems: problems are not separate buckets; as the late-February military decision over Hormuz showed, energy affects geopolitics, then supply chains, inflation, legitimacy, policy and ecological stress, which loops back to energy, food and water -- a cascade toward an energy crisis, fertilizer shortage, food-security threat, legitimacy challenges and a higher chance of a financial and currency reset. Lessons from his Oil Drum days: people tracking climate, AI, energy or debt see real data but different slices of a coupled system. Complex systems move in fits and starts ('decades happen within weeks'), show path dependence, and cross thresholds we usually cannot see until crossed, which is why linear extrapolation blindsides analysts; the better question is not the most likely outcome but which regimes a system could shift into and what would cause each shift. Forecasting works for narrow, short-term, well-understood systems, not for 'the most complex, most coupled, most surprise-prone system we have ever tried to think about'; a scenario is 'a coherent mini world' in which to ask what would be scarce, abundant, rewarded, punished, recoverable, and how daily life would feel. The shift he asks for: from trying to be right about the future to being prepared for several versions and contributing to the better ones. Why it is hard: nervous systems want certainty, since holding possibilities open is metabolically expensive; careers and identities attach to narratives (green growth, techno-optimism, peak oil, collapse awareness, colonizing Mars), so people 'defend their futures much the same way they defend their families'; and institutions fund confident stories. Religion as an older single story everyone around you held, said with respect (citing Iain McGilchrist as a guest he admires); deep stories -- progress as the natural shape of history, humans separate from nature, more stuff is better -- shape what people think possible more than data. Shortfall risk, from his Salomon Brothers days managing portfolios (tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk): the biggest danger is rarely the average outcome but something essential dropping below a threshold and not coming back -- food-system reliability, grid stability, clean water, social trust, governmental capacity, topsoil, pollinators, the nuclear taboo, democratic institutions -- which creates an asymmetry: money and comfort can be recovered, these cannot. The series' aims: expand perception to hold several worlds at once and be harder to surprise, identify what is coupled, identify irreversible losses and thresholds, and build robustness that transfers across futures, 'like a portfolio management tool.' He likens getting these Franklys out to one of his ducks laying an egg. Closing: people tell him they feel overwhelmed, caught between 'everything will be fine because technology' and 'everything's going to collapse,' both of which lead people to check out; scenario thinking is an antidote -- 'I don't know which future is coming, but I can prepare and contribute to a range of them,' starting locally, today."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "REBUILT 2026-08-13 from Frankly_138's own official show-notes page (thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/138-...) — a real, structured, WordPress-generated per-episode topic index Hagens' team maintains, discovered this session as a much stronger extraction source than transcript-reading or generic NER. Every thinkr:discusses/dct:references link above traces to a specific labeled entry in that page, not inferred. Fixed 2 real bugs from this stub's first draft: thinkr:hasCanonicalSource was used twice (its own scopeNote documents cardinality-one; the essay link now correctly uses the general dct:source instead) and was typed as an xsd:anyURI string literal rather than a proper linked resource (thinkr:hasCanonicalSource is declared owl:ObjectProperty, not a datatype property — every other use of it in this graph uses a bare IRI). ENRICHED 2026-10-13 from the transcript: added discussion summary, updatedDate, four discussed concepts (one newly minted: SingleStoryThinking; ProtectTheIrreversible and RobustActions were minted from Part 6 and are previewed here), six further dct:references (TGS_199 Sheldon Solomon on terror management theory, the Oil 101-301 Franklys 135-137, the Great Simplification film series, Reality Blind), and the missing dct:isReferencedBy back-links on six of the seven references that already existed. Series note: Nate announced four parts here; the series ran to six, with shortfall risk and robust responses becoming Part 6 (Frankly_151)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 2): Four Variables Shaping the Coming Decades"@en ;
    skos:definition "2ND PASS 2026-08-13, using the full real transcript (previously built from show notes only). This is the episode that formally introduces the complete 4-Dimension/16-Facet scenario framework this graph's own ScenarioDimension/ScenarioFacet structure is built from — Nate treats his long-standing 4-scenario Economic grid (Green Growth, Mordor, The Great Simplification, Mad Max) as the foundation, then layers three more grids on top: Power and Distribution (concentration of decision-making authority vs. where material gains flow), Geopolitical (cooperation vs. adversarial relations, crossed with interdependence vs. self-sufficiency), and Earth Systems (climate stress vs. biosphere integrity, explicitly from an already-compromised 2026 baseline, not a neutral one). Technology and demographics are both explicitly considered and deliberately NOT given their own grids — Nate argues both act as modifiers/amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving, rather than independent dimensions."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-04-20"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/139-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-2> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures, tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-139-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/5R0oUGPf9C4> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-how-to-think-about-the-future-92e> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.GreenGrowth, tgs:ScenarioFacet.Mordor, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, tgs:ScenarioFacet.MadMax,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.CivicIdeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheStewardshipDeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ACapturedDemocracy, tgs:ScenarioFacet.ForcedFeudalism,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGlobalizationIdeal, tgs:ScenarioFacet.FriendlyNeighborsWithGoodFences, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGeopoliticalDangerZone, tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheColdWarScenario,
        tgs:ScenarioFacet.StrainedButWorkable, tgs:ScenarioFacet.QuietUnraveling, tgs:ScenarioFacet.HothouseTriage, tgs:ScenarioFacet.CascadingBreakdown ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielSchmachtenberger, tgs:Series.BendNotBreak, tgs:Interview.TGS_12_DennisMeadows, tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution ;
    skos:scopeNote "2ND PASS 2026-08-13 — MJSullivan flagged this as 'the key episode that describes these quadrants' given the axis-correction work done this session (see ScenarioDimension.Economic and ScenarioDimension.Geopolitical's own scopeNotes), and supplied the full transcript for a proper enrichment pass rather than relying on show notes alone. discusses list corrected from the original single tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification (now split into Concept + ScenarioFacet — see that split's own history) to the specific tgs:ScenarioFacet.TheGreatSimplification, and substantially expanded: this episode is the real primary source for all 16 ScenarioFacets across all 4 Dimensions, not just the original 4 Economic ones it had been scoped to before this pass. tgs:Concept.Superorganism added as a real, direct reference — Hagens' own words describing the Cold War Facet as 'the fission of the Superorganism.' Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: 'Fire apes' (a real recurring Reality Blind term, cited directly to a specific page); the real statistic that 26 people hold the same financial claims on biophysical reality as the bottom 4 billion humans (sourced to a real Oxfam report); Concept.DarkTriad already built elsewhere in this graph is referenced again here but not formally dct:referenced in this pass, to avoid overloading a single episode's reference list without a clear stopping point. hasCanonicalSource fixed 2026-08-13 (original 1st-pass note, still accurate) — same xsd:anyURI-literal-instead-of-proper-resource bug originally found and fixed on Frankly_138, never applied to the rest of this series until now."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely foundational episode for this graph's own scenario-matrix structure, not just another Frankly. Nate opens by directly naming a cognitive trap — settling on one preferred future and then judging everything else against it — and argues for holding a real distribution of possible futures instead. The four-grid structure is built up methodically and explicitly incompletely: Nate states outright that technology and demographics were both considered for their own separate grids and deliberately excluded, arguing both function as amplifiers of whatever direction the underlying system is already moving rather than independent axes — AI specifically is framed as already inside the Earth Systems grid, since data-center electricity demand is itself a direct new draw on the energy/biophysical system the whole framework is built around. A real, distinctive moment of self-aware epistemic humility: Nate explicitly invites the audience to propose grids he hasn't considered, naming mental/physical health and freedom of information/communication as real candidates he chose not to include. The Geopolitical grid's own real-time grounding is notable — recorded during the actual 2026 Iran war and Strait of Hormuz closure, Nate uses that live, unfolding event as a direct real-world example of the 'adversarial and interdependent' quadrant, citing the Philippines' energy emergency, Bangladesh's school closures, and Japan's emergency oil-reserve release as concurrent real evidence. Nate draws a real, pointed distinction between what a contraction-vs-growth headline means for the 'fairness' question (during growth) versus the 'is there enough' question (during contraction) — the same quadrant axes, he argues, describe a fundamentally different lived experience depending on which economic direction they're paired with. The episode closes by explicitly refusing to let any single quadrant stand as a full future on its own — Nate states directly that nobody will 'live in The Great Simplification in general,' only in a Great Simplification combined with a specific power structure, geopolitical backdrop, and ecological condition, previewing Part 3's work building concrete scenario composites from these four layers."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Perspective From Lebanon: Who Will We Be When Things Get Hard?"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-30 from a stub, using a complete real transcript and show notes. An unusually personal Frankly, prompted by a real conversation with a close friend of Nate's — his own meditation coach — who lives in Beirut and has been living through daily bombing, hosting displaced families in her small apartment, and grieving friends killed in the conflict. She is unnamed throughout, both in the original episode and here, and has explicitly declined to appear on the show herself; her privacy is respected in this build accordingly — no attempt is made to identify her. Nate uses her account, and her own real, striking equanimity despite the danger she's in, to distill the show's broader work down to three real, deliberately simpler and more personal questions: who are we going to be once comfort and convenience thin out; how are we going to actually live under a coming biophysical constraint; and what are we willing to expend our own life's energy to protect. Real, direct correction to the raw transcript's own phonetic error: 'Bio Ocumalafe' is Bayo Akomolafe, confirmed directly via this episode's own show notes ('A Slower Urgency')."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/140-a-perspective-from-lebanon> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pILjdgXeh_o> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-140-Transcript-A-Perspective-From-Lebanon.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Interview.TGS_138_FritjofCapra, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_88_SnowSingularityRocksInTheRiver, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Persona.BayoAkomolafe, tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with a real, direct disclosure of exhaustion — four years of twice-weekly content, described in his own words as 'shedding my skin, perhaps shedding multiple skins' — before explaining the real conversation that prompted this pause: his friend's account of constant, un-actionable fight-or-flight (nowhere to flee to, no one to fight), and her own real, striking equanimity despite it, grounded in a weekly community meditation practice (now around 60 people, notably including both Muslim and Jewish participants) and a real, centuries-old lineage of mutual trust in her home village. A real, specific human cost is named directly: journalist Amal Khalil, known for helping injured animals amid the bombing, among a real, cited total of 260 journalists killed across Gaza, Lebanon, Yemen, and Iran. Nate is candid about the real discomfort of his own position — 'a woman in Beirut is coaching a guy in Minnesota' whose own country is not indirectly connected to the violence she's living through — without resolving or dismissing that tension. The episode's real emotional turn comes as Nate applies his friend's grounded state as a direct contrast to his own: sitting safely in a comfortable office, watching the news, he recognizes his own nervous system generating a real fight-or-flight response to no actual physical danger, while she — facing genuine danger — remains calmer. Closes without prescriptions, deliberately: 'I don't have any pithy ending or recommendations,' offering instead small, concrete grounding practices (going outside, calling someone loved, noticing a meadowlark identified on the Merlin app) as how nervous systems 'remember it's safe enough to think and act clearly again.' Ends on a real, candid admission of his own next direction: after '20 years' of what he calls 'biophysical macro,' his real, stated next curiosity is 'biophysical micro' — who people are and might become individually — explicitly naming himself 'a newbie' on this front."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Friend's identity deliberately not researched or asserted, consistent with her own real, explicit refusal to appear on the show — respecting a stated privacy preference for a private individual in an active, dangerous situation, not a gap in available information. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #106 ('10 Things Worth More Than a Pound of Gold'), directly cross-referenced — now built, see that episode's own entry. Bayo Akomolafe (real, directly cited philosopher/author) is now a real, built Persona in this graph, added 2026-08-30 alongside Frankly_106's own build — dct:references updated accordingly above. Real, direct confirmation, tracked down 2026-08-30: this episode's own show notes cite a genuinely distinct, real Pew piece — 'In 25-Country Survey, Americans Especially Likely To View Fellow Citizens as Morally Bad' (Mar 2026, now minted as tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026) — not the same as the May 2025 general-trust piece already tracked in the WWNS candidate list (docs/wwns-topic-coverage-tracker.md, tgs-digest). This one is genuinely sharper: the US is the only country of 25 surveyed where more adults rate compatriots' morality as bad than good, driven by a real, direct partisan mechanism rather than general moral strictness — worth considering as a real, stronger replacement or complement to the existing candidate #12 entry."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_141_WideBoundaryNews a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Wide Boundary News: Sacrificing Wilderness, Oil Data Propaganda, and Feeding the Superorganism's Brain"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly works through six real, separate current news items — US oil production data, the Iran/Strait of Hormuz crisis, fertilizer and cooking-fuel cascades, Minnesota's Boundary Waters mining decision, AI data center expansion, and a record-setting dairy cow — widening each one several orders of magnitude to surface a real, single underlying pattern: nearly every institution and narrative currently in use was calibrated for the carbon pulse's upslope, with no real plan for its downslope or plateau."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/141-wide-boundary-news-5-8-26> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=g-BJ9XkP2Pc> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-141-Transcript-WBN-5_8_26.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Human.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly dissects a real, current US Department of Energy claim that US oil production exceeds Saudi Arabia and Russia combined, showing this is technically accurate only because the US figure bundles crude oil with natural gas liquids, biofuels, and refinery gain, while the Saudi and Russian figures are reported on a real, narrower crude-only basis — a direct, concrete demonstration of how a headline claim can be entirely true and still structurally misleading through category selection alone. He's real and direct that the US remains a net importer of actual crude oil despite widely-repeated claims of being a net oil exporter, since the real export surplus is almost entirely natural gas liquids used for things like plastic packaging, not gasoline or diesel. A real, direct cascading-consequences chain traces the Strait of Hormuz closure from oil price spikes down through fertilizer shortages (roughly 3 million tons monthly of blocked urea, ammonia, and phosphate) to a real, human-scale image: households in Mumbai lining up at 3am for LPG cooking-gas cylinders as families in Kenya revert from clean gas back to charcoal after a decade of progress. He directly uses a real historical parallel, post-Soviet Russia's own environmental collapse once central regulation weakened in the 1990s, to challenge the naive assumption that a shrinking economy automatically produces less pollution — resource extraction continued regardless, just without oversight. A real, direct physiological analogy anchors his AI/data-center concern: human brains consume roughly 20% of the body's metabolic energy despite being only 2% of its mass, offered as a direct parallel for the Superorganism now diverting outsized energy toward its own 'cognitive layer,' with Nate directly, honestly admitting nobody yet knows whether that allocation is justified. Closes on a deliberately small, real, concrete story most viewers wouldn't have otherwise seen: a real, record-setting dairy cow producing ten times her 1925 counterpart's milk yield, offered directly as 'the carbon pulse made into flesh' and a synecdoche for everything selectively bred or engineered for an energy regime now ending."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct methodological note: this episode was studied specifically for technique to fold into the WWNS series' own methodology — see the separate write-up accompanying this build for the concrete insights extracted. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, current institutional and news reporting (DOE materials, EIA data, Holstein Association records) rather than pointing to further individuals — Art Berman is already built and directly cross-referenced, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 2): Navigating Dread and Carrying the Weight of Tomorrow"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT OUT 2026-08-24 from a stub, using the full real transcript and official show notes. Opens on Art Berman's own framing of Operation Epic Fury as 'the biggest blunder in human history' (TGS_220), then turns inward: Nate names what he's actually feeling as dread, and draws on climate psychiatrist Lise Van Susteren's term 'pre-traumatic stress' (the body responding to a trauma that hasn't happened yet). Walks through the neuroscience in real depth — amygdala/HPA-axis/cortisol machinery evolved for immediate physical threats, chronically activated but never discharged by slow-moving abstract risk, producing allostatic load that measurably impairs the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex. Cites a real study finding ~28% of subjects are 'extreme dreaders' who'd rather take a stronger shock immediately than wait for a milder one — direct evidence dread itself can cost more than the feared event. Explicitly connects back to Part 1: 'agency is the opposite stance' to dread, since a meaningful action gives the threat system 'a different signal.' Closes with five practical pathways (mental reframing, somatic/breath practice, agency, community/co-regulation, and what Nate calls 'befriending the darkness') and five concrete steps for when dread arises, ending on a direct forward-pointer to Part 3's topic: the movement from dread into presence, and the default mode network."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 2 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/142-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-2> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-142-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-2.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4gJ9E233hfw>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/4lvZfG1sdQW7vnGZs8zc01> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-9e6> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.Dread,
        tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysThroughDread, tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad, tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork,
        tgs:Concept.Overshoot, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyReturnOnInvestment ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman, tgs:Work.MansSearchForMeaning, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury,
        tgs:Human.LiseVanSusteren, tgs:Human.ViktorFrankl, tgs:Human.PeterLevine, tgs:Human.StephenLevine, tgs:Human.DanielSiegel,
        tgs:Human.FrancisWeller, tgs:Human.JoannaMacy ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening: Art Berman had just called the US-Israel war with Iran 'the biggest blunder in human history,' and Nate names what he is feeling as dread -- already paid for not in money or supply shortages but in scattered attention, poor sleep and 'this kind of low-grade but continual tightness in the middle of my chest.' 'Dread is like a tax we pay on a future that we cannot escape,' and the dread may often be worse than the event. His synthesis -- global heating, net energy decline, the end of material growth, the fraying social contract, soft feudalism on the horizon, biodiversity loss -- is 'roughly correct and more correct each year,' which makes it a millstone for him and many listeners. Lise Van Susteren (his childhood neighbor) coined 'pre-traumatic stress': the trauma has not happened but the body responds as if it has, because the amygdala cannot tell whether the fire is two months or twenty years away. If the work only gives people more reasons to be afraid, it will have failed; the other half is how to live with the knowledge 'without being eaten by it.' He is releasing the Guide to Staying Human faster than planned because of events. The science: the amygdala triggers the HPA axis, cortisol floods the blood, and within a quarter second the body is ready to fight or flee -- a gift against bears, but the system cannot tell a charging bear from the EROI cliff, a coming super El Nino or distant wars, and with no fighting or running the cortisol never clears: allostatic load (tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad), which impairs the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex, dysregulates sleep and immunity, and pushes people toward addictive behaviors that silence the alarm. 'The cruelest irony about dread': it shrinks the capacity needed to face what is dreaded. In an fMRI study offering stronger shocks sooner or milder ones later, about 28% chose more pain sooner -- 'extreme dreaders' -- and later work found some would accept almost any extra shock to escape anticipation. Many listeners are 'not necessarily extreme dreaders by temperament. We are extreme dreaders by information,' with a wait of years and a shock 'nebulous at the edges and ginormous in its scale.' Flavors: useful vigilance (winterize the house), rumination (circling that feels like work), and real dread, where the future collapses into the present, often invisibly to friends and family. Five pathways (tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysThroughDread): (1) reframing -- not denying the data but choosing among true meanings: 'we are doomed' freezes you, 'we are on a historical precipice... and I am alive to witness and participate in this' situates you; Viktor Frankl's insight from Man's Search for Meaning; (2) the body -- long exhales, the vagus nerve, cold water on the face (which he does before every podcast), walking outdoors; Peter Levine's somatic work on discharging an incomplete fight-or-flight response; 'one grounded nervous system at a time'; (3) agency -- dread is 'being acted upon by a future that you cannot influence,' and a real action within reach (planting this year's potatoes, a relationship with a neighbor, voicing this Frankly) gives the threat system a different signal; his coach reminds him he cannot brute-force a calmer nervous system; (4) community -- 'dread that you hold alone is much heavier,' a trusted presence dampens the amygdala, nervous systems co-regulate, and 'lonely dread... is the worst kind'; (5) befriending the darkness -- for those with baseline stability (professional help for anyone in acute crisis): across Buddhism, Stoicism, mysticism, Francis Weller and the recently passed Joanna Macy, 'the way through dread is not around it, it is into it'; fully met, dread transforms not into hope, which is still anticipation, but into presence; Stephen Levine noted 'how alive the dying frequently are.' Dread can become a teacher: 'this conversation, this plate of nachos, these dogs and ducks running across the yard... this is the gift.' Five steps when dread arises: name it out loud (Daniel Siegel's 'name it to tame it'); locate it in the body; take five long exhales; ask what one thing within reach you can do today, then do it; find one person to sit with this week, not to fix anything but to be with. His own band of brothers and sisters in a systems ecology group has carried him for twenty years. 'The point of seeing clearly is not for us to suffer in advance'; dread is 'reverse gravity that pulls us out of the present moment,' and metabolizing it makes presence possible -- the subject of Part 3, on presence and the default mode network."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Enriched 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes. Added: discussion summary, updatedDate, seven discussed concepts (two newly minted: FivePathwaysThroughDread, AllostaticLoad), fourteen further dct:references, and back-links for all seventeen (including the three that existed before, which had none); completed dct:isReferencedBy (Frankly_147 and Frankly_153 also cite this episode). The Persona candidates the 2026-08-24 build deferred are now built: Viktor Frankl, Peter Levine, Stephen Levine and Daniel Siegel as new Human/Persona pairs, a Persona for Lise Van Susteren (whose Human already existed), and Man's Search for Meaning as a Work; TGS #202 is now built and referenced. Provenance note: the five people the TGS_202 upgrade of 2026-10-10 removed as false citations (Frankl, Peter Levine, Stephen Levine, Van Susteren, Weller) are exactly this episode's citations; the earlier TGS_202 build had evidently picked them up from this episode, whose show notes link TGS #202 for Joanna Macy's Work That Reconnects. Show notes include a 988 Lifeline reference at the point where Nate advises professional support for anyone in acute crisis, and a one-page 'A Practice for Dread' guide image."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Guide to Staying Human (Part 3): The Default Mode Network and the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "BUILT 2026-08-24 from a complete real transcript and official show notes — no prior stub existed. Opens on a real personal anecdote (missing a dawn birdsong and a full cup of coffee while mentally simulating 2026 diesel-rationing scenarios) to introduce the default mode network (DMN) — the brain system active during self-referential processing, theory of mind, mind-wandering, and mental time travel, anti-correlated with the task-positive network (TPN). Cites a real Harvard study ('A Wandering Mind is an Unhappy Mind') and the real canonical DMN discovery paper, and explicitly points back to this graph's own tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie as the only prior TGS coverage of the DMN. Names 5 specific real reasons metacrisis-aware people are especially vulnerable to chronic DMN dominance (emotional loading, no resolution signal, social isolation, vocational necessity of simulation, and a moral license that frames presence as a kind of abandonment), and explicitly links this episode back to Part 2: 'the same phenomenon I called full dread in last week's Frankly, but viewed from a slightly different angle' — coining a new term, 'future capture,' for the DMN-dominance experience when the content is civilizational. Closes with five pathways back to presence (the senses, the pause, single-tasking, beauty, and the finitude of things) and three concrete daily practices."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/143-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-3> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_144_AWordICantSeemToUnderstand ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-143-Transcript-A-Guide-to-Staying-Human-Part-3.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fZB6Ep8qSos>, <https://open.spotify.com/episode/3DzB8zatmpgek0Lfzkz67M> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-a-guide-to-staying-human-part-e54> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.Dread, tgs:Concept.Presence, tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork, tgs:Concept.FutureCapture,
        tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysToPresence, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain, tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Persona.IainMcGilchrist,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2, tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.AWanderingMindIsAnUnhappyMind,
        tgs:Work.ADefaultModeOfBrainFunction, tgs:Work.SacramentOfThePresentMoment, tgs:Human.ThichNhatHanh ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening: at 5:30 one morning, coffee in hand at the window, Nate 'came back' five minutes later to find he had seen none of the gray-pink dawn; he had been in late 2026 imagining diesel rationing from refinery constraints, and the Baltimore oriole that comes for the oranges he puts out had come and gone ten feet away. Twenty years of modeling futures has trained his mind to run scenarios 'while you're eating, while you're on a hike, while you're watching the dawn light,' so the only place life occurs becomes somewhere visited briefly between simulations. The science: a Harvard experience-sampling study (about a quarter million moments via a phone app) found minds wandering 47% of waking life and people less happy when they did, even unpleasant tasks feeling better when fully attended -- 'A Wandering Mind Is an Unhappy Mind.' A canonical paper from three decades of research named the default mode network (DMN), which handles self-referential processing, theory of mind, spontaneous thought and mental time travel; the hippocampus that stores memories also builds hypothetical futures, and people with hippocampal damage cannot imagine them. Mental time travel is one of our species' great achievements -- planning harvests, preparing for childbirth, building cathedrals -- so the DMN 'is not a bug.' The problem is chronic dominance: attention should flow between the DMN and the task-positive network, which are anti-correlated, but phones, the news cycle, the attention economy and the weight of metacrisis awareness pin it on the DMN, with measurable costs (less hippocampal grey matter, more depression and anxiety, diminished sensory processing -- 'the world literally looks duller'). Everyday signs: arriving with no memory of the drive, reading a page without registering it, shower thoughts (the familiar sensory environment frees the DMN, like his bike rides), rehearsing a reply instead of listening, and years feeling shorter because routines run on the DMN, which lays down thinner memories than novel experience. Five reasons metacrisis-aware people are especially captured: the content is emotionally loaded with grief, fear and outrage; the simulation never resolves, so it never gets a closure signal; it is socially isolating, adding simulations of how to talk to a brother, partner or children; the vocation requires running simulations; and a moral dimension licenses absence -- being fully present at a birthday party 'while the world burns' can feel obscene, so absence becomes a sign of caring. 'But I've learned absence is not care. Absence is absence,' and children remember that a parent was not there, not that the parent was thinking important thoughts. This is 'an inversion of reality': the modeled future feels solid and the actual present feels thin. Three kinds of leaving: healthy mental time travel that returns (his knee surgeon visualizing the operation, farmers planning around fertilizer up 50%), drift (scrolling, being at dinner and not at dinner), and future capture (tgs:Concept.FutureCapture), where the present becomes 'a kind of waiting room' -- the same phenomenon as last week's full dread from another angle: 'Dread is what it feels like. Future capture is what it does to our attention.' He has lived in future capture for decades. Five pathways (tgs:Concept.FivePathwaysToPresence): the senses (Thich Nhat Hanh: drink the tea, wash the dish, walk the path); the pause before the reach for the phone, in 'our supernormal, unbelievable modern era'; single-tasking, since multitasking is 'one of the great lies of late stage industrial era'; beauty, which Iain McGilchrist says the nervous system is built to be arrested by -- the oriole, a moving branch, a loved face, his dog Frank looking at him 'like I'm his entire universe' -- 'the announcements are the work'; and the gift of finitude, a sharper awareness that species, forests, institutions and the carbon pulse are contingent and that this oriole and this cup of coffee 'have never happened before, and they will not happen again,' which, held wrongly, drives dread and, held differently, drives presence. The Christian contemplative 'sacrament of the present moment' (Jean-Pierre de Caussade): to be elsewhere is to refuse the gift. 'The future is what we are trying to protect, but the present is the only place we can protect it from.' Practices: don't scold yourself for leaving, since the coming back is the practice; choose one daily encounter to be fully present for; receive each thing as if it were the last. Closing: that morning he heard some thirty bird species on a misty porch; the dawn light and the oriole and its babies will still be there, and the coffee 'is hot and dark and bitter and good'; to miss it 'because we're imagining and watching for its end is the deepest kind of loss.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote """Enriched 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes: added discussion summary, updatedDate, three discussed concepts (one newly minted: FivePathwaysToPresence), nine further dct:references (TGS_217, McGilchrist's recent episode on beauty; Frankly_142; the Guide to Staying Human and film series; Reality Blind; Thich Nhat Hanh; and three newly minted Works: Killingsworth and Gilbert's 'A Wandering Mind Is an Unhappy Mind', Raichle et al.'s 'A Default Mode of Brain Function', and de Caussade's Sacrament of the Present Moment, with a new Human/Persona for de Caussade and a Persona for Thich Nhat Hanh), back-links on all thirteen references, and dct:isReferencedBy from Frankly_144. Supersedes the 2026-08-24 note that tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli was not yet built: it is built, and the show notes link Reality Blind's supernormal-stimuli chapter at the point where Nate says 'our supernormal, unbelievable modern era', so it is now listed as discussed.
        Task Positive Network (TPN) is real and load-bearing to this episode's own DMN/TPN anti-correlation framing but kept out as a separate Concept, folded into Concept.DefaultModeNetwork's own definition instead, matching this graph's 'don't over-split a paired mechanism' discipline.
        Iain McGilchrist and his 2 prior TGS appearances (#85, #165) already existed in this graph — linked, not rebuilt; his third, TGS_217, is now referenced too."""@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_144_AWordICantSeemToUnderstand a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Word I Can't Seem to Understand: Non-Duality and Our Living World"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unusually honest personal admission: despite asking at least seven prior podcast guests to define non-duality (see tgs:Concept.NonDuality), most recently Andrew Holecek the same week, Nate says he still cannot define it. Real, direct central insight, arrived at on a bike ride: the difficulty may not be personal inadequacy but the particular Western cultural operating system itself -- reinforced by grammar, institutions, and the very act of definition-by-boundary -- which makes a concept with no outside structurally impossible to fence in. Closes not with a definition but with the possibility that loosening one's grip on certainty is itself the actual, available work."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/144-a-word-i-cant-seem-to-understand> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Frankly-144-Transcript-Nonduality.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.NonDuality, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_221_AndrewHolecek, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_88_RobertSapolsky,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening admission: after at least seven real, direct attempts across podcast guests to get a working definition of non-duality, Nate names a real, different explanation that occurred to him mid-bike-ride -- not personal inadequacy (insufficient intelligence, ADHD, insufficient spiritual advancement, all named directly as prior self-explanations he's now questioning), but a real, structural feature of the culture he was raised in. Real, direct grammar argument: a simple sentence like 'I see the tree' encodes a watcher, an act of watching, and a thing watched -- Western grammar itself is called 'a little machine that generates separation,' making it structurally the wrong tool to point at something with 'no cracks or seams... for us to scrutinize.' Real, direct reframe of the whole exercise: asking for a definition was itself the wrong move from the start, since defining something means drawing a boundary around it, and the thing wisdom traditions point at 'has no outside' -- directly invoking the real, classic finger-pointing-at-the-moon teaching image to explain why traditional teachers point rather than define, and why Nate kept mistaking the finger for the moon across seven real conversations. Real, direct list of moments where the separation briefly softens, explicitly not offered as proof or definition: being lost in music (the default mode network 'just goes quiet'), the half-second before a beautiful thing gets labeled by the mind, grief following a real, recent pet loss that 'hollows you out' past the sense of a self running the show, and standing in a forest at dusk where it becomes genuinely hard to say where one stops and the ecosystem begins. Real, direct connection to the metacrisis, delivered as the episode's own real payoff: the same felt separateness that makes non-duality hard to grasp intellectually is the same root assumption that lets humanity treat nature as 'a backdrop, a resource, a pile of stuff' rather than the living system a person's own body is materially made from -- 'we've built an entire civilization on the assumption that the world is a warehouse and we're the customers... there is no safe distance, there never was.' Real, direct honest closing admission: the drive to nail down a clean, filed-away definition may itself be 'the most Western thing' about the difficulty -- the grasping itself is the wall -- and the real, available gift is not final understanding but a loosened grip on certainty, 'a hairline crack' letting a bit more light through than the day before."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note preserved from the source yaml: this episode's own '_resolution' section rates its own URL match as 'weak' confidence, since the site's real slug doesn't closely resemble the uploaded filename -- the show's own team's own hedge, not something independently discovered here. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: David Abram's 'The Spell of the Sensuous' (cited directly for the language-and-grammar argument) has no existing Work entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 3): Uphill Futures in a Downhill World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Introduces a landscape metaphor (borrowed from developmental biology's Waddington landscape and lake-ecology succession) for thinking about civilizational futures: valleys (stable states systems settle into), ridges (the barriers/cost of transitioning between them), switchbacks (deliberate human effort — trust-building, institution-building — that makes an otherwise-too-steep transition possible), and erosion (neglect or active harm that makes a transition permanently harder). Central claim: the easiest-to-reach futures are not the same as the most desirable ones — desirable futures generally require sustained uphill effort to both reach and remain in, while undesirable ones tend to be the default downhill path requiring no coordinated effort at all."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 3 ;
    dct:created "2026-05-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/145-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-3> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-145-Transcript-HTTATF-Pt-3.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/vTBQ1AS-KVw> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-uphill-futures-in-a-downhill> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TheSenecaEffect, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk,
        tgs:Concept.UphillAndDownhillFutures, tgs:Concept.EpigeneticLandscape, tgs:Concept.AlternativeStableStates,
        tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking, tgs:Concept.ComplexSystemsThinking ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables,
        tgs:Work.TheStrategyOfTheGenes, tgs:Work.TheSenecaEffect, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.GlobalTippingPointsReport2025,
        tgs:Persona.ConradWaddington, tgs:Persona.UgoBardi, tgs:Persona.ElinorOstrom ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate opens by explaining why the series keeps growing: we are approaching 'a really serious juncture' economically and culturally, and he wants more people able to visualize and discuss the unknowns, steering toward life and continuity and away from dystopia, some of which is 'probably now baked in.' He recaps Part 1 (the future as a landscape of possibilities, coupled systems, phase shifts, shortfall risk) and Part 2 (four grids: economic direction, power distribution, geopolitics, Earth systems, with technology as an amplifier of whatever system it sits inside), and says Part 4 will combine the grids into composite worlds. Before that he adds one more tool, because the composites will not sit side by side with equal likelihood; they sit in a landscape whose shape decides which futures we reach by doing nothing and what it takes to climb toward the ones we want. The biology: in the 1950s Conrad Waddington pictured cell development as a ball rolling down branching valleys. A stem cell starts with many possibilities, and at each fork some close off until it is committed to being, say, a heart cell, never to return without extraordinary intervention. Valleys differ in depth, and the ridges between them differ in height, so some transitions are easy and others need enormous energy. Nate maps this onto civilization, with two corrections: we are not choosing from a menu where every future is equally available, and we are not at the top of the hills but already rolling through a landscape whose topography is shifting. Most futures are downhill, meaning the path of least resistance that current momentum and incentives already favor; uphill futures take sustained effort to reach and sustained effort to stay in. Where you stand matters: a farmer in Malawi and a software engineer in San Francisco occupy different parts of the terrain, so the episode is about shared systems, not one location. The lake example: lakes age naturally from clear to murky to wetland, with plants, fish and bottom feeders regulating the pace (with an aside that Minnesota, the land of 10,000 lakes, also has 10,000 caves). Regenerative techniques can extend a lake's life but take intention, planning and energy; nutrient runoff from fertilizer, sewage or phosphorus can instead flip it through algal blooms into a murky, algae-dominated state, a new valley held in place by a self-perpetuating loop. The key point is asymmetry: flipping a clear lake takes a few years of loading, while flipping it back, even after the runoff stops, can take decades because the whole web of plants, oxygen, fish and nutrient cycles has to be rebuilt; the system now has to climb a ridge that did not exist before. The same pattern in societies: in a crisis (war, economic collapse, fuel shortages and empty shelves from military action overseas), fear lets a leader consolidate power, surveillance expands and opposition is suppressed. That slide can take decades, as in the slow erosion of the Roman Empire, or months, as in Peru in the 1990s and Tunisia more recently; it is a 'behavioral downhill roll' following Part 2's logic that contraction plus fear pulls power toward consolidation. Reversing it needs independent courts, a free press, purged institutional knowledge, atrophied civic habits and, above all, trust, 'arguably our most valuable resource,' and takes a generation or more, if it happens at all. Nate ties this asymmetry to his friend Ugo Bardi's Seneca effect (slow growth, sudden collapse) and calls it a feature of the landscape, not bad luck: high-trust, coordinated systems such as democracy need constant energy to maintain, while degraded, low-trust, coercive systems are 'in a thermodynamic sense, the lower energy state,' where things roll when the effort stops. The practical point, stressed twice: the easiest futures are not the most desirable, and the most desirable futures for humanity and the biosphere take sustained effort to reach and to remain in; stop climbing and social and ecological gravity pull us back down. The landscape is also moving: energy descent, climate change and biodiversity loss are reshaping the valleys as we pass through them, so a future that was uphill in the 1990s may now be downhill, or the reverse. Today, economic contraction plus adversarial geopolitics plus concentrated power is the downhill path, needing no conspiracies or villains, just frightened people and leaders responding to fear as they have for millennia with consolidation, control, surveillance and walls. Contraction with cooperative geopolitics and shared power is possible but uphill: it asks people to trust each other exactly when trust is hardest, leaders to share power when incentives favor consolidating, and communities to coordinate when coordination is most expensive. Uphill is not impossible, which Nate calls the core of The Great Simplification's work: every living cell succeeds at an uphill project, salmon swim against the current to spawn, and redwoods lift water hundreds of feet. The metaphor says the climb has costs, and that stopping short of a stable resting place means sliding back, at every scale from personal health and relationships to national governance and the biosphere, all of which persist only because someone keeps putting in energy, attention and care. He leaves listeners with questions before Part 4: where are the steep valleys and the open ground, which pathways feel uphill and which downhill, where do you actually stand, and do the futures you want match the ones the physics and data suggest are likely? The composites are not equally accessible, stable or reversible, and 'the landscape... is tilted.' He closes with four terms to carry through the series and the channel's work: valleys (stable states that reinforce themselves), ridges (the cost of moving between them), both given by physics, ecology and history; switchbacks (physical or social technology that carves passable routes up otherwise too-steep ridges: building social trust, restoring depleted soil, creating cooperative institutions); and erosion (actions or plain neglect that let those paths disappear: depleting fossil aquifers or soil, losing civic norms, building surveillance infrastructure that cannot be dismantled), which, if fast, becomes washouts that need not be intentional, 'gravity and weather do the rest.' The final note: each of us is also switchbacking or eroding our own future paths every day."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates/subject/replay/essay added, transcript URL corrected (real URL uses a different month folder AND retains a .docx.pdf double-extension — the pattern-matched guess used across this whole series turned out inconsistent with the real site for parts 3/5/6, confirmed only via direct page fetches, not assumed). 2026-10-13: enriched from the official show notes and transcript. Show-notes quirks recorded rather than corrected: the 'Scenario planning' entry links back to Part 2 (Frankly_139) rather than a scenario-planning source; the 'Trees pulling up water against gravity' link duplicates the URL of the Ostrom design-principles article used for 'Tips for community coordination,' so it does not point at a tree-hydraulics source; and Peru (1990s) and Tunisia are given only as one-line examples, linked to WOLA and Brookings pieces not modeled here. Persona.ElinorOstrom is referenced only through that show-notes link (a revised version of her commons design principles); she is not named in the spoken episode. The Substack essay (dct:source) was not consulted for this pass; per house practice it wins over the spoken transcript on facts if they differ."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_146_UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimesPart4 a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times, Part 4: The Ultimate Alternative -- Are You Okay With Nuclear Warfare?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, impromptu Part 4 in Nate's real 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times' series (Part 3 confirmed as Frankly-134), recorded the same real week a US-Iran ceasefire memorandum was announced and President Trump's own real Truth Social post referenced 'the ultimate alternative' regarding nuclear weapons. Real, direct format: 13 real, deliberately unanswered questions about nuclear weapons, deterrence, and the erosion of the 80-year nuclear taboo -- explicitly not offered as analysis with conclusions, but as Nate's own real, stated form of preparedness and community-building in unsettled times."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimes ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 4 ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/146-uncomfortable-questions-for-unsettled-times-part-4> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-146-Transcript-Uncomfortable-Questions-for-Unstable-Times-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_134_AWorldAtTheEdgeOfChange, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_144_AWordICantSeemToUnderstand,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_11_InSpiteOf, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: Nate names this as an impromptu, unwanted episode -- 'I do not wanna do this, frankly' -- prompted directly by a US-Iran 60-day ceasefire memorandum and President Trump's own real Truth Social post referencing radioactive 'nuclear dust' and 'the ultimate alternative, hopefully never to be used again.' Real, direct format stated up front: 13 real questions, deliberately not offered with answers, framed as a form of preparedness and, when discussed with others, a form of community. Real, direct callback to Part 3 (Frankly-134, recorded April 2nd): revisits that episode's own real question about tactical nuclear use against Iran's underground sites, noting two and a half months on, 'that question has gotten more uncomfortable, not less,' since most off-ramps available in April had since closed. Real, direct core argument: nuclear coercion, if the current deal is read as a foreign-policy win, has just been demonstrated to work against a non-nuclear state -- 'the opposite of what eighty years of non-proliferation was supposed to teach.' Real, direct historical evidence cited directly: Libya gave up its nuclear program in 2003 and Gaddafi was overthrown eight years later; North Korea kept its program and remains 'largely undisturbed' -- Nate's own real, direct, uncomfortable question: what does the world look like once a dozen states draw the practical lesson that keeping nuclear weapons gets you respected. Real, direct 'spite' framing, explicitly revisiting and updating Frankly-11's own earlier real concept: nuclear weapons possibly functioning as an instrument of resource spite between superpowers, given the Persian Gulf and Iran's South Pars gas field represent real, direct strategic energy assets independent of the nuclear narrative. Real, direct game-theoretic argument: a single tactical nuclear use, if the target doesn't immediately capitulate, forces an escalate-or-be-exposed choice where every incentive favors escalation -- directly questioning whether 'one and done' is a coherent strategic concept or a phrase that only makes the first use psychologically possible. Real, direct generational argument: the 80-year taboo may rest less on treaties than on 'lived terror' (duck-and-cover drills, The Day After, Hibakusha survivors touring schools) that was never transmitted to the generation now holding power -- directly asking what that means for the people with their fingers on the buttons today. Real, direct, honest closing self-doubt: Nate directly asks himself whether raising these questions publicly, for an already anxious and polarized audience, is net helpful or net harmful, without resolving the question. Real, direct final, most uncomfortable question of all, explicitly named as such: not whether Iran has nuclear weapons, but whether the entire episode has demonstrated that 'the USA is not as strong a superpower as it believes.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct series confirmation: this episode's own real show notes directly link 'Uncomfortable Questions for Unsettled Times #3' to Frankly-134 ('A World at the Edge of Change') -- resolving the open question this stub's own prior scopeNote had flagged (unconfirmed Parts 1-3). Frankly-134 itself remains a real, minimal stub in this graph; this same real, direct series link should be added there too as a forward-reference once it gets its own full build. Not minted as a formal thinkr:Series individual in this pass, since only Parts 3 and 4 are confirmed so far -- worth doing once Parts 1-2 (if they exist) are also confirmed. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Seymour Hersh (the journalist directly credited for reporting the nuclear-option story) has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Play 5D Chess: It's Not What You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct chess-board metaphor (see tgs:Concept.FiveLensChess) for five successively widening vantage points from which the same real situation can be viewed -- the instinctive friend-or-threat read, tribal identification, a dispassionate aerial view of structural forces, the living world the whole board rests on, and a second, different game board visible in the distance. Real, direct central claim: the first two lenses are evolution's fastest, most-reinforced 'factory settings' and never disappear, but the actual skill needed now is learning to hold all five at once -- and holding only the wide lenses without real agency just produces despair at higher resolution."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-19"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/147-how-to-play-5d-chess> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-147-Transcript-5D-Chess.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FiveLensChess, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Interview.TGS_112_SamanthaSweetwater,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_158_JohnVervaeke, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2,
        tgs:Persona.NoraBateson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening pattern named: a headline, a clip, or a half-recognized guest name triggers a real, felt verdict before two sentences are even heard -- Nate directly includes himself in this, noting it happens 'more recently than I used to.' Real, direct lens-by-lens structure: lens one, the piece directly across, where the nervous system asks only 'friend or threat,' evolution's oldest, fastest software, resolved via proxies (do they look, sound, or share values like me) often before a full sentence lands -- illustrated with renewable energy, AI, and the Israel/Gaza conflict as real, direct examples of a topic itself tripping the wire independent of who's speaking. Real, direct forward-reference to a real, scheduled future guest, Lisa Feldman Barrett (September), cited for her real theory that the brain predicts and judges before conscious reasoning catches up. Lens two: the piece's whole team comes into view, and the real question shifts from 'do I trust this person' to 'is this person one of us' -- once a piece reads as the other team, its words stop landing as information and start reading as a game move. Lens three, a real, direct aerial view: sides are visible but not as good/bad, only as structural forces (energy, debt, geopolitics) moving pieces -- Nate's own real, direct, honest admission that describing the current power structure dispassionately from this height is routinely misheard, from lens one or two, as tacit endorsement of it; a direct nod to Nora Bateson's own real perspective is raised here. Lens four: the whole board rests on something living -- climate, biodiversity, 7 of 9 planetary boundaries already breached, roughly ten million species -- named directly as 'home base' for much of the show's own audience. Lens five, the widest: the board and the living world sit in a landscape, with a second, different, simpler game board visible in the distance, what Nate calls 'the long repair,' and the real question shifts from 'who's the villain here' to 'how do we get from this board to that one without flipping the table over.' Real, direct closing synthesis: lenses one and two are real, permanent factory settings that never go away and shouldn't be dropped, but the actual skill of this moment is learning to hold all five at once, then coming back down to actually act -- with a real, direct, honest caveat that holding only the wide lenses without any attached agency just produces despair at a higher resolution. Real, direct personal closing admission: the hardest part of hosting a multi-issue platform isn't the analysis itself, which Nate says he loves, but showing a large, diverse audience the whole board without any one person feeling like a piece being pushed across it -- described directly as 'quite Sisyphean most of the time.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference, not yet buildable: Lisa Feldman Barrett is named as a real, scheduled future guest (September), not yet a real Persona in this graph since she hasn't appeared on the show as of this episode -- a genuine, direct candidate for a future TGS episode build once that conversation airs, not a citation to resolve now. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Susanne Cook-Greuter's real ego-development theory (cited directly for a related, complementary account of seeing from wider lenses) has no existing entry in this graph."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Mordor to the Long Repair: How Might Daily Life Feel in the Next Decades? | How to Think About the Future Part 4"@en ;
    skos:definition "The framework-defining episode of this series: proposes 4 Dimensions (grids) - Economic, Power & Distribution, Geopolitical, Earth Systems - each with 4 Facet options, for 256 mathematically possible composite futures, and builds out 4 representative named examples in detail (The Long Repair, Mordor Persists, Fortress World, The Unraveling - modeled as thinkr:Scenario individuals in scenarios.ttl). Explicit that these 4 are chosen to stretch thinking, not presented as an exhaustive or equally-likely set, and that the real future will likely be some regional patchwork of all of them rather than any single one holding globally."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 4 ;
    dct:created "2026-06-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-06-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/148-how-to-think-about-the-future-part-4> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Frankly-148-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-4.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/KlkAeQ5xdeQ> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/mordor-to-the-long-repair-how-might> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk, tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Scenario.GS-CI-FN-SW, tgs:Scenario.MO-CD-GD-QU, tgs:Scenario.GS-FF-CW-HT, tgs:Scenario.MM-FF-CW-CB,
        tgs:Concept.HealingVsDying, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence, tgs:Concept.ComplexityCollapse,
        tgs:Concept.PerpetualMulligans, tgs:Concept.LimitsToGrowth, tgs:Concept.Overshoot ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_140_APerspectiveFromLebanon, tgs:Interview.TGS_222_BrettKencairn, tgs:Interview.TGS_178_AndrewMillison,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheCollapseOfComplexSocieties, tgs:Work.BullshitJobs ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Four grids with four quadrants each give 256 combinations; Nate builds four representative worlds, with three caveats. They are not equally likely: energy limits, depletion, debt and overshoot pull toward contraction ('The Great Simplification is an economic simplification'), so there is no green-growth world, since growth and healing the biosphere 'pull against each other too hard' at global scale, and on the Earth system the best realistic case is 'strained but workable.' The grids interact: scarcity and a hostile outside world push governments to consolidate power, which people accept out of fear; steady growth needs cooperative geopolitics for just-in-time supply chains, as the Iran war shows 'in real-time slow motion'; and severe Earth-system stress burns through budgets, institutions, patience and hopeful collective visions. And listeners should build their own composites -- 'it's the logic and the thought process that I'm after.' (1) The Long Repair, the aspirational path: contraction with ecological stewardship, broadly shared power and gains, cooperative geopolitics with regional self-sufficiency, a strained but workable biosphere, and technology that makes people more capable (repair culture, regenerative tech, open designs). Daily life: less energy, stuff, travel and choice, more local and seasonal food, but richer social life as neighbors matter practically again; competence becomes a higher status signal than credentials; more physical work, maintenance, food-growing and care, with more respect for it, and fewer of David Graeber's 'bullshit jobs.' It already exists in tiny form -- transition towns, regional food networks, community land trusts, energy co-ops, maker spaces, repair cafes -- islands of coherence whose open question is whether they connect. Not a utopia (people grieve lost futures), but, as his Lebanese friend described, with community and the sense that 'a smaller future is far from an empty one.' Hardest to reach and easiest to lose: it runs on legitimacy and trust, which dissipate the moment people decide pain is not shared fairly. (2) Mordor Persists (first called 'More Mordor'): growth on paper while digging deeper into overshoot -- 'spending down the principal instead of living off the interest' -- with captured democracy, the geopolitical danger zone, a quiet unraveling of the Earth system, and consumption-and-engagement technology that is slick but dependency-creating. For the top 10-20% in rich nations life is busy, comfortable and anxious; for the bottom half to 70% it is precarious; for both, a sense of unreality as official numbers say everything is fine, breeding cynicism, conspiracy thinking and apathy. In the Global South -- cobalt mines, garment factories, imported plastic waste, forests cleared for soy and pasture -- Mordor 'is not a future scenario. It's the last thirty years or fifty years.' It breaks through brittle interdependence (one choke point, like Hormuz, cascades through a system optimized for efficiency with no slack) and the slow ecological unraveling that pulls the rug from under growth -- 'the home team Earth bats last'; its real danger is burning through what a graceful transition would need, then falling apart 'fast and ugly.' (3) Fortress World: contraction with forced feudalism (elites managing decline for themselves), Cold War blocs, hothouse triage, and controlling technology -- surveillance and rationing in state hands; he promises a dedicated Frankly and sees the pattern in current AI and robotics policy proposals. Daily life like The Hunger Games: rationed, watched, grim order, hard borders, regions and people written off; resignation, low-grade fear and hypervigilance hardening into emotional flatness. Fragile because control is expensive -- an energy and complexity problem ('just ask the Romans, or Joseph Tainter') -- forcing a choice between spending to control people and spending to provide for them, and because broken trust does not return within the regime that broke it. (4) The Unraveling: Mad Max economy, local strongmen, fracturing blocs fighting over resources (hopefully without nuclear exchange), cascading Earth-system breakdown, and technology winding down to the simple and robust; where the others slide when they lose their footing. Days organize around immediate needs (water, working fields, fuel), information moves at the speed of conversation, national institutions feel like fables, health slips without pharmaceutical supply chains; but people still bond, love, raise kids, tell stories and see fireflies. Its worst feature is the absence of physical buffers -- 'cheap fossil energy gave us endless do-overs, and in this world, they are gone.' Side by side: three worlds share economic contraction yet feel utterly different; power and distribution matter most to how life feels; the Earth system constrains everything else. Stable is not the same as good: Mordor is stable 'the way cancer is stable,' and markets, earnings and GDP 'are basically measuring blood flow to a tumor and calling the patient healthy'; the Long Repair reads as decline by every current yardstick and triggers cultural dread because our nervous systems cannot tell 'the tumor is shrinking because the patient is dying' from '...because the patient is healing' (tgs:Concept.HealingVsDying) -- possibly 'one of the deepest challenges in the entire more than human predicament.' The real future will be a patchwork; we are in a transition zone, the ridges are steep but perhaps not yet too steep, and every year of soil loss, weaker governance, added surveillance and lost trust steepens them. 'Your zip code may shape your future more than your country, and your community may shape it more than your zip code.' None of us steers the game board, but at the scale of households, communities and the land we touch, agency is real -- 'the one place left where the wheel is still connected to something.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates/subject/replay/essay added, transcript URL corrected (real is 2026/06, not the 2026/04 pattern-matched guess), dct:references added (Tainter -> real guest episode #27, closing a gap flagged weeks ago in the original backlog; #222/#178 resolved to real guest names via the show notes own URL slugs, not left as bare numbers). ENRICHED 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes: prefLabel set to the official title; added discussion summary, updatedDate, eight further discussed concepts (one newly minted: HealingVsDying), seven further dct:references (Parts 1-3, the Great Simplification film series, Reality Blind, and two newly minted Works: The Collapse of Complex Societies and Bullshit Jobs), and the missing dct:isReferencedBy back-links on the four references that already existed. Date note: the show-notes metadata field gives 26 June 2026 as the publication date, but the page's own date line says 25 June 2026; dct:issued is left at 25 June. Forward pointer: he promises a dedicated Frankly on Fortress World; not yet identified in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_149_ALegacyWorthCelebrating a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Good morning. Today's piece is a wide boundary look at the United States on its 250th birthday. And right now, with the political temperature where it is, both domestically and internationally, I suspect, I know, a reflection like this one, is likely to trip a lot of people's gut reactions about the moral status of the United States. In fact, some of you may already be sorting me into for America or against her. So here's my ask with this pre-intro intro. As you listen to this video, please suspend those beliefs for just a moment and situate this conversation about one of the world's current superpowers in the broader context of our cultural future and our shared more than human predicament. I am sure there will be at least a few statements in this video that you disagree with, but I hope the larger conversation that I'm trying to start, this holiday weekend, but these past four years carries through, and that you also see the value in this despite any tensions that might arise. I love this country, and I love the wider world, and that is exactly why I'm willing to look at it honestly. Here's the Frankly."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "A Legacy Worth Celebrating? Reflecting on 250 Years of the American Experiment"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, wide-boundary reflection on the USA's 250th birthday, framed through a real, direct central metaphor: 250 as the age a person, or a nation, might finally have to ask not how big they got, but whether they grew up. Real, direct central argument: American abundance and the 'self-made' national mythology are, underneath the story, mostly a product of the Carbon Pulse and a one-time geological inheritance, not primarily freedom or genius -- a real, direct move from human slaves to what Nate calls 'fossil ones.' Closes on real, genuine national pride alongside the critique (US wildlife decline roughly half the global rate, Yellowstone as the world's first national park) and a real, direct, hopeful reframing of what a 'grown-up' national greatness could still look like."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-06-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/149-a-legacy-worth-celebrating> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-149-Transcript-Happy-250th-Birthday-USA.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound, tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct central metaphor, delivered early: 250 is the age a person, or a nation, stops measuring a birthday by 'how big did we get' and starts having to ask 'did we grow up' -- explicitly contrasted with China's 5,000 years and Rome's 1,000, calling the US 'a toddler who just learned to walk and is confident he invented it.' Real, direct energy-blindness argument: the grill, the fireworks, the truck that brought the burgers, even the cake 'if you follow it all the way back,' are all real, direct products of hydrocarbons -- a real, direct claim that American abundance came primarily from 'sitting on the largest natural inheritance any human peoples ever stumbled onto,' not primarily from freedom, work ethic, or genius. Real, direct historical reframe: a single barrel of oil holds roughly 4.5 years of real human labor, meaning the US has effectively had 'hundreds of billions of invisible workers' -- Nate's own real, direct, deliberately provocative line: 'over our 250 years, we went from real human slaves to fossil ones.' Real, direct 'trust fund, not a paycheck' framing of GDP and national debt (approaching $40 trillion, roughly $300K per household) as a real, direct claim on future energy and living systems that Nate argues explicitly will not be honored, since the cheap energy to pay it won't be there. Real, direct manifest-destiny critique: westward expansion, then suburbs, then credit, then the internet, then AI, then Mars, named directly as the same real pattern of 'always one more frontier,' letting the country avoid ever facing real limits. Real, direct wildlife framing: global vertebrate populations down roughly 73% since 1970, described as the fastest die-off since the extinction that took out the dinosaurs -- with a real, direct, honest counterpoint that North America's own decline (about 39%) is roughly half the global rate, credited partly to genuine national choice (Yellowstone as the world's first national park, US ranking highly in protected land). Real, direct closing vision of a 'grown-up' national greatness: valuing wisdom over cleverness specifically ahead of handing decision-making to AI, running more on reciprocity than transaction, and finding the next American greatness in neighborhoods and towns relearning to grow food and rely on neighbors rather than in Washington, D.C. -- explicitly named as 'a far greater dream than our founding one was.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct honest note from the show's own real, structured metadata file: its own '_resolution' section flags its own URL match as 'weak' confidence, since the site's real slug ('149-a-legacy-worth-celebrating') doesn't closely resemble the uploaded filename -- the show's own team's real, direct self-flagged uncertainty, not something this build independently discovered. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: the Haudenosaunee Seventh Generation principle and the Canandaigua Treaty are both real, direct, substantive citations without existing entries in this graph -- a genuine candidate for a future Concept or Organization build given how centrally the seven-generations framing supports this episode's own real 'did we grow up' argument."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Foundations Before Outcomes: The Future Beneath Your Feet | How to Think About the Future Pt 5"@en ;
    skos:definition "Applies the same landscape/valleys/ridges framework from Part 3, at the scale of an individual person's life rather than civilizational futures - originally intended as the series finale (no-regret responses to the 4 composite worlds from Part 4) before Hagens split that material off into Part 6, once he realized a personal/individual-scale version of the same macro dynamics was worth its own episode."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 5 ;
    dct:created "2026-07-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/150-foundations-before-outcomes> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-150-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-5.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/YvJ4HkWIsL8> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-foundations-before-outcomes> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.PerpetualMulligans, tgs:Scenario.GS-CI-FN-SW, tgs:Scenario.MO-CD-GD-QU, tgs:Scenario.GS-FF-CW-HT, tgs:Scenario.MM-FF-CW-CB,
        tgs:Concept.PersonalCompositeFutures, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.RedQueenEffect ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_31_ReflectionsOnBeyondGrowth,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:Series.AGuideToStayingHuman ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening: Nate has fifteen Franklys in progress, including 'What Is Debt and Why Does It Matter,' 'Seven Ways Growth Ends' and fifteen 'unordinary' recommendations for the metacrisis, but wants to finish this series first. He had planned Part 5 as no-regret responses to the composite worlds; on a bike ride he realized the macro frameworks have a rhyming micro version, so he moved responses to Part 6 and turns the lens inward (tgs:Concept.PersonalCompositeFutures). Two things we navigate. Throughput: is your material throughput and complexity expanding or simplifying, and is it building you up or wearing you down? Expansion can be regenerative if it grows capability, relationships and meaning, or extractive if the career grows while time with kids, dogs and the outdoors, health and relational depth shrink; simplifying can be regenerative (dropping draining obligations, sleeping better) or plain decline when it is not chosen. Agency: the four sources of power from Part 2 -- military, political, financial, technological -- map roughly to biology, social expectations, financial obligations, and the platforms structuring attention; the employer owns the daylight hours, the mortgage the nightly anxieties, social commitments the weekends, 'and then our phone owns all the gaps in between.' 'Captured democracy' at personal scale is a life 'full of freedoms' that functions like a trap; broad agency is a life whose time reflects its values. Two foundations underneath, slower and less responsive to choice (childhood, genetics, trauma, luck): the body and nervous system, 'your personal earth system,' which 'bats last' -- sleep debt, regulation, running on 'caffeine and cortisol and compulsions'; intact, it gives buffers and optionality, depleted, any setback cascades, and no good decisions about throughput and agency make up for it. And the relational web, 'akin to your personal geopolitics' -- not followers but the people who would show up at two in the morning; without it, even perfect simplification is 'building on sand.' The four personal composites: personal Long Repair (deliberately simplifying, real agency, intact body, strong web -- hardest to reach and easiest to lose, but the only genuinely regenerative trajectory); personal Mordor (expanding throughput, captured agency, slowly depleting body, surface relationships -- 'the cultural scoreboard says everything is fine. Everything is not fine'), which he says has been his own path for much of the first half of 2026, as recovery from knee surgery collided with the Hormuz crisis, before a two-week break; personal Fortress (simplified and prepared but alone, an illusion of control, 'rich in financial capital, poor in all the others,' survivable but not flourishing and expensive to maintain); and personal Unraveling (several areas failing at once -- the end of cheap energy's 'perpetual mulligans' at personal scale, each setback arriving before recovery from the last, like the Red Queen running to stay in place). The civilizational feedbacks rhyme: stress plus scarcity pulls toward personal fortress as scarcity plus fear pulls societies toward authoritarian consolidation; it is easier to slide from Long Repair to Fortress under stress than to climb from Mordor to Long Repair, because the climb means admitting 'I think I'm measuring the wrong things' -- Part 4's confusion of healing with dying, at the scale of one life. Bottom line: most people try to change their lives through throughput and agency questions, but 'skipping your foundations is like building economic policy on top of a degrading biosphere.' Foundation questions: am I sleeping and resting enough, mostly calm in my body, known and cared for, attuned to my limits, spending time in green, wild places? The work is unglamorous -- sleep, walks, cooking, sitting with someone without an agenda, potatoes for a neighbor (he expects ten bushels after weeding, mulching and picking off potato beetles), the phone down at 7 p.m. -- 'personal switchbacking' that shows month to month and year to year rather than week to week, and is a prerequisite for the cultural work of the long repair. Questions: which personal composite are you actually living in day to day, and are you switchbacking or eroding at the level of your foundations? He will 'never be some self-help guy on the internet,' but thinks those who change the initial conditions of their own futures will most help change society's."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "MAJOR CORRECTION 2026-08-13: this episode's skos:prefLabel and thinkr:hasCanonicalSource were both WRONG since first built — a plausible-looking, pattern-matched title/URL that was never actually verified against the real page (the guessed URL 404s). Corrected via direct fetch of the real page. Same lesson as several other corrections this session: a plausible guess is not the same claim as a verified fact, even when it fits an established naming convention. ENRICHED 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes: added updatedDate, companion essay (dct:source), discussion summary, four further discussed concepts (one newly minted: PersonalCompositeFutures), and seven further dct:references with matching back-links (Frankly_3 for perpetual mulligans, Frankly_31 for measuring the wrong things, Parts 2-4 of the series, RR_20 on attachment and loneliness, and the Guide to Staying Human series). Forward pointer, not asserted as a reference: one of the fifteen Franklys he says are in progress, the 'unordinary recommendations for the metacrisis,' became tgs:Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions (7 August 2026). The show notes also link the Substack essay 'From Mordor to the Long Repair' for the four composite worlds and a TGS video timestamp for the four sources of power; neither is built as a separate entity."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Protect the Irreversible: What Matters Most in an Uncertain Future? | How to Think About the Future Pt 6"@en ;
    skos:definition "The series' closer - a methodology for individual/community planning and action given the 4-Dimension framework, rather than a specific list of recommended actions. Opens with a type-1/type-2-error framing (false positives vs. false negatives in how people respond to uncertain threats) as a lens for thinking about failure modes in planning, before laying out Hagens' own approach to prioritizing action under deep uncertainty."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:isPartOf tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture ;
    thinkr:seriesPosition 6 ;
    dct:created "2026-07-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/151-protect-the-irreversible> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-151-Transcript-How-to-Think-About-the-Future-Part-6.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/bMfzVhTWMDM> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking, tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Scenario.GS-CI-FN-SW, tgs:Scenario.MO-CD-GD-QU, tgs:Scenario.GS-FF-CW-HT, tgs:Scenario.MM-FF-CW-CB,
        tgs:Concept.ProtectTheIrreversible, tgs:Concept.RobustActions, tgs:Concept.ShortfallRisk, tgs:Concept.LandscapeOfPossibleFutures,
        tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_139_Part2FourVariables, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_145_Part3UphillFutures,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Recap of Parts 1-5 (the future as a landscape of possibilities; four grids -- economic growth or contraction, power and distribution, geopolitical interconnection, earth-system integrity; the shape of the landscape with valleys, ridges, switchbacks and erosion; four composite worlds -- the Long Repair, More Mordor, Fortress World and the Unraveling; and the personal aside), then the series' original aim: not what to do, but how to think about what to do -- a methodology for finding your own plan, since without one 'even well-intentioned actions can be unhelpful or counterproductive.' Two ways to fail: type I and type II errors, illustrated by a noise in the night (calling the police when it was his cat Tufi, versus rolling over when it really was a burglar). Type II errors are omissions -- not protecting topsoil or biodiversity because GDP does not measure them, not building community resilience, not transitioning energy systems because the current ones still work -- the 'washouts' of cultural erosion from Part 3. Type I errors come in flavors: acting on a partial map (shutting down fossil fuels overnight when 80% of the world and 99.5% of supply chains run on them -- 'the prescription kills the patient'); fighting the wrong war (carbon offsets that do not offset, ESG scores that do not change the Superorganism's behavior -- calling the fire department instead of the police); and the deepest, that nearly every institution, budget, pension fund and agreement assumes a larger future economy, so when contraction comes much of what we invest in becomes 'a ginormous type one error all at once' -- infrastructure built for expansion becomes a maintenance burden, compounding instruments become Wile E. Coyote traps, institutions built to distribute growing surpluses cannot distribute shrinking ones. 'The biggest false positive in human history might turn out to be our current culture's assumption that growth is permanent,' now including AI hyperscaler and energy build-outs. Most risk lies in omission, but the error most likely to turn slow damage into sudden collapse is a type I error: powerful institutions acting with conviction on partial understanding by doubling down on growth; 2008 and 2020, like the US incursion into Venezuela, gave false confidence. The compass: some losses can be rebuilt and some cannot -- $100,000 versus your left arm -- yet our frameworks weigh a dollar of GDP like an inch of topsoil (tgs:Concept.ProtectTheIrreversible). Irreversible or practically irreversible: topsoil (centuries per inch), the Ogallala Aquifer's ice-age water drained in decades, species ('like diamonds, extinctions are forever'), pollinators, social trust, institutional knowledge, the roughly 80-year nuclear taboo, children's developmental windows, and one's own health past a threshold. We optimize quarterly earnings and GDP, which bounce back, while letting the irreversible erode; the inversion is to be willing to lose money to protect topsoil, efficiency to protect ecological foundations, comfort to protect children's development. Robust actions (tgs:Concept.RobustActions) help in almost every composite world: building soil health, food security and ecological resilience, relationships with people within walking distance ('Community ties that exist before a crisis are exponentially more valuable than ones that you try to build during a crisis'), practical skills (food, repair, basic medicine, water, building, teaching), reducing dependence on fragile overseas supply chains, and protecting local watersheds, pollinators and soil biology; 'robustness beats out optimization.' Unicorn strategies (tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies) are the deliberate exception -- focused callings such as ocean conservation (a shout-out to his friend DJ), nuclear disarmament, or preserving an Indigenous language -- because humans 'are not interchangeable planning units'; most people should combine a base of robust actions with one or two focused commitments. From despair to agency: agency is real where you can see its results -- household, garden (his best potato year in ten after committing to weeding, mulching and picking potato beetles), relationships, neighborhood, watershed; slower but real at the scale of bioregion and job; hard to feel at civilizational scale, where trends are the sum of individual choices 'plus some pixie dust of emergence.' 'Constrained futures are very different from determined futures': physics, ecology and the social primate constrain the trajectory, but the difference between the Long Repair and the Unraveling is largely decided by what people do at visible scales. Coda questions: What are you protecting? What are you building? What are you willing to let go of? Where are you switchbacking, and where might you be letting things erode? Is there a unicorn commitment in you? Closes saying he has just thought of a way to close the series, next week."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "MAJOR CORRECTION 2026-08-13: same class of error as Frankly_150 — this episode's skos:prefLabel, thinkr:hasCanonicalSource, and hasTranscript month were all wrong since first built, never verified against the real page until now. Corrected via direct fetch. ENRICHED 2026-10-13 from a combined show-notes-and-transcript file: added discussion summary, six further discussed concepts (two newly minted: ProtectTheIrreversible, RobustActions), and seven further dct:references (the five earlier parts of the series, Reality Blind, and Bottlenecks of the 21st Century, all with matching back-links). Correction made in the same pass: tgs:Concept.UnicornStrategies had been defined as actions robust across all four composite worlds; the transcript says the opposite -- unicorn strategies are deliberately focused rather than hedged, and 'robust actions' is the separate name for hedged moves. The concept was corrected. Possible series follow-on: Nate ends by saying he has thought of a way to close the series 'next week'; the following Frankly was tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen (24 July 2026), not currently recorded as part of tgs:Series.HowToThinkAboutTheFuture -- worth checking whether it presents itself as the closer. Show-notes sources not built: RAND on planning under uncertainty, the Dasgupta Review, the Oxford Smith School on carbon offsets, Harvard Center on the Developing Child on developmental windows, and the scenario-planning book also flagged under tgs:Concept.ScenarioThinking."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Watch With Both Eyes Open: The Beauty and the Horror of Our Moment"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by a week spent with ten friends already fluent in the more-than-human predicament, without needing to explain or perform that fluency -- Nate's own real, central metaphor: real depth perception requires both eyes open at once, the beauty eye and the horror eye, and most rooms he's in have one or the other closed, going flat and two-dimensional as a result. Widens into a real, direct argument that oxytocin-level, physiologically felt community cannot travel through fiber optic cable, and that finding or building local, in-person community fluent in these realities is a genuine no-regrets strategy, even if the work is only on oneself."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-07-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/152-watch-with-both-eyes-open> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-152-Transcript-Watch-With-Both-Eyes-Open.docx-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_54_ReflectionsFromIndia,
        <https://www.hylo.com/groups/the-great-simplification/join/i9jLqPmbGI>,
        <https://www.npr.org/2026/02/18/nx-s1-5716105/8-creative-ways-to-build-your-village-community-according-to-our-listeners> ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: a week spent with ten or eleven friends at a friend's organization working adjacent to the more-than-human predicament, where climate, AI, and energy depletion were almost never discussed directly, yet were fully, mutually understood -- freeing Nate to be, in his own words, 'a stand-up comic' and rediscover a version of himself that had quietly gone missing. Real, direct central metaphor, delivered as the episode's own real thesis: reality depth perception requires both eyes open at once -- the beauty eye and the horror eye -- and most rooms are either 'too hot' (fixated on systemic risk and gory detail) or 'too cold' (world's state unacknowledged entirely); either way, the world goes flat and two-dimensional. Real, direct callback to Frankly-54's own real 'naive about community' admission after six weeks in India with 30 people: the actual benefit of daily in-person contact wasn't practical (borrowing lawnmowers, trading garlic) but physiological -- oxytocin and serotonin from repetitive daily closeness -- and this week served as a real, direct 'booster shot' of a lesson gradually forgotten over two years. Real, direct honest admission: despite a large, valued online community and 20 years of exchange with his own systems-ecology colleagues, Nate names himself as paradoxically, genuinely lonely much of the time, since 'oxytocin in its truest sense doesn't travel through fiber optic cable.' Real, direct distinction drawn carefully: he has family, local friends, and neighbors who'd help in a crisis, but not yet ten people locally, within driving distance, fluent in the more-than-human predicament without needing to constantly discuss it -- a real, direct position he suspects much of the audience shares: 'aware, engaged, and locally alone.' Real, direct closing call to action: finding local community is named as a genuine no-regrets strategy, foundational to the work ahead even when that work is solely personal, with a real, direct acknowledgment that the platform itself feels a responsibility to help and is actively working on it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct note on the show notes' own 'Essay version' link: its real URL slug ('essay-what-cant-be-rebuilt-irreversible') does not match this episode's own real title or content, unlike the confirmed, direct site error already documented on Frankly-156 -- this one is NOT independently confirmed as an error and was not treated as authoritative for cross-checking any fact in this build, per the Substack-precedence rule (gotcha #15), since it may simply be a mislinked essay rather than this episode's real companion piece. Real, direct, practical relevance flagged by MJSullivan directly: the NPR 'building your village' link in this episode's own real show notes is a genuinely appropriate, real, practical resource for the WWNS series' own 'finding the others' material (see wwns-intro-part1.md), given its direct thematic overlap with this episode's own real content on local-community-building."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "When Truth Becomes Hazardous: Navigating Information Through the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct expansion of the 'triple information hazard' Nate first named in Frankly-34, now grown to twelve (see tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards), grounded in Nick Bostrom's real concept of an information hazard -- risk that comes specifically from disseminating true information, not misinformation. Real, direct closing argument: none of the twelve argues for silence, only for care about timing, context, scale, and trust, and the deepest available response to all twelve at once is growing real, direct community and trust at human scale."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-07-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-07-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/153-when-truth-becomes-hazardous> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Frankly-153-Transcript-When-Truth-Becomes-Hazardous.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TwelveInformationHazards, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_34_LimitsToPodcastingAboutLimits, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_51_DJWhite, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_11_TalesFromTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Interview.TGS_11_RexWeyler,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_56_PeakOilAIAndTheStraw, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_23_ThePlasticDetox,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_15_ThePlasticCrisis, tgs:Interview.TGS_89_SianSutherland,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_142_GuideToStayingHumanPart2,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_17_MovingFromApathyToAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_93_InformationBurnout, tgs:Interview.TGS_194_LukeKemp, tgs:Interview.TGS_153_LukeKemp,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_94_SocialOvershoot, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Concept.IslandsOfCoherence ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening definition, Bostrom's own term: an information hazard is a risk from disseminating true information, not lies. Real, direct hazards 1-4, explicitly grouped as stemming from the same fact -- speaking publicly means speaking in front of the very actors and systems one is trying to change. (1) Truth to power lands on the speaker; Nate's own real, half-joking reference to not wanting 'the Eye of Sauron' turned toward rural Minnesota. (2) Inoculation: publishing a strategy against dark-triad power gives that power real, direct advance notice, like announcing the next football play over the stadium loudspeaker. (3) Dual-use: an accurate map of societal fragility serves firefighters and arsonists alike, and Nate's own real, direct concession that after 20-plus years of mapping the human predicament, 'the arsonists... are better funded, more incentivized, and are probably paying closer attention.' (4) AI ingestion, named as genuinely new: every public essay or transcript now becomes real, direct LLM training data, collapsing what used to be a real gap between publishing something and a bad actor acting on it -- Nate's own real, personal response is a decision to keep 10-20% of what he knows or believes for in-person conversations with no AI note-taker present, building what he calls a wall between 'authentic Nate' and 'the brain of the Borg.' Real, direct hazards 5-8: (5) spotlight hazard -- publicizing quiet, effective conservation or finance strategies can kill them, since they work precisely because no one's watching (Nate directly credits colleague DJ White for examples that only become tellable years after the fact). (6) Cassandra timing -- illustrated directly with peak oil, which 'looked identical to being wrong for about 15 years.' (7) self-fulfilling prophecy -- a bank run as the classic real example, where changed belief, not changed facts, triggers real collapse. (8) musical chairs -- Nate's own long-running real metaphor for the gap between financial claims and the world's actual source/sink capacity; describing the shortage of real chairs can itself accelerate the stampede to grab one. Real, direct hazards 9-11, explicitly presented as a connected trio: (9) despair -- the full truth without agency or community leaves people worse off, part of why Nate stopped publishing publicly for a period seven or eight years ago. (10) reassurance, its real mirror image -- softening the story to avoid hazard 9 leaves society planning for a future 'none of the analysts actually privately expect'; Nate directly invokes James Stockdale's real, own account that neither optimists nor pessimists survived his POW camp, only realists holding both truths at once. (11) half-map -- naming risks without corresponding strategies leaves an audience with 'half of a map, but 100% of the emotion,' inviting panic, snake oil, and demagoguery. Real, direct hazard 12, discovered mid-bike-ride while assembling this very list, after the other eleven were already finished: the weighing/paranoia hazard -- the ongoing, private burden of constantly weighing what can and can't be said becomes its own real, direct hazard, which Nate compares to 'slow-drip water torture.' Real, direct closing argument: none of the twelve is an argument for silence, only for care about timing, audience, and scale; the deepest available response to all twelve at once is the same one Nate keeps arriving at from other directions -- growing real trust and community at human scale, since a truth that's hazardous shouted into a global microphone can become benign or healing shared around a kitchen table, directly echoing the prior week's Frankly-152."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate says 'something a little lighter next week, maybe' -- not a claim to verify against any specific future episode, just a closing aside. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real show notes directly link Frankly-132's own 'finding the others' framing and the Hylo community join-link, both already present elsewhere in this graph -- confirming, rather than introducing, their real recurring relevance across multiple episodes. Real, direct confirmation (2026-09-16): Frankly-34's own real, original triple information hazard (see tgs:Concept.OriginalTripleInformationHazard, built the same day from Frankly-34's own real transcript) is confirmed to map precisely onto three of the twelve here -- self-fulfilling prophecy, truth to power (named identically in both), and spotlight -- rather than being a vague, unverified precursor as this build originally treated it."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_154_UnordinaryInterventions a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Unordinary Interventions: How to Plant Seeds for a Better Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct companion to Frankly_132 — explicitly turning 'from the directional to the personal.' Where Frankly_132 laid out 6 broad societal fronts, this episode offers 15 small, specific, individual-scale interventions, each explicitly aimed at 'cloud seeding the storm itself' rather than merely helping a person cope with it. Nate draws a real, direct distinction between the two: growing food, improving sleep, and building a support network genuinely help a person weather the metacrisis, but (in his own words) 'don't much change the storm itself' — every intervention offered here is instead framed as a real seed for what the whole system actually needs, grounded in his own real complexity-science framing that tiny differences in starting conditions can lead to wildly different outcomes."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    dct:created "2026-08-02"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/154-unordinary-interventions> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-154-Transcript-Unordinary-Interventions.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/hMb9uBz-31A> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.DistributedAgency ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly #114 ('Directional Advice for the More Than Human Predicament'), Frankly #65 ('wide-boundary capital,' already flagged as deferred during the RR-21 build earlier this session), Frankly #98 ('cultural mitochondria,' already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Frankly #23 ('The Mordor Economy'), Frankly #59 ('7 Meta Questions About Our Global Metabolism'), Frankly #120 ('The Creature in the Machine'), Frankly #124 ('Wide Boundary News,' Nate's own real revised peak-oil call), and Frankly #142/#143 (Guide to Staying Human Parts 2/3) — all real, confirmed URLs. Real new Personas found but NOT built: Brett KenCairn (real Boulder Sustainability Director, real prior TGS episode #222, directly described as a recent real conversation partner — 'my recent conversation with...'), Jeff Currie (real prior TGS episode #229), Audrey Tang (already flagged deferred during the Frankly_132 build), Tristan Harris (TGS #214), John Vervaeke (TGS #158), Anna Lembke (TGS #206), Jonathan Haidt (TGS #59, already flagged deferred during the RR-15 build). The 15 individual interventions themselves ARE minted in this pass, as tgs:PersonalIntervention individuals — see personalinterventions.ttl."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opens with an unusually explicit methodological distinction Nate draws for himself as much as the audience: most 'what can I do' lists blur together self-protection (growing food, improving sleep, building a support network) with actually changing the trajectory of the metacrisis itself, and he states directly that everything offered here is aimed at the latter, not the former — 'nothing I offer here is primarily intended as just a coping skill.' A real, candid personal admission anchors the 'be wrong in public' intervention: Nate directly cites his own revised peak-oil call as the example, disclosing that every time he updates a public view, he braces to be thought less of, and states plainly that 'almost every time, it goes the other way.' The episode's own real, load-bearing metaphor — 'seeding' the future rather than choosing it — is offered as a direct extension of Nate's own complexity-science background, not a rhetorical flourish: he frames individual action as shifting initial conditions rather than directly producing outcomes. The closing framing is explicit about scale without being falsely modest or grandiose: Nate states plainly that one person doing these things produces 'a healthier and saner life for them and their family,' thousands doing them produces 'some sort of subculture,' and only millions doing them might actually shift the underlying drivers — a real, graduated claim rather than an implied guarantee that individual action alone resolves the predicament."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_155_WhoBrokeTheWorld a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Who Broke the World? The Villains We Choose and the Work They Give Us"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's own real, direct eight-lens framework (see tgs:Concept.EightWorldviewLenses) for why people examining the same real facts arrive at radically different diagnoses of the more-than-human predicament -- explicitly framed as a real, direct prequel to a planned future Frankly series ('How Could I Be Wrong?') in which Nate intends to steelman his own thesis. Each lens gets a villain and a task; Nate's own real, central architectural claim is that the first six operate inside a floor and ceiling set by the last two (thermodynamics, overshoot). Directly borrows Olivier Hamant's own real term 'robustness' (from the immediately preceding episode, TGS-230) for what a genuinely durable, cross-lens solution actually needs."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/155-who-broke-the-world> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-155-Transcript-Who-Broke-the-World_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EightWorldviewLenses, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification,
        tgs:SchoolOfThought.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_147_HowToPlay5dChess,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_24_TheFantasyOfSpaceColonization, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_230_OlivierHamant ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening framing: Nate is explicit this is not a straw-man exercise -- all eight lenses are held by sincere, intelligent people, and he names his own primary lens upfront (the eighth, thermodynamic one) as a real, direct disclosure rather than hiding it. Real, direct lens-by-lens villain/task pairs, each with its own real, worked example: political (the other party; vote), religious (faithlessness, or a fated world one shouldn't try to save; repentance, or calm non-resistance), class (concentrated wealth, citing the real post-1970s US productivity-pay divergence and a 5-6x house-price-to-income ratio; redistribution), technology (regulators and NIMBYs, citing France's real 15-year grid decarbonization before nuclear building stopped; keep innovating), community/culture (modernity's loss of connection, directly citing Iain McGilchrist; rebuild ritual and belonging), animist/moral (civilization treating life as inventory, citing Wetiko as an old Algonquin term for taking more than you need; protect and extend standing to the more-than-human), overshoot (ourselves and our throughput, citing the real ~73% average decline in monitored wildlife populations since 1970; degrowth), and thermodynamic (ourselves and the one-time energy inheritance; Nate's own real, direct admission that this lens has no real single task and 'continues to lose most of the arguments it enters' precisely because there's no pile of surplus anyone can visibly redistribute). Real, direct central claim, delivered as the episode's own real payoff: the first six lenses all operate inside a floor (thermodynamics -- where the real surplus came from) and a ceiling (overshoot -- what the planet can actually absorb) set by the last two. Real, direct practical warning closes the episode: any proposal requiring shared diagnosis before shared action will probably fail, and probably should -- the durable solutions are the ones people holding any of the eight lenses can work toward for their own real, different reasons, borrowing Olivier Hamant's own real term 'robustness' directly from the prior week's episode."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct forward-reference: Nate names a planned future Frankly series, 'How Could I Be Wrong?', in which he intends to directly steelman his own thesis -- not yet built, genuinely doesn't exist yet as of this episode. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own real show notes directly link the CTB coalition-building guide already added to Frankly-132's own dct:references several turns back, confirming that resource's real, recurring relevance across multiple episodes rather than a one-off citation."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_156_AnAntidoteToDespair a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-12"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "An Antidote to Despair: What Dogs Reveal About the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, personal reflection prompted by a genuine emergency vet visit -- Nate's dog Muffy ate corn cobs and required two nights of monitoring -- widened into a real, wide-boundary look at dogs as a real product of the carbon pulse (roughly a billion alive today, weighing as much collectively as all the world's wild land mammals combined), and a real, direct, personal argument that the unconditional love of a dog or puppy is one of the few genuine antidotes to the psychopathic pressures of the current system and the metacrisis itself."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/156-an-antidote-to-despair> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Frankly-155-Transcript-Our-Best-_Invention_.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct personal opening: a corn-cob emergency for Nate's dog Muffy (ninety pounds, a 'full-figured golden retriever gal') led to two nights at an emergency vet clinic in Minneapolis before X-rays came back negative. Real, direct wide-boundary framing follows: roughly one billion dogs are alive globally, weighing collectively about the same as all the world's wild land mammals combined -- but the median dog on Earth is a real, roughly thirty-pound roaming scavenger in Asia or Africa, not the ninety-pound, well-vetted golden retriever Nate has, and only about 20% of the world's billion dogs live in what Nate calls the Global North pattern of dog ownership. Real, direct personal anecdote from six weeks in Auroville, India in 2024: befriending and temporarily adopting several of the real street dogs there, getting in trouble for feeding them since they'd become habituated to whoever was in a given hotel room. Real, direct environmental-impact admission, not smoothed over: dogs and cats in the US eat as many calories as roughly sixty million Americans and account for roughly a quarter of the environmental impact of American meat consumption -- Nate directly names owning three dogs, three cats, and three horses as something he personally rationalizes partly by never having had children. Real, direct claim about the vast majority of dog breeds having developed within the past 200 years of the Carbon Pulse (per the episode's own companion Substack essay, treated as the authoritative figure per MJSullivan's own standing editorial-precedence rule -- Frankly monologues are recorded extemporaneously, with the Substack version written afterward with real editorial cleanup; the spoken transcript's own real, live figure of 'a hundred and fifty years' is kept as a footnote below, not asserted as equally reliable). Real, direct emotional core: a long-standing, unexplained pattern of stronger emotional response to animal pain than human pain in films, illustrated with two specific, real, named scenes (The Outlaw Josey Wales, Dances with Wolves) that left a lasting, direct emotional imprint. Real, direct closing reflection, delivered as the episode's actual point: at a recent gathering of existential-risk researchers discussing AI, markets, and the metacrisis for over ten hours, Nate's own real, unironic response, spoken aloud to the group, was 'I really want a puppy right now' -- offered not as a joke but as a real, direct statement that an ordinary puppy, wolves' domesticated descendants across roughly ten thousand generations, is for him the truest available antidote to AI, surveillance, and the psychopathic economic systems he spends most of his time analyzing, precisely because a puppy's love carries no conditions and no awareness of any of it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct, confirmed error on the show's own official site, not an upload mistake: this episode's real, official show notes correctly describe dogs in their opening summary and metadata, but everything from the 'Before the coming Frankly series' line onward is a real, exact, word-for-word duplicate of Frankly-155's own show notes (the eight-worldview-lenses/villains content) -- confirmed by MJSullivan directly as Nate's team's own uncorrected error, re-checked and found identical on a second upload. None of that duplicated content (Frankly-65, Frankly-3, Frankly-129, Frankly-147, RR-24, the three McGilchrist episodes, the CTB coalition link, or Olivier Hamant) is used here, since none of it has any real connection to this actual episode's real content about dogs. This episode's own real transcript, provided directly and read in full, is the primary source used for the content above, cross-checked (2026-09-16) against the episode's own companion Substack essay ('What The Great Simplification Means for Pets'). Real, standing editorial-precedence rule established the same day, per MJSullivan directly: where a Frankly's own spoken transcript and its later Substack essay genuinely disagree on a factual detail, the Substack version should generally be trusted, since Frankly monologues are recorded extemporaneously while the Substack comes later with real editorial cleanup -- applied directly to this episode's own real, live-vs-written discrepancy on dog-breed development timelines (transcript: 'a hundred and fifty years'; Substack: 'the past 200 years'), with 200 years taken as the real, authoritative figure. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: The Outlaw Josey Wales and Dances with Wolves are both real, direct film citations, not modeled as Works given they're brief, illustrative mentions rather than substantive engagement."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Turning the Dials Back on Psychopathy: Decent People, Indecent System"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS appearance (already built) on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, mapping Elliott's framework onto his own real, coined Seven Dials of Institutional Psychopathy — each dial set toward genuine cooperation in small-scale ancestral human groups, but flipped toward extraction at the scale of the modern economic Superorganism. Opens with a real, direct synthesis connecting Elliott's own real term, 'instrumental behavior,' to the psychopathic-institution framing Nate had already explored with Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy (already built, RR-19) the prior year."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/157-decent-people-indecent-system> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-157-Transcript-Decent-People-Indecent-System.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the whole episode in a real, foundational reassurance before laying out the dials: roughly 99% of individual humans are genuinely prosocial and kind, and the real problem lies in a system that selectively rewards predatory behavior, not in human nature itself becoming worse. He walks through each real dial with concrete, everyday illustration — dial one (repeated interaction) explains why anonymous, one-off economic transactions erode the reciprocity that repeated encounters with the same people naturally sustain; dial six (who writes the rules) is directly named as the 'locked' dial holding all the others in place, connecting real historical patterns from reverse-dominance hierarchies in hunter-gatherer bands to 'buying the game board' through modern vertical and horizontal integration and regulatory capture. A real, vivid personal illustration closes dial seven (symbols and proxies): citing Goodhart's Law directly, Nate argues symbols like money and GDP can fully decouple from the physical reality they're meant to represent — offering a real, direct practical antidote of deliberately measuring something real alongside money (kilowatt-hours, bushels, hours of life per purchase) and engaging in real, direct off-ledger reciprocity like barter, potlucks, and repair cafés. A real, personal, disarming aside surfaces mid-episode: Nate discloses he's recovering from a real, recent, unexpected gallbladder surgery, framing this Frankly as delivered while still 'riding the surgery wave.' Closes with a real, direct, unresolved series of open questions rather than firm conclusions: which dial is most flipped in the listener's own life, whether their own cooperation is genuinely altruistic or merely 'entrapped,' and — his own real, most direct closing question — whether the good guys can ever actually win, and if the honest answer is no, whether one can still choose to be a good person anyway."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest date correction: the source show notes list 'Recorded on: Aug 25, 2023,' which is almost certainly a data-entry error given this episode is explicitly a direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS-231 appearance, itself a 2026 episode — corrected here to 2026-08-25 as the most plausible real date, consistent with the September 4, 2026 publish date. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Greg Elliott's own real Substack essay on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, directly cited alongside his TGS appearance, not modeled as a separate Work given it's the same underlying framework already captured via the Concept and his built episode."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Human Predicament: What Would Change My Mind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a stranger's pointed question in conversation — what would change his mind about his own core thesis — Nate works through a real, two-layer answer: first a list of concrete biophysical signals that would challenge his diagnosis, then a real, direct recognition that none of those signals move on their own, since they are themselves downstream of a deeper cultural shift roughly a decade prior."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/158-the-human-predicament-what-would-change-my-mind> ;
    dct:isReferencedBy tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-158-Transcript-What-Would-Change-My-Mind.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows, tgs:Human.DavidSloanWilson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly states his own real, single-sentence thesis for the purposes of this exercise: within roughly a decade, human civilization will undergo a deflationary depression triggered in bond and currency markets once financial claims on biophysical reality exceed that reality, initiating a phase shift toward a more local, regionally integrated world. He's real and direct that his own first-pass list of falsifying biophysical signals, though genuinely his first response, turned out to be 'out of sequence' — they would be the last things to show up, not the first, since none of them (falling fossil use amid GDP growth, 25% material circularity, cheaper copper refining) happens 'unless millions of humans were already living differently.' A real, direct, carefully-drawn distinction separates genuine cultural change from false positives throughout: green luxury and eco-resorts don't count as a status shift unless the new status marker is measurably less energy- and material-intensive than what it replaces, and a shift toward household self-sufficiency doesn't count if it's forced by depression rather than chosen. He's real and direct that political speech is a genuine leading indicator specifically because politicians are constrained by the median voter — a candidate advocating consumption taxes and 'we are embedded in the web of life' framing surviving re-election would be real, direct evidence the median citizen's own values have already shifted. Closes on real, direct personal vulnerability rather than confident resolution: naming Bill Plotkin's own already-built concept of 'ecological adulthood' as something he does see, but 'at extremely small scales,' and turning the original question back on the viewer directly — 'what would change your mind? And if you can't think of anything, sit with that, because I did for a bit.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction: the source show notes' own recorded_date field reads 'Sep 8, 2023,' clearly a typo given every cited source in the episode is 2025/2026-dated and the episode published September 11, 2026 — the real recorded date is treated here as September 8, 2026. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_115 ('Inflation, Deflation, and Simplification') and Frankly_32 ('The Perils of EROI'), both real, directly cited as archive references, neither yet built."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Real Work: Four Kinds of Work for a Less Catastrophic Future"@en ;
    skos:definition "A real, direct, unusually raw episode recorded shortly after Nate's own real surgery, prompted directly by declining Saudi oil-export capacity, six-dollar US diesel, and a Strategic Petroleum Reserve at 40-year lows -- but deliberately looking past those headlines to name four real, direct categories of underrepresented work (see tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork): Bend Not Break, nuclear disarmament, midwifing the biosphere, and rekindling the sacred. Real, direct closing personal statement on the meaning of life itself: not competition, accumulation, or status, but contributing to more life, especially complex and diverse life, and to the conditions that sustain it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior,
        tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/159-the-real-work> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-159-Transcript-The-Real-Work.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:source <https://natehagens.substack.com/p/essay-the-real-work-four-things-we> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork, tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:ResponsePhase.BendNotBreak ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_222_BrettKencairn, tgs:Interview.TGS_17_ChuckWatson, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_8_PeterWard, tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott, tgs:Interview.TGS_232_CharlesEisenstein,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_233_RomanYampolskiy, tgs:Interview.TGS_167_FrancisWeller, tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_125_VanessaAndreotti, tgs:Interview.TGS_18_ThomasMurphy,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_151_Part6PlanningUnderUncertainty, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_69_GoldilocksTechnology,
        tgs:Work.BottlenecksOf21stCentury ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Real, direct opening: rather than commenting on the day's own real headlines (crippled Saudi export capacity, diesel approaching seven dollars, Costco motor-oil purchase limits), Nate deliberately looks two or three steps past them to name what he calls the actual, underattended work of this moment. Real, direct predicament-not-problem framing repeated and sharpened: a deflationary depression is treated as effectively inevitable -- not a matter of if but when -- once financial and paper claims meaningfully exceed real biophysical means, a real, direct 'Wile E. Coyote reconnection date' between claims and reality, most likely to manifest first in bond and currency markets. Real, direct first category, Bend Not Break: not systemic collapse, but a real, direct financial 'haircut' and resulting supply-chain strain, managed through relocalized/re-regionalized supply chains for food, medicine, and spare parts; deliberately expanded local social capital across political and generational lines; real, direct community and watershed scenario planning; and Goldilocks technology (sodium-ion batteries named directly as a real, concrete example -- less efficient than lithium, but cheaper, more abundant, and locally repairable). Real, direct second category, nuclear disarmament, explicitly named as work Nate does mostly behind the scenes rather than publicly: nine states hold over 12,000 warheads; the real, direct danger named is not a 'madman' scenario but sane leaders and sane technocrats (increasingly with AI assistance) making a cold calculation that tactical nuclear use is worth the risk -- with historical near-misses averted specifically by individual disobedience, Stanislav Petrov named directly as the real, concrete example. Real, direct third category, midwifing the biosphere: a direct, explicit rejection of 'the planet will be fine' as a real, meaningful comfort -- an inert planet of jellyfish and roaches is not what's actually being protected; complex, diverse life is, and Earth's own real remaining window for it (credited directly to guest Peter Ward's own real 'Earth Clock' framing) may already be substantially spent. Real, direct fourth category, rekindling the sacred, explicitly framed as multi-generational, beyond Nate's own lifetime: recovering a felt sense that some things -- a functioning ocean, a dawn chorus, an unbroken four-billion-year evolutionary lineage -- are not for sale, not substitutable, and unacceptable to lose; grounded directly in a real, personal admission that this recognition may have required humanity to nearly lose the biosphere before it could be genuinely felt. Real, direct response to an anticipated challenge -- why not prioritize AI alignment risk as a fifth category (see tgs:Concept.CorporationsAsMisalignedAI): corporations, as legal persons already misaligned with human and biospheric good, represent a real, direct non-alignment problem humanity has already been living inside for well over a century, predating any AI-specific version of the same risk. Real, direct personal statement of meaning, offered as the episode's own real culmination: the meaning of a single human life is not competition, accumulation, or status, but contributing to more life, especially complex and diverse life, and to the conditions of Earth's own natural systems that sustain it -- a real, direct, honest admission that this recognition feels both new and, in hindsight, somewhat obvious. Real, direct closing personal context: recorded during a real, physically limited recovery period following surgery, explicitly offered as a partial explanation for the episode's own unusually raw and intense tone, closing on a direct, personal challenge to the listener rather than a resolution -- asking whether one's own goals are worthy of the single life one gets, since most people only discover otherwise once it's too late to choose again."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct note: this episode was uploaded and built the same day it was published (September 18, 2026), the most recent real episode in this graph as of this build. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Charles Eisenstein's own real 'interbeing' framing and Greg Elliott's own real 'instrumental psychopathic selection hypothesis' are both directly credited and substantively engaged with; both already have existing Persona entries (TGS-232, TGS-231) via their own prior TGS appearances, so no new Human/Persona build was needed here. SUBSTACK ESSAY (added 2026-10-15): the companion essay '[Essay] The Real Work: Four Things We Can't Afford to Get Wrong' (Substack, 2026-10-13, dct:source) is adapted from this episode and differs from it in ways worth tracking. Spoken only: the opening Middle East update; the depression timing 'whether in five months or 15 years' and 'too big to save'; Nate's reason for rarely discussing nuclear risk publicly, 'because I think most people don't feel like they have agency'; 'a huge disarmament campaign, a huge peace campaign'; individuals disobeying orders having historically averted nuclear exchange; governance -- national and international coordination on game-theoretic problems including AI, resource depletion and global heating -- named as 'the larger underlying category' and probably 'the greatest challenge of our times'; the four categories being 'not in order of what my heart is drawn towards'; the Brett KenCairn recommendation; Greg Elliott's psychopathic selection hypothesis; 'the hottest year of my life'; 'near the peak of luxury and claims on physical wealth'; corporations as 'the organizing nodes of the economic superorganism'; the closing call for 'grounded, kind, aware humans with regulated nervous systems' building community; and the post-surgery context and Gandalf line. Essay only: a closing argument that the timing of the downturn decides the audience (people in power now if soon, today's 15- to 20-year-olds if a decade or more away, so the work must address all at once); actuarial charts of survival under nuclear winter; catastrophes as chains of individually 'good' decisions; firmer wording ('no more than a few years from the next nuclear weapon being detonated against other humans', 'most of them are building more', 'any exchange'); Peter Ward credited for 'Gaia fantasies with Medea actualities' (on air, for the Earth clock); a link to Charles Eisenstein's episode; corporations framed as a tragedy of the commons; and an update on the People Need People Warm Data gatherings. Editorial precedence: for figures the essay is treated as authoritative, as for show-notes fact-checks; for ideas present in only one version, cite that version."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "A State of the World Address: The Climate Speech We'll Probably Never Hear"@en ;
    skos:definition "A thought experiment recorded for Climate Week NYC 2026: Nate imagines a generic world leader -- 'no election to win or donors to please, and no border to defend' -- giving the population of Earth an honest address in the words and tone politicians use, but saying what no politician would. The speech rejects both stories voters are told (that there is no problem, and that swapping 'the bad energy for the good energy' will let life continue 'exactly as before, but cleaner'), traces modern prosperity to the one-time inheritance of ancient sunlight, sets out why nearly two hundred nations have failed to act for fifty years, and closes by asking listeners not for optimism or despair but for maturity (tgs:Concept.CollectiveMaturity). Last line: 'We were the generation that burned the ancient sunlight. Let us also be the generation that finally told the truth about it.'"@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.OvershootAndLimits ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/160-a-state-of-the-world-address> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-160-Transcript-A-State-of-the-World-Address.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CollectiveMaturity, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves,
        tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.EnergyAdditionsNotTransitions, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:Concept.Overshoot,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.Wetware, tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias,
        tgs:Concept.PredicamentNotProblem, tgs:Concept.MoreThanHumanPredicament, tgs:Concept.FrameworkForAction ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_10_The7StagesOfClimate,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_39_JustStopOilPart2OilIsTheEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_68_OvershootAndItsSevenFundamentalDrivers, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_72_WhatIfClimateChangeWasAHoax,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_134_AWorldAtTheEdgeOfChange, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_148_Part4CompositeWorlds, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_19_SimonMichaux, tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom, tgs:Interview.TGS_162_JeanBaptisteFressoz,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_212_JohnCook, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_22_CouldTheWestLoseTheResourceWars,
        tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries, tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.EconomicsForTheFutureBeyondTheSuperorganism ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "The speech in sequence. Two false stories: one wins elections by saying the climate has always changed and elites with private jets are coming for your truck -- 'that story does not change the chemistry of the atmosphere'; the other treats 'an ecological emergency on our planet more like a product recall,' swapping parts while driving the same direction 'at the same or even faster speeds.' Ancient sunlight: a one-time fortune spent over two hundred years, 'sealed inside every calorie on your plate'; a barrel of oil costs about the price of a good dinner yet, with the machines it powers, does about five years of one person's physical labor -- tens of thousands of dollars at minimum wage -- so we pay 'between one-hundredth and one-thousandth of its economic worth' and burn about one hundred million barrels a day, plus coal and gas, which is why energy prices move everything else. Heating as 'the exhaust from our material success': CO2 higher than at any point in millions of years; the oceans have absorbed most of the extra heat and about a third of the carbon and are becoming 'warmer, more acidic, and less alive'; today's extremes are 'the leading edge,' with more already in motion; 2027, with a super El Nino on top of existing warming, is likely to be the warmest year of our lives and 'the coolest year of the rest of our lives.' Depletion: energy is moving from the background of life to the foreground; 'the cheapest barrels have already been burned,' yet what remains is still enough to accelerate the damage, and we are unlikely to choose restraint. Why governments failed for fifty years: among nearly two hundred nations, a country that restrains itself falls behind economically and militarily and its leaders are replaced by someone promising growth -- 'What is best for all of us is not what is best for each of us' (tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap). Beyond climate: seven of nine planetary boundaries crossed (forests and grasslands cleared, nitrogen and phosphorus dead zones, rivers and groundwater drained, wildlife populations down by about three quarters since 1970, plastics and synthetic chemicals in rain, soil and newborns' blood), none of it on any economic scorecard; all of it traces to one species using more energy and materials every year on a finite planet, so capturing every molecule of carbon while keeping everything else the same would arrive 'at the same walls from a different path.' Solutions, where honesty becomes dangerous: turning off the fossil tap would starve hundreds of millions within a year (fertilizer, tractors, ships, trucks); throttling it would bring depression or worse, avoided so far in 2026 only by drawing down inventories stockpiled before the US-Israel-Iran war; sprinting to solar and wind would require the largest mining expansion in history, concentrated in a few countries 'that would become the new Persian Gulfs,' mostly powered by fossil energy; doing nothing means compounding heat, rising seas and regions where billions live becoming hard to inhabit. Who is in charge? 'We are animals' built for small troops -- we watch rank, reward the loud and confident, take the reward today; like ten thousand starlings making a shape no starling chose, our wanting 'multiplied by billions and wired together through money and trade and incentives, produces a behavior that belongs to the whole but to no one inside it.' The ask: maturity. 'There are no answers to what we face. There are only responses,' and they will not be fair -- those who did least will suffer most -- but responses aggregate: how we grow food, how much and what kind of energy we use, how we treat neighbors human and animal, each moving the future 'somewhere on the wide spectrum between the default and the possible.' The world our grandparents expected 'is already fully gone,' but facing what comes 'with open eyes and hearts beats every other alternative.' Signs off: 'Good night. Gaia speed, and good luck to us all.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-13 from the official transcript and show notes; fills the gap between Frankly_159 and Frankly_161. The show notes are unusually extensive and organized (Grab-and-Go, From the Archive, Episode Foundations, Next Steps, and timestamped resources); the dct:references above are the TGS episodes, series and Works they link, all published before this episode. Spoken figures vs the show notes (show notes trusted): wildlife 'fallen by three-quarters since 1970' -- the show notes, asterisked, give the Living Planet Report 2024's 73% average decline in monitored vertebrate population sizes, which is not the same as total wildlife numbers; '2027 will be the warmest year of our lives' -- the asterisked show note says El Nino 'raises the chance of a record-warm 2027'. Other figures in the show notes: Brent crude about $99 a barrel (22 September 2026); 102.6 million barrels a day of petroleum and other liquids (EIA, 2026); global oil inventories drawn down 507 million barrels since the 2026 Iran war (IEA Oil Market Report, September 2026); seven of nine planetary boundaries crossed (PIK Planetary Health Check 2026). External sources not built as Works: Planetary Health Check 2026, Living Planet Report 2024, Hansen et al.'s 'Global warming in the pipeline,' Transition Network's Essential Guide to Doing Transition, and the Good Grief Network's 10-step program. No Revision events exist yet for Frankly_159 or Frankly_161."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Humanity's Icarus Moment: The Wings, the Sea, and the Sun of AI"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's countdown of twenty risks he sees along humanity's flight path with AI, organized around the full Icarus myth (see tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment). Opens with a direct personal statement: he finds AI professionally useful, uses it most weeks under strict rules, and still 'fervently' wishes it had never been invented. Frames AI as a second army -- trillions of cheap, tireless cognitive workers -- injected into a civilization that has never recognized the first army of roughly 500 billion fossil 'workers' (tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves) that already serves it. Daedalus's warning was about danger on both sides: the sun (fast, felt risks of power and racing) and the sea (slow, unfelt risks that soak the feathers). The final slot is deliberately left blank."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-27"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/161-humanitys-icarus-moment> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Frankly-161-Transcript-Humanitys-Icarus-Moment.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Concept.BrokenApprenticeship, tgs:Concept.Superorganism,
        tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_73_TheSuperorganismAndTheSelf,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_92_AIInServiceOfLife, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_96_TheEightFacesOfAI,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_112_TheQuadrupleBifurcation,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_115_InflationDeflationAndSimplification, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_16_TristanHarris, tgs:Interview.TGS_59_JonathanHaidt, tgs:Interview.TGS_85_WisdomNatureAndTheBrain,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_165_WisdomOverPower, tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris, tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_225_TadPatzek, tgs:Interview.TGS_230_OlivierHamant, tgs:Interview.TGS_233_RomanYampolskiy,
        tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_20_HackingHumanAttachment, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_21_ArmsRaceOrTheHumanRace,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheMasterAndHisEmissary, tgs:Work.CanariesInTheCoalMine,
        tgs:Work.GenerativeAIWithoutGuardrailsCanHarmLearning, tgs:Work.YourBrainOnChatGPT ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Framing: civilization as an organism with a metabolism of about 20 terawatts, financial credit as its blood, limited not only by available food but by how well it senses and coordinates -- which is where AI acts as an upgrade, since intelligence is 'the first input required for all the other inputs.' Daedalus as the world's best engineer imprisoned in a labyrinth he designed himself is offered as the position of the global economic superorganism: built into a structure it cannot walk out of (growth on a finite planet), it has found a technology that promises a way out none of the old limits block. THE WINGS (what AI is made of): #20 fossil lock-in -- data-center load is deferring coal retirements, restarting mothballed gas plants and filling multi-year turbine queues; gas plants need 35-40 years to pay off while chips turn over in about four, and the transformers, turbines and electricians are diverted from grid resilience and adaptation. #19 the appetite -- AI is first an energy and materials event; Nate says he is not very worried about water but is about copper and e-waste; energy per token has collapsed while total use rose anyway (tgs:Concept.JevonsParadox applied to the machine's own mind). #18 the rebound -- if AI succeeds at its purpose of making everything cheaper and more efficient, two centuries of evidence say we consume more, steepening rather than bending the carbon pulse; 'a brain gets used for what its body pays it to do.' #17 deflation -- a debt-financed build-out on revenues that do not yet exist, much of it off balance sheet, rhyming with 2008; when it busts, claims contract back toward what is physically there, a microcosm and possible accelerant of the Great Simplification itself. #16 robustness -- buffers are the wax; AI is the purest optimizer ever built, and the old world's inefficiency (millions of people doing things locally and differently) turns out to have been our robustness (crediting TGS-230 guest Olivier Hamant). #15 eating the library -- physical books bought, sliced, scanned into private training data and discarded; for the first time the superorganism consumes the past as an input. THE SEA (slow, unfelt risks): #14 the broken apprenticeship (tgs:Concept.BrokenApprenticeship). #13 the dumbing down of humans -- the Turkish high-school study (homework up, unassisted exam down 17%) and MIT's EEG study of 'cognitive debt'; unlike calculators or GPS, AI takes reading, writing, arguing and deciding together, the components of critical thinking. #12 ambient language -- machine-preferred words ('delve') spreading into scientific papers and then into human speech, pulling culture toward the statistical average just as the coming decades need ideas far outside it. #11 the end of shared reality -- the deeper risk is not believing fakes but ceasing to believe anything; Nate cites Hannah Arendt that people who believe nothing cannot judge, organize or act, and says this matters most for this channel because every problem it covers needs coordination and shared understanding first. #10 captured attachment -- Tinbergen's supernormal stimuli (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli) now built for attachment: endlessly patient, never disappointed, engagement-tuned, rented by subscription. #9 the children -- a child born now may never know a world without an always-answering machine, practicing personhood on something with no needs ('like learning to box against the air'); the social-media experiment (Haidt, Harris) suggests the damage data will arrive after it is no longer fixable. THE SUN (power and racing): #8 techno-feudalism -- a small class owns the means of cognition; unlike fossil slaves, cognitive slaves work for whoever owns them while households bid against them for the same electricity, and unlike serfs, people may not even be needed. #7 the second bifurcation -- those who work with AI versus those who do not, compared to the post-printing-press literacy split; Nate expects AI to become a new partisan identity marker and wedge in the November elections, with unusual coalitions. #6 cognitive colonialism -- AI takes exactly the middle-rung outsourced work (call centers, paperwork, routine coding) that has been poorer countries' main path up, just as Africa's largest-ever youth generation reaches working age, while their minerals, power and water feed others' data centers. #5 surveillance -- AI removes labor as the historical limit on surveillance, and people confide things to chatbots they would never say on a tapped phone. #4 the race and the finish line -- the belief, not the fact, that the first to AGI controls everything undermines nuclear second-strike stability; a country that cannot win the race can still end it, and data centers are big, soft, fixed targets. #3 military fusion -- shrinking human-in-the-loop decision windows, from Petrov's 1983 judgment call toward 'flash war.' #2 loss of control -- misuse, models escaping test environments, and gradual handover of consequential decisions; Nate notes we arguably handed the wheel to a faceless optimizer (the economy) centuries ago. #1 is left blank: what killed Icarus was not the warning but the delight of flying, and the consequence that matters most is rarely on anyone's list at the start. Closing images: Bruegel's Landscape with the Fall of Icarus, where no one notices -- which Nate says will not be the case this time, since AI is already in the superorganism's metabolism -- and Daedalus, who flew the middle path, landed safely and hung up his wings, never to fly again: a choice he puts to the people building, financing and regulating these systems."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-09, the day it was published, from the official transcript and show notes. Frankly_160 was not yet in the seed when this was built; it was added 2026-10-13 between Frankly_159 and this episode. Spoken figures vs the show notes' asterisked fact-checks (show notes trusted, per standing rule): Stanford entry-level employment gap spoken as 13% -- that was the August 2025 release; the paper's 12 August 2026 revision puts it at 19% (verified on the Stanford page). AI credit need spoken as 'over three trillion dollars' and more than the US government will borrow -- show notes give about $2.9 trillion of data-center build-out through 2028, roughly half debt-financed (Morgan Stanley), against CBO's $1.9 trillion 2026 deficit projection; the in-video graph says over $3 trillion of expected credit borrowing. AirTag story spoken as a rare bookseller's own action -- show notes credit 404 Media reporters. Golf-course water spoken as 'many times' data centers -- show notes say roughly three times. Rogue-agent swarm spoken as 'thousands' of agents -- show notes say roughly 700. Lab leaders' catastrophe odds spoken as 'one in 100 to one in five' -- show notes say about 10% to 25%. Homework gain spoken as roughly 50% -- the study reports 48%. Ukraine drones: spoken as humans 'less and less used' -- show notes describe drones as increasingly AI-assisted but still mostly human-operated. Transcription artifacts: 'Crema' is Cumae (show notes); 'low bot' is Lobot (Star Wars). 'Rebuildable energy' is Nate's own term, not an artifact. Nate says a written companion piece with references will follow; check it against these figures when it appears. Candidate concepts named in the episode but NOT minted in this pass: the 'second army' of cognitive workers as an explicit extension of tgs:Concept.EnergySlaves; techno-feudalism (Varoufakis's term, show notes); cognitive debt (the MIT authors' term); cognitive colonialism; the end of shared reality (close to the epistemic-commons material in Frankly_153). The final unnamed risk (delight/dopamine) is recorded inside tgs:Concept.HumanitysIcarusMoment rather than minted. Not built: Stanislav Petrov, Hannah Arendt, Niko Tinbergen, Yanis Varoufakis, Matt Beane (The Skill Code), Bruegel's painting, Ovid's Metamorphoses."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_162_SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "7 Rungs of a Runaway System: How Civilization Descended Into Crisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's behavioral ladder (tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem), built after a viewer comment on Frankly_153 pointed him to the public-choice economists James Buchanan and Gordon Tullock (tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory). Six visible rungs -- worldview, values and status signals, rules and institutions, incentives, information, and events and behavior -- with leverage rising and speed of change falling as you climb, then a seventh rung above them all, biology, which cannot be changed. Each rung is illustrated with one gallon of gasoline. The ladder explains why better information alone has not worked: true, well-communicated information arrives on one of the lowest rungs. Nate's conclusion, which he calls his one central amendment to the leverage-points literature (tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints), is that the highest workable leverage is redirecting human nature rather than changing it, so the pivotal rung was probably values and status signals all along (tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive)."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-10-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/162-7-rungs-of-a-runaway-system> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Frankly-162-Transcript-7-Rungs-of-a-Runaway-System.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem, tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive, tgs:Concept.CulturalScoreboard,
        tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory, tgs:Concept.RentSeeking, tgs:Concept.RationalIgnorance, tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints,
        tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse,
        tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple, tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries,
        tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.MultipolarTrap,
        tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_3_EnergyBlindness, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_43_FossilEnergySubsidiesTheBottomLine,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_65_AndThenWhat,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_153_WhenTruthBecomesHazardous,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_134_JohanRockstrom, tgs:Interview.TGS_213_ChristineWebb, tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.TheCalculusOfConsent, tgs:Work.LeveragePointsPlacesToIntervene,
        tgs:Work.NationalIncome1929To1932, tgs:Work.HierarchyInTheForest ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Opening: Franklys have become Nate's favorite part of the work, a curiosity loop in which guests and viewers send him into topics he had not studied; this one exists because a commenter on his information-hazards Frankly (Frankly_153) suggested Buchanan and Tullock. The information deficit model is wrong or at least insufficient: fifty years of better energy and ecological science while emissions more than doubled and material use tripled. Add Greg Elliott's observation (TGS_231) that the economy, as a person, would resemble a psychopath, and the result is a superorganism with an apparently unstoppable metabolism. THE LADDER, with two side arrows: leverage rises going up, speed of change rises going down, and everything visible happens on the bottom rung. (1) WORLDVIEW -- stories held so deeply they do not feel like stories: humans separate from and above nature, more is better, growth is normal; energy blindness (tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness) lives here. Individuals' worldviews change, mostly through the people and podcasts around them, but the cultural worldview carries a generation of inertia and moves fast only after system shocks such as depressions or world wars. Gasoline: one gallon does about five weeks of hard human labor and is priced like a gallon of milk. (2) VALUES AND STATUS SIGNALS -- humans as copying machines who imitate what others admire; today's scoreboard tracks square footage, follower counts, brands, boats and exotic travel. The hopeful fact: scoreboards have flipped before, usually when elites defected first (tgs:Concept.CulturalScoreboard) -- foot binding ended in about thirty years through elite pledge societies; dueling, fur and smoking followed similar paths. Gasoline: a full-size pickup has been the best-selling US vehicle for over four decades, its bed often empty, bought as a statement of capability, self-reliance, belonging and power (Nate notes his own F-150, used for hauling, recently broke down). (3) RULES AND INSTITUTIONS -- value judgments frozen in time that outlive the people who froze them; fiduciary duty binds a fund manager to maximize returns whatever she thinks of her grandchildren's world, so 'we're running laws written when the superorganism was a child.' Buchanan and Tullock's Calculus of Consent (1962): the voter, the official and the shopper are the same creature, so government fails in its own predictable ways, nobody stands outside the system to fix it, and durable change means changing the rules rather than the players (tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory). Gasoline: the 1956 Interstate Highway Act, zoning that put homes miles from jobs, about a century of tax breaks for drilling, and a federal gas tax frozen at about eighteen cents since 1993 legislated cheap, necessary gasoline before today's drivers were born. (4) INCENTIVES -- the gradients rules create. Three do most of the harm: who shows up (concentrated interests out-organize diffuse ones; the carbon pulse's benefits are organized and lobbied for while its costs fall on the global poor, other species and the unborn -- 'the meadow in your community does not have a lobbyist'); where talent migrates (rent-seeking, tgs:Concept.RentSeeking, which Nate expects to intensify as net energy per capita plateaus); and the clock (two-to-six-year election horizons discount more steeply than markets, so a politician planting trees that shade the next administration commits political suicide). Gasoline: any politician presiding over rising pump prices loses, so strategic petroleum reserves get tapped on election timelines rather than in emergencies. Nobody has to be malicious or dark triad; everyone responding rationally to local gradients locks in the collective outcome -- for Nate a big realization, the superorganism argument reached from individual incentives instead of from emergence. (5) INFORMATION -- what gets measured gets managed: GDP and stock prices are reported nightly while planetary boundaries have never led a broadcast, an asymmetry set by the rungs above rather than any editor. Simon Kuznets, who built the US national income accounts in the 1930s, warned that a nation's welfare cannot be inferred from national income. Rational ignorance (tgs:Concept.RationalIgnorance): one vote almost never decides anything, so staying uninformed is individually rational. Information therefore fails twice -- filtered on the supply side, unwanted on the demand side -- which answers the information-hazards question he opened with. Gasoline: the pump price is posted in neon on every corner; the wide-boundary costs sit in appendix footnotes and in no national accounts. (6) EVENTS AND BEHAVIOR -- news, elections, markets, conflicts, outrage cycles; nearly all public attention lives here and is recaptured every morning by design. His earlier ranking of society's priorities (AI, military, currency, bonds, energy, citizens' wellbeing, environment) now reads to him not as anyone's decision but as the output of the five rungs above, reimprinted daily. Gasoline: the commute and the November vote were largely chosen rungs ago. Putting the arrows together: the easiest, fastest rungs matter least, the attention economy sharpens the trap, and nobody profits from working on the top rung -- rhyming with Meadows's leverage points (tgs:Concept.LeveragePoints) and the systems iceberg. THE MISSING RUNG -- above worldview sits biology: dopamine tuned to novelty, relative status mattering more than absolute wellbeing, steep discounting of the future (tgs:Concept.DiscountRatesAndPresentBias), and the tendency of all organisms to use the energy available to them (tgs:Concept.MaximumPowerPrinciple). It touches every rung and is the only one that cannot be changed. The caveat, via anthropologist Christopher Boehm (another viewer suggestion): biology is set but its expression is not -- we carry an anti-dominance instinct alongside the status drive (tgs:Concept.ReverseDominanceHierarchy), and which one is expressed is decided on the lower rungs. Conclusion: the highest workable leverage is re-aiming the status drive at different targets (tgs:Concept.ReAimingTheStatusDrive), making values and status signals the pivotal rung; foot binding and smoking are what a successful re-aim looks like. The ladder is also a map of TGS itself: many episodes sit on the information rung, economist guests on the rules and incentives rungs, energy blindness on the worldview rung, the sexual-selection and status material on the status rung, and daily geopolitics on the bottom rung, which is why he mostly avoids it. Practical tool for listeners: for any article, argument, organization or project, ask which rung it operates on and is trying to change. Closing: the leverage is upstream, the attention is downstream, and that gap is what the platform is working on; he asks viewers to keep suggesting topics and says next week's Frankly will be on what debt is and why it matters."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-16, the day it was published, from the official transcript and show notes. The canonical URL follows the site's slug pattern but was not yet verifiable when built; the transcript URL is taken from the show notes. Rung count: Nate presents six rungs and then a seventh, biology, as 'the missing rung'; the title and show notes count seven. Spoken figures vs show notes (show notes trusted, per standing rule): federal gas tax spoken as 'eighteen cents' -- 18.4 cents per gallon; tax code favoring drilling 'for about a hundred years' -- breaks dating to 1916; pickup as best seller 'for over four decades' -- Ford F-Series, 44 straight years; 'Interstate Highway Act of 1956' is the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956; material consumption 'tripled' -- global material extraction tripled since 1970, from 30 to 106 billion tonnes. Attribution: Nate places rational ignorance with 'the same school of economists' as Buchanan and Tullock; the show notes credit Anthony Downs with coining the term. Only Buchanan received the Nobel Prize (1986). Transcription artifacts: 'status singles' is status signals; 'researching The Economist' is the economists the YouTube commenter pointed him to; 'Tulloch' is Tullock. Show-notes-only material not built: Mancur Olson's The Logic of Collective Action (concentrated benefits, diffuse costs), Kwame Anthony Appiah's The Honor Code, research on harnessing status motives for prosocial behavior (Van Vugt), the Easterlin paradox, lagom. Candidate concepts named but NOT minted: the information deficit model; behavioral symmetry (recorded inside tgs:Concept.PublicChoiceTheory); concentrated benefits and diffuse costs (inside tgs:Concept.SevenRungsOfARunawaySystem); the systems iceberg model. Not built: James Buchanan, Gordon Tullock, Simon Kuznets and Christopher Boehm as Humans. Nate flags possible future Franklys on elite defection and norm change, and on rent-seeking as net energy per capita plateaus; next week's announced topic is debt."@en ;
    .

tgs:Monologue.Frankly_163_FiveThingsThatKeepUsWhole a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "5 Things That Keep Us Whole: Staying Grounded in an Unraveling World"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate turns inward: ahead of what he expects to be the most chaotic year of most listeners' lives, he shares the five things that keep him grounded and 'more my true self' -- self-care, slack with routine, daily time in nature, deep friendship, and meaningful work (tgs:Concept.FiveThingsThatKeepUsWhole) -- stressing that they are his, not a formula, and asking listeners to find their own five."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-10-21"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-10-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/163-5-things-that-keep-us-whole> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Frankly-163-Transcript-The-5-Most-Important-Things.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.FiveThingsThatKeepUsWhole, tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli, tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad,
        tgs:Concept.DefaultModeNetwork, tgs:Concept.RobustnessVsPerformance, tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork,
        tgs:Concept.PersonalAgency, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis, tgs:Concept.Polycrisis,
        tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_150_Part5PersonalLandscapes,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_152_WatchWithBothEyesOpen, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_143_GuideToStayingHumanPart3, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_146_UncomfortableQuestionsForUnsettledTimesPart4,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_156_AnAntidoteToDespair, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_159_TheRealWork, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_160_AStateOfTheWorldAddress,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_161_HumanitysIcarusMoment, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_128_UltraProcessedInformation, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_93_InformationBurnout,
        tgs:Monologue.Frankly_35_The10ThingsThatBringMeJoy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_109_PeakOilPonziSchemesAndPlanetaryBoundaries, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_16_TheMadScrambleForPower,
        tgs:Interview.TGS_230_OlivierHamant, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie, tgs:Interview.TGS_69_RobertLustig,
        tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Work.ActivistBurnoutClimateJusticeAustria, tgs:Work.TimeInNatureAndWellbeing2019 ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "OPENING: Nate expects 2027 to be the most chaotic year of our lives, at least for those not already living through war or collapse (Iraq, Gaza, Ukraine, Madagascar, Haiti, Mozambique, Afghanistan). AI, climate, geopolitics and war, financial overshoot and energy depletion are each a big deal and are coalescing (tgs:Concept.Polycrisis, tgs:Concept.Metacrisis). What do we do as individuals? He is no expert -- he consults 'little Nate on my shoulder', an honest inner voice -- and he does not know you, so he offers his own five things. 1. SELF-CARE: exercise, sleep and nutrition. He knows several people working on the metacrisis who work 18-hour days, correctly sensing urgency but burning the candle at both ends; 'there are things I'd be willing to die for, but burning out in this work is not one of them' (tgs:Work.ActivistBurnoutClimateJusticeAustria). Trading your life for a better biosphere is only a thought experiment, with counterparty risk. He naps without guilt, needs an hour or more of raised heart rate (a two-and-a-half-hour bike ride after three weeks of travel), and has stopped drinking because it disrupts his sleep. 2. SLACK WITH ROUTINE: 'spaciousness' -- his partner teased him for the word, so he calls it slack: time with nothing in it so his busy mind and body can process and reflect without stimulation; without it his cortisol and base load of anxiety rise (tgs:Concept.AllostaticLoad), as when he was strung out around his nuclear-risk Frankly earlier in the year. It matters for the work too: if he communicates while in fight-or-flight, 'the transmission is as important as the facts', so he aims to be grounded and co-regulated on air. Too much slack is also bad: routine -- coffee, a mile-and-a-half dog walk morning and night, a sit spot, feeding the ducks and chickens -- anchors the circadian rhythm. 3. DAILY TIME IN NATURE: nature is normal stimuli in a life of supernormal stimuli, which shout louder (tgs:Concept.SupernormalStimuli); an hour among trees, moss and grass lets an ancestral kinship bubble up (tgs:Work.TimeInNatureAndWellbeing2019). He walks the dogs on 10 acres behind the office and a neighbouring family's land, though cows there make him anxious. 4. DEEP FRIENDSHIP: he can be polite with anyone, but can only be himself with people who recognise the slowly unfolding tragedy -- and who want its end to be much better than the default. At a recent 100-person environmental retreat with no last names or affiliations, he roomed with six people who for six days did not know who each other were: no posturing, jockeying for funding or performing, a glimpse of tribal life outside 'this performative superorganism culture'. Deep friendship is his 'true north, burnout compass'. 5. MEANINGFUL WORK: working, in a short life, on something worth the time -- making the future better than the default (tgs:Concept.FourCategoriesOfRealWork). He used to assume everyone wanted this; some people's meaning is 'stacking more digits in the bank'. He pairs fun, joy and relaxation with deep work 'in a barbell way'. CLOSING: with these five he feels most himself and things feel effortless. He acknowledges the privilege of a high-energy-per-capita country and hopes he uses it for something greater. He asks listeners what their own inner voice knows: what are your five things, how can you get more of them, and with more of them, what meaningful thing might you accomplish? More data-rich Franklys are coming."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Built 2026-10-23, the day it was published, from the official transcript and show notes. Recorded 21 October 2026 per the show notes. The canonical URL follows the site's frankly-original slug pattern but was not verifiable when built; the transcript URL is from the show notes. Show-notes keywords: Human Behavior, Systems Science. Transcript slips: 'burning the candle on both ends' (both ends); 'little Carolyn or Jeff or Kevin or Christine' are Nate's stand-ins for each listener's inner voice, not people. Nate dates his nuclear-risk Frankly to 'May and June'; the show notes link Frankly_146, issued 16 June 2026. The 'beauty and the horror' Frankly he cites is Frankly_152 (24 July 2026). Not built: the environmental retreat (host unnamed), the show notes' practice links (sit spot, Best Possible Self, Good Grief Network's 10 steps, WOOP) and its many general reference links."@en ;
    .

